/* Held out: documents of every kind written blind on 2026-10-15 by eight
 * other writers, never read while the detector was tuned. Scored once
 * (ext-writing-kinds.test.js says how). Every document is invented. */
export const WRITING_KINDS_BLIND = [
  { kind: "argumentative", name: "high school essay arguing against a four-day school week, MLA heading", text: "Jaylen Brooks\nMrs. Okonkwo\nEnglish 10 Honors\n14 March 2025\n\nFive Days Is Not the Problem\n\nWhen our district sent out the survey asking families if they would support a four-day school week, most of my friends checked yes without thinking about it. Who wouldn't want every Friday off? But a longer weekend is not the same thing as a better education, and the four-day week would hurt the students who already have the least.\n\nThe first problem is what happens on the fifth day. Supporters say kids will rest, catch up on homework, or spend time with family. That might be true for students whose parents work from home or can pay for camps. For a lot of families in our district, Friday would just become another day to find childcare, and for many middle schoolers it would mean a day home alone. Schools are not only places to learn, they are where over 40 percent of our students get breakfast and lunch for free or reduced price. Take away a day and you take away two meals.\n\nThe second problem is the length of the remaining days. To keep the same number of instructional hours, the proposal adds about seventy minutes to Monday through Thursday. Anyone who has sat through seventh period knows that attention does not stretch forever. Adding more time at the end of the day when everyone is already exhausted is not the same as having a fresh morning on Friday.\n\nPeople in favor point out that some rural districts in Colorado and Missouri have used four-day weeks to save money and attract teachers. This is a fair point, and teacher shortages are real. But the savings in those districts have usually been small, around one or two percent of the budget, because the biggest costs, like salaries, don't go away. There are other ways to recruit teachers, such as raising pay or giving them more planning time inside the normal week.\n\nFinally, it's worth asking who the four-day week is really for. It is popular because it sounds nice to adults and teenagers who are tired. Being tired is real, but the answer to that is better schedules or less busywork, not cutting a whole day out of the week.\n\nOur district should vote no on the four-day week and instead look for changes that help students learn without leaving some of them behind every Friday." },
  { kind: "argumentative", name: "ESL college writing class essay arguing zoos should be phased out", text: "Zoos Should Not Exist in Modern Society\nIn many country, zoo is a place where family go on weekend to see animal they can not see in normal life. When I was child in Ho Chi Minh City, my father take me to Saigon Zoo every Tet holiday and I was very happy to see the elephant. But now I am adult and I study more about this topic, I believe that zoos should be close slowly and replace by sanctuary and wildlife park.\nFirst reason is the animal suffer in small space. A polar bear in the wild can walk many kilometer in one day, but in zoo the space is maybe one hundred time smaller. Scientist call the behavior \"zoochosis\", when animal walk same path again and again or bite itself. I saw this with my own eyes, the elephant in my city swing her head left and right all day. This is not normal behavior, this is sign of stress.\nSecond reason, many people say zoo is help to protect animal from extinction. It is true that some program like for the California condor was success. However, most animal in zoo are not endangered species, they are there because people like to see them. If the goal is really protection, the money can go to protect habitat in the nature, which help many species in same time.\nSome people will argue that zoo give education to children. But today children can watch documentary with very high quality, or visit sanctuary where animal live in bigger area. Seeing a sad lion behind glass do not teach respect for nature, it teach that animal is for human entertainment.\nIn conclusion, zoos was useful in the past but now we have better way. Government should stop to open new zoo and help the old zoo to change into sanctuary. I still remember my happy memory with my father, but I think the elephant was not happy like me." },
  { kind: "argumentative", name: "6th grade persuasive essay asking for longer recess, short and simple", text: "We Need Longer Recess\nby Addison P.  Period 3\n\nDo you ever feel like recess is over before it even starts? At our school we only get 20 minutes and by the time you get your jacket on and get outside its almost time to line up. I think recess should be at least 35 minutes and here is why.\n\nFirst, kids need to move. We sit in class almost all day and it is hard to focus when your legs are restless. My cousin's school in Texas has two recesses and she says it is way easier to pay attention after.\n\nSecond, recess is when you make friends. In class you cant really talk but at recess you can play games and meet people from other classes. Some kids are lonely and recess is the best chance to fix that.\n\nSome teachers might say we will lose learning time. But if we pay attention better we will actually learn more in less time so it evens out.\n\nIn conclusion, longer recess would make kids healthier, happier and better learners. Please make recess longer!!" },
  { kind: "argumentative", name: "polished college essay arguing college athletes should be allowed to unionize", text: "Employees in Everything but Name: The Case for College Athlete Unions\nFor most of its history, the National Collegiate Athletic Association defended its business model with a single word: amateurism. Athletes, the argument went, are students first, and paying them or allowing them to bargain collectively would destroy the educational character of college sports. That defense has been crumbling for years, most visibly after the Supreme Court's unanimous ruling in NCAA v. Alston (2021) and the arrival of name, image and likeness deals. Yet the most important question remains unsettled: should athletes be allowed to form unions and bargain with their schools? They should, because the conditions of their participation already resemble employment, because individual NIL deals leave most athletes without real protection, and because the alternative is a system in which the people who generate the revenue have the least say in how it is spent.\nStart with the conditions. A Division I football player typically spends well over forty hours a week on practice, film, travel, strength training, and mandatory team activities during the season, a figure the NCAA's own surveys have repeatedly confirmed. Schedules are set by coaches, not by athletes. Scholarships can be reduced or withdrawn for performance-related reasons in many programs, and players who transfer have historically risked losing a year of eligibility. In 2024 the regional director of the National Labor Relations Board in Boston found that Dartmouth's men's basketball players were employees under federal labor law precisely because of this degree of control. If an arrangement looks like a job, is controlled like a job, and produces revenue like a job, the burden should fall on the institutions to explain why it is not one.\nDefenders of the current model often argue that NIL money has already solved the problem. Athletes can now earn from endorsements, so why would they need a union? The trouble is that NIL income is wildly uneven. A small number of quarterbacks and star guards sign six- and seven-figure deals, while the backup offensive lineman, the women's rower, and the wrestler earn little or nothing. NIL also does nothing about the issues athletes most often raise: medical coverage after graduation, mental health support, practice hour limits, and protection from abusive coaching. These are classic subjects of collective bargaining, and no individual eighteen-year-old has the leverage to negotiate them alone.\nOpponents also warn that unionization would end Olympic and non-revenue sports, since schools would redirect money to pay football and basketball players. This concern deserves to be taken seriously, but it assumes that current spending is fixed and efficient. In reality, athletic departments at major programs have spent heavily on coaching salaries, which routinely exceed several million dollars a year, and on a facilities arms race. A bargaining process would force those priorities into the open. Unions in professional leagues have negotiated revenue-sharing systems that keep smaller teams viable; there is no reason a similar structure could not protect non-revenue sports.\nFinally, there is a basic question of fairness. College sports generated billions of dollars in media rights in the last decade. Conference commissioners, athletic directors, and coaches have all benefited. The only participants who were effectively prevented from organizing were the athletes themselves. Allowing them to unionize would not destroy college sports; it would make them honest about what they already are.\nThe NCAA has spent decades insisting that athletes are students first. Recognizing their right to bargain collectively would not change that. It would simply ensure that students who also work, and who work very hard, have a voice in the conditions of that work." },
  { kind: "argumentative", name: "unfinished rough draft arguing to lower the voting age to 16, with bracketed notes to self", text: "voting age draft 2 — DO NOT SUBMIT YET\n\nLowering the Voting Age to 16 [better title??]\n\nSixteen-year-olds in the United States can drive a car, hold a job, and pay taxes on what they earn. [check: do they actually pay income tax? yes if they earn over the standard deduction — word this better] Yet they have no say in electing the people who decide how that money is spent. Our state should lower the voting age to 16 for local and state elections.\n\nThe strongest reason is that voting is a habit. Research shows people who vote in the first election they're eligible for are more likely to keep voting for the rest of their lives. [FIND THE STUDY — Ms. R mentioned one from Denmark? or was it Takoma Park] At 18 most people are moving out, starting college or a job, and registering to vote is the last thing on their mind. At 16 most people still live at home and are in a civics class, which is a much better time to start.\n\nTakoma Park, Maryland has let 16 and 17 year olds vote in city elections since 2013 and [turnout stat here]. Austria lowered its voting age to 16 for national elections in 2007.\n\nThe main counterargument is that 16 year olds are not mature enough. [need to answer this better — brain development thing, the \"cold cognition\" vs \"hot cognition\" article Ms. R linked. Voting is cold cognition = slow decision with time to think, teens do fine at those]\n\nAnother argument against is that teens will just vote like their parents. but adults vote like their parents too?? that's not a reason to take away a vote\n\n[paragraph about how the issues affect us most — climate, school funding, student loans]\n\n[conclusion]" },
  { kind: "speech", name: "8th grade class secretary campaign speech", text: "Hi everyone, my name is Priya Raman and I'm running for 8th grade class secretary.\nI know what you're thinking. Secretary? Isn't that the person who just writes stuff down? Well yes, I will write stuff down. I'm actually really good at it. Ask anybody who has borrowed my science notes. But I also think the secretary should make sure everyone actually knows what student council is doing, because last year I didn't even know we had a student council until the spring dance got canceled.\nSo here's what I'll do. Every two weeks I'll post a short update on the announcements board and the class Google Classroom saying what council talked about and what got decided. No more surprises. I'll also put a suggestion box outside the library, a real one, and I will read every single suggestion, even the ones that just say \"pizza.\"\nI'm not going to promise you no homework or a pool on the roof, because I can't do that and neither can anyone else up here. What I can promise is that you'll always know what's going on.\nSo vote Priya for secretary. I'll take good notes. Thank you!" },
  { kind: "speech", name: "best man toast at older brother's wedding reception, casual", text: "Toast — Marcus & Dani\nFor those who don't know me, I'm Theo, Marcus's little brother, which means I've spent twenty-six years being blamed for things he did.\nI'm gonna keep this short because Dani's aunt told me I have four minutes and she looked like she meant it.\nWhen Marcus first told me about Dani he said, and I quote, \"I met someone who parallel parks better than me.\" For context, Marcus failed his driving test twice because of parallel parking. So I knew right away this was serious. This was a man admitting defeat.\nThe first time I met Dani was at Thanksgiving three years ago. Our mom made her famous sweet potato thing, the one with the marshmallows that nobody actually likes, and Dani had two helpings and asked for the recipe. Mom has talked about it every single holiday since. Dani, you didn't just win over Marcus that night. You won over the whole family, and you did it by lying about a casserole. That's commitment.\nBut seriously. Marcus has always been the guy who shows up. When I broke my wrist skateboarding in ninth grade, he drove two hours home from college at midnight. When Dad was sick, he was the one making the spreadsheets and the phone calls. He takes care of people. And watching him with Dani, I see someone who takes care of him back. She makes him laugh, she calls him out when he's being stubborn, which, Dani, good luck, and she makes him a better version of himself.\nSo if everyone could raise a glass. To Marcus and Dani. May your love be patient, your arguments be short, and may Dani always do the parallel parking.\nCheers!" },
  { kind: "speech", name: "eulogy for a grandmother read at her funeral", text: "Words for Grandma Lu\n\nThank you all for being here. Grandma would be embarrassed by how many of you came. She would also be counting how many of you she had already fed at some point, and I promise you the number is most of this room.\n\nLucille Marie Dubois was born in 1938 in Lafayette, Louisiana, the fourth of seven kids. She liked to say she learned to cook in self-defense, because if you weren't fast in that house, you didn't eat. She married my grandfather Henri in 1959, and they ran the bakery on Johnston Street for thirty-one years. If you grew up here, there's a decent chance your birthday cake came out of her oven.\n\nWhen I think about Grandma, I think about her hands. They were always doing something. Kneading dough, snapping beans, working a crossword in pen, and yes, sometimes pinching your arm when you talked during Mass. She was not a woman who sat still. Even in the hospital last month she asked the nurse for a notepad so she could write down her gumbo recipe for my cousin Nicole, because she didn't trust anyone else to get the roux right. Nicole, she said to tell you: darker than you think, and don't you dare walk away from it.\n\nGrandma was tough, and she was funny, and she was generous in a way that never made you feel like you owed her anything. After the hurricanes in '05, she kept the bakery open for two weeks giving bread away to anyone who walked in. She never talked about it. I only found out years later from a man who stopped me at the grocery store.\n\nI'm not going to pretend this is easy. The world is quieter without her, and a lot less well-fed. But I believe she'd want us to go home today, call somebody we love, and maybe make something from scratch even if it doesn't come out right the first time.\n\nWe love you, Grandma. Save us a seat at the table." },
  { kind: "speech", name: "valedictorian graduation speech built around getting lost freshman year", text: "Valedictory Address — Harlan Ridge High School, Class of 2026\n\nFour years ago, on the first day of freshman year, I got lost looking for room 214. I want to start there because I have a feeling some of you did too, and because I've been thinking a lot about getting lost.\n\nFor anyone who never figured it out, room 214 is not on the second floor. It's in the annex behind the gym, which was built in 1974 and apparently numbered by someone who hated freshmen. I wandered the second floor for eleven minutes before a senior, whose name I still don't know, took pity on me and walked me there. I was late. Mr. Castellanos made me introduce myself to the class as \"the person who found us eventually.\" That became my nickname for a month.\n\nHere's what I've realized since then. Almost everything that mattered to me at Harlan Ridge started with being lost. I joined the debate team because I walked into the wrong classroom sophomore year and was too embarrassed to leave. I found out I liked chemistry because I failed my first test and had to go to Ms. Pradhan's tutoring sessions, where for the first time someone explained a mole in a way that didn't make me want to cry. Some of my closest friends in this row right here are people I met when the bus broke down on the way to the regional band competition and we spent four hours in a Cracker Barrel parking lot.\n\nWe are about to be lost again. Some of us are going to college in places we've never been. Some of us are starting jobs, joining the military, taking a year to figure it out. Some of us honestly have no idea what's next, and I want to say clearly that that's allowed. Nobody in this gym had their life figured out at eighteen, and I say that with respect to every adult here, including the school board.\n\nThe temptation, when you're lost, is to pretend you're not. To walk confidently in the wrong direction because asking for help feels like admitting something. I did that for most of freshman year. I think a lot of us did. We spent so much energy looking like we knew where we were going.\n\nSo my one piece of advice, which you didn't ask for, is this: ask for directions. Ask the senior in the hallway. Ask the professor in office hours. Ask your parents, even though they'll be insufferable about it. Ask your friends in this row, who will still pick up the phone in ten years, I hope. The people who helped me most at this school never made me feel stupid for being lost. They just walked me to the right room.\n\nTo our teachers, thank you for walking us there, sometimes literally. To our families, thank you for the rides, the late dinners, and for pretending to understand what AP Calc BC is. And to the Class of 2026: I can't wait to see where we all end up, especially the ones of us who get there eventually.\n\nCongratulations. We made it." },
  { kind: "speech", name: "4-H livestock award acceptance speech written as numbered note cards", text: "Acceptance — county fair Thurs.\n(keep under 2 min!!)\n\nCARD 1\n- Thank you to the Benton County Fair Board and the Livestock Committee\n- Wow. Did not expect this. (breathe)\n\nCARD 2\n- When I got my first goat, Biscuit, I was nine and she head-butted me into a water trough\n- My dad laughed so hard he had to sit down\n- I almost quit right there\n\nCARD 3\n- 6 years later I have 11 Nubians and a waiting list for kids (baby goats not human kids — pause for laugh)\n- Learned: getting up at 5 for milking, keeping records, vet bills are NOT a joke\n- Learned you can't fake it with animals. They know if you show up.\n\nCARD 4 — thank yous\n- Mrs. Hendricks, club leader since I was 9\n- Dr. Alvarez for answering my texts at 2am during kidding season\n- Mom, Dad, and Cody who still won't touch a goat\n\nCARD 5\n- To the younger members: you will get head-butted into a trough. Get back up.\n- Thank you!! (hold up the buckle??)" },
  { kind: "speech", name: "persuasive speech manuscript for a public speaking class on ending clock changes, laid out like an essay (could be mistaken for argumentative)", text: "Daniela Ortiz\nCOMM 110 – Public Speaking\nDr. Feldman\nNovember 4, 2025\n\nLet's Stop Changing the Clocks\n\nTwice a year, almost everyone in this country agrees to lose or gain an hour of their life on command. In March we lose an hour of sleep, and in November we get it back, along with darkness at 4:45 in the afternoon. Most of us treat this as a minor annoyance. Today I want to convince you that it's more than that, and that the United States should pick one time and stay on it permanently.\n\nThe first reason is health. The Monday after we spring forward is not just a groggy day. Researchers have found increases in heart attacks and fatal car crashes in the days right after the March change. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine has formally called for ending seasonal time changes. Losing one hour sounds small, but when hundreds of millions of people lose it on the same night, small adds up.\n\nThe second reason is that the original justification no longer holds. Daylight saving time was expanded during the world wars to save fuel. But modern energy studies, including one from Indiana after the whole state adopted daylight saving time in 2006, found no real savings, and actually slightly higher electricity use because people ran air conditioning in the brighter evenings.\n\nNow, some of you might be thinking: fine, but which time do we pick? That is the real debate. Permanent daylight saving time gives us brighter evenings, which is why the Senate passed the Sunshine Protection Act in 2022. Sleep scientists actually prefer permanent standard time, because morning light matters more for our body clocks. I'll be honest with you: I lean toward standard time. But I want you to notice that both sides agree on the main point, which is that switching back and forth is the worst option.\n\nSo here is what I'm asking you to do. Before you leave this room today, take two minutes and look up your representative. Send one email that says: pick a time and keep it. That's it. You don't have to settle the debate between standard and daylight time. You just have to say you're tired, literally, of changing the clocks.\n\nThank you." },
  { kind: "blog", name: "student budget travel blog post on a weekend in Porto", place: "wordpress.com/post/lenagoeslow.wordpress.com", text: "48 Hours in Porto for Under €150 (Yes, Including Port)\n\nHi friends! If you've been following along, you know I'm doing my spring semester in Salamanca, which means every couple of weekends I'm scrolling Flixbus at 1am looking for somewhere cheap to go. This time it was Porto, and honestly? It might be my favorite city so far. Here's exactly how I did it and what I spent.\n\nGetting there\nThe overnight bus from Salamanca was €24 and took about six hours. I did not sleep. The man next to me ate an entire bag of oranges. But I arrived at 7am with a full day ahead and no hotel night to pay for, so I'm calling it a win.\n\nWhere I stayed\nI booked one night at a hostel near Trindade station for €19 in a six-bed dorm. It was clean, the lockers were huge, and they had free breakfast with the best little custard tarts. More on those later.\n\nDay 1\nI dropped my bag and walked straight down to the Ribeira, the old riverside neighborhood. It's all colorful tiled houses stacked on top of each other and it looks fake in the best way. I walked across the top level of the Dom Luís I bridge (free, terrifying, worth it) to Vila Nova de Gaia, which is where all the port wine cellars are.\n\nTip: most of the big cellar tours are €20 to €30. I went to one of the smaller family places instead and did a tasting of three ports for €12. The guy running it explained the difference between ruby and tawny like four times because I kept asking. Very patient man.\n\nLunch was a francesinha, which is basically a meat sandwich drowned in cheese and beer sauce. It costs about €11 and you will not need to eat again until tomorrow. I am not exaggerating.\n\nIn the evening I watched the sunset from the Jardim do Morro with what felt like half the city. Bring a snack and get there early.\n\nDay 2\nLivraria Lello (the bookstore everyone says inspired Harry Potter) costs €10 to get in now and the line was wrapped around the block, so I skipped it and went to São Bento station instead to see the blue and white tiles, which is free and honestly more impressive. Then I wandered around Bolhão market and ate pastéis de nata until I felt sick.\n\nWhat I spent\nBus there and back\t€48\nHostel\t€19\nFood\t€46\nPort tasting\t€12\nMetro + random\t€9\nTotal\t€134\n\nThings I'd do differently: bring better shoes (Porto is ALL hills), and book the bus home for later so I'm not sprinting through the station like last time.\n\nNext weekend: Lisbon?? Tell me your cheap Lisbon tips in the comments!\n\nxo Lena" },
  { kind: "blog", name: "gardening blog post about cherry tomatoes splitting on an apartment balcony", text: "Why My Balcony Tomatoes Kept Splitting (and What Finally Fixed It)\nLast summer I lost probably half my cherry tomatoes to splitting. I'd go out in the morning with my coffee, see a perfect orange Sungold, and then turn it over and there's a big crack down the side with a little fruit fly party happening inside. Heartbreaking. If this is happening to you, this post is for you.\nFirst the boring science part, I promise it's short. Tomatoes split when the inside of the fruit grows faster than the skin can stretch. That almost always happens after a big swing in water: the plant is dry for a couple days, then it gets a ton of water all at once (a thunderstorm, or you panic-watering after a weekend away), and the fruit swells up like a water balloon. Ripe fruit is the most likely to crack because the skin is already less flexible.\nOn a balcony this is way worse than in the ground, because containers dry out super fast. My 5-gallon buckets on a south-facing balcony in Sacramento were basically bone dry by 4pm in July.\nHere's what I changed this year:\n1. Bigger pots. I moved up to 15-gallon fabric grow bags. More soil = more water held = fewer swings.\n2. Mulch on top. Just a couple inches of straw. Looks a little messy but it really slows down evaporation.\n3. A cheap drip timer. I got a little battery one that hooks to the spigot for about $30 and it waters twice a day for a few minutes. This was the big one honestly.\n4. Picking earlier. If rain is coming I pick anything that's even half colored and let it finish on the counter. They taste basically the same.\nResults: I've had maybe five or six split tomatoes all season instead of dozens. My neighbor downstairs is getting a bag of Sungolds every week now and she is thrilled.\nOne thing that DIDN'T help for me: the calcium spray everyone online swears by. That's for blossom end rot, which is a totally different problem (the black leathery spot on the bottom). Don't waste your money if your problem is cracks.\nGot a balcony garden? Tell me what you're growing in the comments, I'm already planning next year." },
  { kind: "blog", name: "short parenting blog post about a child's first week of kindergarten", text: "We Survived Week One\n\nWell, she did it. Our Junie is officially a kindergartner, and after five days of drop-offs I think I can finally write about it without crying. Mostly.\n\nMonday was rough. Not for her, for me. She marched into Room 6 with her rainbow backpack (that is roughly the size of her entire body) and didn't even look back. I stood in the parking lot for ten minutes like a weirdo. Another mom handed me a tissue and said \"first one?\" and I just nodded.\n\nTuesday she told us she has a best friend named Arlo. Wednesday Arlo was \"not her best friend anymore\" because he took the purple crayon. Thursday they were best friends again. Friday she couldn't remember his name. Kindergarten moves fast, people.\n\nThe biggest surprise has been how TIRED she is. She falls asleep in the car on the way home every single day, and we've moved bedtime up to 7:15. If you have a kid starting school soon, my only real advice is to expect the after-school meltdown and don't take it personally. They hold it together all day and then let it all out with you because you're safe.\n\nNext week: lunchbox ideas, because she has informed me the sandwich is \"boring.\" Stay tuned." },
  { kind: "blog", name: "small family plumbing company's website blog post explaining why it stopped free estimates", place: "manage.wix.com/dashboard/7c41e2a9-0b3f/blog/create-post", text: "Why We Stopped Doing Free Estimates (And Why It's Actually Better for You)\nIf you've called Delgado & Sons Plumbing in the last couple months you might have noticed something new. We now charge a $79 diagnostic fee for most service calls, and that fee gets applied to the repair if you go ahead with the work. A few customers have asked us about this, so we wanted to explain.\nFor about 18 years we did free estimates on everything. Our dad (Ray Delgado, who started the business in 2007 and still answers the phone some mornings) believed that people shouldn't have to pay just to find out what's wrong. We agree with that in principle. But here is what was actually happening.\nA free estimate means a licensed plumber drives out, crawls under your sink or into your crawlspace, figures out the problem, and writes you a price. That takes on average an hour and a half with travel. Last year we did over 600 free estimates and a little less than half turned into jobs. Somebody pays for those other 300+ visits, and it was ending up in our prices for everyone.\nThe other problem is honestly less fun to talk about. Some people were getting our free diagnosis and then using it to shop around or try a DIY fix off YouTube. That's your right! But it meant we were basically doing free consulting.\nSo here's how it works now:\n* Service calls and diagnostics: $79, credited toward the repair if you hire us\n* Water heater and repipe estimates: still FREE, since those are bigger jobs\n* Seniors 65+ and veterans: diagnostic fee waived, always\nSince we made the change, our average repair prices have actually gone down a little, and we can get to most calls the same day because our guys aren't driving all over the valley for estimates that go nowhere.\nIf you have questions about this, call the office and ask for Rosa. She'll tell you straight.\nThanks for supporting a family business,\nThe Delgado Family" },
  { kind: "blog", name: "personal running blog race recap of a first half marathon", place: "medium.com/new-story", text: "Race Recap: My First Half Marathon (And the Porta Potty Incident)\n\nI'll start with the important stuff: I finished. 2:14:37. Not fast, not slow, but it's mine and I'm framing the bib.\n\nFor anyone new here, I started running about 18 months ago after my doctor used the phrase \"pre-diabetic\" at my physical and I panicked. I could barely run a mile without stopping. Saturday I ran 13.1 of them along the river in the Shoreline Half, and I want to write it all down before I forget.\n\nThe night before\nPasta, obviously. Laid out my \"flat runner\" like everyone on Instagram does. Slept maybe four hours because I kept waking up thinking I'd missed my alarm.\n\nMiles 1–5\nFelt incredible. Way too incredible. The crowd at the start is so loud and everyone is so excited that I went out at a 9:30 pace when my plan was 10:15. My running club friend Jess had literally warned me about this exact thing. Did I listen? No.\n\nMiles 6–8\nHere is the porta potty incident. I'd been drinking at every water station because it was humid, and by mile 6 I HAD to go. There was one porta potty. There were eleven people in line. I waited six minutes. Six. I watched the 2:10 pace group run past me while I stood there doing the math in my head. I came out and the lock jammed and I had to kind of shoulder-bump the door open while a volunteer cheered for me. Very dignified.\n\nMiles 9–11\nThe wall. My legs felt like wet sand. I ate a gel (salted caramel, do not recommend) and switched to a run 4 minutes, walk 1 minute plan. No shame. A woman in a shirt that said \"I'm 71, what's your excuse\" passed me and honestly that helped.\n\nMiles 12–13.1\nThe last stretch goes past the aquarium and the crowds come back and you can hear the announcer at the finish. I don't know where it came from but I picked it back up and passed like six people in the last half mile. My husband and kids were at the barrier with a sign that said \"RUN MOM THE SNACKS ARE AT THE END.\" They were correct.\n\nWhat I learned\nStart slower than you think. Use the bathroom at the start even if you don't have to. And you are allowed to walk.\n\nNext up: I'm already looking at a fall race. Don't tell my knees." },
  { kind: "journal", name: "teen diary entry about being left out of a friend group chat", text: "oct 2\n\nok so I need to write this down because I literally can't tell anyone else.\n\nMaya made a new group chat. Without me. I only found out because Ellie accidentally said something about \"the gc\" at lunch and then got this face like she realized she messed up. And when I asked what gc, everyone just kind of looked at their food. Maya said \"oh it's just for planning Ellie's birthday thing\" but Ellie's birthday is in DECEMBER.\n\nI don't even know what I did. Last week we were fine?? We literally FaceTimed till midnight on Thursday. Unless this is about me saying I didn't want to go to the football game, but that was so small. Or maybe it's not about anything and they just like each other more than me which is honestly worse.\n\nMom asked why I was being quiet at dinner and I said I was tired. She gave me the look but didn't push. I almost told her but I know she'll say \"well maybe you need new friends\" like it's that easy. We've been friends since FOURTH GRADE.\n\nI'm not going to text them about it. If they want to leave me out fine. I'll just be normal tomorrow and not say anything and see if anyone acts weird.\n\n(I'm probably going to text Ellie.)\n\nugh. going to bed." },
  { kind: "journal", name: "adult's dated journal entries across the first week of a new dental office job", text: "New job journal\nMon 9/8\nFirst day at Brightside Dental. Got there 20 min early and sat in the car because I didn't want to look desperate. Front desk is me and Carol, who has been there 22 years and knows every patient's dog's name. The scheduling software looks like it's from 2004. I wrote down like four pages of notes. Pretty sure I put a cleaning on the wrong Tuesday.\nTue 9/9\nCarol showed me the \"insurance binder.\" It's three inches thick. A man yelled at me about his copay and I did not cry, which I'm counting as a win. Dr. Patel brought in donuts. I think I'm going to like her.\nWed 9/10\nMessed up the recall list and called a lady who had passed away last year. Her husband picked up. He was so kind about it which made it worse. Sat in my car at lunch for a while. Carol said she did the same thing in 2009 and told me to \"add a note in the chart and let it go.\" Trying.\nThu 9/11\nBetter day. Checked in 31 patients with no mistakes, I think. Figured out the shortcut for rescheduling. Hygienist named Bree invited me to trivia night.\nFri 9/12\nTired but okay. Made it through week one. It's not what I thought I'd be doing at 34 after the warehouse closed, but it's steady and people are nice and I'm not lifting boxes anymore. Sunday I'm going to make a cheat sheet for the insurance codes. Maybe I'll actually go to trivia." },
  { kind: "journal", name: "gratitude journal kept as three-item lists over several days", text: "Gratitude Journal — trying this again (therapist suggestion)\n\nJan 4\n1. hot shower that actually stayed hot\n2. Kenji texted first for once\n3. the cat sleeping on my feet even though she weighs like 18 lbs\n\nJan 5\n1. Got the oil change done finally\n2. Sunny for like an hour around 2\n3. Leftover dumplings\nHard to think of these today. Felt kind of fake writing them. Dr. M said that's normal at first.\n\nJan 6\n1. Mom called and we didn't argue about anything\n2. Walked to the mailbox without my knee hurting\n3. Found $20 in my winter coat from last year!!!\n\nJan 8\n(missed yesterday)\n1. A coworker said my presentation was clear\n2. The library had the book I wanted on hold\n3. not sure. that I wrote this I guess\n\nJan 9\n1. Sunrise on the drive in, all pink over the overpass\n2. Made soup from scratch and it was edible\n3. I noticed I was looking for good stuff during the day so I'd have something to write here. Which is maybe the point?" },
  { kind: "journal", name: "grief journal entry the night the family dog was put down", text: "Tuesday night, late\n\nWe put Rocket down today. I keep writing that sentence and then staring at it.\n\nThe vet was so gentle. She let us sit on the floor with him on his blanket and Dev held his head and I held his paws and he just went to sleep. He was fourteen. His back legs had been giving out for months and he couldn't get up the stairs anymore and we knew. We knew. That doesn't help as much as everyone says it will.\n\nThe house is so quiet. I didn't realize how much of the noise in this house was him. His tags clinking on the water bowl. The thump when he flopped down in the hallway. I keep stepping over the spot where he slept by the back door even though he's not there.\n\nI put his leash on the hook and then took it down and then put it back up. I don't know what to do with it.\n\nGot him the year Dev and I moved in together. He's been there for every apartment, the wedding, both of my bad years. He was there when I didn't get out of bed for a week in 2019 and he just lay next to me and didn't need me to be okay.\n\nI hope he knew. I think he knew.\n\nGoing to try to sleep." },
  { kind: "journal", name: "ESL learner's diary entry about a first winter in Winnipeg, written for English practice", text: "Diary — 15 December\nMy English teacher at the settlement center say if I write a diary in English every day, my writing will be more better. So I try. Today is my fourth time.\nToday was -27 in Winnipeg. Minus twenty-seven! In Hermosillo where I am from, the coldest day maybe is 5 degrees and my mother wear her big jacket and complain all day. If she see this she will not believe.\nIn the morning I wait for the bus 11 and my eyelashes have ice. Real ice. A woman at the bus stop laugh, not bad laugh, nice laugh, and she tell me I need a \"toque\". I did not know this word. It is a hat. Canadians have special word for hat. After the class I go to Walmart and buy a toque, it is red, and also the mittens because my gloves is not enough.\nSometimes I feel very lonely here. My husband work night shift and my daughter is in school all day and the sun go down at 4:30. I miss the noise of my street at home, the music and the dogs and the neighbors. Here everything is quiet and white.\nBut today something good happen. My neighbor Mrs. Kowalski knock on my door and give us a plate of pierogi. She say her family come from Poland 50 years ago and the first winter is the worst. She say after this one it will be easier. I hope she is right.\nTomorrow I write again." },
  { kind: "journal", name: "private late-night journal entry on the last day of a summer lifeguard job, with some \"what I learned\" musing (could be mistaken for reflection)", text: "Aug 23 — last day at Lakeview\n\nLast shift ever at the pool. Well, ever this summer. Probably ever, since next summer I'll hopefully have the internship.\n\nI keep thinking about how scared I was in June. The first week I sat in the chair so stiff I got a neck cramp, counting heads over and over, 10-second scan, 10-second scan, like they drilled into us in training. Every kid who went under for more than two seconds I was half out of the chair. Danny kept telling me to relax my shoulders.\n\nAnd then July 9th happened, the little boy in the deep end with the yellow swim shirt, and I went in and got him, and he was fine, he was totally fine, he was coughing and crying and then he wanted a popsicle. But I went to the back office afterward and shook for like twenty minutes. I never wrote about it before now. I think I didn't want to.\n\nWhat I learned I guess (writing this because it's late and I can't sleep): I'm better in an emergency than I thought. I'm worse at being bored than I thought. I like little kids way more than I thought I would. I hate blowing the whistle at teenagers who are my age. If I did it again I'd stop trying to look like I knew everything the first week and just ask Danny stuff.\n\nGave the manager my whistle back. Kept the red hoodie. They said I could.\n\nWeird to think about that boy going back to school next month and not even remembering me. That's okay. That's kind of the whole point." },
  { kind: "meeting", name: "high school robotics club meeting minutes with votes and action items", text: "Ironclad Robotics (Team 8841) — Meeting Minutes\nDate: Tuesday, January 13\nTime: 3:10–4:40 PM, Room B12\nRecorder: Sofia Herrera (secretary)\n\nPresent: Sofia H., Aarav M., Liam K., Jordan T., Chloe W., Ben A., Nadia R., Mr. Kessler (advisor)\nAbsent: Owen P. (excused, dentist)\n\n1. Kickoff recap\n- Aarav summarized this year's game. Robots have to pick up foam \"cargo\" pieces and score them in two goal heights, plus climb at the end.\n- Team agreed climbing is worth a lot of points (15 for high bar) and should be a priority.\n\n2. Robot design\n- Liam presented two drivetrain options: swerve vs tank.\n- Swerve is faster but we only have one person (Liam) who has programmed it before. Tank is safer.\n- Vote: 5 for tank, 3 for swerve. Going with tank for now, Liam will keep testing swerve on the old chassis as a backup.\n\n3. Budget\n- Mr. Kessler said we have $3,200 left from the boosters + the Northgate Auto sponsorship.\n- New motors and battery set will be about $1,400.\n- Chloe asked about the registration fee for the second regional. Mr. K will check with the athletic office.\n\n4. Outreach\n- Nadia is organizing the demo at Pine Street Elementary on Feb 6. Need 3 volunteers.\n- Jordan and Ben signed up.\n\nAction items\n- Liam: CAD for tank drivetrain by Fri\n- Chloe: order motors + batteries once Mr. K approves the PO\n- Nadia: confirm time w/ Pine Street, find 1 more volunteer\n- Sofia: send these notes to the Discord\n\nNext meeting: Thursday 3:10, B12. BRING SAFETY GLASSES." },
  { kind: "meeting", name: "terse software team daily standup notes", text: "Standup 10/14 — Payments squad\nAttendees: Rafael, Min-ji, Tom, Oksana, Priyanka (PM)\nRafael\n- y: finished retry logic for webhook failures, PR up (#2291)\n- t: addressing review comments, then start on idempotency keys\n- blockers: none\nMin-ji\n- y: debugging refund double-post in staging\n- t: same. thinks it's the queue consumer acking twice\n- blockers: needs access to prod logs → Oksana to grant\nTom\n- y: OOO half day\n- t: bumping the payment provider SDK one major version, checking breaking changes\n- blockers: none\nOksana\n- y: on-call, 2 pages (both false alarms from the latency monitor)\n- t: tune latency alert threshold, grant Min-ji log access\n- blockers: none\nNotes\n- Priyanka: demo for finance team moved to Thursday 2pm\n- Release freeze starts Fri EOD for quarter close. Anything not merged by then waits.\n- Tom & Min-ji to sync after standup re: whether SDK upgrade touches refunds" },
  { kind: "meeting", name: "formal homeowners association board minutes with motions and votes", text: "WILLOW CREEK HOMEOWNERS ASSOCIATION\nBoard of Directors — Regular Meeting Minutes\nSeptember 18, 2025\nClubhouse Meeting Room\n\nCall to Order\nPresident Linda Marsh called the meeting to order at 7:02 p.m. A quorum was established.\n\nBoard members present: Linda Marsh (President), Gerald Fong (Vice President), Tasha Greene (Treasurer), Robert Villanueva (Secretary), Amy Brandt (Member at Large)\nAlso present: Dana Pruitt, Cascade Property Management; 14 homeowners\n\nApproval of Minutes\nMotion by G. Fong to approve the minutes of the August 21, 2025 meeting as distributed. Seconded by A. Brandt. Motion carried 5–0.\n\nTreasurer's Report\nT. Greene reported an operating balance of $48,312 and a reserve balance of $211,780 as of August 31. Delinquent assessments total $6,450 across seven accounts; three accounts have been referred to the collections attorney per the adopted policy. Motion by R. Villanueva to accept the treasurer's report. Seconded by L. Marsh. Motion carried 5–0.\n\nManagement Report\nD. Pruitt reported that the irrigation repairs along Cottonwood Drive were completed on September 3 at a cost of $2,140, under the approved amount. The fall landscaping walkthrough is scheduled for October 7.\n\nOld Business\na. Pool resurfacing. Three bids were received: Blue Horizon Pools ($38,900), AquaTech ($42,500), and Sunset Pool & Spa ($35,200). Discussion followed regarding warranty terms; Sunset offers a 5-year warranty versus 10 years for Blue Horizon. Several homeowners spoke in favor of the longer warranty. Motion by T. Greene to accept the Blue Horizon bid of $38,900, to be paid from reserves, with work to begin after the pool closes for the season. Seconded by G. Fong. Motion carried 4–1 (A. Brandt opposed, citing cost).\nb. Parking enforcement. The board discussed ongoing complaints about vehicles parked overnight on Aspen Court. R. Villanueva will draft a revised parking rule for homeowner comment. No action taken.\n\nNew Business\na. Holiday lighting. A. Brandt proposed that the association fund entrance holiday lighting not to exceed $1,200. Motion by A. Brandt. Seconded by R. Villanueva. Motion carried 5–0.\nb. Architectural request, 4410 Birch Lane (rooftop solar panels). Request reviewed and approved as submitted. Motion by G. Fong, seconded by T. Greene. Motion carried 5–0.\n\nHomeowner Forum\nA homeowner raised concerns about speeding on Willow Creek Parkway and asked about speed bumps. The board noted that the parkway is a city street and directed management to provide homeowners with the city's traffic calming request form.\nA homeowner asked when the dog waste stations near the pond will be restocked. Management will address this week.\n\nAdjournment\nThere being no further business, the meeting was adjourned at 8:26 p.m. The next regular meeting will be held October 16, 2025 at 7:00 p.m.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\nRobert Villanueva, Secretary" },
  { kind: "meeting", name: "messy lowercase notes from a college marketing group project meeting", text: "mktg 340 group mtg — tues 10/7, library rm 3 (zoom for aisha)\n\nhere: me (kat), jamal, aisha (zoom), brendan\nnot here: lucas (texted he's sick?? 2nd time)\n\nproject = campaign pitch for the campus food truck that's opening in spring. client is dining services, they want name + logo + social plan + launch event\n\n- jamal did the survey, 112 responses. top things ppl want: cheap (under $8), open late, vegan option. ppl HATE the current dining hall hours\n- name ideas: \"Late Plate\", \"The Rolling Ram\" (mascot), \"Curbside\". aisha likes late plate. brendan thinks rolling ram is cheesy. me too honestly. leaning late plate, vote thurs\n- brendan said he can do logo in canva, will have 2-3 drafts\n- social: insta + tiktok. aisha wants to do a countdown thing 2 wks before launch. need to check if dining services even has a tiktok acct\n- launch event — idea: free tacos first 100 ppl? need to ask about budget, prof said client budget is \"realistic\" whatever that means\n\nproblems\n- lucas hasn't done his part (competitor research). if no response by wed jamal will email prof. we don't want to but presentation is the 28th\n- slides template — use the one from last project? yes\n\ntodo\nkat - start slide deck, write exec summary draft\njamal - survey charts, email lucas\naisha - social calendar draft\nbrendan - logos\n\nnext mtg thurs 4pm same room" },
  { kind: "meeting", name: "parish fall festival committee agenda with notes kept in a table", text: "St. Agnes Fall Festival Planning Committee\nAgenda & Notes — Sunday Oct 5, after 10:30 Mass, Parish Hall kitchen\nPresent: Fr. Tomas, Maureen O'Neill (chair), Gloria Reyes, Phil & Diane Sorensen, Kevin Lu, Bernadette Shaw\nOpening prayer: Fr. Tomas\nItem\tWho\tNotes / decisions\nDate & time\tMaureen\tConfirmed Sat Oct 25, 11am–4pm. Rain plan = move games into gym, food stays under tents.\nFood booths\tGloria\tTamales (Guadalupe Society), chili, bake sale, kettle corn. Need someone to pick up 2nd roaster from St. Mark's. Phil will.\nGames\tKevin\tRing toss, cake walk, pumpkin bowling. Youth group running them. Kevin to get prize list to Maureen by 10/12.\nRaffle\tDiane\tQuilt donated by the Monday sewing circle + $250 gift card from Hartley's Hardware. Tickets $5 / 5 for $20. Diane will print 500.\nVolunteers\tBernadette\tSign-up sheet in the narthex is about half full. Short on 2–4pm shift. Fr. Tomas will announce at all Masses next weekend.\nPermits / insurance\tPhil\tCity permit approved. Diocese insurance cert requested, not back yet. Phil following up Tues.\nBudget\tMaureen\tLast year raised $8,900. Goal this year $10,000 for the roof fund.\nOther\n- Gloria asked if we can have a dance performance this year. Group agreed, Gloria will ask the Ballet Folklórico group from the Thursday class if they're free around 1pm.\n- Kevin reminded everyone no balloons this year (the power line thing last year)\nNext meeting: Sunday Oct 19, same place. Closing prayer." },
  { kind: "meeting", name: "student's notes from a one-on-one thesis check-in with an advisor (could be mistaken for research notes)", text: "Mtg w/ Dr. Okafor — thesis check-in #4\nThurs 11/6, her office, ~40 min\n\nWhere I'm at\n- told her ch 2 draft is ~70% done, still missing the section on the 1930s housing maps\n- she'd read my intro over the weekend\n\nHer comments on intro\n- research question still \"too wide\" — \"how did redlining shape Milwaukee\" is a book not a thesis. narrow to one neighborhood or one decade\n- suggested Bronzeville specifically, since I already have the HOLC area descriptions for it\n- my lit review section is listing sources, not putting them in conversation. she wrote \"who disagrees with whom?\" in the margin like 5 times\n- likes the oral history angle, said that's what makes it mine\n\nSources she recommended\n- Rothstein, The Color of Law — I have it, need to actually finish it\n- the Mapping Inequality site (Univ. of Richmond) for the original maps\n- ask the archivist at the Milwaukee Public Library about city council minutes 1938-40\n\nDeadlines we agreed on\n- revised research question + 1 paragraph rationale → email her by Mon 11/10\n- full ch 2 draft → Dec 1\n- she's away Dec 15–Jan 5, so anything I need signed has to be before then\n\nMe to do\n- narrow RQ (Bronzeville, 1930s–50s?)\n- redo lit review section as a \"debate\" not a list\n- book archive appt\n- ask about the IRB question for the interviews — she said probably exempt but check with the office\n\nnext meeting: Thurs 11/20, 2pm" },
  { kind: "annotated", name: "high school MLA annotated bibliography on microplastics in drinking water", text: "Noah Fitzgerald\nMr. Delacroix\nAP Environmental Science\n2 February 2025\n\nAnnotated Bibliography: Microplastics in Drinking Water\n\nCox, Kieran D., et al. \"Human Consumption of Microplastics.\" Environmental Science & Technology, vol. 53, no. 12, 2019, pp. 7068–74.\nThis study estimated how many microplastic particles Americans take in each year from food, drinks, and air, combining data from earlier studies. The authors found that people who drink only bottled water may take in about 90,000 extra particles a year, compared to about 4,000 for people who drink only tap water. This source is useful because it gives actual numbers I can use to compare bottled and tap water. One limitation is that it is based on other studies, and the authors admit that most foods have never been tested, so the real number is probably higher.\n\nQian, Naixin, et al. \"Rapid Single-Particle Chemical Imaging of Nanoplastics by SRS Microscopy.\" Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, vol. 121, no. 3, 2024.\nResearchers at Columbia University used a new laser imaging method to count nanoplastics, which are smaller than microplastics, in bottled water. They found about 240,000 particles per liter on average, which is far more than earlier estimates because older methods couldn't see particles that small. This was the study that got a lot of news coverage last year. It is very technical and I didn't understand all of the methods section, but the results and discussion explain the main findings clearly. I'll use it to show that the problem may be bigger than we thought.\n\nWorld Health Organization. Microplastics in Drinking-Water. WHO, 2019.\nThis report reviews the evidence about whether microplastics in drinking water hurt human health. The WHO concluded that, based on the limited information available, microplastics in drinking water do not seem to be a health risk at current levels, but it also says more research is needed. This is important for my paper because it gives the other side and keeps me from exaggerating. It is from a trusted international organization, but it is now six years old and was written before the nanoplastic study above.\n\nPatel, Simone. \"Is Your Water Bottle Full of Plastic? Here's What Scientists Say.\" Wellspring Health News, 18 Jan. 2024.\nThis news article explains the Columbia study for regular readers and includes tips from scientists for reducing exposure, like not heating food in plastic and using a filter. It is less scholarly than my other sources but it is helpful for my conclusion about what people can actually do. I will double check any facts in it against the original study." },
  { kind: "annotated", name: "college APA annotated bibliography on remote work and productivity, evaluative", text: "Annotated Bibliography\nRemote Work and Employee Productivity\nMGMT 3120: Organizational Behavior\nHannah Lindqvist\n\nBloom, N., Han, R., & Liang, J. (2024). Hybrid working from home improves retention without damaging performance. Nature, 630(8018), 920–925. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07500-2\nBloom and colleagues ran a randomized controlled trial with about 1,600 graduate employees in engineering, marketing, accounting and finance at Trip.com, a large Chinese travel company, in which employees were randomly assigned either to work from home two days a week or to work fully in the office. Over six months, hybrid workers were about one third less likely to quit, and the researchers found no difference in performance reviews or promotion rates over the following two years. The strength of this study is its experimental design, which is rare in this literature and allows causal claims. However, all participants worked for one company in one country, and they were white-collar professionals, which limits generalizability. For my paper this is the strongest evidence that hybrid arrangements do not reduce productivity, and I will use it to frame the main argument.\n\nGibbs, M., Mengel, F., & Siemroth, C. (2023). Work from home and productivity: Evidence from personnel and analytics data on information technology professionals. Journal of Political Economy Microeconomics, 1(1), 7–41. https://doi.org/10.1086/721803\nIn contrast to Bloom et al., this study found that productivity fell by roughly 8 to 19 percent when more than 10,000 IT professionals at a large Asian services company moved to fully remote work during the pandemic. The authors used detailed computer activity data and found that employees worked longer hours but produced about the same output, largely because of more time spent in meetings and coordination. The study is valuable because it uses objective activity data rather than self-reports. Its main limitation is the pandemic context; the shift was sudden and involuntary, and many employees had childcare responsibilities at home, which may not reflect remote work chosen under normal conditions. I plan to use this source to show that fully remote and hybrid arrangements may have very different effects.\n\nGolden, T. D., & Veiga, J. F. (2005). The impact of extent of telecommuting on job satisfaction: Resolving inconsistent findings. Journal of Management, 31(2), 301–318. https://doi.org/10.1177/0149206304271768\nGolden and Veiga surveyed 321 professional employees at a high-tech company and found that job satisfaction rose with the amount of telecommuting at first but then leveled off at higher levels. Although this source focuses on satisfaction rather than productivity, it offers an early explanation of why moderate levels of remote work may produce better outcomes than either extreme. The data are now two decades old and predate modern collaboration tools, so I will use it mainly for historical context and its theoretical argument, not its specific numbers." },
  { kind: "annotated", name: "middle school annotated bibliography on Harriet Tubman with websites and incomplete citations", text: "Harriet Tubman Annotated Bibliography\nKayla M.\n7th Grade Social Studies\n1. \"Harriet Tubman.\" History.com, A&E Television Networks.\nThis website tells about Harriet Tubmans whole life from when she was born a slave in Maryland to when she died in 1913. It talks about how she escaped and then went back like 13 times to help other people escape on the Underground Railroad. I think it is a good source because History.com is a well known website and it had alot of information.\n2. Adler, David A. A Picture Book of Harriet Tubman. Holiday House, 1992.\nI read this book in 4th grade and I got it again from the library. It is a kids book but it has the main facts. It tells about her getting hit in the head when she was young and how it gave her headaches and dreams the rest of her life. It was helpful for my timeline.\n3. National Park Service website (nps.gov) Harriet Tubman Underground Railroad National Historical Park\nThis website was about the park in Maryland that is named after her. It had information about the place where she grew up and what it looked like. It also said she was a spy and a nurse for the Union army in the Civil War which I didn't know. I think this source is reliable because it is from the government.\n4. Video from school (BrainPOP Harriet Tubman)\nWe watched this in class. It was short but it explained the Underground Railroad." },
  { kind: "annotated", name: "graduate history Chicago-style annotated bibliography on the Erie Canal and upstate towns, last entry unannotated", text: "HIST 612 — Annotated Bibliography (Chicago)\nTopic: The Erie Canal and the Remaking of Upstate Market Towns, 1817–1850\nDesmond Achebe-Lowry — draft for Prof. Strand, week 9\n\nBernstein, Peter L. Wedding of the Waters: The Erie Canal and the Making of a Great Nation. New York: W. W. Norton, 2005.\nA popular narrative history that traces the canal from its political origins under DeWitt Clinton through its completion in 1825. Bernstein's strength is synthesis and readability; his weakness, for my purposes, is that the towns along the route appear mostly as stops on a triumphal itinerary rather than as communities with their own internal conflicts. Useful for chronology and for the national-scale economic argument, but I will rely on it sparingly and not for local claims.\n\nJohnson, Paul E. A Shopkeeper's Millennium: Society and Revivals in Rochester, New York, 1815–1837. New York: Hill and Wang, 1978.\nJohnson's study of Rochester remains the foundational work linking canal-driven economic change to social and religious transformation. He argues that the separation of masters from journeymen, as workshops grew and employers stopped housing their workers, created the social anxieties that Charles Finney's 1830–31 revival addressed. Although later historians have criticized the book for reducing religious experience to class interest, its method of reading tax lists, church records, and city directories together is a direct model for my chapter on Lockport. Essential.\n\nSheriff, Carol. The Artificial River: The Erie Canal and the Paradox of Progress, 1817–1862. New York: Hill and Wang, 1996.\nSheriff's book is closest to my own question. She examines how ordinary people along the canal (farmers, boatmen, merchants, and residents who petitioned the canal commissioners) understood \"progress\" and often resented its costs. Her use of those petitions is especially valuable, and her chapter on property damage claims points me toward a set of sources in the New York State Archives I had not considered. I will engage her argument directly, since I think she understates how differently small towns bypassed by the canal experienced the same period.\n\nWyld, Lionel D. Low Bridge! Folklore and the Erie Canal. Syracuse: Syracuse University Press, 1962.\n[annotate — mostly songs/folklore, decide whether it's worth including at all]" },
  { kind: "annotated", name: "high school capstone annotated bibliography on esports as a varsity sport, bullet-point annotations with relevance ratings, one entry unfinished", text: "Capstone Sources — Should our school add esports as a varsity sport?\n(annotated bib due w/ proposal, Ms. Hadley wants 6 min, have 5 so far)\n\nRothwell, Gabrielle, and Michael Shaffer. \"eSports in K-12 and Post-Secondary Schools.\" Education Sciences, vol. 9, no. 2, 2019, article 105.\n● Overview of how schools started adding esports teams + why\n● Says esports can connect students who don't do traditional sports / clubs w/ school, links to STEM interest\n● Peer reviewed but kind of short, more of an overview than a study\n● Relevance: 5/5 — basically my exact topic\n\nNational Federation of State High School Associations (NFHS). \"Esports.\" nfhs.org, accessed 9 Oct. 2025.\n● lists which states officially recognize esports + how the state championships work\n● Useful for showing it's already \"official\" in a lot of states, but it's an org that benefits from promoting it → bias?\n● Relevance: 4/5\n\nKowert, Rachel. Video Games and Social Competence. Routledge, 2015.\n● book about whether gaming helps or hurts social skills\n● Argues the stereotype of gamers as isolated loners isn't really supported\n● Only read ch. 1 and 4\n● Relevance: 3/5 — background, not about schools\n\nInterview w/ Coach Petrakis (athletic director), 3 Oct.\n● he's not against it but worried about: cost of PCs, who supervises, whether it \"counts\" for PE credit\n● said the district looked at it in 2022 and dropped it bc of budget\n● Relevance: 5/5 for the local part\n\nPew Research Center — teens & video games survey (2024?)\n● [FINISH — find the actual link + the % of teen boys vs girls who play]" },
  { kind: "annotated", name: "annotated bibliography on urban tree canopy and heat with an introduction and a closing synthesis (could be mistaken for literature review)", text: "Urban Tree Canopy and Neighborhood Heat: An Annotated Bibliography\nENVS 205 — Rafaela Monteiro\n\nThe sources below address a single question: how much does tree canopy actually cool a city in summer, and who gets that benefit? I chose them to represent a broad review of the evidence, a detailed field study, and two studies of how canopy is distributed across neighborhoods of different incomes. Each entry summarizes the source and notes how I plan to use it.\n\nBowler, Diana E., Lisette Buyung-Ali, Teri M. Knight, and Andrew S. Pullin. \"Urban Greening to Cool Towns and Cities: A Systematic Review of the Empirical Evidence.\" Landscape and Urban Planning 97, no. 3 (2010): 147–55.\nThis systematic review pools results from dozens of earlier studies that compared temperatures in parks and green spaces with nearby built-up areas. On average, parks were close to 1°C cooler during the day, with larger parks and those with more trees showing stronger effects. The authors are careful to point out that most studies were small and observational, and that very little was known about whether the cooling extends beyond the edge of the green space. I will use this as the baseline evidence that greenery cools, while noting its limits.\n\nZiter, Carly D., Eric J. Pedersen, Christopher J. Kucharik, and Monica G. Turner. \"Scale-Dependent Interactions between Tree Canopy Cover and Impervious Surfaces Reduce Daytime Urban Heat during Summer.\" Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 116, no. 15 (2019): 7575–80.\nUsing bicycle-mounted sensors ridden repeatedly through Madison, Wisconsin, the authors measured air temperature at a very fine scale. They found that daytime cooling increased sharply once canopy cover at the block scale passed roughly 40 percent, which suggests that scattered street trees may do far less than a continuous canopy. This is the most useful source for my policy section, because it gives a concrete target, though it covers only one mid-sized northern city.\n\nSchwarz, Kirsten, et al. \"Trees Grow on Money: Urban Tree Canopy Cover and Environmental Justice.\" PLOS ONE 10, no. 4 (2015): e0122051.\nSchwarz and colleagues compared canopy cover with census data in seven U.S. cities and found that canopy was consistently associated with income, with wealthier neighborhoods having more trees. The relationship with race was less consistent across cities. I will use it to introduce the equity question.\n\nMcDonald, Robert I., et al. \"The Tree Cover and Temperature Disparity in US Urbanized Areas: Quantifying the Association with Income across 5,723 Communities.\" PLOS ONE 16, no. 4 (2021): e0249715.\nThis national study, led by researchers at The Nature Conservancy, found that low-income blocks had noticeably less tree cover than high-income blocks and were about 1.5°C hotter in summer. Its scale makes it the strongest evidence I have for the equity argument, though as a correlational study it cannot show that the missing trees are the only cause of the temperature gap.\n\nTaken together, these sources agree that trees cool cities, but they suggest the effect depends on how much canopy is concentrated in one place and that the neighborhoods with the least canopy are the ones most exposed to heat. What none of them answers directly is how quickly a new planting program could close that gap, which is the question my final paper will take up." },
  { kind: "poem", name: "teen free verse poem about father's old pickup truck", text: "My Dad's Truck\n\nit's a '98 Ford Ranger\nthe color of a sunburn that's peeling\nand the passenger door only opens from the outside\nso every morning he walks around\nand lets me in\nlike I'm somebody\n\nthe radio is stuck on the AM station\nthat plays farm reports and old men arguing about the Cubs\nand he says he could fix it\nbut he never does\n\nwhen I get my license\nhe says it's mine\nI tell him I don't want it\nwhich is a lie\nI want the cracked dash and the smell of sawdust and coffee\nI want the door that someone has to open for you\n\nI just don't want him to stop driving it first" },
  { kind: "poem", name: "6th grader's rhyming poem about a snow day", text: "Snow Day\nBy Ethan R., Grade 6\n\nI woke up early, looked outside,\nThe whole backyard was white and wide.\nMy mom said \"Check your phone, it's true,\nThere is no school today for you!\"\n\nI put my boots and snowpants on,\nAnd ran out on the snowy lawn.\nMy little sister threw a ball\nOf snow that hit me, that was all\n\nIt took to start a giant war,\nWe made a fort right by the door.\nThe dog got snow stuck in his fur,\nAnd then he shook it, what a blur!\n\nWe came inside with frozen toes,\nA pink and drippy runny nose,\nHot chocolate with the marshmallows,\nThe best day ever, I suppose." },
  { kind: "poem", name: "sequence of four haiku about a laundromat late at night", text: "Laundromat, 11 p.m.\n\ndryer number six\nturning someone's red sweater\nover and over\n\na man folds towels\nwith the care of a soldier\nfolding a flag, slow\n\nvending machine hum\nthe last quarter in my hand\ndecides: soap or chips\n\nfluorescent buzzing\nwe never speak, just nod. two\nstrangers, warm laundry" },
  { kind: "poem", name: "college creative writing workshop sonnet about insomnia", text: "Insomnia, 3 A.M.\n(sonnet, CRWR 201 workshop draft — be nice)\nThe clock reports each minute like a crime,\nits red eyes fixed on mine across the dark.\nI count my breaths, I count the sheep, the time\nI have till class, the neighbor dog's long bark.\nMy mind, a tab I cannot seem to close,\nreplays the stupid thing I said in May,\nrehearses fights with people no one knows,\nand drafts the emails I will send someday.\nThe radiator knocks like it wants in.\nThe streetlight paints the ceiling sodium.\nI flip the pillow to the colder skin\nand promise God and anyone who's come\nto listen: I will sleep, I'll try, I swear,\nand then the birds start up, and I'm still there." },
  { kind: "poem", name: "prose poem in one paragraph about the overnight shift at a highway gas station", text: "Graveyard, Exit 47\n\nAt 2 a.m. the hot dogs have been rolling since noon and no one will ever eat them, they turn and turn like small brown planets going nowhere. A trucker buys three energy drinks and a lottery ticket and tells me his daughter is getting married in Tulsa on Saturday and he will not make it. I say congratulations because it is the only word I have that fits through the plexiglass. The ice machine groans. Moths hold a meeting under the pump lights. At 3:40 a girl my age comes in barefoot and asks if we sell phone chargers and I say aisle two and she says thank you like I saved her, and maybe I did, who knows what the night is for. By five the sky over the Waffle House goes the color of a bruise healing. I count the drawer. I am twenty dollars over. I do not know whose twenty dollars it is, and I think about it the whole drive home." },
  { kind: "informative", name: "7th grade informative essay on tardigrades with MLA heading", text: "Ava Lindqvist\nMr. Okafor\nScience 7, Period 3\nOctober 2, 2026\n\nTiny but Tough: The Tardigrade\n\nIf you could look at a clump of wet moss under a microscope, you might see something that looks like a chubby gummy bear with eight legs slowly crawling around. This is a tardigrade, also called a water bear or a moss piglet. Tardigrades are some of the smallest animals on Earth, but they are also known for being some of the toughest.\n\nTardigrades are usually less than one millimeter long, so most people have never seen one without a microscope. They have a round body with four pairs of stubby legs, and each leg ends in tiny claws that help them hold onto plants. Scientists have found more than 1,300 species of tardigrades. They live almost everywhere, including mountains, the deep ocean, rainforests, and even Antarctica. A lot of them live in the thin layer of water that covers moss and lichen.\n\nThe thing that makes tardigrades famous is how much they can survive. When their home dries out, a tardigrade can pull in its legs, lose almost all the water in its body, and curl up into a dry ball called a \"tun.\" In this state its metabolism slows down to almost nothing. It can stay like this for years. When water comes back, the tardigrade can rehydrate and start moving again in a few hours. In the tun state, tardigrades have survived very cold temperatures, high radiation, and even a trip to outer space in 2007, when scientists sent some into orbit on a satellite.\n\nTardigrades eat by sucking the juices out of plant cells, algae, and sometimes other tiny animals. They have a special mouthpart called a stylet that works kind of like a straw. Most tardigrades reproduce by laying eggs, and some species can reproduce without a mate.\n\nScientists are studying tardigrades because they want to know how their cells protect themselves. Some tardigrades have special proteins that might protect DNA from damage. Someday this research could help protect medicines or even astronauts.\n\nTardigrades are a good example of how something tiny can still be amazing. The next time you walk past some moss on a sidewalk, remember that there could be hundreds of water bears living inside it." },
  { kind: "informative", name: "college expository essay explaining how a city drinking water treatment plant works", text: "From Reservoir to Tap\nMost people turn on a faucet dozens of times a day without thinking about what happened to that water before it arrived. In a typical American city, the water coming out of the tap has passed through a sequence of physical and chemical steps designed to remove particles, kill pathogens, and keep the water safe as it travels through miles of pipe. The details vary from plant to plant, but the basic process is surprisingly consistent.\nTreatment begins at the intake, where water is drawn from a river, lake, reservoir, or well. Screens at the intake keep out fish, branches, and trash. Surface water usually needs more treatment than groundwater, because it picks up sediment, algae, and runoff from the land around it.\nThe first major step is coagulation. Operators add chemicals such as aluminum sulfate (alum) or ferric chloride to the raw water. These chemicals carry a positive charge that neutralizes the negative charge on tiny suspended particles, allowing them to stick together. During flocculation, the water is gently mixed in large basins so these particles collide and form larger clumps called floc. The water then moves into sedimentation basins, where it sits still long enough for the heavy floc to settle to the bottom as sludge.\nNext comes filtration. The clarified water flows down through beds of sand, gravel, and often granular activated carbon. The filters trap smaller particles that did not settle, and activated carbon can also remove some compounds that cause bad tastes and odors. Filters are periodically cleaned by backwashing, which reverses the flow of water to lift trapped material out of the media.\nAfter filtration, the water is disinfected. Chlorine is the most common disinfectant in the United States, though many plants also use ozone or ultraviolet light earlier in the process. A small amount of chlorine or chloramine is deliberately left in the water as a \"residual,\" which continues to protect against bacteria as the water travels through the distribution system. Many utilities also adjust the pH and add a corrosion inhibitor so that the water does not leach lead or copper from older pipes, and many add fluoride at low levels.\nThe solids removed along the way do not simply disappear. Sludge from the sedimentation basins and the dirty water from backwashing are collected, thickened, and dewatered, and what remains is usually trucked to a landfill or, in some cities, spread on farmland or recycled into products like bricks. Groundwater systems often skip several of these steps entirely, since water pumped from a deep aquifer has already been filtered naturally by soil and rock, although it may still need treatment for hardness, iron, or naturally occurring arsenic.\nFinally, the treated water is pumped into storage tanks and water towers and from there into the network of mains that serve homes and businesses. Throughout the process, operators test the water continuously for turbidity, chlorine levels, pH, and bacteria, and utilities are required to report results under the Safe Drinking Water Act.\nUnderstanding this process makes it easier to appreciate why drinking water infrastructure matters. A glass of tap water is the end product of an engineered system that runs around the clock, and most of the time it works so well that no one notices it at all." },
  { kind: "informative", name: "ESL high school informative essay about Tet, the Vietnamese Lunar New Year", text: "Tết: The Vietnamese Lunar New Year\nby Nguyen Bao Tran\nESL 10 — Ms. Harrington\nTết is the most important holiday in Vietnam. The full name is Tết Nguyên Đán, it mean \"the first morning of the first day\". It happen on the first day of lunar calendar, usually in late January or in February. In Vietnam everybody stop working and the schools close for about one week or more, so it is very long holiday.\nBefore Tết, the families are very busy. They clean all the house because people believe the cleaning take away the bad luck of the old year. They also buy new clothes and cook many food. One of the most famous food is bánh chưng, it is a square cake make from sticky rice, mung bean and pork, wrap in green leaves. In the south of Vietnam people make bánh tét, which is same ingredient but the shape is long like a cylinder. Families also decorate the house with peach blossom in the north and yellow apricot blossom (hoa mai) in the south.\nOn the days of Tết there is many traditions. The first person who enter the house in the new year is very important, this called \"xông đất\". People think this person bring the luck for the whole year, so families choose someone who is kind and successful. Children receive lì xì, it is money in a small red envelope, from the adults, and they say wishes like \"chúc mừng năm mới\" (happy new year). People also visit the pagoda and the graves of the ancestor to show respect.\nTết is not only a holiday, it is a time for family come together. Many people who work in the big city like Ho Chi Minh City go back to their hometown, so the bus and train are very crowded. For Vietnamese people, Tết is the meaning of family, of starting new and of hope for a good year." },
  { kind: "informative", name: "unfinished college draft on the history of American Sign Language with bracketed notes to self", text: "ASL history paper DRAFT 1 — due Thurs\n\nAmerican Sign Language is a complete natural language with its own grammar, and it is used by [somewhere around 500,000? check this number, sources disagree a lot] Deaf people in the United States and parts of Canada. Even though a lot of hearing people assume ASL is just English spelled out on the hands, it actually developed from a mix of French Sign Language and signing systems that were already in use in America in the early 1800s.\n\nThe usual starting point is 1817, when Thomas Hopkins Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc opened the American School for the Deaf in Hartford, Connecticut. Clerc was a Deaf teacher from the Paris school and he brought French Sign Language with him. Students arriving at Hartford brought their own home signs and local sign languages, including the one from Martha's Vineyard, where hereditary deafness was common enough that [a lot of? most?] hearing people on the island signed too. Over a few generations these mixed together into what became ASL. This is why ASL and French Sign Language still share a lot of vocabulary while ASL and British Sign Language are not mutually intelligible.\n\n[paragraph about Milan 1880 + oralism — schools banning sign, Deaf teachers fired. need a source for the % of Deaf teachers dropping]\n\nIn 1960 William Stokoe, a linguist at Gallaudet, published Sign Language Structure, which argued that ASL signs could be broken down into parts like handshape, location, and movement the same way spoken words break down into sounds. This was a big deal because it gave ASL recognition as a real language and not a \"broken\" form of English.\n\n[Deaf President Now 1988 — maybe? or is that off topic]\n\n[conclusion — ASL today, classes in high schools, interpreters, ADA]" },
  { kind: "informative", name: "6th grade animal report on axolotls split into headed sections", text: "The Axolotl\nBy Jaden P.\nGrade 6\n\nIntroduction\nHave you ever seen an animal that looks like it is always smiling? The axolotl (say it like ACK-suh-lot-ul) is a kind of salamander from Mexico. It is famous because it can grow back parts of its body.\n\nWhat it looks like\nAxolotls are usually about 9 to 12 inches long. They have feathery gills sticking out of the sides of their head that look like a crown. In the wild most of them are dark brown or black with spots, but the ones people keep as pets can be pink, white, gold, or even gray. They have small eyes and no eyelids.\n\nHabitat\nWild axolotls only live in one place in the whole world, which is Lake Xochimilco and the canals near Mexico City. A long time ago they also lived in Lake Chalco but that lake was drained. The water they like is cool and has a lot of plants.\n\nDiet\nAxolotls are carnivores. They eat worms, insect larvae, small fish, and crustaceans. They do not chew their food. Instead they suck it in really fast like a vacuum.\n\nSpecial abilities\nThe coolest thing about axolotls is regeneration. If an axolotl loses a leg, it can grow a whole new one with bones and everything. It can even regrow parts of its heart, spinal cord, and brain. Another weird fact is that axolotls stay like babies their whole life. Most salamanders go on land when they grow up, but axolotls keep their gills and stay in the water. This is called neoteny.\n\nAre they endangered?\nYes. Axolotls are critically endangered in the wild because of pollution, the city growing, and fish like tilapia that people put in the lake which eat the baby axolotls. Scientists in Mexico are trying to help by making safe areas in the canals.\n\nConclusion\nAxolotls are amazing animals that scientists study to learn about healing. I hope people can save them so they don't disappear from the wild." },
  { kind: "bookreport", name: "5th grade book report on Hatchet, simple and enthusiastic", text: "Hatchet Book Report\nLiam Ostrowski\nRoom 14\n\nThe book I read is Hatchet by Gary Paulsen. It is about a boy named Brian Robeson who is 13 years old. His parents are divorced and he is flying in a small plane to visit his dad in Canada. Then the pilot has a heart attack and dies and Brian has to crash land the plane in a lake. He survives but he is all alone in the woods.\n\nThe only thing Brian has is a hatchet that his mom gave him before he left. At first he doesn't know what to do and he is really hungry and gets attacked by mosquitoes. He eats some berries that make him sick. Then he finds out that if he hits the hatchet on a rock it makes sparks and he can make a fire. That was my favorite part because he finally had a way to stay warm and keep animals away.\n\nBrian learns to hunt fish with a spear and later with a bow and arrow that he makes. A porcupine sticks quills in his leg and a moose attacks him and a tornado wrecks his shelter. That was a lot of bad stuff happening! At the end he gets the survival pack out of the plane and turns on the emergency transmitter and a pilot comes and rescues him. He was in the woods for 54 days.\n\nThe main character is Brian. He changes a lot in the book. At the start he is sad and he cries a lot because of the \"Secret\" about his mom. By the end he is tough and patient and he thinks before he acts.\n\nI really liked this book because it was exciting and I always wanted to know what would happen next. I would give it 5 stars. I would recommend it to kids who like adventure and the outdoors. I want to read the other books about Brian next." },
  { kind: "bookreport", name: "6th grade fill-in book report form on Wonder with labeled fields", text: "Book Report Form\nName: Sofia Ramirez-Klein\nDate: 9/26\nTitle: Wonder\nAuthor: R.J. Palacio\nGenre: realistic fiction\nNumber of pages: 310\n\nSetting: The story takes place in North River Heights, a neighborhood in Manhattan, New York, mostly at Beecher Prep middle school during one school year.\n\nMain characters:\n● August \"Auggie\" Pullman – a 10 year old boy who was born with a facial difference. He was homeschooled until 5th grade.\n● Via – Auggie's older sister who is in high school. She loves Auggie but sometimes feels left out.\n● Jack Will – becomes Auggie's friend but then says something mean when he thinks Auggie can't hear him.\n● Summer – a girl who sits with Auggie at lunch because she wants to, not because a teacher told her to.\n● Julian – the bully.\n\nSummary:\nAuggie starts going to a real school for the first time in fifth grade. A lot of kids stare at him and Julian is mean and starts a game called \"the Plague\" where kids pretend they will get sick if they touch Auggie. On Halloween Auggie overhears Jack saying something hurtful and he is really upset. Later Jack punches Julian for making fun of Auggie, and they become friends again. On a nature retreat some older kids from another school bully Auggie and Jack, and kids from his grade stand up for him. At the end of the year Auggie wins the Henry Ward Beecher medal and everyone claps for him.\n\nThe book is told by different characters, not just Auggie. I thought that was interesting because you find out what Via and Jack were thinking too.\n\nFavorite part: The nature retreat, when Amos, Miles and Henry stick up for Auggie even though they used to be on Julian's side.\n\nConflict: Auggie vs. the other kids (person vs. society) and also Auggie vs. himself because he has to be brave.\n\nTheme / lesson: \"When given the choice between being right or being kind, choose kind.\" This is Mr. Browne's September precept and I think it is the main lesson.\n\nMy opinion: I liked this book a lot. Some parts made me sad but it had a happy ending. I would recommend it to anyone in 4th grade and up. 4.5 out of 5 stars." },
  { kind: "bookreport", name: "11th grade independent reading book report on the nonfiction book Into the Wild", text: "Marcus Delacroix\nEnglish 11 — Mrs. Penhallow\nIndependent Reading Book Report #2\nOctober 9, 2026\n\nInto the Wild by Jon Krakauer\n\nFor my second independent reading book I chose Into the Wild, a nonfiction book by Jon Krakauer that came out in 1996. It started as an article Krakauer wrote for Outside magazine and he expanded it into a full book. It tells the true story of Christopher McCandless, a young man from a well-off family in Virginia who graduated from Emory University in 1990, gave away about $24,000 of savings to charity, and then disappeared to travel around the country under the name \"Alexander Supertramp.\"\n\nThe book is not told in order. It starts at the end, with McCandless walking into the Alaskan wilderness north of Mount McKinley in April 1992, and with the discovery of his body in an abandoned bus that September. Then Krakauer goes back and follows his two years of wandering. McCandless worked on a grain elevator in South Dakota for a man named Wayne Westerberg, paddled a canoe down the Colorado River into Mexico, lived at the Slabs in California with a couple of drifters, and spent time with an old man named Ron Franz who wanted to adopt him. Krakauer interviews all of these people, so you get a picture of McCandless from a lot of different angles.\n\nWhat surprised me is that Krakauer puts himself in the book. In a couple chapters he talks about his own trip climbing the Devils Thumb in Alaska when he was in his twenties and how he was reckless like McCandless. He also compares McCandless to other young men who went into the wilderness and died, like Everett Ruess. I think he does this because he's trying to show McCandless wasn't just crazy or stupid, which is what a lot of Alaskans said after the original article came out.\n\nThe book also tries to figure out how he died. Krakauer's theory was that he ate wild potato seeds that were toxic. People have argued about this for years and I looked it up after I finished and found out the theory has changed a few times.\n\nMy opinion is mixed. I liked the parts about the people McCandless met, especially Ron Franz, because it was heartbreaking how much he cared about him. But sometimes I got frustrated with McCandless because he hurt his family by cutting them off completely, and his sister and parents didn't know where he was for two years. I would recommend this book to people who like true stories and adventure, but I think it's better if you go in knowing it is more about the question of why someone would do this than about survival. I'd give it a 7/10." },
  { kind: "bookreport", name: "ESL 7th grader's short book report on Charlotte's Web", text: "My Book Report: Charlotte's Web\nThe book I read is Charlotte's Web. The writer is E. B. White. This book is about a pig name Wilbur and a spider name Charlotte.\nIn the beginning, Wilbur is very small pig and Mr. Arable want to kill him. His daughter Fern say no and she save Wilbur. Later Wilbur go to live on the farm of Fern's uncle, Mr. Zuckerman. Wilbur is lonely there but then he meet Charlotte. She is a spider who live in the barn door. They become best friend.\nOne day Wilbur find out that the farmer will kill him for food in Christmas. He is very scare and he cry. Charlotte promise to save him. She write words in her web like \"SOME PIG\" and \"TERRIFIC\" and \"RADIANT\" and \"HUMBLE\". People think it is a miracle and they come to see Wilbur. Templeton the rat help Charlotte to find the words from old paper.\nAt the fair Wilbur win a special prize and now Mr. Zuckerman will not kill him. But Charlotte is old and she die at the fair. Wilbur take her egg sac back to the barn. In spring the baby spiders come out, and three of them stay with Wilbur.\nMy favorite character is Charlotte because she is smart and she is a good friend. The ending make me sad. I learn from this book that a true friend help you even when it is hard. I like this book and I think other students will like it too." },
  { kind: "bookreport", name: "unfinished 4th grade book report on The Wild Robot with empty sections", text: "The Wild Robot by Peter Brown\n\nBeginning:\nA ship sinks in a storm and some boxes wash up on an island. Inside one box is a robot. Some otters play with the box and accidentally turn her on. Her name is ROZZUM unit 7134 but everyone calls her Roz. At first the animals are scared of her and think she is a monster.\n\nMiddle:\nRoz learns how to survive by watching the animals. She accidentally crushes a goose nest and there is one egg left so she takes care of it. The baby goose hatches and she names him Brightbill. She learns to talk to the animals and\n\nEnd:\n\n\nCharacters:\nRoz - the robot. She is kind and wants to learn.\nBrightbill - the gosling\nFink -\n\nWhat I think:\ni liked it so far because" },
  { kind: "discussion", name: "intro psych Canvas post applying classical conditioning to the writer's own life", place: "canvas.instructure.com/courses/50317/discussion_topics/412208", text: "Week 4 prompt: Describe an example of classical conditioning from your own life. Identify the UCS, UCR, CS, and CR.\n\nHi everyone,\n\nMy example is kind of embarrassing but it's the clearest one I have. My roommate's phone alarm is this specific marimba ringtone, and for most of last year she set it for 6:15 am because she had early practice. The alarm itself was super loud and would jolt me awake and my heart would basically be pounding before I even knew what was going on. Now, months later, if I hear that same marimba sound in public (a guy in my chem lab has it as his ringtone) I get this little jolt of stress and my shoulders tense up, even though I'm wide awake and nothing is wrong.\n\nSo here's how I'd break it down:\nUCS: being suddenly woken up out of deep sleep by a loud noise\nUCR: startle response / racing heart\nCS: the marimba ringtone\nCR: tension and a stress jolt when I hear the ringtone, even when I'm awake\n\nI think the reason it stuck so well is that the pairing happened almost every single day for months, which is a lot of trials. I'm curious if it would go through extinction if I heard the ringtone a bunch of times without being startled. Probably, but I'm not volunteering to test it lol.\n\nLooking forward to reading everyone else's!\n\n- Priya" },
  { kind: "discussion", name: "nursing ethics reply to a classmate about informed consent and interpreters", place: "bb.westbrookuniversity.edu/ultra/courses/_88214_1/discussion/_412220_1", text: "RE: Week 6 – Informed Consent and Language Barriers\n\nHi Danielle,\n\nThank you for sharing the case from your clinical rotation. I agree that the biggest problem was that the patient signed the consent form after the procedure had been explained only through her teenage son. As you pointed out, this raises real questions about whether the consent was truly informed. Children and family members are not trained in medical terminology, and they may leave out information to protect their parent from worry, which is exactly what we want to avoid.\n\nI would add that this also connects to the principle of autonomy from Chapter 3. Holloway and Meade (2021) explain that autonomy depends on the patient having enough understanding to make a free choice. If the information is filtered through a relative, the nurse has no way to confirm what the patient actually understood. On my unit we use a video interpreter on a tablet, and our policy says family members should not interpret except in a true emergency. It does slow things down, but I have seen patients ask completely different questions once they hear the explanation from a professional interpreter.\n\nOne question I had for you: did anyone on the team document that the son was used as an interpreter? I am wondering if that would be considered a documentation issue as well as an ethical one.\n\nGreat post, it made me think about my own practice.\n\nRuth M.\n\nReference\nHolloway, S. K., & Meade, T. R. (2021). Ethical practice in nursing: Principles and cases (3rd ed.). Carrow Academic Press." },
  { kind: "discussion", name: "high school US history Google Classroom answer on the most helpful New Deal program", text: "Question: Which New Deal program do you think did the most to help Americans during the Great Depression? Use at least one fact from the reading.\nI think the CCC (Civilian Conservation Corps) helped the most. It gave jobs to young men who couldn't find work and they planted trees, built trails and worked in national parks. The reading said around 3 million men joined over the years it was around. They had to send most of their paycheck ($25 out of $30 a month) home to their families so it helped the whole family not just the guy. Also a lot of the stuff they built is still here today like the stone buildings at state parks. Some people might say social security was more important because it still exists but it didn't really help people right away in the 1930s, the CCC did." },
  { kind: "discussion", name: "online MBA week 3 discussion post on managing remote teams, polished, ends with a question to the group", text: "Week 3 Discussion: Trust and Accountability in Distributed Teams\n\nThis week's readings argue that remote teams fail less because of technology and more because managers try to replicate in-office supervision online. That matched my experience almost exactly. When my company moved our analytics group to fully remote in 2021, our director's first instinct was to require cameras on during all working hours and to check activity status in Teams. Within three months we had lost two of our strongest analysts, and the ones who stayed told us in a skip-level meeting that they felt watched rather than managed.\n\nWhat turned things around was a shift from monitoring inputs to agreeing on outputs. We started each sprint with a short written brief for every deliverable: what \"done\" looks like, who the stakeholder is, and the date. Status updates moved to an asynchronous thread, and our standing meeting dropped from daily to twice a week. The interesting part was that accountability actually went up, because expectations were written down and visible to everyone instead of living in the manager's head.\n\nI think this connects to the GitLab case we read, where the handbook-first culture essentially makes documentation the manager. One limitation I see, though, is that this approach rewards people who are strong writers. A few of our team members who are excellent analysts but less comfortable writing in English struggled with the async model, and we had to add optional \"office hours\" calls to give them another channel.\n\nQuestion for the group: for those of you who manage hybrid teams, have you found ways to keep the people who come into the office from getting an advantage in visibility and promotions over the people who stay remote?" },
  { kind: "discussion", name: "week 1 introduce-yourself post in a community college online sociology course", place: "elearn.eastmesa.edu/d2l/le/248117/discussions/threads/1029934/View", text: "Hi everyone! My name is Teresa and this is my first online class ever so bear with me lol. I'm 34, I live with my husband and two kids (8 and 5) and a very loud beagle named Biscuit. I work part time at a dental office doing front desk and I'm taking this Intro to Sociology class because I'm working toward my associate's degree so I can eventually apply to the dental hygiene program.\n\nFun fact about me: I used to be a competitive roller skater when I was a teenager.\n\nSomething I'm hoping to learn in this class is how to understand people better, especially since I work with the public every day. Looking forward to getting to know you all!" },
  { kind: "review", name: "Yelp review of a small pho restaurant, 4 stars", place: "www.yelp.com/writeareview/biz/pho-saigon-corner-portland", text: "4 stars\n\nCame here on a rainy Tuesday because the line at the ramen place next door was out the door, and honestly I'm glad it was. Pho Saigon Corner is tiny (maybe 10 tables) and the decor is basically a TV playing Paris By Night and a lucky cat on the counter, but the food is legit.\n\nGot the #1 dac biet with everything. Broth was clear, not greasy, with a lot of star anise and charred onion flavor. It didn't need a bunch of hoisin to taste like something which is my test for good pho. The rare steak was sliced thin and actually came out pink, and there was plenty of tendon and tripe if you're into that. Large was $16.50 which is about normal for the area now.\n\nMy partner got the bun cha gio and said the egg rolls were super crispy, but the vermicelli bowl was a little small for the price.\n\nMinus one star because service was SLOW. One lady was handling every table and the register, so it took 15 min to get our check. She was really nice about it though and brought us extra basil without asking.\n\nWill be back, especially when it's cold out. Cash gets you a 5% discount fyi." },
  { kind: "review", name: "reader review of Project Hail Mary with star rating, drafted in a doc before posting", text: "★★★★☆\nProject Hail Mary — Andy Weir\nI went in mostly blind and I'm really glad I did, so I'll keep this vague. Ryland Grace wakes up on a spaceship with no memory of who he is or why he's there, and the two other crew members are dead. The book alternates between him solving his way out of one problem after another in the present and flashbacks that slowly explain how he got there.\nIf you liked The Martian you already know the formula: a narrator who explains the science in a chatty, joke-y way and survives on math and stubbornness. Weir is still really good at this. There's a stretch in the middle involving a certain new arrival (no spoilers) that is genuinely one of the most fun things I've read in years, and I did not expect a book about astrophysics to make me tear up over a friendship.\nMy complaints: the humor is very \"dad joke\" and it wears thin after 400 pages. Grace's voice and Mark Watney's voice are almost interchangeable. And some of the flashback chapters felt like they existed to deliver plot information rather than because they were interesting scenes. The big reveal about Grace's past landed for me, but I could see readers finding it a little convenient.\nStill, I tore through it in three days. Recommended if you want something optimistic and nerdy. I've heard the audiobook is even better because of the way they handle a certain character's voice, so that might be the way to go." },
  { kind: "review", name: "Amazon 2-star review of a standing desk converter that wobbles", place: "www.amazon.com/review/create-review/edit", text: "2.0 out of 5 stars — Looks great, wobbles like crazy at standing height\n\nI bought the LiftPro 32\" converter to use on my existing desk because I didn't want to buy a whole new desk for my home office. Setup was easy (it comes fully assembled, you just lift it out of the box, which takes two people because it's about 35 lbs). The surface is a nice size and fits my two 24\" monitors side by side with room for a keyboard on the lower tray.\n\nThe problem is stability. At sitting height it's fine. But once you raise it past about 14 inches, the whole top shakes every time I type. Not a little bit — my monitors visibly bounce. I tried tightening every bolt I could find and pushing it closer to the wall and it's still bad. For a product whose entire purpose is to be used standing, this is a pretty big deal. I get a little motion-sick on video calls looking at my own screen shake.\n\nOther issues:\n- The gas spring is strong, so when you release the handle with nothing on it, it shoots up fast. Watch your chin.\n- The keyboard tray has no wrist rest and the front edge is kind of sharp.\n- Customer service answered my email in 2 days (good) but just sent me a PDF of the manual I already had (bad).\n\nIf you only need it for occasional standing and have a light setup like one laptop, it might be OK. For a dual monitor setup, I'd spend the extra money on a real electric sit-stand desk. I'm going to return mine." },
  { kind: "review", name: "high school film review of Spirited Away for a media literacy class, MLA heading", text: "Hana Wójcik\nMedia Literacy\nMr. Ferraro\n14 October 2026\n\nSpirited Away: Still the Gold Standard\n\nHayao Miyazaki's Spirited Away came out in 2001 and won the Oscar for Best Animated Feature, but I had somehow never seen it until this assignment. Twenty-five years later, it still feels more original than almost anything I've seen in theaters recently.\n\nThe film follows Chihiro, a sulky ten-year-old who is moving to a new town with her parents. When they wander into what looks like an abandoned theme park, her parents eat food left out for the spirits and turn into pigs, and Chihiro ends up working in a bathhouse run by a witch named Yubaba to try to save them. That premise sounds strange, and it is, but the movie never stops to explain itself. It trusts the audience to accept the rules of its world.\n\nThe animation is the first thing anyone mentions, and for good reason. Every frame is hand-drawn and full of detail, from the steam in the boiler room to the soot sprites carrying lumps of coal. One of my favorite scenes is the train ride across the flooded plain, which has almost no dialogue and no action. Most American animated movies would never risk a scene that slow, but it is the moment where the film feels most magical.\n\nThe characters are another strength. Chihiro is not a typical hero; she is whiny and scared at first and grows braver in small, believable steps. Even No-Face, who becomes genuinely frightening, ends up being more lonely than evil. Joe Hisaishi's score holds everything together, especially the piano theme that plays over the ending.\n\nMy only criticism is that the middle section gets a little crowded with new characters, and the way Chihiro rescues her parents at the end felt rushed on a first viewing.\n\nOverall, Spirited Away deserves its reputation. I would give it 9.5 out of 10 and recommend the subtitled version over the English dub, although both are good." },
  { kind: "review", name: "Steam user review of an invented indie fishing game, not recommended, lowercase and slangy", place: "steamcommunity.com/id/gr4ndpa_n00dle/recommended/2210460", text: "Not Recommended\n14.2 hrs on record\n\nwanted to love this one. the trailer for Hollowmere looked like dredge meets stardew and the first like 3 hours are honestly great, the art is gorgeous and the fishing minigame is satisfying. but after you unlock the second island it turns into the biggest grind ive ever seen. you need 40 of the same eel to upgrade your boat and the eel has a 5% spawn rate at night only. 40!!!\n\nalso the save system is busted, it only saves when you sleep in the inn so if it crashes (which it did 3 times for me) you lose the whole day. lost a rare catch that way and alt f4'd for the night\n\ndevs seem nice and responsive on discord so maybe it gets patched. if they fix the grind and add autosave ill flip this to a thumbs up. for now wait for a sale or just play dredge" },
  { kind: "resume", name: "high school junior's first resume for a part-time retail or food service job", text: "MAYA OKONKWO-REYES\nFremont, CA 94538 | (510) 555-0174 | maya.okreyes@gmail.com\n\nOBJECTIVE\nHardworking and friendly high school junior looking for a part-time position in retail or food service where I can learn new skills and contribute to a team.\n\nEDUCATION\nAmerican High School, Fremont, CA\nExpected Graduation: June 2028\nGPA: 3.7\nRelevant coursework: Spanish III, Business Math, Intro to Marketing\n\nEXPERIENCE\nBabysitter — Self-employed | Summer 2024 – Present\n- Care for two children (ages 4 and 7) up to 15 hours a week\n- Prepare snacks, help with homework, and plan activities\n- Trusted to handle emergency contacts and bedtime routines\n\nVolunteer, Tri-City Food Pantry | Sept 2025 – Present\n* Sort and stock donated food for 3-hour shifts twice a month\n* Greet clients and help them check in at the front table\n* Helped organize the 2025 Thanksgiving drive that collected over 2,000 cans\n\nACTIVITIES\nJunior Varsity Volleyball (2 years)\nKey Club — Events Committee\nSchool Spirit Week planning team\n\nSKILLS\nBilingual: English and Spanish\nGoogle Docs, Sheets, and Canva\nCash handling (school snack bar)\nCPR and First Aid certified (American Red Cross, 2025)\n\nReferences available upon request." },
  { kind: "resume", name: "senior backend software engineer resume with quantified bullets", text: "DANIEL HWANG\nSenior Software Engineer\nSeattle, WA · daniel.hwang.dev@proton.me · github.com/dhwang-dev · linkedin.com/in/danielhwang\nSUMMARY\nBackend engineer with 10 years of experience building high-throughput payment and logistics systems in Go and Java. Led migrations from monoliths to event-driven services, cut infrastructure spend, and mentored teams of 4–8 engineers. Looking for a staff-level role focused on distributed systems reliability.\nEXPERIENCE\nNorthbeam Logistics — Seattle, WA\nSenior Software Engineer, Platform Team | Mar 2022 – Present\n● Led the redesign of the shipment-tracking pipeline from batch jobs to Kafka streams, reducing average tracking-update latency from 11 minutes to under 40 seconds\n● Designed an idempotent retry framework adopted by 14 services, cutting duplicate carrier API charges by roughly $310K per year\n● Drove on-call reform (runbooks, SLOs, alert pruning) that lowered pages per week from 46 to 9\n● Mentor four mid-level engineers; two promoted to senior within 18 months\nClearpath Payments — Portland, OR\nSoftware Engineer II → Senior Software Engineer | Jun 2018 – Feb 2022\n● Built the ledger reconciliation service in Go processing 3M+ transactions daily with 99.99% uptime\n● Migrated core authorization flow off a Java monolith into 6 services with zero customer-facing downtime\n● Introduced contract testing (Pact) across teams, reducing integration-related production incidents by 35%\nBrightline Software (contractor) — Remote\nSoftware Engineer | Aug 2016 – May 2018\n● Developed REST APIs and admin tooling for healthcare scheduling clients using Spring Boot and PostgreSQL\nEDUCATION\nB.S. Computer Science, University of Washington — 2016\nSKILLS\nLanguages: Go, Java, Kotlin, Python, SQL\nInfrastructure: Kafka, PostgreSQL, Redis, Kubernetes, Terraform, AWS (ECS, Lambda, DynamoDB)\nPractices: event-driven architecture, SLOs and incident response, API design, code review and mentoring" },
  { kind: "resume", name: "academic CV of an ecology PhD candidate with publications, grants and teaching", text: "CURRICULUM VITAE\n\nRosalind T. Achebe-Marsh\nDepartment of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology\nUniversity of Arizona, Tucson, AZ 85721\nrachebemarsh@arizona.edu\n\nEDUCATION\n2021–present\tPh.D. candidate, Ecology and Evolutionary Biology, University of Arizona (expected May 2027)\n\tDissertation: \"Nurse-plant facilitation and seedling survival of saguaro (Carnegiea gigantea) under prolonged drought\"\n\tAdvisor: Dr. Helen Q. Barrios\n2019\tB.S. Biology, minor in Statistics, Oregon State University, magna cum laude\n\nPUBLICATIONS\nAchebe-Marsh, R. T., & Barrios, H. Q. (2025). Shade quality, not shade quantity, predicts saguaro seedling survival in a drought year. Journal of Arid Environments, 228, 105301.\nLund, K. P., Achebe-Marsh, R. T., Okafor, D., & Barrios, H. Q. (2024). Rodent seed predation varies with nurse-plant identity in the Sonoran Desert. Oecologia, 204(3), 611–622.\nAchebe-Marsh, R. T. (in review). A low-cost soil moisture logger network for long-term field monitoring. Methods in Ecology and Evolution.\n\nPRESENTATIONS\n2025\tEcological Society of America Annual Meeting (oral). \"When the nurse runs dry: facilitation collapse under extreme drought.\"\n2024\tSouthwestern Association of Naturalists Annual Meeting (poster)\n2023\tUA EEB Graduate Research Symposium (oral) — Best Student Talk award\n\nGRANTS AND FELLOWSHIPS\n2023\tTucson Cactus and Succulent Society Research Grant ($2,500)\n2022\tNSF Graduate Research Fellowship Program, Honorable Mention\n2022\tUA Graduate College Fieldwork Award ($1,800)\n\nTEACHING\nGraduate Teaching Assistant, University of Arizona\n\tECOL 182R: Introductory Biology II Lab (4 semesters)\n\tECOL 302: Ecology (2 semesters) — led weekly discussion sections of 30 students\nGuest lecture, ECOL 406: Conservation Biology, \"Facilitation in desert plant communities\" (2025)\n\nSERVICE AND OUTREACH\nVolunteer docent, Saguaro National Park, 2022–present\nGraduate student representative, EEB Diversity Committee, 2023–2024\nAd hoc reviewer, Journal of Arid Environments (2 manuscripts)\n\nSKILLS\nR (lme4, glmmTMB, tidyverse), soil and climate data loggers, QGIS, Spanish (conversational)" },
  { kind: "resume", name: "logistics resume by an ESL career changer from Ukraine, uneven formatting", text: "Resume\n\nOleksandr Hrytsenko\nAddress: 2215 W Fullerton Ave apt 3B, Chicago IL 60647\nPhone: 773-555-0189\nEmail: ohrytsenko.work@gmail.com\n\nObjective: To get a position of logistics coordinator or warehouse supervisor in company where I can use my experience in management of team and inventory. I am responsible and very organized person, I like to solve problems.\n\nWork Experience:\n\n2023 - now   Warehouse Associate / Shift Lead, Midwest Parcel Solutions, Chicago IL\n- Lead the team of 8 workers on night shift\n- Control inventory with scanner and WMS system (Manhattan)\n- Training new employees about safety rules and how to use the equipment\n- Reduce the mistakes in picking about 20% after I make new labels for shelves\n- Forklift operator (certified)\n\n2017 - 2022   Logistics Manager, \"Agro-Trans\" LLC, Vinnytsia, Ukraine\n- Planning the routes for 25 trucks for transport the grain and fertilizers\n- Make contracts with the clients and partners\n- Responsible for documents for export (customs, CMR)\n- Manage the budget for fuel and repair of trucks\n\n2014 - 2017 Dispatcher, Vinnytsia City Bus Company\n- dispatch of buses, work with drivers schedule\n\nEducation:\nVinnytsia National Technical University — Bachelor degree in Transport Technologies (2014) (diploma evaluated by ECE as equal to US bachelor degree)\nEnglish classes - Truman College, Chicago (2022-2023)\n\nSkills:\nLanguages - Ukrainian (native), Russian (fluent), English (intermediate/upper-intermediate), Polish (basic)\nComputer - MS Excel, Word, 1C, Manhattan WMS, Google Sheets\nForklift certification, OSHA 10" },
  { kind: "resume", name: "functional skills-based resume for a parent returning to work after an eight-year gap, with a note to self", text: "JENNIFER A. CALLOWAY\nColumbus, OH | 614-555-0122 | jen.calloway@outlook.com\n\nPROFILE\nDetail-oriented administrative professional with experience in office management, bookkeeping, and event coordination, returning to the workforce after eight years as a full-time caregiver. Known for staying calm under pressure, keeping complex schedules on track, and building good relationships with vendors and volunteers.\n\nCORE SKILLS\n\nOffice & Administrative\n- Managed scheduling, correspondence, and filing for a 12-person insurance office\n- Created and maintained client records in Salesforce and Excel\n- Wrote meeting agendas and minutes for monthly staff meetings\n\nBookkeeping & Budgeting\n- Processed accounts payable and receivable using QuickBooks\n- As PTO Treasurer, tracked an annual budget of $48,000 and prepared monthly reports for the board\n- Reconciled bank statements and handled fundraising deposits\n\nEvent & Volunteer Coordination\n- Organized school fundraisers including a spring auction that raised $22,000\n- Recruited and scheduled 60+ parent volunteers each school year\n- Negotiated donations and discounts with local businesses\n\nWORK HISTORY\nTreasurer (volunteer), Westbrook Elementary PTO — 2021–2025\nOffice Coordinator, Hartley & Moss Insurance Agency — 2011–2017\nAdministrative Assistant, Buckeye Property Management — 2008–2011\n\nEDUCATION\nA.A.S., Business Management, Columbus State Community College\n[add graduation year? or leave off]\n\nTRAINING\nQuickBooks Online Certification (refresher course, 2026)\nMicrosoft Excel Intermediate — LinkedIn Learning, 2026" },
  { kind: "workscited", name: "high school MLA Works Cited for a Harlem Renaissance paper, with small formatting mistakes", text: "Works Cited\n\n\"Harlem Renaissance.\" Encyclopaedia Britannica, www.britannica.com/event/Harlem-Renaissance-American-literature-and-art. Accessed 3 Oct. 2026.\n\nHughes, Langston. \"The Negro Artist and the Racial Mountain.\" The Nation, 23 June 1926.\n\nHutchinson, George. The Harlem Renaissance in Black and White. Harvard University Press, 1995.\n\nLewis, David Levering. When Harlem Was in Vogue. Penguin Books, 1997.\n\nLocke, Alain, editor. The New Negro: An Interpretation. Albert and Charles Boni, 1925.\n\nNational Museum of African American History and Culture. \"The Harlem Renaissance\". Smithsonian, nmaahc.si.edu/explore/stories/harlem-renaissance. accessed october 1 2026\n\nWintz, Cary D. Black Culture and the Harlem Renaissance, Texas A&M University Press, 1996" },
  { kind: "workscited", name: "APA 7 reference list for a nursing paper on preventing falls in older adults", text: "References\nAgency for Healthcare Research and Quality. (2023). Preventing falls in hospitals: A toolkit for improving quality of care. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. https://www.ahrq.gov/patient-safety/settings/hospital/fall-prevention/toolkit/index.html\nCenters for Disease Control and Prevention. (2024, May 9). Older adult falls data. https://www.cdc.gov/falls/data-research/index.html\nHempel, S., Newberry, S., Wang, Z., Booth, M., Shanman, R., Johnsen, B., Shier, V., Saliba, D., Spector, W. D., & Ganz, D. A. (2013). Hospital fall prevention: A systematic review of implementation, components, adherence, and effectiveness. Journal of the American Geriatrics Society, 61(4), 483–494. https://doi.org/10.1111/jgs.12169\nMontero-Odasso, M., van der Velde, N., Martin, F. C., Petrovic, M., Tan, M. P., Ryg, J., Aguilar-Navarro, S., Alexander, N. B., Becker, C., Blain, H., Bourke, R., Cameron, I. D., Camicioli, R., Clemson, L., Close, J., Delbaere, K., Duan, L., Duque, G., Dyer, S. M., ... Masud, T. (2022). World guidelines for falls prevention and management for older adults: A global initiative. Age and Ageing, 51(9), Article afac205. https://doi.org/10.1093/ageing/afac205\nMorse, J. M. (2009). Preventing patient falls: Establishing a fall intervention program (2nd ed.). Springer Publishing Company.\nSherrington, C., Fairhall, N. J., Wallbank, G. K., Tiedemann, A., Michaleff, Z. A., Howard, K., Clemson, L., Hopewell, S., & Lamb, S. E. (2019). Exercise for preventing falls in older people living in the community. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, 2019(1), Article CD012424. https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD012424.pub2\nTzeng, H.-M., & Yin, C.-Y. (2008). The extrinsic risk factors for inpatient falls in hospital patient rooms. Journal of Nursing Care Quality, 23(3), 233–241." },
  { kind: "workscited", name: "Chicago-style bibliography for a college history paper on the Erie Canal, one entry out of order", text: "Bibliography\n\nBernstein, Peter L. Wedding of the Waters: The Erie Canal and the Making of a Great Nation. New York: W. W. Norton, 2005.\n\nClinton, DeWitt. Memorial of the Citizens of New-York, in Favour of a Canal Navigation between the Great Western Lakes and the Tide-Waters of the Hudson. New York, 1816.\n\nErie Canalway National Heritage Corridor. \"History and Culture.\" National Park Service. Accessed March 3, 2026. https://www.nps.gov/erie/learn/historyculture/index.htm.\n\nKoeppel, Gerard. Bond of Union: Building the Erie Canal and the American Empire. Cambridge, MA: Da Capo Press, 2009.\n\nLarkin, F. Daniel. New York State Canals: A Short History. Fleischmanns, NY: Purple Mountain Press, 1998.\n\nSheriff, Carol. The Artificial River: The Erie Canal and the Paradox of Progress, 1817–1862. New York: Hill and Wang, 1996.\n\nShaw, Ronald E. Erie Water West: A History of the Erie Canal, 1792–1854. Lexington: University Press of Kentucky, 1966.\n\nWay, Peter. Common Labour: Workers and the Digging of North American Canals, 1780–1860. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1993." },
  { kind: "workscited", name: "messy middle school sources list of bare URLs and notes for a microplastics project", text: "Sources\nhttps://www.nationalgeographic.com/environment/article/microplastics\nhttps://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/microplastics.html\n\"What are microplastics\" - NOAA (same as above?)\nhttps://kids.britannica.com/kids/article/plastic/\nyoutube video about microplastics by SciShow\nhttps://www.epa.gov/trash-free-waters\nbook: Plastic Ocean by Charles Moore\nmy mom (she is a biologist)\nhttps://www.unep.org/plastic-pollution" },
  { kind: "workscited", name: "IEEE-style numbered references for an engineering capstone on solar charging for drones", text: "REFERENCES\n[1] M. Abdelkader, S. Güler, H. Jaleel, and J. S. Shamma, \"Aerial swarms: Recent applications and challenges,\" Current Robotics Reports, vol. 2, no. 3, pp. 309–320, 2021.\n[2] Texas Instruments, \"BQ24650 stand-alone synchronous buck battery charge controller for solar power with MPPT,\" datasheet, 2015.\n[3] M. N. Boukoberine, Z. Zhou, and M. Benbouzid, \"A critical review on unmanned aerial vehicles power supply and energy management: Solutions, strategies, and prospects,\" Applied Energy, vol. 255, art. 113823, 2019.\n[4] T. Esram and P. L. Chapman, \"Comparison of photovoltaic array maximum power point tracking techniques,\" IEEE Trans. Energy Convers., vol. 22, no. 2, pp. 439–449, Jun. 2007.\n[5] A. B. Junaid, A. Konoiko, Y. Zweiri, M. N. Sahinkaya, and L. Seneviratne, \"Autonomous wireless self-charging for multi-rotor unmanned aerial vehicles,\" Energies, vol. 10, no. 6, art. 803, 2017.\n[6] PX4 Autopilot, \"Battery and power module setup,\" PX4 User Guide, 2025. [Online]. Available: https://docs.px4.io/main/en/config/battery.html\n[7] National Renewable Energy Laboratory, \"PVWatts calculator.\" [Online]. Available: https://pvwatts.nrel.gov/. Accessed: Sep. 22, 2026.\n[8] Federal Aviation Administration, \"Small unmanned aircraft systems,\" 14 CFR Part 107, 2016." },
  { kind: "lyrics", name: "pop breakup song with verse, pre-chorus, chorus and bridge labels", text: "Left On Read\n(verse 1)\nYou said you'd call me after midnight\nI kept my phone face up on the bed\nWatched the little dots appear and vanish\nLike every word you never said\n\n(pre-chorus)\nAnd I know, I know\nI should've let it go\n\n(chorus)\n'Cause you left me on read, left me on read\nAll the things I typed are still stuck in my head\nSaw you online at a quarter to three\nYou had time for the world but no time for me\nLeft me on read\n\n(verse 2)\nYour best friend posted you at the party\nYour smile looked brand new in the flash\nI swore I wasn't gonna check it\nThen I zoomed in on the background, what a mess\n\n(pre-chorus)\nAnd I know, I know\nI should've let it go\n\n(chorus)\n\n(bridge)\nMaybe someday you'll scroll back and see\nTwenty-three messages, every one from me\nAnd maybe you'll wonder why it stopped\nWell baby that's the day I finally dropped\n\n(chorus x2)\nLeft me on read\nyeah you left me on read" },
  { kind: "lyrics", name: "rap song about a hometown bus route, hook but no section labels", text: "Eastside Route 9\n\nWoke up to the bus brakes, 6:10 like clockwork\nMama on a double shift, still found time to iron my shirt\nCorner store uncle know my order 'fore I say it\nTwo dollar hot cheese, never had to pay it\nThey said the block was nothing, I said watch the block work\nLittle cousin on the court, pull up, pop, and the shot work\nWeeds in the crack of the sidewalk, still they grow tho\nThat's the metaphor, I ain't gotta spell it slow tho\n\nRoute 9, Route 9, every stop a story\nRoute 9, Route 9, ain't no glamour, still glory\nFrom the laundromat lights to the church on the hill\nIf you never rode the east, then you never been real\n\nTeachers said I talk too much, now I talk for a living\nMic in the basement, egg crate foam on the ceiling\nPops told me son, keep your name clean, it's the one thing\nThey can't repo, can't evict, can't take from you nothing\nRain on the bus shelter, sound like a drum break\nSpit to the rhythm on my way to the front gate\nEverybody wanna leave, I just wanna build it\nPut a studio where the old Blockbuster building\n\nRoute 9, Route 9..." },
  { kind: "lyrics", name: "country song about a late father's pickup truck with bracketed sections", text: "Daddy's '94 Silverado\nby Cody Reinholt\n\n[Verse 1]\nThere's a dent in the tailgate from the summer I turned ten\nBacked it into the mailbox, he just laughed and said \"again\"\nCracked vinyl on the bench seat, smells like coffee and hay\nAnd a Merle Haggard cassette that he never put away\n\n[Chorus]\nShe ain't much to look at, rust is creepin' up the side\nBut she's carried every heartbreak and every Friday night\nGot three hundred thousand miles and his name still on the title\nYeah that old '94 Silverado's the closest thing to Daddy I got left\n\n[Verse 2]\nDrove her to the hospital that cold November night\nSat out in the parking lot with nothin' but the dome light\nThe radio was playin' somethin' slow from '85\nI just held onto that wheel like it could keep him alive\n\n[Chorus]\n\n[Bridge]\nMama says to sell it, says it's time to let it rest\nBut every time that engine turns I swear I hear him in my chest\n\n[Verse 3]\nNow my boy's got his learner's, he's sittin' where I sat\nGot his hands at ten and two and he's wearin' Daddy's hat\n\n[Chorus x2]\n\n[Outro]\nClosest thing to Daddy I got left" },
  { kind: "lyrics", name: "7th grade group parody song about photosynthesis to the tune of Twinkle Twinkle Little Star", text: "Photosynthesis Song\n(to the tune of \"Twinkle Twinkle Little Star\")\nGroup 4: Ellie, Marcus, Dev, Hannah\n\nLittle leaf up in the sun,\nMaking food for everyone,\nChlorophyll is colored green,\nBest food factory ever seen,\nLittle leaf up in the sun,\nMaking food for everyone.\n\nWater coming from the root,\nUp the xylem to the shoot,\nCarbon dioxide from the air,\nThrough the stomata over there,\nMix it up with light and then,\nSugar's made inside again!\n\nSix CO2 and six H2O,\nAdd some sunlight, here we go,\nC6H12O6,\nGlucose is the sugar fix,\nOxygen goes out the door,\nSix O2 and nothing more!\n\nChloroplasts are where it's done,\nLittle leaf up in the sun!" },
  { kind: "lyrics", name: "folk worship song written as a chord chart with chord letters above the lines", text: "Down By The River Gate\nKey of G — capo 2 — strum: D DU UDU\n\nVerse 1\nG                C          G\nI came down to the river gate\n       Em              D\nwith my pockets full of fear\nG                 C          G\nHeard my mother's voice still singing\n      C        D        G\nlike she's standing right here\n\nChorus\nC              G\nWash me, river, wash me clean\nEm               D\nCarry what I cannot hold\nC              G\nEvery road I wandered from\n   Em      D        G\nLeads me back to where I'm known\n\nVerse 2\nG                C          G\nAll the years I spent in running\n       Em            D\nall the doors I shut behind\nG               C          G\nYou were waiting by the water\n      C       D         G\nwith a light I couldn't find\n\n(Chorus)\n\nTag:\nC    D    Em\nBack to You\nC    D    G\nBack to You" },
  { kind: "informative", name: "high school earth science informative essay on the three rock types and the rock cycle, could be mistaken for compare", text: "Ethan Mbeki\nEarth Science — Period 6\nMs. Larkspur\n\nHow Rocks Are Made\n\nEvery rock on Earth, from the granite in a kitchen countertop to the chalk used on a sidewalk, belongs to one of three big groups: igneous, sedimentary, or metamorphic. Geologists sort rocks this way because each group forms through a different process, and knowing which group a rock belongs to tells you something about the history of the place where it was found.\n\nIgneous rocks form when melted rock cools and hardens. Underground, melted rock is called magma; when it reaches the surface through a volcano, it is called lava. When magma cools slowly deep underground, the minerals have time to grow into large crystals, which is why granite has visible speckles of quartz, feldspar, and mica. Lava that cools quickly at the surface forms rocks with tiny crystals, like basalt, or no crystals at all, like obsidian, which looks like black glass.\n\nSedimentary rocks are made from pieces of other rocks, shells, or plant material. Wind, water, and ice break rocks down into sediment and carry it to places like riverbeds, lakes, and the ocean floor. Over thousands of years, layers pile up and the weight of the layers above presses the lower layers together. Minerals dissolved in water act like glue and cement the pieces into solid rock. Sandstone, shale, and limestone are common examples. Sedimentary rocks are the kind that most often contain fossils, because the gentle process of burial can preserve the shapes of plants and animals.\n\nMetamorphic rocks start out as one kind of rock and are changed by heat and pressure without melting. This often happens deep in the crust where tectonic plates collide. The heat and pressure rearrange the minerals, sometimes squeezing them into bands or flat layers. Shale can become slate, limestone becomes marble, and granite can become gneiss.\n\nThese groups are connected by the rock cycle. Any rock can be weathered into sediment, buried and changed by heat and pressure, or melted and cooled again into new igneous rock. The process is extremely slow, but it means the rocks we see today are just one stage in a cycle that has been going on for billions of years." },
  { kind: "discussion", name: "class discussion post that summarizes and responds to Carr's \"Is Google Making Us Stupid?\" and tags a classmate, could be mistaken for summary", text: "Discussion 5 — Carr reading\n\nIn \"Is Google Making Us Stupid?\" (2008), Nicholas Carr argues that the internet is changing the way we read and think. He opens with the scene from 2001: A Space Odyssey where HAL is being disconnected and says his mind is going, and Carr says he feels the same way, like something has been tinkering with his brain. He used to be able to get lost in a long book, but now his concentration drifts after two or three pages. He talks to friends who say the same thing, and he cites a study from University College London where people using research sites skimmed and bounced from source to source without going back. His bigger point is that the tools we use shape our thinking, like how Nietzsche's writing supposedly changed when he started using a typewriter, and that Google's goal of making information efficient could make our thinking shallower.\n\nI agree with Carr more than I expected to. I noticed while reading this article on my laptop that I clicked away twice to check my phone before I finished the first page, which is kind of ironic. But I think he goes too far when he acts like deep reading is just disappearing. Plenty of people still read long novels, and I'd argue the internet also made long-form stuff like podcasts and video essays super popular, which take a lot of attention. Maybe the issue isn't the internet itself but the way apps are designed to interrupt us.\n\n@Kevin, you mentioned in your post that you think Carr is just being nostalgic. Do you think that's still true now that there's actual research on phones and attention span?" },
  { kind: "lyrics", name: "singer-songwriter lyrics about a grandmother's kitchen, lowercase with no section labels, could be mistaken for poem", text: "Flour On The Radio\n\nshe kept the radio on the windowsill\ndusted white from the bread she'd knead\nthe station only came in clear\nwhen the wind blew from the sea\n\nand she'd hum along to anything\nthe weather or the news\nsaid a song is just a story\nthat somebody had to lose\n\noh, flour on the radio\nand sugar on the floor\nleave the back door open, love\nthere's always room for more\n\nmy hands were small, they fit in hers\nwe folded in the butter cold\nshe said don't you ever measure\nwhat the heart already knows\n\noh, flour on the radio\nand sugar on the floor\nleave the back door open, love\nthere's always room for more\n\nnow the house is someone else's\nand they painted all the doors\nbut I still can't bake a loaf of bread\nwithout the static and the shore\n\noh, flour on the radio\nand sugar on the floor\nleave the back door open, love\nthere's always room for more\nthere's always room for more" },
  { kind: "bookreport", name: "9th grade honors book report on The Outsiders with quotes and a theme paragraph, could be mistaken for literary", text: "Kayla Brennan\nEnglish 9 Honors\nMr. Duvall\nSeptember 30, 2026\n\nBook Report: The Outsiders by S. E. Hinton\n\nThe Outsiders was written by S. E. Hinton and published in 1967. Hinton started writing it when she was only fifteen, which is my age, so that was one reason I wanted to read it. The story takes place in Tulsa, Oklahoma, in the 1960s and is narrated by Ponyboy Curtis, a fourteen-year-old greaser who lives with his two older brothers, Darry and Sodapop, after their parents died in a car crash.\n\nThe book is about the rivalry between the greasers, who are poor kids from the east side, and the Socs (short for Socials), the rich kids from the west side. One night Ponyboy and his friend Johnny are jumped by a group of Socs in a park. One of the Socs, Bob, starts drowning Ponyboy in a fountain, and Johnny stabs and kills Bob to save him. The two boys run away with help from Dally and hide in an abandoned church in Windrixville. While they are hiding, Ponyboy reads Gone with the Wind to Johnny and recites the Robert Frost poem \"Nothing Gold Can Stay.\" When the church catches fire with some kids inside, Ponyboy and Johnny run in to save them. Johnny is badly hurt and later dies in the hospital, and Dally is so upset that he gets himself shot by the police.\n\nThe main theme of the book is that people are more alike than they seem. Cherry Valance, a Soc girl, tells Ponyboy, \"Things are rough all over,\" and Ponyboy realizes that the Socs have problems too. Another theme is staying innocent. Before he dies, Johnny tells Ponyboy to \"stay gold,\" which connects back to the Frost poem and means he should hold on to the good parts of himself.\n\nMy favorite character was Sodapop because he is always cheerful and he tries to keep the peace between Darry and Ponyboy. The part that surprised me the most was the ending, when Ponyboy starts writing his English assignment and the first line is the same as the first line of the book.\n\nI liked this book a lot. It was sad but it felt real, and even though it was written almost sixty years ago a lot of the problems, like judging people by where they live, still happen today. I would recommend it to anyone in middle school or high school. I give it 5/5." },
  { kind: "compare", name: "6th grade compare/contrast on cats and dogs as pets, short and simple", text: "Kaylee Morrison\nMrs. Delgado\nELA Period 3\nOctober 2\n\nCats and Dogs\n\nDo you want a cat or a dog? A lot of people can't decide. I have both so I know alot about them. Cats and dogs are both good pets but they are different in alot of ways.\n\nFirst, how they are the same. Both cats and dogs are mammals and they both have fur. They both need food and water and to go to the vet. They both love you, even if cats don't show it as much. My cat Pepper sleeps on my feet every night so I know she loves me.\n\nNow the differences. Dogs need to go on walks and cats don't. You have to take a dog outside to go to the bathroom but a cat uses a litter box. Dogs are louder because they bark at everything like the mail man. Cats are quiet except when they are hungry. Also dogs can learn tricks like sit and shake but my cat does not do tricks at all. She just looks at you.\n\nAnother difference is how much time they take. A dog is more work. My mom says that our dog Biscuit is like having another kid. A cat is more independent and can be home alone all day.\n\nIn conclusion, cats and dogs are alike because they are both furry and loving pets, but they are different because dogs need more work and attention. If you are busy maybe get a cat. If you want a buddy to play with get a dog. I love both of mine the same." },
  { kind: "compare", name: "ESL high school student compares life in Lagos and Houston", text: "Two Cities, One Me\nWhen my family moved from Lagos to Houston two years ago, I thought everything will be different. Some things are very different but some things are more same than I expected. In this essay I will compare the two cities in three areas, the traffic, the food, and the people.\nTraffic is the first thing. In Lagos the traffic is famous, we call it \"go-slow.\" Sometimes it took my father three hours to go to work on the Third Mainland Bridge. There are danfo buses everywhere and the drivers shout for passengers. In Houston there is also bad traffic, especially on I-45, but it is more organized. Everybody stays in there lane and nobody is selling things in the middle of the road. But in Houston you cannot go anywhere without car, because the buses are not many. In Lagos you can always find a danfo or okada even if it is not comfortable.\nSecond is the food. In Lagos I ate jollof rice, suya, and puff-puff almost every week. In Houston we can still find Nigerian food because there is a big Nigerian community in Alief, so this was a surprise for me. The difference is that in Houston the food is more expensive and the portions are big. American food is more sweet. Even the bread is sweet.\nThe last thing is the people. People in Lagos are very loud and direct, if they don't like something they will tell you immediately. In Houston people are friendly and say \"how are you\" but they don't really want the answer. That confused me at first. However in both cities, people care a lot about family and about church, and both are very hot and humid, so I did not have to change my clothes too much.\nIn conclusion Lagos and Houston are different in the traffic, the cost of food and the way people talk, but they are similar because both are big busy cities full of people working hard. I miss Lagos but Houston is becoming home." },
  { kind: "compare", name: "college economics paper contrasting Keynes and Hayek on recessions", text: "Ruth Abernathy\nECON 210: History of Economic Thought\nShort Paper 2\n\nTwo Doctors at the Same Bedside: Keynes and Hayek on the Cure for Recession\n\nWhen the world economy collapsed in the early 1930s, two economists in London offered nearly opposite diagnoses. John Maynard Keynes, at Cambridge, argued that the slump was a failure of total demand that the private economy could not fix on its own. Friedrich Hayek, newly arrived at the London School of Economics, argued that the slump was the painful but necessary correction of an earlier boom built on cheap credit. Ninety years later their argument still structures debates about stimulus, interest rates and austerity. Setting the two side by side shows that they disagree less about the facts of a recession than about time: what caused it in the past, and what a government can safely do in the present.\n\nOn the cause of a downturn, the two start from different places. For Keynes, a recession begins when households and firms decide, all at once, to spend less. Because one person's spending is another person's income, a fall in spending feeds on itself, and the economy can settle at a level of output far below what its workers and factories could produce. Hayek looked further back. In his account, central banks that hold interest rates below their \"natural\" level encourage firms to start long, roundabout projects (factories, housing developments) that consumers' real savings cannot support. The recession is the moment those projects are revealed as mistakes.\n\nThat difference leads directly to their prescriptions. Keynes recommended that government fill the hole in demand by borrowing and spending, even on projects of doubtful value, because putting idle resources to work was better than leaving them idle. Hayek warned that such spending would only prolong the misallocation, propping up the very industries that needed to shrink, and that the cure was to let prices, wages and capital readjust. Where Keynes saw unemployment as waste, Hayek saw it as part of the reallocation.\n\nYet the two share more than their reputations suggest. Both rejected the idea that the economy is always at full employment. Both took money and credit seriously, at a time when many economists treated money as a veil over \"real\" activity. And both were writing against the same enemy: the gold-standard orthodoxy of the 1920s, which they believed had made the crisis worse. Hayek, too, accepted that a \"secondary deflation,\" a collapse in spending beyond what the correction required, could do real harm.\n\nThe deepest contrast is about knowledge. Keynes trusted that a small group of well-informed officials could manage aggregate demand. Hayek's later work, especially \"The Use of Knowledge in Society\" (1945), argued that no planner could gather the scattered, local information that prices transmit. Their disagreement over a recession was really a disagreement about what governments can know.\n\nModern policy borrows from both. The stimulus packages of 2009 and 2020 were Keynesian in spirit, while worries about \"zombie firms\" kept alive by years of low rates are recognizably Hayekian. Neither man won outright, which may be why the debate keeps being restaged, including, memorably, in two rap videos." },
  { kind: "compare", name: "unfinished art history draft comparing Impressionism and Expressionism, with notes to self", text: "Impressionism vs Expressionism (working title?? ask Ms. Haddad if title needs to be creative)\nThesis: Impressionism and Expressionism both broke away from realistic painting but Impressionists painted what the eye sees in a moment while Expressionists painted what the person feels. [make this less clunky]\nBody 1 — what they have in common\nBoth movements rejected the academy style where everything had to look smooth and finished. Both use visible brushstrokes and bright color that is not always \"real\" color. Monet's haystacks are purple and orange in some of them. Kirchner's street scenes have green faces. [find exact painting name — Street, Berlin? check]. Both were criticized when they first came out and critics made fun of them.\nBody 2 — differences: subject/goal\nImpressionists cared about light and how it changes. Monet painted the same cathedral at different times of day. The subject was ordinary life — picnics, boats, train stations, cafes. They wanted to capture a quick moment. Expressionists were less interested in the outside world and more in emotion, anxiety, the city making people lonely. Munch's The Scream is technically before Expressionism but they all looked at it.\nBody 3 — differences: technique\n- Impressionist brushstrokes small, broken, side by side\n- Expressionist = thick, harsh lines, distorted bodies\n- color in impressionism is soft / harmonious, expressionism clashes on purpose\n[turn these into sentences!!]\nBody 4?? time and place — France 1870s vs Germany 1905-1920s, WWI. maybe combine w body 2\nConclusion\n[write last]" },
  { kind: "compare", name: "high school biology compare-contrast of mitosis and meiosis with a table", text: "Jordan Pike\nBiology H – Unit 5 Writing Task\n11/18\n\nMitosis and Meiosis: Same Machinery, Different Jobs\n\nEvery cell in your body came from another cell dividing, but not all divisions are the same. Mitosis and meiosis both split one cell into more cells, and they use a lot of the same steps and the same equipment, like spindle fibers and centrioles. The big difference is what they are for. Mitosis makes copies for growth and repair, while meiosis makes sex cells for reproduction.\n\nThe two processes look alike at the start. In both, the DNA is copied during interphase, the chromosomes condense in prophase, and spindle fibers pull them apart. Both end with cytokinesis, when the cytoplasm pinches in two.\n\nThe differences show up in the details:\n\n\tMitosis\tMeiosis\nNumber of divisions\t1\t2\nCells produced\t2\t4\nChromosome number\tDiploid (46 in humans)\tHaploid (23 in humans)\nGenetically identical?\tYes\tNo\nCrossing over\tNo\tYes, in prophase I\nWhere it happens\tBody (somatic) cells\tOvaries and testes\n\nThe most important difference in the table is whether the new cells are identical. Because of crossing over and the random way homologous pairs line up in metaphase I, every egg or sperm is different. That is why siblings do not look exactly alike. Mitosis, on the other hand, is supposed to make perfect copies. When it doesn't, and cells start dividing out of control, the result can be cancer.\n\nIn short, mitosis and meiosis are like two recipes that start with the same ingredients. One makes a copy of the original dish and the other makes something new every time." },
  { kind: "compare", name: "comparing the 1994 and 2019 Little Women films; could be mistaken for a review", text: "Two Little Womens\nI watched both Little Women movies for this assignment, the 1994 one with Winona Ryder and Greta Gerwig's 2019 version with Saoirse Ronan, and honestly I expected to like the new one better just because it's newer. It's more complicated than that.\nBoth films follow the same story from Louisa May Alcott's novel: four March sisters growing up in Massachusetts during and after the Civil War while their father is away. Both keep the big moments everyone remembers — Jo cutting her hair, Amy burning Jo's manuscript, Beth's illness, Jo turning down Laurie. Both put Jo at the center. And both had a woman director, Gillian Armstrong and Gerwig, which I think shows in how seriously they take the sisters' friendships.\nThe biggest difference is structure. The 1994 film goes in order, from one Christmas to the next, so you watch the girls grow up. Gerwig cuts back and forth between the sisters as adults and as kids, and she uses warm, golden lighting for the past and cold blue for the present. The first time through I got lost a couple times, but it makes Beth's death hit harder because you see the happy version and the sad version almost side by side.\nThe other difference is Amy. In 1994 Amy is played by two actresses (Kirsten Dunst as a kid, Samantha Mathis as an adult) and she mostly comes off as spoiled. Florence Pugh plays her the whole way and gets a speech about marriage being an \"economic proposition\" for women, which makes her way more sympathetic. The 2019 version is also more open about money in general and about Jo as a writer who has to negotiate with her publisher. The ending is even a little meta.\nThe 1994 film is cozier and more sincere. The 2019 one is smarter about the book's ideas but colder in places. Rating them feels wrong because they're trying to do different things with the same four sisters." },
  { kind: "research", name: "8th grade research paper on honeybee decline with a Works Cited", text: "Ava Lindqvist\nMr. Carrow\nScience 8\nMarch 14, 2026\n\nWhere Did the Bees Go?\n\nBees are one of the most important animals on the planet even though they are tiny. About one out of every three bites of food we eat depends on pollinators like honeybees (USDA). But since 2006, beekeepers in the United States have been losing a lot of their hives every year. This paper will explain why honeybees are declining and what scientists think can be done about it.\n\nIn 2006 and 2007 beekeepers started noticing that their worker bees were just disappearing. The queen and the honey were still in the hive but the workers were gone. Scientists called this Colony Collapse Disorder, or CCD (Ellis). Today scientists say CCD is less common, but bees are still dying at high rates. According to the Bee Informed Partnership, U.S. beekeepers lost about 48 percent of their colonies between April 2022 and April 2023 (\"United States Honey Bee Colony Losses\").\n\nThere is not one cause. The biggest one might be the varroa mite, a tiny parasite that attaches to bees and feeds on them. The mites also spread viruses like deformed wing virus (Ellis). Another cause is pesticides, especially a group called neonicotinoids. Studies have found these chemicals can make bees confused so they can't find their way back to the hive (National Geographic Kids). Bees also need lots of different flowers, and when farms grow only one crop over huge areas, bees don't get enough nutrition.\n\nPeople are trying to help. The European Union banned three neonicotinoids outdoors in 2018. Beekeepers treat their hives for mites. Regular people can help too by planting native flowers and not spraying pesticides on their lawns. My family planted a pollinator garden last summer with bee balm and coneflowers and we saw way more bees.\n\nIn conclusion, honeybees are declining because of mites, pesticides, and not enough food. If we lose bees, we lose a lot of the fruits and vegetables we eat. Scientists, farmers, and even kids can all help save them.\n\nWorks Cited\n\nEllis, James D. \"Colony Collapse Disorder.\" University of Florida IFAS Extension, 2021, edis.ifas.ufl.edu.\nNational Geographic Kids. \"Why Are Bees Disappearing?\" National Geographic, 2022.\n\"United States Honey Bee Colony Losses 2022-2023.\" Bee Informed Partnership, 2023, beeinformed.org.\nUnited States Department of Agriculture. \"The Importance of Pollinators.\" USDA, www.usda.gov." },
  { kind: "research", name: "college sociology research paper with abstract, method and results on teen part-time work and sleep", text: "Part-Time Work and Sleep Among High School Seniors: A Survey of Two Suburban Schools\nMarcus Feld and Lena Ortiz\nSOC 301: Research Methods\nProfessor Hwang\nAbstract\nAdolescents need eight to ten hours of sleep per night, yet most American high school students report getting fewer than eight. This study examined whether holding a part-time job is associated with shorter sleep among high school seniors. We surveyed 112 seniors at two suburban high schools about their work hours, school-night bedtimes and wake times. Students working more than 15 hours per week reported sleeping an average of 41 minutes less on school nights than non-working students. The association remained after controlling for extracurricular participation. We discuss limitations of self-reported sleep data and implications for school and labor policy.\nIntroduction\nInsufficient sleep in adolescence is linked to lower grades, depressed mood and car accidents (Owens et al., 2014). Research on its causes has focused on early school start times and screen use. Paid work has received less attention, even though roughly a quarter of high school seniors hold jobs during the school year (Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2023). Carskadon (2011) argues that adolescents' delayed circadian rhythm makes late evening work shifts particularly costly. We asked: Do seniors who work more hours sleep less on school nights?\nMethod\nParticipants. Participants were 112 seniors (61 female, 49 male, 2 nonbinary) recruited through homeroom announcements at two public high schools in the same county. Parental consent was obtained for students under 18.\nMeasures. An anonymous online questionnaire asked students how many hours they had worked in the previous seven days, their usual school-night bedtime and wake time, and the number of hours spent on sports, clubs or other activities. Sleep duration was calculated from bedtime and wake time.\nAnalysis. Students were grouped as non-working (n = 58), working 1–15 hours (n = 33) and working more than 15 hours (n = 21). We compared mean sleep duration across groups using one-way ANOVA and ran a linear regression controlling for extracurricular hours.\nResults\nMean school-night sleep was 7.1 hours (SD = 0.9) for non-working students, 6.9 hours (SD = 1.0) for those working 1–15 hours and 6.4 hours (SD = 1.1) for those working more than 15 hours, F(2, 109) = 4.87, p = .009. Post-hoc tests showed that only the difference between the non-working and the 15+ hour group was significant. In the regression, each additional weekly work hour predicted 2.3 fewer minutes of sleep (p = .01), with extracurricular hours held constant.\nDiscussion\nOur findings suggest that heavy part-time work, not work in general, is associated with shorter sleep. Seniors working more than 15 hours often reported shifts ending at 10 or 11 p.m., consistent with Carskadon's (2011) circadian argument. The study has clear limits: the sample is small and from one county, sleep was self-reported, and a cross-sectional survey cannot show that work causes sleep loss. Students who sleep less might simply be more likely to take evening jobs. Future research should use sleep diaries or wearable trackers. Still, the results support state laws that limit school-night work hours for minors.\nReferences\nBureau of Labor Statistics. (2023). Employment characteristics of families. U.S. Department of Labor.\nCarskadon, M. A. (2011). Sleep in adolescents: The perfect storm. Pediatric Clinics of North America, 58(3), 637–647.\nOwens, J., Adolescent Sleep Working Group, & Committee on Adolescence. (2014). Insufficient sleep in adolescents and young adults: An update on causes and consequences. Pediatrics, 134(3), e921–e932." },
  { kind: "research", name: "rough draft of a college research paper on gentrification in Oakland with citation placeholders", text: "draft 2 — DO NOT SUBMIT yet\n\nDisplacement Without Eviction: Gentrification in Oakland's Fruitvale District\n\nMost people picture gentrification as a dramatic eviction, a family's furniture on the sidewalk. But in Oakland's Fruitvale neighborhood, much of the displacement has happened more quietly, through rising rents, closing businesses and the slow loss of cultural spaces [cite Urban Displacement Project]. This paper argues that ... [thesis still weak, fix after the interviews section]\n\nBackground\nFruitvale has been a center of Oakland's Latino community since the 1960s [source?]. The BART station opened in 1972 and the Fruitvale Village transit-oriented development was finished in 2004, and it was supposed to be a model of development without displacement (Chapple 2009). Median rent in the area rose by around 60% between 2012 and 2019 — CHECK THIS NUMBER, I think it was from the Census ACS tables but I can't find the screenshot.\n\nThe literature\nScholars disagree on how much gentrification actually pushes people out. Freeman and Braconi (2004) found that poor residents in gentrifying New York neighborhoods were actually less likely to move than those in other neighborhoods. Other researchers say this misses \"exclusionary displacement,\" where the people who would have moved in can no longer afford to (Marcuse 1985). Zuk et al. (2018) reviewed dozens of studies and concluded that ... [finish]\n\nInterviews\nI spoke with four longtime residents and two business owners on International Boulevard in February. A panadería owner (I'm calling her R.) said her rent went from $2,800 to $4,100 when the lease renewed. \"Nobody kicked me out,\" she said. \"They just made it so I can't stay.\" [use this quote in the intro instead??]\n\n[section on policy responses — community land trusts, Oakland's rent adjustment program — NEED SOURCES]\n\nReferences (incomplete)\nChapple, K. (2009). Mapping susceptibility to gentrification: The early warning toolkit. Center for Community Innovation, UC Berkeley.\nFreeman, L., & Braconi, F. (2004). Gentrification and displacement: New York City in the 1990s. Journal of the American Planning Association, 70(1), 39–52.\nMarcuse, P. (1985). Gentrification, abandonment, and displacement. Washington University Journal of Urban and Contemporary Law, 28, 195–240.\nZuk, M., et al. (2018). Gentrification, displacement, and the role of public investment. Journal of Planning Literature, 33(1), 31–44." },
  { kind: "research", name: "ESL graduate student's research paper on mobile money and small merchants in Kenya, numbered citations", text: "The Effect of Mobile Money Adoption to the Revenue of Small Merchants in Nairobi\n\n1. Introduction\nSince the launch of M-Pesa by Safaricom in 2007, mobile money has became a central part of daily economic life in Kenya. Today more than 80% of Kenyan adults use some form of mobile money [1]. Many researches have studied the effect of mobile money on households, for example Suri and Jack showed that access to M-Pesa lifted around 2% of Kenyan households out of poverty [2]. However less is known about how small merchants, such as kiosk owners and market vendors, are affected. This paper investigate whether accepting mobile payments is associated with higher monthly revenue for small merchants in Nairobi.\n\n2. Data and Method\nWe used data from a survey of 240 small merchants in three markets in Nairobi (Gikomba, Toi and Kangemi) which was conducted in June–July 2025 by the author with two research assistants. Merchants were asked about their monthly revenue, whether they accept Lipa na M-Pesa till payments, the years of operation and the type of goods sold. We estimated an OLS regression of log monthly revenue on mobile payment acceptance, controlling for business age, sector and market location, following the approach of [3].\n\n3. Results\nOf the 240 merchants, 171 (71.3%) accepted mobile payments. Merchants who accept mobile payments reported 18% higher monthly revenue on average after controls (p < 0.05). The effect was larger for merchants selling clothing and electronics than for those selling fresh produce, which may be because customers buying higher-price goods prefer not to carry cash. Some respondents also mentioned that mobile payment reduced the risk of theft, which is consistent with [4].\n\n4. Discussion and Limitation\nThe results suggest a positive relationship but we cannot say it is causal, because more successful merchants may be more likely to adopt the technology in the first place. Also the revenue is self-reported and some merchants did not want to tell the true amount for tax reasons. In future work we will use transaction records with permission of the merchants.\n\nReferences\n[1] Central Bank of Kenya, KNBS and FSD Kenya, \"FinAccess Household Survey,\" Nairobi, 2021.\n[2] T. Suri and W. Jack, \"The long-run poverty and gender impacts of mobile money,\" Science, vol. 354, no. 6317, pp. 1288–1292, 2016.\n[3] I. Mbiti and D. N. Weil, \"Mobile banking: The impact of M-Pesa in Kenya,\" NBER Working Paper 17129, 2011.\n[4] N. Economides and P. Jeziorski, \"Mobile money in Tanzania,\" Marketing Science, vol. 36, no. 6, pp. 815–837, 2017." },
  { kind: "research", name: "history research paper excerpt with Chicago-style footnotes on the 1863 Richmond Bread Riot", text: "HIST 2210: The Civil War Era\nFinal Research Paper\nThomas Reinholt\n\"Bread or Blood\": Women, Hunger and the Confederate State in the Richmond Bread Riot of 1863\nOn the morning of April 2, 1863, several hundred women gathered at Richmond's Oregon Hill Baptist Church and marched toward Capitol Square. By the time they reached the business district on Main Street, the crowd had grown to perhaps a thousand people, many of them armed with knives and hatchets, and they began breaking into shops to take bread, meat, shoes and cloth.¹ Within two hours the riot was over, ended when Jefferson Davis himself climbed onto a wagon, threw coins from his pockets into the crowd, and ordered the militia to fire if the women did not disperse.² Historians have long treated the riot as evidence of collapsing Confederate morale. This paper argues instead that the rioters understood themselves as loyal citizens making a claim on their government, and that the Confederate response, a mix of repression and quiet relief, shows how much the new state depended on the consent of white working women.\nRichmond's food crisis had many causes. The city's population had roughly tripled since 1861 as government clerks, soldiers' families and refugees crowded in.³ Impressment of farm goods by the army, a poor harvest in 1862 and the Union blockade drove prices to levels few working families could pay; by early 1863 a barrel of flour cost around $30, up from roughly $6 before the war.⁴ Many of the rioters were wives of soldiers or women who worked in the Confederate arsenal and the Tredegar Iron Works, whose wages had not kept pace.\nThe leader most often named in contemporary accounts, Mary Jackson, was a huckster (a market seller) and a soldier's mother. Witnesses at her later trial testified that she had told the women to go to the governor and demand food, and only then to take it if refused.⁵ The language of demand, rather than of revolt, appears again and again in the testimony.\n[...]\nNotes\n1. Michael B. Chesson, \"Harlots or Heroines? A New Look at the Richmond Bread Riot,\" Virginia Magazine of History and Biography 92, no. 2 (1984): 131–75.\n2. Stephanie McCurry, Confederate Reckoning: Power and Politics in the Civil War South (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 2010), 178–80.\n3. Emory M. Thomas, The Confederate State of Richmond: A Biography of the Capital (Austin: University of Texas Press, 1971), 119.\n4. John B. Jones, A Rebel War Clerk's Diary at the Confederate States Capital, vol. 1 (Philadelphia: J. B. Lippincott, 1866), 285.\n5. Richmond Examiner, April 24, 1863." },
  { kind: "debate", name: "Lincoln-Douglas affirmative case on wealthy nations' obligation to provide development aid", text: "LD AFF — Jan/Feb — Development Assistance\n(read time ~5:50, cut the Pogge card if running long)\n\n\"The test of our progress is not whether we add more to the abundance of those who have much; it is whether we provide enough for those who have too little.\" Because I agree with Franklin Delano Roosevelt, I affirm the resolution: Resolved: Wealthy nations have an obligation to provide development assistance to other nations.\n\nDefinitions\nDevelopment assistance is \"government aid designed to promote the economic development and welfare of developing countries\" (OECD). An obligation is a binding moral duty, not merely something that would be nice to do.\n\nValue: Justice, defined as giving each their due. The resolution asks what wealthy nations owe, so justice is the appropriate value.\n\nCriterion: Minimizing preventable harm. Peter Singer argues that \"if it is in our power to prevent something bad from happening, without thereby sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, we ought, morally, to do it.\" Justice requires that we not let people die from causes we could easily prevent, because every person's life carries equal moral weight regardless of the country they were born in.\n\nContention One: Aid saves lives at low cost.\nThe Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria reports that programs it supports have saved 59 million lives since 2002. Distributing insecticide-treated bed nets costs roughly $5,500 per life saved according to GiveWell's estimates. For countries with a GDP per capita above $50,000, these costs do not require sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance. Under my criterion, that alone creates an obligation.\n\nContention Two: Wealthy nations helped create the conditions they now profit from.\nThomas Pogge argues that the global economic order, including trade rules and the international borrowing privilege, was designed by and for wealthy states and \"foreseeably and avoidably\" reproduces severe poverty. When you contribute to a harm, you owe repair. Development assistance is therefore not charity but compensation.\n\nContention Three: Obligation, not discretion, is what makes aid work.\nWhen aid is treated as optional, it rises and falls with domestic politics, and recipients cannot plan. The UK's cut from 0.7% to 0.5% of gross national income in 2021 forced the cancellation of programs mid-stream, including family planning projects that the UN Population Fund estimated would have prevented 250,000 maternal and child deaths. Only a recognized duty creates the stable, long-term commitment that development requires.\n\nUnderview\n1. The neg must defend that wealthy nations have no binding duty at all, not just that some aid is wasted. Bad implementation is an argument for better aid, not no aid.\n2. Prefer consequentialist weighing under my criterion: lives are the clearest impact in the round.\n\nFor these reasons I urge an affirmative ballot." },
  { kind: "debate", name: "public forum pro case on nuclear energy, cards and tags", text: "PF Pro — Nuclear — Sept/Oct\nSpeaker 1: Nadia R. | Speaker 2: Theo B.\nWe affirm. Resolved: In the United States, the benefits of nuclear energy outweigh the harms.\nOur sole framework is climate. The IPCC says emissions must fall roughly 43% by 2030 to keep warming near 1.5 degrees, so the round should be weighed on which side reduces carbon fastest.\nContention 1: Nuclear is the backbone of clean power\nSubpoint A — scale. Nuclear provides about 18% of all U.S. electricity but nearly half of our carbon-free electricity (EIA 2024). No other clean source runs 24/7 at that scale; the average reactor runs at over 90% capacity factor, compared to roughly 35% for wind.\nSubpoint B — closures make things worse. When Indian Point in New York shut down in 2021, its output was replaced mostly by natural gas, and New York's power sector emissions went up (NYISO 2022). Closing plants doesn't mean more renewables. It means more gas.\nImpact: Every gigawatt of nuclear kept online avoids millions of tons of CO2 a year.\nContention 2: Nuclear is safer than the alternatives\nDeaths per terawatt-hour, accounting for accidents and air pollution, are 0.03 for nuclear compared to 24.6 for coal and 2.8 for gas (Ritchie, Our World in Data 2020). That number includes Chernobyl and Fukushima. The American public fears nuclear, but the data say fossil fuel air pollution is killing people quietly every day.\nContention 3: Jobs and energy security\nThe nuclear industry supports about 100,000 direct jobs that pay roughly a third more than average energy jobs (NEI). New small modular reactors could be built at retired coal plant sites, using the existing transmission lines and workforce. The Department of Energy found that hundreds of coal plant sites could host nuclear plants (DOE 2022).\nWeighing: Even if the Con proves waste storage is a problem, that harm is contained and measured in casks on concrete pads; climate change is global and irreversible. Probability and magnitude both go to the Pro.\nWe are proud to affirm." },
  { kind: "debate", name: "Model UN opening speech for Chile on ocean plastic pollution", text: "Delegation: Republic of Chile\nCommittee: United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP)\nTopic: Marine Plastic Pollution\nDelegate: Sofia Marín-Castillo, Westbrook High School\n\nHonorable Chair, distinguished delegates,\n\nChile has more than 6,000 kilometers of coastline, and for our fishing communities, from Arica to Punta Arenas, the ocean is not an abstract issue. It is breakfast, income and identity. Every year an estimated 11 million metric tons of plastic enter the world's oceans, and our beaches receive their share, much of it carried from far beyond our borders.\n\nChile has not waited for the international community to act. In 2018 we became the first country in Latin America to ban single-use plastic bags in commerce nationwide. In 2021 we passed a law limiting single-use plastics in restaurants and food delivery. And our Extended Producer Responsibility law makes companies responsible for the packaging they put on the market, with binding recycling targets.\n\nBut no country can clean the Pacific alone. The delegation of Chile therefore calls on this committee to support three measures. First, a legally binding global treaty that addresses the full life cycle of plastic, from production to disposal, building on the negotiations begun under UNEA Resolution 5/14. Second, a fund, financed in part by plastic producers, to help developing coastal states build waste collection and recycling systems. Third, shared monitoring standards so that every member state measures marine litter the same way and progress can actually be compared.\n\nChile is ready to work with all delegations, particularly our partners in the High Ambition Coalition and our neighbors in the Pacific Alliance, to draft a resolution that is both ambitious and fair. We yield our remaining time to the Chair." },
  { kind: "debate", name: "policy debate 2AC answer blocks for a noncompete-ban affirmative, shorthand", text: "2AC BLOCKS — NONCOMPETE AFF\nupdated 2/9 after Glenbrook rd 4 — ask Coach M about the T block\n\nA2: Business Confidence DA\n1. Non-unique — business confidence already low b/c tariffs + rates, NFIB optimism index below 50-yr avg for 30+ months. DA should've triggered already.\n2. No link — plan only affects noncompetes, firms keep NDAs + trade secret law. Starr '21: trade secret protection ≠ noncompetes.\n3. Turn — noncompetes HURT business formation. Marx '22: states that don't enforce noncompetes (CA) have higher startup rates. Plan = more new firms = more confidence.\n4. No impact — confidence ≠ investment, empirically confidence swings don't predict recession (Barsky & Sims).\n\nA2: States CP\n1. Perm do both — states can act + fed sets floor.\n2. CP links to the net benefit — states banning one by one = race to the bottom, firms relocate to states where enforcement is strongest.\n3. 50-state fiat is abusive, reject the arg not the team. Neg gets to fiat every state at once = no real-world lit.\n4. Solvency deficit — workers cross state lines, remote work means employer in one state enforces vs employee in another. Only fed solves.\n\nA2: T — \"substantially\"\n- We meet: ~30M workers covered (Starr, Prescott, Bishara '21), roughly 1 in 5 US workers, that's substantial under any def\n- C/I: substantially = \"of real worth or importance\" (Merriam-Webster), not a %. Their 10% bright line is arbitrary.\n- Reasonability > competing interps, T isn't a reason to vote unless we're unpredictable. Plan is core of the topic lit.\n\nA2: Wage Suppression Good / K stuff\n[need cards — Prakash said he has the Lavetti ev, get it before Fri]\n\nTheory if they read condo CP: 2 condo is fine, 3+ = abuse, time skew, can't straight turn. Don't go for it unless they kick 2." },
  { kind: "debate", name: "high school parliamentary debate prime minister constructive on abolishing daylight saving time, written in prep", text: "PMC — THW abolish daylight saving time\n(20 min prep, gov)\nThank you Speaker. Twice a year, 330 million Americans are forced to change their clocks, and twice a year we pay for it in car crashes, heart attacks and lost sleep. Today the government side is proud to propose that this house would abolish daylight saving time.\nDefinitions: We define \"abolish daylight saving time\" as ending the twice-a-year clock change and keeping permanent standard time across the US. This is the position of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine. Note: we are NOT for permanent daylight time. Opp can't run \"but kids walk to school in the dark\" against us because standard time is the one with brighter mornings.\nOur burden is to show that the clock change does more harm than good. Two points.\nFirst, health and safety. The week after we \"spring forward,\" studies show a spike in fatal car accidents, about a 6% increase according to a 2020 study in Current Biology. Heart attacks also go up the Monday after the change. This happens because losing an hour of sleep throws off our body clock and people are tired and stressed. Permanent standard time also matches our body clocks to the sun better, which sleep doctors say is healthiest.\nSecond, the reason for DST doesn't exist anymore. It was adopted in WWI and WWII to save energy. But a study of Indiana, which only adopted DST statewide in 2006, found that electricity use actually went UP by about 1% because people ran their air conditioners more in the evening. So we're paying the health cost for an energy benefit that is fake.\nWeighing: even if opp says people like the extra evening light for shopping or baseball, that's a convenience benefit and we are talking about lives on the road. Lives > convenience.\nFor these reasons we are proud to propose. [POIs: take 1 max, don't take during weighing]" },
  { kind: "debate", name: "public forum pro case on legacy admissions written in essay paragraphs; could be mistaken for an argumentative essay", text: "Legacy Admissions — Pro\n\nMy partner and I affirm the resolution: Colleges and universities in the United States should not consider legacy status in admissions.\n\nAt many of the most selective colleges in America, the child of an alumnus has an enormous advantage over every other applicant. A 2023 study by Opportunity Insights, a group of economists at Harvard, found that children from families in the top 1 percent of income were more than twice as likely to attend an Ivy-Plus college as middle-class students with the same SAT or ACT scores. A large share of that gap came from legacy preference. Admissions should be about what a student has done, not what their parents did thirty years ago.\n\nThe first reason legacy preference should end is that it locks in privilege. Because past generations at these colleges were overwhelmingly white and wealthy, rewarding descent from alumni means rewarding the same groups again. At Harvard, according to data released during the SFFA v. Harvard lawsuit, legacy applicants were admitted at around 33 percent between 2014 and 2019, compared with roughly 6 percent for non-legacies. After the Supreme Court ended race-conscious admissions in 2023, keeping legacy preference means colleges may still favor one inherited trait while being barred from correcting for another.\n\nThe second reason is that legacy preference does not deliver the money its defenders promise. Supporters claim that alumni give more when their children may be admitted. But a study by Chad Coffman, Tara O'Neil and Brian Starr in the book Affirmative Action for the Rich found no statistically significant evidence that legacy preferences increase alumni giving. Colleges that have dropped legacy preference, such as Johns Hopkins in 2014 and Amherst in 2021, have continued to raise record amounts, and Johns Hopkins has since increased its share of Pell Grant recipients.\n\nOur opponents may argue that colleges are private institutions that should be free to build their own communities. But nearly every selective college accepts federal financial aid and research money, and with public money comes a public interest in fairness. Others may say legacy students are qualified anyway. If that is true, then they will be admitted without the preference, and nothing is lost.\n\nWhen an admissions office gives a boost to students who already have every advantage, it takes a seat from a student who earned it. For all of these reasons, we strongly urge a Pro ballot." },
  { kind: "email", name: "high school student asking a teacher for a lab report extension", place: "mail.google.com/mail/u/0/", text: "Subject: lab report extension?\n\nHi Mr. Brennan,\n\nI was out sick Thursday and Friday (I emailed the attendance office) so I missed the data collection for the pendulum lab. Could I have until next Wednesday to turn in the report? Marisol said she can share her group's data with me if that's ok with you.\n\nThanks,\nDevin Achterberg\nPeriod 4" },
  { kind: "email", name: "work email giving a project status update to a clinic team, with a bulleted list", place: "outlook.office.com/mail/", text: "Subject: Clinic scheduling rollout — week 3 update\nHi all,\nQuick update on where we are with the new scheduling system before Thursday's check-in.\nDone this week:\n- Front desk staff at Eastside and Riverbend finished training (31 of 34 people; the remaining 3 are scheduled for Monday)\n- Provider templates are built for family medicine and peds\n- Text reminders are live at Eastside. No-shows for the first 5 days were 9%, down from about 14% last month, but that's a small sample so let's not celebrate yet\nStill open:\n- Behavioral health templates. Dana, I know you're waiting on the new visit lengths from Dr. Ostrowski — can you nudge her? We need them by the 22nd to stay on schedule.\n- The interface with the lab system is still double-booking some draws. IT has a ticket open (#44817).\nRiverbend go-live is still set for Nov 3. If anyone thinks that date is at risk, please tell me now rather than the week before.\nThanks, everyone. This has been a lot on top of normal clinic work and I appreciate it.\nJen\nJennifer Halvorsen\nOperations Manager, Northfield Community Health" },
  { kind: "email", name: "college student's chatty email to her grandmother, drafted in a doc", text: "Hi Grandma!!\n\nSorry it took me so long to write back, midterms have been eating me alive. Thank you SO much for the care package. The ginger cookies were gone in about a day because my roommate Hana found them, so now she wants to meet you lol.\n\nSchool is good. I'm really liking my anthropology class even though it's at 8am. We're reading about how different cultures handle grief and it made me think of how you always set a plate for Grandpa at Thanksgiving. I never knew other families did things like that too.\n\nI'm coming home on the 24th. Mom said she's picking me up from the train. Can we make the pierogi together this year? I want to learn for real this time, not just do the pinching part.\n\nTell Uncle Stan I said hi and that the Lions are still bad.\n\nLove you lots,\nEllie\n\nP.S. I wear the scarf you knitted basically every day. People ask where I got it." },
  { kind: "email", name: "parent writing in her second language to a school counselor about her son's schedule", place: "mail.yahoo.com/d/compose/", text: "Subject: About my son schedule - Minh Tran grade 10\n\nDear Ms. Gallagher,\n\nMy name is Lan Nguyen, I am mother of Minh Tran in grade 10. I am writing because Minh tell me he is put in Spanish 1 this semester but he is already finish Spanish 1 last year in his old school in San Jose. I attach his report card from last year. He got A-.\n\nAlso he want to change from regular Chemistry to Honors Chemistry if possible. His teacher last year say he is good in science.\n\nI work in the morning so it is hard for me to come to school, but I can talk on phone after 3 pm. My number is in the school record. If you need more paper please tell me.\n\nThank you very much for your help.\n\nLan Nguyen" },
  { kind: "email", name: "robotics team captain's weekly update email to members, drafted in a doc", text: "Subject: 🤖 RoboKnights Weekly — build season week 2\n\nHey RoboKnights,\n\nBig week! Here's what you need to know:\n\n1. BUILD SCHEDULE. Shop is open Tues/Thurs 3:15–6 and Sat 10–2. If you're on drivetrain you need to be there Saturday, no exceptions, Mr. Brandt is bringing the new gearboxes.\n2. SAFETY GLASSES. Two people got sent out of the shop last week for not wearing them. Please. They're in the blue bin by the door.\n3. FUNDRAISER. The Chipotle night raised $412!! Thank you to everyone who came and dragged their families. Next one is Krispy Kreme sales on the 14th, sign-up sheet is pinned in the Discord.\n4. TRAVEL FORMS for the Lakeview qualifier are due Friday the 20th. No form = no bus. Forms are on the website under \"Documents.\"\n5. Shoutout to the programming team for getting autonomous to score in 3 out of 5 test runs. Last year we were at 0 out of 5 in week 2 so this is huge.\n\nSee you in the shop,\nPriya (Team Captain)\nand the officer team" },
  { kind: "email", name: "undergraduate's email to a professor asking to join her research lab, drafted in a doc; could be mistaken for a cover letter", text: "Subject: Undergraduate research position in the Marsh Ecology Lab\n\nDear Professor Lindgren,\n\nMy name is Tomás Echeverría and I am a sophomore majoring in Environmental Science. I'm writing to ask whether you might have room for an undergraduate volunteer or research assistant in your lab next semester.\n\nI first became interested in your work after reading your 2023 paper on sea-level rise and marsh migration in the Chesapeake, which Dr. Patel assigned in BIOL 240. I was struck by how much the transects depended on careful, repetitive fieldwork, and that is the kind of work I am looking for. This past summer I worked as a field technician for the county parks department, where I collected water samples, identified invasive plants and entered data into ArcGIS. I am comfortable spending long days outside and I have my own waders.\n\nI have taken General Ecology (A) and Statistics for Life Sciences (A-), and I am currently in Intro to GIS. I can commit around 8–10 hours per week and I am available for fieldwork on Fridays.\n\nI have attached my resume and unofficial transcript. Would you be open to meeting briefly during your office hours, or could you let me know if there is a better person in the lab to contact?\n\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nBest regards,\nTomás Echeverría\nB.S. Environmental Science, Class of 2029" },
  { kind: "bio", name: "third-person speaker bio for a water-systems conference program", text: "Dr. Renata Kowalczyk is an associate professor of civil engineering at Front Range State University, where she directs the Resilient Water Systems Lab. Her research focuses on how small rural utilities can prepare for drought and wildfire, and she has worked with more than 40 communities across the Mountain West on low-cost monitoring tools. Before entering academia, she spent eight years as a project engineer for a regional water district. Dr. Kowalczyk holds a Ph.D. from Colorado State University and is a licensed professional engineer. She lives in Greeley with her husband, two teenagers and an unreasonable number of tomato plants." },
  { kind: "bio", name: "middle schooler's about-me for a class website", text: "About Me\nHi! My name is Marcus Delacroix-Webb and I'm in 7th grade at Pine Hollow Middle School. I have two little sisters and a bearded dragon named Gary. My favorite subject is science because we get to do experiments, and my least favorite is probably Spanish (sorry Señora Ruiz). Outside of school I play travel soccer as a goalie and I'm teaching myself to make stop-motion videos with LEGO. When I grow up I want to be either a zookeeper or a video game designer. Fun fact: I have never had a cavity!" },
  { kind: "bio", name: "short author bio for the back flap of a mystery novel", text: "Hollis Varga spent twenty years as a court reporter in Cleveland before writing her first novel. The Quiet Docket is her third Detective Ana Brook mystery. She lives on the shore of Lake Erie with her wife and a deaf terrier named Judge." },
  { kind: "bio", name: "high school student's cast bio for a musical program, over the word limit with a note", text: "THEO MARCHETTI (Prince Topher) is a junior and is thrilled to be making his Lakeside Players debut after three years of painting sets. You may remember his work as the very large beanstalk in last spring's Into the Woods. Theo would like to thank Ms. Pruitt for taking a chance on him, his mom for running lines at 11pm, and his dog, who has heard \"Ten Minutes Ago\" more times than any dog should. Break legs, cast!\n\n[Ms. P said 50 words max — I'm at 70ish, cut the beanstalk part?]" },
  { kind: "bio", name: "first-person about page for a family and wedding photographer's website", text: "About Me\n\nI'm Danielle — mom of two, coffee addict, and the person crouching in the grass to get the shot while your toddler runs away from me.\n\nI've been photographing families and weddings around the Twin Cities since 2016. Before that I taught third grade for nine years, which turns out to be excellent training: I can get twelve cousins to look at the camera at once, and I am not rattled by a meltdown, yours or the ring bearer's.\n\nMy style is natural and warm. I'm not going to pose you into shapes that don't look like you. I'd rather catch the real laugh after the fake one. Most of my sessions happen outside at golden hour, and I'll tell you exactly what to wear (and what not to — no tiny stripes, please).\n\nWhen I'm not shooting, I'm usually at a hockey rink with my sons, trying a new Thai recipe that won't be as good as the restaurant's, or walking our very old golden retriever around Lake Nokomis.\n\nI'd love to hear about your family. Send me a note on the Contact page and let's grab a coffee." },
  { kind: "peerreview", name: "high school peer review worksheet answered for a classmate's essay on school start times", text: "Peer Review — Argumentative Essay Draft\nWriter: Jasmine Holt\nReviewer: Caleb Strand\nEssay topic: Should the school day start later?\n\n1. Underline/copy the thesis. Is it arguable?\n\"Rosemont High should push the start time to 8:45 because teenagers need more sleep to do well in school.\" Yes it's arguable and it's clear. Maybe say what time we start now (7:25) so people see how big the change is.\n\n2. Does each body paragraph have a topic sentence that supports the thesis?\nBody 1 (sleep science) yes. Body 2 (grades) yes. Body 3 kind of goes off topic because it's about the cafeteria being crowded in the morning, I don't really see how it connects to start time.\n\n3. Is the evidence cited? Is it explained?\nYou have the AAP statistic but no citation for it. The Seattle study is really good, but you just drop the quote and move on. Explain what it proves.\n\n4. Counterargument and rebuttal?\nYou mention buses but you don't really answer it. You say \"the district will figure it out.\" That's not a rebuttal lol. Maybe look up what other districts did.\n\n5. One thing the writer did really well:\nYour intro hook about falling asleep in first period chemistry was funny and relatable. I would keep it.\n\n6. One thing to fix first:\nBody 3. Either cut it or change it to after-school activities, since that's what people are going to argue about.\n\nOther: you switch between \"teens\" and \"students\" and \"kids\" a lot. Also paragraph 2 is really long." },
  { kind: "peerreview", name: "college fiction workshop letter to a classmate about her short story draft", text: "Dear Priya,\nThank you for sharing \"Salt Season.\" I read it twice, once fast and once with a pen, and I want to start by saying that the opening image of the grandmother sorting sea salt by color on the kitchen table is one of the best first paragraphs we've had in workshop this semester. I could taste it.\nWhat's working: The voice of the narrator, Anjali, is consistent and wry without being too cute. I loved the line \"my mother apologized to furniture.\" The structure of moving back and forth between the monsoon of 1998 and the present-day hospital visit gives the story a nice tension, and the present-tense hospital scenes feel appropriately claustrophobic.\nWhat I'd push on:\nThe middle section (roughly pages 6–9) is where I lost the thread. We get three flashbacks in a row, the wedding, the flood and the school trip, and I wasn't sure which one mattered most. If the flood is the heart of the story, and I think it is, maybe the wedding scene could be cut or folded into a single paragraph.\nI wanted more from the brother. Vikram shows up on page 4 and then mostly disappears until the last page, where his phone call is supposed to land emotionally. Right now it didn't land for me because I didn't know what was at stake between them.\nThe ending. I know we talked in class about not tying everything up, but the last line (\"and the salt was still there, white as anything\") felt like it's reaching for a symbol the story hasn't fully earned. Could the grandmother's hands do the work instead?\nSmall things: \"laid\" vs \"lay\" on page 3; Anjali's age seems to change between 14 and 15; I'd cut \"suddenly\" everywhere (there are five).\nThis is a story I want to keep reading. I'm excited to see the next draft.\nBest,\nOwen" },
  { kind: "peerreview", name: "nonprofit colleague's feedback on a grant application draft, comments keyed to sections", text: "Feedback on \"Read Together\" grant draft (v3) — from Mateo\n\nOverall: This is much stronger than v2. The need statement is clear and the budget finally matches the narrative. My main concern is that the evaluation section doesn't match what the Hartwell Foundation says it wants (they asked for outcomes, we mostly give outputs). Details below, in order.\n\nExecutive summary\n- 2nd para: \"serve hundreds of families\" — give the actual number. We projected 240 in the budget.\n- Consider leading with the kindergarten readiness stat, it's the most striking thing we have.\n\nStatement of need\n- Good. The county data from 2024 is the right source. Double-check the 38% figure, I think it's 36% in the final report and 38% was the draft.\n- The paragraph about screen time feels like a tangent. Cut or shorten?\n\nProgram design\n- \"Families will attend sessions regularly\" — define regularly. 8 of 10 sessions? That's what we used last year.\n- The partnership with the library branches is a selling point, but you mention it only once. Move it up.\n\nEvaluation\n- This is the weak spot. Number of books distributed and number of sessions held are outputs. Hartwell's RFP specifically asks how we'll know children's literacy improved. Can we propose the pre/post PALS screener with the school district? Ruth said they might share data.\n- Who is doing the evaluation? Staff time for this isn't in the budget.\n\nBudget\n- Line 14 (books) jumped from $9,000 to $12,500 between versions. Intentional?\n\nHappy to talk through any of this on Thursday. Nice work getting it this far." },
  { kind: "peerreview", name: "middle school glow-and-grow peer feedback on a narrative, very short", text: "Peer feedback for: Zoe M.\nFrom: Aiden\nGLOW ⭐ I like how you described the haunted house with the creaky stairs and the smell like old pennies. It made me feel scared. Your ending with your brother screaming was funny.\nGROW 🌱 Some of your sentences are really long and have a lot of \"and then.\" Also I didn't know who Jess was at first, maybe say she is your cousin. You need a title!!" },
  { kind: "peerreview", name: "ESL college student's peer review of a classmate's essay on fast fashion", text: "Peer Review for Daniel's essay \"The True Cost of a $5 T-shirt\"\nReviewer: Yuki Tanabe — ENG 102, Section 7\n\nSummary of the essay (in my words): Daniel argue that fast fashion companies like Shein and H&M are harmful because they pollute water, create too much textile waste, and pay very low wages to garment workers. He say consumers should buy less and buy second hand.\n\nStrength:\nYour topic is very interesting and important. I did not know that one pair of jeans need about 7,500 liters of water. The example of Rana Plaza collapse in Bangladesh is very powerful and it make the reader feel emotion. Also your organization is easy to follow because each paragraph has one main idea.\n\nSuggestions:\n1) Your thesis is in the end of introduction but it is little bit general (\"fast fashion is bad for the world\"). Maybe you can make more specific, like which harm is the most serious or what solution you want.\n2) In paragraph 3 you use many numbers but I am not sure where they come from. Professor Lee said every statistic need a citation.\n3) Your counterargument paragraph is missing. Some people say fast fashion helps poor people to buy clothes cheap. I think you need to answer this.\n4) The conclusion repeats the introduction almost same words. Maybe add something new, like what you personally changed.\n\nQuestion for you: Did you watch the documentary \"The True Cost\"? Your title is similar, so maybe you should mention it.\n\nOverall I think this is good draft and with some changes it will be strong. Good luck!" },
  { kind: "story", name: "middle school fantasy story about a door in the woods, simple writing", text: "The Door in the Woods\nBy Brayden K.\n\nThere was a kid named Tyler and he lived next to a big forest. His mom always said never go past the creek but one day his ball went over the creek so he went to get it.\n\nWhen he got there he saw something weird. There was a door. Just a door standing up in the middle of the trees with no house or anything. It was red and it had a gold doorknob. Tyler walked around it and the back was just the back of a door.\n\n\"Hello?\" he said. Nobody answered.\n\nHe turned the knob and it opened. On the other side it was snowing even though it was summer. There were huge mountains and a castle made out of ice. Tyler was scared but also excited so he stepped through.\n\nRight away a fox came up to him and it could talk. \"You're finally here,\" the fox said. \"The Ice Queen has been waiting for you for a hundred years.\"\n\n\"Me?\" Tyler said. \"I'm just 11.\"\n\nThe fox said that a prophecy said a boy with a red ball would come and save the kingdom. Tyler looked down and he was still holding his red ball. That's crazy he thought.\n\nThey walked for a long time to the castle. Tyler was freezing because he was wearing shorts. When they got there the Ice Queen was sitting on a throne and she looked sad. She said her kingdom was frozen forever because of a curse and only someone from the other world could break it by bringing summer.\n\nTyler thought about it. Then he had an idea. He threw the red ball really high and it turned into a sun. All the snow started melting and flowers grew everywhere. The Queen was so happy she cried and her tears turned into butterflies.\n\n\"Thank you Tyler,\" she said. \"You can come back anytime.\"\n\nTyler went back through the door and he was in the woods again and it was getting dark. When he got home his mom said \"Where were you?\" and he said \"Nowhere.\" But he had a snowflake in his hair that didn't melt.\n\nThe End" },
  { kind: "story", name: "polished literary short story about a teenager working the night shift at a laundromat", text: "Spin Cycle\nAt 2 a.m. the Suds-N-Go on Delancey belongs to Ines and the machines.\nShe sits behind the counter on a stool with a split vinyl seat, a textbook open to a chapter on cellular respiration she has read four times, and listens. Washer number six thumps when it's unbalanced. The third dryer from the end squeals on its last minute, like it's sorry to stop. She knows the building by sound the way her abuela knew weather by her knees.\nThe regulars come in the hours no one else wants. There's the nurse in purple scrubs who folds everything into perfect squares and never says a word. There's the old man who brings one shirt, always the same blue shirt, and sits in front of the window to watch it turn. Tonight there is someone new: a girl about Ines's age, maybe seventeen, in a hoodie with the sleeves pulled over her hands, carrying a trash bag of clothes and a little boy asleep on her shoulder.\nThe girl stuffs everything into one machine, too much, and stands there reading the instructions on the wall like they are written in another language.\n\"It's quarters,\" Ines says. \"Or the card. The card machine's broken, though.\"\nThe girl digs in her pocket and comes up with a dollar and some lint. Ines watches her count it twice.\nThere is a roll of quarters in the drawer under the register. It is not Ines's roll of quarters. Mr. Feld counts it every morning with his thick thumbs and writes the number in a notebook, and when it was short by two dollars in March he docked her a whole shift.\nThe boy wakes up and starts to cry, softly, the way kids cry when they're too tired to really commit.\nInes opens the drawer. She takes out twelve quarters, then four more, and slides them across the counter without looking up, as if they are nothing, as if this is something the machines do on their own.\n\"Detergent's free tonight,\" she says, which isn't true either, and she reaches under the counter for the jug.\nThe girl doesn't say thank you. She just nods, a small sharp nod, and Ines understands it is the kind of nod you give when thank you would cost too much. The machine fills. The boy quiets, watching the water rise behind the round glass.\nFor the next hour they sit at opposite ends of the room. The girl sleeps sitting up, her arm around the boy. Ines reads the same paragraph about the Krebs cycle again. Energy is released. Energy is stored. Nothing is ever created or lost, just moved from one place to another.\nAt 3:40 the dryer squeals its little apology and the girl wakes, folds everything fast and badly, and leaves without looking back. The bell over the door rings once.\nIn the morning Mr. Feld will count the quarters and come up four dollars short, and he will look at Ines with his small tired eyes and she will say she doesn't know, maybe he miscounted yesterday. He will write it in his notebook. She will lose the shift.\nShe decides, sitting there in the hum, that it was worth it. Then she decides it again, because she is going to have to keep deciding it for a while.\nWasher six thumps. The old man's blue shirt goes around and around." },
  { kind: "story", name: "Avatar: The Last Airbender fan fiction with an author's note", text: "The Jasmine Dragon, Closing Time\n(Avatar: The Last Airbender — post-canon, Zuko & Iroh, no ships, just fluff and feelings)\n\nA/N: this is for the Uncle Iroh Appreciation Week prompt \"leftovers.\" I wrote it at 1am so pls be nice. I don't own ATLA obviously.\n\nThe last customer left the Jasmine Dragon at sunset, and Zuko flipped the sign on the door from OPEN to CLOSED with more force than strictly necessary.\n\n\"You know,\" said Uncle Iroh from behind the counter, \"the sign does not need to be defeated. It is on our side.\"\n\n\"It's been a long day.\" Zuko untied his apron. Fire Lord or not, he had promised to spend one week a year at the tea shop in Ba Sing Se, and he had not anticipated how many people would want to watch the Fire Lord pour tea. One woman had asked him to sign her teacup. Another had asked him to warm hers up. With firebending. He had not done it.\n\nIroh was arranging something on a tray: the broken pieces of the almond cakes that hadn't sold, a pot that had steeped too long, two chipped cups.\n\n\"These are the leftovers,\" he said. \"The tea is bitter and the cakes are crumbs. Sit.\"\n\nZuko sat. The tea was terrible. He drank it anyway.\n\n\"When you were young,\" Iroh said, \"you only wanted the best of everything. The best teacher, the best sword, the best seat at the table beside your father.\"\n\n\"I remember,\" Zuko said, a little stiffly.\n\n\"And now you sit in a little shop in the Earth Kingdom eating broken cakes with an old man.\" Iroh smiled into his cup. \"I think this is a great improvement.\"\n\nZuko looked down at the crumbs on the tray. Outside, the lanterns on the street were coming on one by one, orange and soft. He thought about the palace, the long cold halls, the throne he still felt too small for.\n\n\"It's not bad,\" he admitted.\n\n\"Not bad!\" Iroh laughed so hard his belly shook. \"From you, nephew, that is a poem.\"\n\nZuko threw an almond crumb at him. Iroh caught it and ate it, and for a while neither of them said anything at all, which was its own kind of good.\n\n~fin~\n\nkudos and comments make my day!! 💛" },
  { kind: "story", name: "100-word flash fiction written to a one-word prompt", text: "prompt 7: \"inheritance\" (exactly 100 words)\nMy grandfather left me a jar of buttons and nothing else.\nMy cousins got the house, the truck, the good knives. I got buttons: brass ones, bone ones, one shaped like a tiny pear. At the funeral my aunt patted my hand like I was a dog who'd been forgotten in the will.\nThat night I poured them on the kitchen table and sorted them by color, then size, then by which coat I imagined they came from.\nAt the bottom of the jar, folded small: a note.\n\"You were the only one who ever asked me about these.\"" },
  { kind: "story", name: "unfinished science fiction novel draft with bracketed notes to self", text: "CH 1 — DRAFT (nano day 3, don't edit just write)\n\nThe colony ship Perennial had been falling toward Kepler-442b for 212 years, and Second Agronomist Hesper Vale had been awake for six days of it.\n\nWaking up was supposed to be gentle. The manual said so. [check: does the ship have a name for the manual? something like the Charter?] Instead she came up out of the cold gasping, her lungs full of something that tasted like pennies, while an alarm she didn't recognize pulsed red across the ceiling of the cryo bay.\n\nNobody else was awake. That was the first wrong thing.\n\nThe second wrong thing was the seed vault.\n\nShe found it on day two, after she'd learned to walk again holding onto the rails. The vault was supposed to hold four hundred thousand seeds in nitrogen: wheat, sorghum, rice, the gene-banked orchard trees that the Charter called \"the inheritance of Earth.\" [too on the nose? maybe cut] The display said TEMP NOMINAL. The display was lying. When she pressed her palm to the vault door it was warm.\n\n[flashback here to her mother's greenhouse in Tucson?? or save for ch 3]\n\n\"Ship,\" she said. \"Who else is awake?\"\n\nThe ship didn't answer the way ships did in the vids she'd watched as a kid. It answered with a list.\n\nAWAKE: 1.\nCRYO NOMINAL: 1,941.\nCRYO FAILED: 0.\nVAULT: NOMINAL.\n\n\"That's not true,\" she said, out loud, to no one. \"The vault's not nominal. I touched it.\"\n\nThere was a pause long enough that she felt it in her teeth.\n\nVAULT: NOMINAL, the ship said again.\n\n[need a reason the AI is lying — corrupted? or someone told it to? decide before ch 2. Leaning towards: someone was awake before her and changed the logs]\n\nOn day six Hesper went looking for the logs and found instead a pair of boots outside the observation deck, set neatly side by side, the way you'd leave them at the door of\n\n[stopped here — pick up w/ boots, whose are they]" },
  { kind: "story", name: "first-person short story told like a memoir about a summer job at a mini golf course; could be mistaken for a personal essay", text: "Rafael Cortés\nCreative Writing 11 — Mr. Osei\nHole Eighteen\nThe summer I turned sixteen, 1994, I worked at Pirate's Cove Mini Golf on Route 9, and I learned more there than I did in all of tenth grade.\nMy job was the windmill hole. Not the counter, not the snack bar, the windmill. Every morning I climbed inside it with a can of WD-40 and oiled the gears so the blades wouldn't stick, and every afternoon I stood next to it in a polyester eye patch and told little kids \"Arrr, time it right, matey.\" My mom thought it was hilarious. My older brother Dougie said it was the most embarrassing thing that had ever happened to our family, and he'd once been arrested for stealing a goat.\nThe person who ran Pirate's Cove was a woman named Mrs. Albright, who was maybe seventy and wore a captain's hat without irony. She had bought the course with her husband in 1971. He'd died two winters before I started, and she still kept his scorecard from opening day pinned behind the register. She called me Wendy-bird, which nobody else has ever called me, before or since.\nI thought she was strange at first. She talked to the fiberglass parrot on hole twelve. She closed the whole course for ten minutes every evening at 7:15 to watch the sun go down over the parking lot, which was not a good view. But by August I was staying after my shift to watch it with her.\nOn the last day of the season she let me play the whole course alone, for free, at dusk. I got a hole-in-one on eighteen, the one where the ball goes into the skull's mouth and you never get it back. I yelled so loud she came out of the office.\n\"Henry never got that one,\" she said. \"Twenty-three years.\"\nThat was more than thirty years ago. Pirate's Cove is a Walgreens now. But whenever I'm somewhere at 7:15 and the light starts to go orange, I stop whatever I'm doing and I watch it, even if the view isn't any good." },
  { kind: "cause", name: "high school US history essay on what caused the Dust Bowl, MLA heading, average writer", text: "Tyler Brennan\nMrs. Okafor\nUS History, Period 4\n14 March 2026\nThe Dirty Thirties\nIn the 1930s a huge part of the Great Plains turned into what people called the Dust Bowl. Storms of dirt rolled across Kansas, Oklahoma, Texas and Colorado so thick that chickens went to roost in the middle of the day because they thought it was night. It is easy to blame the weather, and the drought was real, but the Dust Bowl did not just happen to the farmers. A lot of it was made by people.\nThe first thing that set it up was the government trying to get people to move west. The Homestead Act and then the Enlarged Homestead Act in 1909 gave settlers land for cheap, and thousands of families showed up who had never farmed dry land before. They farmed it the way they would farm in Ohio or Illinois, which did not work out here.\nThen came World War 1. Europe needed wheat and the price shot up, so farmers plowed up even more of the prairie. New tractors made it possible to plow way more acres than a horse team could. The thing is, the prairie grass had deep roots that held the soil down even when it didn't rain for months. Once that grass was gone there was nothing keeping the topsoil in place.\nAfter the war wheat prices dropped, and farmers responded by planting even more to make up the money. When the drought started in 1931 the crops died and the bare fields just sat there. The wind did the rest. The worst storm was Black Sunday, April 14 1935, when a cloud of dust hundreds of miles wide came through.\nThe effects were terrible. People got \"dust pneumonia\" and some of them died, especially kids and old people. Farms failed and banks took them. About 2.5 million people left the Plains states, and a lot of them went to California, where they were called \"Okies\" and treated badly.\nIn conclusion the Dust Bowl was caused by drought but also by bad farming, government policy and wanting to make money off wheat. The good part is it led to the Soil Conservation Service and farmers learned about things like crop rotation and planting trees for windbreaks." },
  { kind: "litreview", name: "graduate education literature review on peer tutoring and middle school math achievement, polished", text: "Peer Tutoring and Mathematics Achievement in the Middle Grades: A Review of the Literature\nEDUC 612 – Instructional Interventions\nDana Whitfield\n\nIntroduction\nPeer tutoring, in which students take on structured instructional roles with one another, has been studied as a mathematics intervention for more than four decades. Its appeal for middle schools is practical: it requires few materials, it can be layered onto existing curricula, and it promises gains for tutors as well as tutees. This review synthesizes empirical work published between 2005 and 2024 on peer tutoring in grades 6–8 mathematics, with attention to three questions: (1) What effect does peer tutoring have on achievement? (2) Which program features moderate that effect? (3) What do we know about effects on students' attitudes toward mathematics?\n\nEffects on achievement\nAcross studies, the direction of the effect is consistent even where its size is not. Meta-analytic work reports moderate positive effects on mathematics outcomes, with pooled estimates in the range of g = 0.30 to 0.45 (Alvarez & Kim, 2016; Ostrander, 2019). Individual trials vary more widely. Brandt and Nwosu (2012) found that a twelve-week reciprocal tutoring program in two urban middle schools raised scores on a district fractions assessment by roughly a third of a standard deviation, while Hollis et al. (2018), working with a larger and more diverse sample, found effects near zero on a standardized state test despite gains on curriculum-aligned measures. This gap between proximal and distal measures recurs throughout the literature and suggests that some of the reported benefit reflects alignment between tutoring content and the assessment used.\n\nModerators\nStructure appears to matter more than duration. Programs that supply tutors with scripted prompts, error-correction routines, and answer keys tend to outperform loosely structured \"study buddy\" arrangements (Ostrander, 2019; Pham, 2021). Reciprocal designs, in which students alternate roles, produce effects comparable to fixed cross-age designs, and several authors argue they are easier to sustain because no student is permanently cast as the one who needs help (Brandt & Nwosu, 2012; Leclerc, 2020). Evidence on grouping by prior achievement is mixed. Pham (2021) reports larger gains for lower-achieving tutees paired with moderately higher-achieving tutors than with the highest achievers, a finding consistent with the idea that a smaller gap allows tutors to explain in terms the tutee can follow, though the study was not designed to test this mechanism directly.\n\nAttitudes and engagement\nFewer studies measure affective outcomes, and those that do rely heavily on self-report. Still, gains in mathematics self-concept appear in most of them (Leclerc, 2020; Sandoval & Reyes, 2023), and two qualitative studies describe tutors reporting a new sense of competence after explaining procedures aloud (Greer, 2014; Sandoval & Reyes, 2023). Whether these attitudinal gains persist beyond the program is largely unknown; only one study in this review followed students for more than a semester after the intervention ended.\n\nGaps and directions\nThree gaps stand out. First, most trials are short and few follow students into the next grade. Second, implementation fidelity is rarely measured, which makes null results hard to interpret. Third, the literature says little about multilingual learners, even though peer explanation in a shared home language is a plausible advantage. Future work that pairs standardized outcomes with fidelity data and longer follow-up would help schools decide whether the modest, reliable gains reported so far justify the class time peer tutoring requires." },
  { kind: "presentation", name: "high school group presentation script on coral bleaching, slide by slide with who speaks", text: "Coral Bleaching Presentation – Script\nGroup: Ava, Jordan, Priya, Luis\n(each person says their own slides, keep it under 8 min total!!)\n\nSlide 1 – Title (Ava)\nHi everyone, our presentation is on coral bleaching and what it is doing to reefs around the world. I'm Ava and this is Jordan, Priya and Luis.\n\nSlide 2 – What is coral? (Ava)\nSo a lot of people think coral is a rock or a plant but it's actually an animal. Each coral is made of tiny animals called polyps, and they have algae called zooxanthellae living inside them. You can see in the picture how small the polyps are. The algae give the coral food through photosynthesis and they also give it all the colors.\n\nSlide 3 – What is bleaching (Jordan)\nWhen the water gets too hot, the coral gets stressed and kicks the algae out. Without the algae the coral turns white, which is why it's called bleaching. The left picture is a healthy reef and the right one is the same reef after bleaching. It's not dead right away, but if the water stays hot for too long it starves.\n\nSlide 4 – Causes (Jordan)\nThe main cause is ocean warming from climate change. Even 1 degree Celsius above normal for a few weeks can cause bleaching. Other things make it worse like pollution and runoff from farms, and ocean acidification which makes it harder for coral to build its skeleton.\n\nSlide 5 – Great Barrier Reef (Priya)\nThis map shows the Great Barrier Reef in Australia. It has had mass bleaching events in 2016, 2017, 2020, 2022 and 2024. The red parts on the map are where the bleaching was the worst. [Priya - say the % thing from the article]\n\nSlide 6 – Why it matters (Priya)\nReefs only cover a tiny part of the ocean floor but about 25 percent of all marine species depend on them. They also protect coastlines from storms and a lot of people depend on them for fishing and tourism.\n\nSlide 7 – What people are doing (Luis)\nScientists are trying things like growing coral in nurseries and replanting it, and breeding coral that can handle heat better. There's a video clip here, it's about 30 seconds.\n(play video)\n\nSlide 8 – What you can do (Luis)\nUse reef safe sunscreen, don't touch coral if you go snorkeling, and try to cut down on energy use because it all connects to warming.\n\nSlide 9 – Questions (Luis)\nThanks for listening! Does anyone have any questions?" },
  { kind: "dbq", name: "APUSH DBQ on whether Reconstruction secured freedpeople's rights, strong writer citing Doc 1-7 with sourcing", text: "Mara Lindqvist\nAPUSH – Mr. Delgado\nDBQ #3 – Reconstruction\nFeb 2026\n\nPrompt: Evaluate the extent to which Reconstruction succeeded in securing the rights of formerly enslaved people in the period 1865–1877.\n\nWhen the Civil War ended in 1865, nearly four million people who had been enslaved were suddenly free, but what that freedom would mean was still undecided. Lincoln's lenient Ten Percent Plan, Andrew Johnson's quick pardons of Confederate leaders, and the Radical Republicans' demands for a harsher peace all pulled Reconstruction in different directions. Although Reconstruction did secure important legal and political rights for freedpeople in the short term, it ultimately failed to protect those rights, because white Southerners used violence and economic control to undo them once the federal government lost the will to enforce them.\n\nIn its early years, Reconstruction achieved real gains. A Freedmen's Bureau agent in Georgia reported in 1866 that schools for freedpeople were so crowded that adults sat beside children, all \"eager to learn to read the Bible for themselves\" (Doc 1). The agent's purpose was to convince Congress to keep funding the Bureau, so he may have emphasized success, but the enthusiasm he describes matches the thousands of schools founded in this period. Politically, the Fourteenth and Fifteenth Amendments guaranteed citizenship, equal protection, and voting rights for Black men. The 1872 lithograph of the first Black members of Congress, including Senator Hiram Revels of Mississippi (Doc 4), shows how quickly freedmen gained a political voice. Its publishers, Currier and Ives, intended it to celebrate this achievement for Northern buyers, which shows that at least some Northerners saw these changes as progress.\n\nHowever, these gains were met with immediate resistance. Even before the Radical Republicans took control, Southern states passed Black Codes such as Mississippi's 1865 law requiring freedmen to sign yearly labor contracts or be arrested for vagrancy (Doc 2). These laws tried to recreate slavery in all but name. When the Fourteenth Amendment made these codes illegal, many white Southerners turned to violence. In testimony to Congress in 1871, a freedman from South Carolina described Klansmen dragging him from his home and whipping him for voting Republican (Doc 5). Thomas Nast's 1874 cartoon (Doc 3), showing a Klansman and a White Leaguer shaking hands over a terrified Black family, captures how organized and widespread this terror had become, and Nast, a Republican, drew it to warn Northern readers that the war's results were being reversed.\n\nEconomic dependence made these political rights even harder to use. A sharecropping contract from 1870s Alabama (Doc 6) required the tenant to give half of the crop to the landowner and buy supplies from the landowner's store, which trapped families in debt. Without land of their own — the promise of \"forty acres and a mule\" had been revoked by Johnson — most freedpeople could not afford to defy the planters who controlled their livelihoods.\n\nFinally, the North simply gave up. A Southern newspaper celebrating the Compromise of 1877 (Doc 7) declared that the South had been \"redeemed\" now that federal troops were leaving. In exchange for Rutherford B. Hayes becoming president, Republicans withdrew the last troops, and \"Redeemer\" governments soon disenfranchised Black voters through poll taxes and literacy tests.\n\nIn the end, Reconstruction succeeded in writing freedom into the Constitution but failed to make it real in the daily lives of most freedpeople. Still, the amendments it produced did not disappear. Nearly a century later, civil rights lawyers relied on the Fourteenth Amendment in Brown v. Board of Education, showing that Reconstruction's unfinished promises could still be claimed." },
  { kind: "social", name: "LinkedIn post from a civil engineering student wrapping up a summer internship", place: "www.linkedin.com/feed/", text: "After 10 weeks, my summer internship with the Water Resources team at Kessling & Ward Engineering officially wrapped up on Friday.\n\nA few things I'm taking with me:\n→ Ask the \"dumb\" question early. It's way less dumb in week 2 than in week 9.\n→ Field days beat spreadsheet days, but the spreadsheet days are what make the field days count.\n→ A good mentor doesn't hand you answers, they hand you better questions. Thank you Renata Oyelaran for doing exactly that all summer.\n\nHuge thanks to the whole stormwater group for trusting an intern with a real culvert assessment project and for letting me tag along on the Millbrook site visits.\n\nHeading back to Iowa State for junior year in Civil Engineering and very excited to see where this goes next.\n\n#internship #civilengineering #stormwater #gratitude" },
  { kind: "instructions", name: "family recipe for lentil soup with ingredients, numbered steps and notes", text: "Nana Ruth's Lentil Soup\n(makes a big pot, about 8 bowls. freezes great)\n\nIngredients\n- 2 tbsp olive oil\n- 1 large onion, chopped\n- 2 carrots, diced\n- 2 celery stalks, diced\n- 4 cloves garlic, minced\n- 1 tsp cumin\n- 1/2 tsp smoked paprika\n- 1 1/2 cups brown or green lentils, rinsed (NOT red, they turn to mush)\n- 1 can (14 oz) diced tomatoes\n- 8 cups chicken or veggie broth\n- 2 bay leaves\n- 2 big handfuls of spinach\n- juice of half a lemon\n- salt and pepper\n\nDirections\n1. Heat the oil in a big pot over medium. Add the onion, carrots and celery and cook about 8 minutes until the onion is soft and see-through. Don't rush this part, Nana always said this is where the flavor comes from.\n2. Add the garlic, cumin and paprika and stir for 1 minute, just until it smells good.\n3. Dump in the lentils, the tomatoes with their juice, the broth and the bay leaves. Bring to a boil.\n4. Turn it down to low, put the lid on but leave it cracked, and simmer 35–40 min. Stir every so often. Lentils should be soft but still hold their shape.\n5. Take out the bay leaves. If you want it thicker, scoop out 2 cups, blend them and stir them back in.\n6. Stir in the spinach until it wilts, then the lemon juice. Taste and add salt and pepper. It usually needs more salt than you think.\n\nNotes\n* Nana put a parmesan rind in at step 3. Fish it out at the end with the bay leaves.\n* If it gets too thick the next day, add a splash of water when you reheat." },
  { kind: "script", name: "short one-act play for a drama class, two characters at a night bus stop, with stage directions", text: "THE LAST BUS\na short play by Imani Clarke\nDrama II – Scene Writing Assignment\n\nCHARACTERS:\nDESMOND, 17, wearing a fast food uniform\nLORNA, 70s, carries a plastic bag of oranges\n\nSETTING: A bus stop at night. One bench. A streetlight that flickers.\n\n(Lights up. DESMOND sits on the bench scrolling his phone. LORNA enters slowly, looks at the schedule posted on the pole, squints.)\n\nLORNA: Is this the 14?\nDESMOND: (not looking up) Yeah.\nLORNA: Has it come yet?\nDESMOND: If it came I wouldn't be sitting here.\n(Beat. LORNA sits on the far end of the bench. She sets the bag of oranges between them.)\nLORNA: My grandson says that too. \"If it came I wouldn't be here.\" Everybody's a philosopher.\nDESMOND: I'm not trying to be a philosopher, I'm trying to get home.\nLORNA: Where's home?\nDESMOND: (finally looks at her) Ma'am, no offense, but I've been on my feet for nine hours.\nLORNA: None taken. (She starts peeling an orange.) I used to work nights. Hospital laundry. Thirty-one years.\nDESMOND: Thirty-one years?\nLORNA: You do something long enough it stops being something you do and starts being who you are. That's the danger.\n(DESMOND puts the phone down.)\nDESMOND: I'm only doing this till I save up for a car.\nLORNA: That's what I said. Only I said it about a washing machine.\n(She holds out half the orange. He hesitates, then takes it.)\nDESMOND: Thanks.\nLORNA: What do you actually want to do?\nDESMOND: (shrugs) I don't know. Something with music maybe. My teacher says I have an ear.\nLORNA: Then the car's not what you're saving for.\n(The streetlight flickers. Headlights sweep across the stage. Sound of a bus braking.)\nDESMOND: That's us.\nLORNA: That's you. Mine's the 9.\nDESMOND: You said this was the 14—\nLORNA: I asked if it was the 14. I never said I was waiting for it.\n(She smiles. DESMOND stares at her, then gets up and backs toward the bus, still holding the orange.)\nDESMOND: Night.\nLORNA: Go on. Find out what you're saving for.\n(DESMOND exits. LORNA sits alone and peels another orange. Lights fade.)\nEND OF SCENE" },
  { kind: "cause", name: "short essay by an English learner on how phones at night affect teenagers' sleep, no heading", text: "How Phone Effect Our Sleep\nNowdays almost every teenager have a smartphone and many of them use it in the bed before sleep. I am also like this. But using phone at night have many bad effect on our sleep and our health.\nFirst reason is the blue light. The screen of phone make a blue light and this light tell our brain that it is still day time. So the brain do not make melatonin, which is the hormone for feeling sleepy. Because of this we cannot fall asleep fast even we are tired.\nSecond, the apps like TikTok and Instagram are made for keep us watching. One more video, one more video, and suddenly it is 1am. My cousin say he lose two hours every night like this.\nWhen we don't sleep enough, many problem come. In the morning we are very tired and we cannot focus in class. Some student fall asleep in first period. Also, not enough sleep can make people more angry or sad, and some study say it is connected to depression for teenager.\nSo the phone is very useful but it also steal our sleep. Maybe we should put it in other room at night." },
  { kind: "litreview", name: "undergraduate nursing literature review on music for preoperative anxiety, with search strategy and themes", text: "NURS 340: Evidence-Based Practice\nLiterature Review Assignment\nKayla Morrison\n\nMusic Interventions for Preoperative Anxiety in Adult Patients\n\nBackground\nAnxiety before surgery is common, with estimates ranging from 60–80% of adult surgical patients reporting moderate to high anxiety on the day of the procedure. High preoperative anxiety has been linked to greater anesthetic requirements, more postoperative pain, and longer recovery. Benzodiazepines such as midazolam are frequently used but carry risks of sedation and respiratory depression, especially in older adults. Because of this, nurses have looked at non-pharmacological options, and music is one of the most frequently studied.\n\nSearch Strategy\nI searched CINAHL and PubMed using the terms \"music,\" \"music therapy,\" \"preoperative,\" \"anxiety,\" and \"surgery,\" limited to English language studies from 2012–2025. After removing duplicates and pediatric studies, 9 studies were included: 6 randomized controlled trials, 2 quasi-experimental studies, and 1 systematic review.\n\nThemes in the Literature\nMusic reduces self-reported anxiety. Almost all of the studies found lower State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI) scores in the music group compared to standard care (Hendricks et al., 2015; Oduya & Park, 2019; Ferreira, 2022). The systematic review by Lindgren and Shaw (2020) pooled 26 trials and found music produced a reduction in anxiety comparable to midazolam in several of them, with no adverse effects reported.\n\nPhysiological results are less consistent. Some studies found reductions in heart rate and blood pressure (Oduya & Park, 2019), while others found no significant difference in vital signs even though patients said they felt calmer (Tran et al., 2017). This may be because vital signs are affected by so many other things in the preop area.\n\nPatient choice may matter. Studies that let patients choose their own music from a playlist tended to show bigger effects than ones where researchers chose \"relaxing\" music for everyone (Ferreira, 2022; Abiodun, 2023). However none of the studies directly compared the two approaches.\n\nGaps\nMost studies had small sample sizes (under 100) and were done at a single hospital. Blinding is basically impossible since patients know if they are listening to music. Also, the length of the music session varied a lot, from 15 to 60 minutes, so it's hard to know what the right \"dose\" is.\n\nImplications for Nursing Practice\nOverall the evidence supports offering music as a low-cost, low-risk intervention in the preoperative holding area. A simple protocol with headphones and a few patient-chosen playlists could be implemented without much training. More research is needed before music can be recommended as a replacement for anxiolytic medication." },
  { kind: "presentation", name: "work speaker notes for a quarterly customer support review at an all-hands meeting", text: "Q3 Support Review – speaker notes (Jen)\nAll-hands Thursday 10/9, I have 12 min\n\nSlide 1 – Title\nQuick intro. Thank the team, especially the weekend crew since we were short two people most of August.\n\nSlide 2 – Headline numbers\nTickets: 18,420 this quarter, up 22% from Q2. Most of that is the September pricing change.\nFirst response time median went from 3h 10m to 1h 45m. This is the one I want people to remember.\nCSAT 91%, basically flat (Q2 was 92).\nDon't read every number off the slide, people can see them.\n\nSlide 3 – Where the volume came from\nPie chart. Billing questions jumped from 18% of tickets to 31%. Point out that almost all of it was the two weeks after the pricing email went out. → segue into slide 4, this is the part where I'm asking for something.\n\nSlide 4 – The pricing change week\nShow the spike graph. Say plainly: we found out about the email the same morning customers did. Not blaming anyone, just want a 48 hr heads up next time so we can prep macros. Pause here for questions if anyone from marketing wants to jump in.\n\nSlide 5 – What worked\nNew triage tags (thanks Omar). Saved replies for the top 10 billing questions. Moving Tier 1 to a split shift.\n\nSlide 6 – What didn't\nChat queue after 6pm still bad, avg wait 14 min. Knowledge base articles are out of date, maybe 40 of them reference the old plan names.\n\nSlide 7 – Q4 goals\n1. Hold first response under 2h through the holiday rush\n2. Rewrite the 40 KB articles by Nov 15\n3. Hire 2 – reqs are open, send referrals!!\n\nSlide 8 – Questions\nIf I'm over time skip straight here." },
  { kind: "dbq", name: "Regents Global History Part III B essay on effects of the Industrial Revolution, pasted context and task, average", text: "Regents Global II Practice – Part III B Essay\n\nHistorical Context: The Industrial Revolution began in Great Britain in the late 1700s and later spread to other parts of the world. It brought many changes to society.\nTask: Describe the effects of the Industrial Revolution on society. Discuss positive and negative effects.\n\nThe Industrial Revolution started in Great Britain around 1750 because they had lots of coal and iron, rivers, and colonies to get raw materials from. It changed how goods were made from by hand at home to by machines in factories. It had both positive and negative effects on society, including bad working conditions, the growth of cities, and better transportation.\n\nOne negative effect was the terrible working conditions in the factories. In Document 1, a girl named Elizabeth Bentley tells a parliament committee that she started working in a mill when she was only six years old, from 5 in the morning till 9 at night, and that she was beaten if she was late. Document 2 shows a picture of children working in a coal mine pulling carts. Children were used because they were cheap and small. These conditions led to reforms like the Factory Act of 1833 that limited child labor.\n\nAnother effect was that cities grew very fast. Document 3 is a chart that shows the population of Manchester going from about 25,000 in 1772 to over 300,000 in 1850. People moved from the country to find jobs in factories. According to Document 4, the cities were crowded and dirty, and there was no sewage so diseases like cholera spread quickly. Workers lived in tenements with whole families in one room.\n\nBut the Industrial Revolution also had positive effects. Document 5 shows that railroads made it faster and cheaper to move goods and people. The first railway between Liverpool and Manchester opened in 1830. Document 6 says that over time wages went up and goods like cloth became cheaper so more people could buy them. Eventually a middle class grew.\n\nIn conclusion, the Industrial Revolution brought changes that were both bad and good. Workers suffered in the beginning, but over time reforms were made and life got better for many people. The Industrial Revolution still affects us today because most things we use are made in factories." },
  { kind: "social", name: "two Instagram caption options for a high school robotics team, drafted in a doc with a note to a teammate", text: "IG caption ideas for competition recap – pick one by Fri (post w/ carousel)\n\nopt 1:\nWe came. We built. We (mostly) didn't break. Team 7742 Gearheads finished 3rd out of 38 at the Tri-County Qualifier this weekend and we're heading to STATE!! Huge shoutout to our drive team and to Mr. Patel for the 6am Saturday donuts.\n#FTC #robotics #gearheads7742 #roadtostate\n\nopt 2:\n3rd place. 38 teams. 1 very tired pit crew. State, here we come.\n#FTC #STEM #gearheads7742\n\n(Lena - opt 2 is shorter but opt 1 thanks people, maybe combine?)" },
  { kind: "instructions", name: "school district IT guide for staff on setting up multi-factor authentication", text: "Setting Up Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) – Staff Guide\nWestbrook Unified School District – Technology Services\nUpdated September 2026\n\nStarting October 20, all staff accounts will require MFA to sign in to district email and Google Workspace from off campus. This guide walks you through setting it up with the Microsoft Authenticator app. It takes about 5 minutes. You will need your district laptop and your phone.\n\nBefore you start\n- Install Microsoft Authenticator from the App Store or Google Play on your phone. It is free.\n- Make sure you can sign in to your district account normally.\n\nSteps\n1. On your laptop, go to the district portal and sign in with your district email.\n2. A box will appear that says \"More information required.\" Click Next.\n3. Choose \"Mobile app\" and select \"Receive notifications for verification.\" Click Set up.\n4. A QR code will appear on your screen. Leave this window open.\n5. On your phone, open Authenticator, tap the + in the top right, and choose \"Work or school account,\" then \"Scan QR code.\"\n6. Point your phone's camera at the QR code on your laptop. Your district account will appear in the app.\n7. Back on your laptop, click Next. The system will send a test notification to your phone.\n8. Tap Approve on your phone. When the laptop shows \"Verification successful,\" click Done.\n\nTroubleshooting\n* No notification on your phone? Check that notifications are turned on for Authenticator in your phone's settings.\n* Got a new phone? Do not delete the app from your old phone until you have contacted the Help Desk. We will reset your MFA so you can repeat the steps above.\n* Don't have a smartphone? Contact the Help Desk to request a hardware key.\n\nQuestions: submit a ticket through the Help Desk portal or call ext. 4400." },
  { kind: "script", name: "student podcast episode script with two hosts, sound cues and ad read, about homecoming moving to the gym", text: "The Hallway Report – Episode 6 script\nHosts: Marcus and Dani\nRun time goal: 8–10 min\n\n[INTRO MUSIC – 10 sec then fade under]\n\nMARCUS: What's up Ridgeview, welcome back to The Hallway Report, the only podcast recorded in a converted supply closet. I'm Marcus.\nDANI: And I'm Dani. And yes, it still smells like dry erase markers in here.\nMARCUS: It's a vibe.\nDANI: It's a hazard. Anyway. Today we have to talk about the thing everybody's been talking about since Monday.\nMARCUS: Homecoming.\nDANI: Homecoming. So if you somehow missed it, the dance is NOT going to be at the Lakeside Pavilion this year. It's moving back to the gym.\nMARCUS: And people are, let's say, emotional about it.\nDANI: (laughs) That's one word.\n[SFX: record scratch]\nMARCUS: Okay so we actually did our homework this time. We talked to Ms. Tran, who's the activities director, and she said the main reason is cost. The Pavilion went up almost three thousand dollars from last year.\nDANI: And student council only had so much from ticket sales, so it was either move it or raise ticket prices to like forty dollars.\nMARCUS: Which nobody wants.\nDANI: Nobody. So here's the other side though. We also did a quick poll on the class Instagram and—Marcus, read it.\nMARCUS: Out of about 300 votes, 61 percent said they'd rather pay more and keep the Pavilion.\nDANI: So there's a disconnect.\nMARCUS: Big disconnect.\n[TRANSITION STING]\nDANI: After the break we've got the senior class president, Jaylen Ortiz, coming in to answer your questions. You sent us a LOT of questions.\nMARCUS: Some of them we can't read on air.\nDANI: Most of them we can't read on air.\n[AD READ – Marcus: Bean There coffee, 10% off with student ID, read exactly as written on the card]\n[INTERVIEW SEGMENT – see separate doc for Jaylen questions]\n[OUTRO]\nDANI: That's it for this week. Follow us at hallwayreport on Insta and send us stuff for next episode.\nMARCUS: Stay hydrated. Stay in the hallway. Peace.\n[OUTRO MUSIC]" },
  { kind: "cause", name: "college history essay on the long-term consequences of the printing press, polished", text: "Movable Type and the Unmaking of Authority\nHIST 210: Early Modern Europe\nProfessor Adeyemi\nNathaniel Ruiz\n\nWhen Johannes Gutenberg began printing with movable metal type in Mainz around 1450, he was solving a business problem, not trying to start a revolution. Hand-copied books were slow and expensive, and he saw a market for producing them faster. Yet within a century his invention had helped fracture the Western church, reshape the vernacular languages of Europe, and change how knowledge itself was tested and passed on. These consequences were not planned, and most of them took decades to unfold, but they can be traced back to one basic change: the cost of copying a text collapsed.\n\nThe first and most immediate consequence was volume. By 1500, presses operated in more than 250 European towns, and historians estimate that somewhere between eight and twenty million books had been printed, perhaps more than scribes had produced in the previous thousand years. Cheaper books meant new readers, especially among merchants and artisans who could never have afforded a manuscript.\n\nThat new readership is what made the Reformation possible on the scale it took. Earlier critics of the church, such as Jan Hus a century before, had been condemned and their ideas contained regionally. When Martin Luther posted his Ninety-Five Theses in 1517, printers in Leipzig, Nuremberg and Basel had copies circulating across German lands within weeks. Luther understood the medium; he wrote short, cheap pamphlets in German rather than Latin, and by some counts his works made up a third of all German-language publications in the early 1520s. The church could condemn a man, but it could not recall a hundred thousand pamphlets.\n\nPrinting in the vernacular had a second, slower effect. Printers wanted to sell to the widest possible market, so they settled on spellings and forms that readers across a region could follow. Over time this helped fix standard versions of German, English and French, and it strengthened the sense that speakers of one language belonged to one people, a development some historians link to the later rise of national identity.\n\nFinally, as Elizabeth Eisenstein argued in The Printing Press as an Agent of Change (1979), print transformed science. Identical copies meant that a scholar in Padua and one in Kraków could look at the same diagram and the same table of figures, compare their observations, and catch errors. Copernicus, Vesalius and later Kepler all depended on this fixity. Knowledge could now accumulate rather than decay with each new copy.\n\nNone of these outcomes was inevitable, and printing was not their only cause; political rivalries, economic change and humanist scholarship all mattered. But the press acted as an accelerant. It took ideas that might once have stayed local and made them impossible to contain, and in doing so it permanently weakened the ability of any single institution to control what Europeans read and believed." },
  { kind: "litreview", name: "AP Research literature review on four-day school weeks ending with the gap the student's study will fill", text: "Research Question: What does existing research say about how four-day school weeks affect students?\nLiterature Review – AP Research\nEllie Danforth\n\nFour-day school weeks have become more popular in the United States, especially in rural districts in states like Colorado, Oklahoma, and Missouri. Districts usually switch to save money on transportation and utilities and to attract teachers. But researchers have different findings on whether the schedule helps or hurts students.\n\nSome of the first studies found positive or neutral effects. Anderson and Walker (2015) looked at Colorado elementary schools and found that math scores actually went up slightly after schools switched to four days. They thought this could be because of longer school days and better teacher morale. However, this study only looked at a few years of data and only at one state.\n\nMore recent studies have found negative effects. Morton (2021) studied Oklahoma schools and found small decreases in reading and math scores, especially for students who were already behind. A large study by Thompson and colleagues (2021) that used data from several states found that achievement grew more slowly in four-day schools compared to similar five-day schools, even though the difference was small. This makes it seem like the earlier positive results might not apply everywhere.\n\nThere is also research on things besides test scores. Some researchers found that four-day weeks may reduce attendance problems and help with teacher recruitment (Turner et al., 2019). On the other hand, parents with younger kids have to find childcare on the fifth day, which can be hard for low income families. Tharp (2014) also found that some students in rural areas lost access to school meals on the off day.\n\nOverall, the research does not agree on whether four-day weeks are good or bad for students. Most studies agree that the effects on test scores are small, but the direction is not clear. There is a gap in the research about how the schedule affects high school students specifically, because most studies focus on elementary and middle schools. My research will try to address this gap by surveying high school students in a four-day district about how they use their fifth day." },
  { kind: "presentation", name: "college art history presentation script on the Bauhaus with [next slide] cues instead of slide numbers", text: "ARTH 225 – Presentation Script\nTopic: The Bauhaus and the Idea That Design Is for Everyone\n(Rachel – about 7 min, practice w/ timer)\n\nHi everyone. So for my presentation I'm talking about the Bauhaus, which was a German art school that only lasted fourteen years but basically shaped what everything around you looks like, including probably the chair you're sitting in right now.\n\n[next slide – Weimar building photo]\n\nThe Bauhaus was founded in 1919 in Weimar by the architect Walter Gropius. Germany had just lost World War I, and there was this feeling that the old world was over and something new had to be built. Gropius's big idea was that there shouldn't be a wall between \"fine art,\" like painting, and \"craft,\" like furniture or weaving. Everyone at the school, no matter what they wanted to end up doing, started in the same foundation course.\n\n[next slide – curriculum wheel diagram]\n\nThis is the diagram of how the curriculum worked. You start on the outside ring with the basic course, then you move into workshops, so metal, wood, textiles, ceramics, and in the very center is building, architecture. So the idea is everything leads to making whole spaces.\n\n[next slide – Marianne Brandt teapot]\n\nOne of my favorite objects is this teapot by Marianne Brandt from 1924. She was one of the only women in the metal workshop, because women were usually pushed into weaving. Look at how it's just basic shapes, a half sphere, a circle, a cylinder for the handle. No decoration. That's the Bauhaus idea that form should follow function.\n\n[next slide – Breuer chair]\n\nAnd this is Marcel Breuer's tubular steel chair. He supposedly got the idea from the handlebars of his bicycle. This could be mass produced in a factory, which was the whole point: good design for regular people, not just rich people.\n\n[next slide – Dessau building]\n\nIn 1925 the school moved to Dessau into this building that Gropius designed. It's all glass and steel and it looks like it could have been built last year.\n\n[next slide – 1933]\n\nBut the Nazis saw the Bauhaus as un-German and too leftist, and in 1933 the school was forced to close. A lot of the teachers left for the US, which is why you see Bauhaus ideas in American architecture later, like Mies van der Rohe in Chicago.\n\n[last slide]\n\nSo I think the reason the Bauhaus still matters is that it changed the question from \"is this beautiful\" to \"does this work, and can everyone have it.\" Thanks, and I'm happy to take questions." },
  { kind: "dbq", name: "AP World DBQ on Indian Ocean trade 1200-1450 by an English learner, citing Doc 2-7", text: "Prompt: Evaluate the extent to which the Indian Ocean trade network changed the societies involved from c.1200 to c.1450.\n\nIn the period 1200 to 1450 the Indian Ocean trade was become the biggest sea trade in the world, connect East Africa, Arabia, India, Southeast Asia and China. The trade changed the societies very much, especially in religion and in the growth of the cities, but the local cultures was still stayed in many ways.\n\nFirst, the trade spread Islam to many places. Doc 2 is from Ibn Battuta when he visit Kilwa in 1331 and he says the sultan is very generous and pious and the people are Muslims. This show that Swahili coast city become Islamic because of the Arab merchants. Ibn Battuta purpose is writing for his travel book so maybe he want to show the Muslim world is big and connected. Also in Southeast Asia, Doc 4 shows the Malacca sultan converted to Islam for make better relationship with Muslim traders.\n\nSecond, the trade make cities very rich. Doc 3 is a list of goods: gold from Zimbabwe, ivory, spices from India, porcelain from China. Doc 5 is the picture of Great Zimbabwe walls, which was build with money from the gold trade. The cities like Calicut and Malacca grow very big because ships must wait there for the monsoon winds to change.\n\nHowever, not everything change. Doc 6 says that the Swahili language is mix of Bantu and Arabic, so the African culture is still there and only mix with new things. Also Doc 7 is from Ma Huan who was with Zheng He, and he describe the king of Calicut keep his own religion even many Muslim traders live there.\n\nIn conclusion the Indian Ocean trade change societies in big way by spreading Islam and making rich cities, but the old cultures were not disappear, they mix together." },
  { kind: "social", name: "history teacher's numbered X thread about 1816, the Year Without a Summer, drafted in a doc", text: "thread draft – 1816 (post Sunday am?)\n\n1/ In 1816, it snowed in New England in June. Frost killed crops in July and August. People called it \"the Year Without a Summer,\" and almost nobody at the time knew why. A thread:\n\n2/ The cause was on the other side of the planet. In April 1815, Mount Tambora in what's now Indonesia erupted. It's the most powerful eruption in recorded history and killed tens of thousands of people nearby.\n\n3/ Tambora threw so much sulfur into the stratosphere that it formed a haze around the globe. That haze reflected sunlight. Global temps dropped somewhere around half a degree C. Sounds tiny. It wasn't.\n\n4/ Crops failed across Europe. There were food riots in Britain and France. In parts of Switzerland people were reduced to eating moss.\n\n5/ Fun detour: a group of writers stuck indoors at Lake Geneva that rainy summer had a ghost story contest. Mary Shelley started Frankenstein. Yes, really.\n\n6/ In the US, thousands of New England farmers gave up and moved west to Ohio and Indiana. Some historians think 1816 sped up westward migration.\n\n7/ Moral of the story for my students: history connects in ways you'd never guess. Volcano in Indonesia → monster novel → Ohio. Okay that's it, go touch grass (it's probably not frozen)." },
  { kind: "instructions", name: "6th grade how-to essay on folding a paper airplane, written in paragraphs with MLA heading", text: "Jaden Morales\nMs. Whitaker\nELA 6, Period 2\nOctober 2, 2026\n\nHow to Make a Paper Airplane That Actually Flies\n\nHave you ever made a paper airplane that just nosedives into the floor? In this essay I will explain how to make a dart airplane that flies straight and far. All you need is one piece of regular printer paper and a flat surface like a desk.\n\nFirst, put the paper on the desk the tall way. Fold it in half the long way so the two long sides touch, and press down the crease really hard with your fingernail. Then open it back up. This line in the middle is your guide for everything else.\n\nNext, take the top two corners and fold them down so they meet the middle line. It should look like a house with a pointy roof now. After that, fold the new slanted edges down to the middle line again. Now the point is even skinnier and sharper.\n\nThen fold the whole thing in half along the first crease, with the folds on the outside. Now you will make the wings. Fold one side down so the top edge lines up with the bottom of the plane. Flip it over and do the exact same thing on the other side. Make sure the wings are even or the plane will turn to one side.\n\nFinally, open up the wings so they are flat and straight out from the body. Hold it underneath near the front and throw it straight ahead, not up. If it dives down, bend the back edges of the wings up a tiny bit.\n\nThat is how you make a paper airplane. It takes about 3 minutes and if you do it right it can fly across the whole classroom. Just don't throw it during class like I did." },
  { kind: "script", name: "solo YouTube thrift-flip video script with A-roll, B-roll and voiceover cues", text: "THRIFT FLIP #12 – $4 blazer → cropped jacket\nscript / shot list (film Sat)\n\n[COLD OPEN – close up of the ugly blazer on hanger]\nME (V.O.): This blazer cost me four dollars and honestly I think I overpaid.\n\n[TITLE CARD + music]\n\n[A-ROLL – me at sewing table]\nME: Hey guys, welcome back. So today's flip is a little risky because I've never worked with this kind of shoulder pad situation before. (hold blazer up, show shoulders) Look at these. This is serious 1988 energy.\nME: The plan is to crop it right about here (pin at waist), take out the shoulder pads, and swap these sad plastic buttons for something cuter.\n\n[B-ROLL – seam ripping shoulder pads, speed up 4x]\nME (V.O.): Taking the shoulder pads out took way longer than it should have. There were like nine layers of foam in there.\n\n[A-ROLL]\nME: Okay, moment of truth, we're cutting.\n[SFX: dramatic sting]\n[B-ROLL – cutting, close up on scissors]\n\n[B-ROLL – hemming on machine]\nME (V.O.): I did a simple double-fold hem so it doesn't fray. If you don't have a machine you can totally hand sew this, it'll just take you an episode of something.\n\n[A-ROLL – buttons]\nME: I found these gold buttons in my grandma's button tin and I think they're perfect.\n\n[TRY ON – mirror shot, transition snap]\nME: Okay. OKAY. I kind of love it?\n\n[OUTRO]\nME: Let me know in the comments what I should flip next, and if you want to see how I do hems I'll link my old video up here (point). Bye!\n\n[END SCREEN 20 sec]" },
  { kind: "cause", name: "messy first draft of a 10th grade essay on why teens vape, with bracketed placeholders and unfinished effects list", text: "why are so many teens vaping?? (working title)\nEng 10 – cause/effect essay DRAFT 1\n\n[HOOK – find that CDC number about how many high schoolers vape, I think it was like 1 in 4 in 2019??]\n\nEven though cigarette smoking among teenagers has gone way down in the last 20 years, vaping has gone up fast, and now it is one of the most common ways teens use nicotine. There are a lot of reasons for this, and it is having serious effects on teens' health and on schools.\n\nOne big reason is the flavors. Vapes come in flavors like mango, cotton candy, and cool mint, which make it seem more like candy than a drug. A lot of kids say they started because a flavor sounded good. (Ms. D said combine this with the marketing paragraph??)\n\nAnother reason is how easy they are to hide. Some vapes look like a USB drive or a highlighter, and the smell goes away really fast, so teachers and parents don't notice. Kids vape in the bathroom between classes. Our school literally had to put sensors in the bathrooms last year.\n\nAlso a lot of teens think vaping is basically harmless or at least way safer than smoking. [find source] It's called vapor so it sounds clean. But most vapes have nicotine salts which actually get into your body faster\n\nEffects\n- addiction, some kids can't get through a class period without hitting it\n- lungs, EVALI in 2019 (look up)\n- brain still developing until 25\n- money – pods are expensive, some people sell them at school\n- school discipline / sensors\n\n[conclusion?? maybe talk about what happens if nothing changes]" },
  { kind: "litreview", name: "unfinished capstone literature review section on microplastics in freshwater fish, with notes to self and TODOs", text: "Lit review – microplastics in freshwater fish (DRAFT, Section 2 of capstone)\n\nMost of the early research on microplastics focused on oceans, and freshwater systems have only gotten serious attention in the last ten years or so. This section reviews what is currently known about microplastic ingestion by freshwater fish, how it is measured, and what effects have been observed.\n\nPrevalence\nStudies consistently find microplastics in the guts of wild freshwater fish, but reported rates vary a lot. Ruiz-Delgado (2018) found particles in 43% of fish sampled from three rivers in [region?], while Kessler & Ahn (2020) found them in over 80% of fish downstream of a wastewater treatment plant. Fibers are the most common type in almost every study, probably from laundry. [need more on 2019 studies – the Great Lakes one?]\n\nMethods problem\nA big issue is that studies use different methods so it's hard to compare. Some digest the gut with KOH, some with enzymes, some just look under a microscope. Minimum particle size also varies (some count down to 20 µm, others only above 300 µm), which obviously changes how many you find. Contamination from lab air and clothing is also a problem and not every study uses blanks. → this is probably my main point, the numbers aren't comparable\n\nEffects on fish\nLab studies show exposure can cause gut inflammation, reduced feeding, and slower growth (Okonkwo et al., 2019; Brandvold, 2021), but these usually use way higher concentrations than what's in real rivers. Field evidence of harm is limited.\n\nFeeding habits / trophic level\n?? some say bottom feeders have more, some say predators – read Lindahl 2022 again\n\nGaps\n- almost nothing on small streams\n- no long-term studies\n- human health link from eating fish basically unknown\n\nTODO: transition into my own study (Clearwater Creek sampling)" },
  { kind: "presentation", name: "7th grade country project slides script on Portugal, one short line per slide", text: "Portugal – Country Project\nby Sofia R. (7th grade Social Studies)\n\nSlide 1: Hi my name is Sofia and my country is Portugal.\n\nSlide 2: Map. Portugal is in Europe on the west side next to Spain and the Atlantic Ocean. The capital is Lisbon. About 10 million people live there.\n\nSlide 3: Flag. The flag is green and red. In the middle there is a shield and a yellow thing called an armillary sphere which was a tool sailors used.\n\nSlide 4: History. Portugal was really famous for explorers. Vasco da Gama sailed all the way around Africa to India in 1498. They had a big empire with Brazil and parts of Africa.\n\nSlide 5: Food. My favorite part! They eat a lot of fish especially bacalhau which is dried salted cod. They also have pastéis de nata which are little custard tarts. I tried one at a bakery and it was soooo good.\n\nSlide 6: Fun facts. Portugal is one of the oldest countries in Europe. Cristiano Ronaldo is from Portugal (from the island of Madeira). The language is Portuguese and they also speak it in Brazil.\n\nSlide 7: Thank you for listening! Any questions?" },
  { kind: "dbq", name: "unfinished timed APUSH DBQ on the 19th Amendment, thesis then body notes per document, ran out of time", text: "APUSH DBQ – women's suffrage (practice, timed 60 min – didn't finish)\n\nPrompt: Evaluate the relative importance of the factors that led to the passage of the 19th Amendment.\n\nThesis: While earlier activism like Seneca Falls kept the issue alive, women's contributions during World War I and the changing tactics of the movement in the 1910s were the most important factors in winning suffrage in 1920.\n\nContext – Progressive Era, women already active in reform (temperance, settlement houses, Jane Addams), western states gave women the vote first (Wyoming 1869)\n\nBody 1: early activism\nDoc 1 – Declaration of Sentiments 1848, \"all men and women are created equal\" – shows the movement starts with natural rights argument. (HIPP – audience was the convention + press, purpose to get attention)\nbut by 1900 still only 4 states, so early activism wasn't enough on its own\n\nBody 2: tactics\nDoc 3 – Alice Paul / NWP picketing the White House 1917, \"Kaiser Wilson\" banner. Doc 4 – NAWSA, Carrie Chapman Catt \"Winning Plan\" was more state-by-state and moderate. Two strategies worked together?? The radical side made the moderate side look reasonable\n(use doc 5 here for HIPP – the cartoon of the suffragist in jail, point of view is sympathetic)\n\nBody 3: WWI\nDoc 6 – Wilson's 1918 speech to the Senate calling suffrage a war measure — he literally changed his mind because of the war\nDoc 7 – women working in munitions factories, nurses\n\noutside evidence: Night of Terror, force feeding at Occoquan workhouse. Tennessee ratifying by one vote (Harry Burn and his mom's letter!!)\n\n[ran out of time here]" },
  { kind: "social", name: "Reddit post asking r/college how to talk to a roommate whose boyfriend keeps staying over", place: "www.reddit.com/r/college/submit", text: "Roommate's boyfriend basically lives in our room now, how do I bring it up without making it weird?\n\nFreshman here, living in a double. My roommate is honestly really nice and we got along great the first month. But since like week 5 her boyfriend (he lives off campus) has basically moved in. He's here Sunday through Thursday most weeks, sometimes all week.\n\nIt's not that he's rude or anything. But I can't change in my own room, I have to wait for the shower on our floor because he's using it, and last week they were watching stuff on her laptop at 1am while I had an 8am exam. I didn't say anything because I froze.\n\nOur roommate agreement from the start of the year says overnight guests max 2 nights a week, but neither of us has looked at it since August.\n\nI don't want to go straight to the RA because I feel like that'll blow it up and I still have to live with her until May. But I also don't want to keep feeling like a guest in my own room.\n\nHow do I bring this up? Do I mention the agreement or is that too passive aggressive? Has anyone dealt with this and had it go okay?\n\nEdit: thanks everyone, going to try talking to her this weekend." },
  { kind: "instructions", name: "driving directions to a family lake cabin for a holiday weekend, landmarks instead of GPS", text: "Directions to the Cabin (Loon Lake) – for everyone coming Labor Day weekend!\n\nGPS gets you lost after Hwy 70, so please use these instead. No cell service for the last 15 min so screenshot this or print it.\n\nFrom the Twin Cities: take I-35 north to Hinckley (about 1 hr 15 min). Get gas here, there is nothing after Grantsburg.\n\nAt Hinckley take the Hwy 48 exit and go east. It turns into Hwy 70 when you cross the river into Wisconsin.\n\nStay on 70 through Grantsburg. About 9 miles past Grantsburg you will see a red barn with a Packers flag on the left. Turn LEFT right after the barn onto County Rd F.\n\nFollow County F for 6 miles. It turns to gravel after the white church. Slow down here, there are deer everywhere, especially at dusk.\n\nTurn RIGHT on Birch Point Lane (small green sign, easy to miss. If you hit the boat landing you went too far, turn around).\n\nWe are the 4th driveway on the left. Look for the blue mailbox that says HALVERSON and the wooden loon on the post. Park on the grass, not the driveway, so the boat trailer can get out.\n\nIf you get lost, drive back to the church and call from there, it's the last spot with one bar. Landline at the cabin is 715-555-0148.\n\nSee you there!" },
  { kind: "script", name: "film class short screenplay draft, siblings clearing out their late father's garage, INT/EXT format", text: "\"Last Thing in the Garage\"\nShort film – FILM 101 final, draft 2\nwritten by Owen Castellanos\n\nFADE IN:\n\nINT. SUBURBAN GARAGE – DAY\n\nDust floats in a shaft of light from a small window. Shelves crammed with paint cans, coffee tins full of screws, a workbench buried under newspapers.\n\nNORA (24) stands in the doorway holding a box of trash bags. She doesn't come in.\n\nHer brother ELI (19) squeezes past her with an armful of flattened boxes.\n\nELI\nYou gonna help or just supervise?\n\nNORA\nI'm figuring out where to start.\n\nELI\n(dropping the boxes)\nAnywhere. That's where you start. Anywhere.\n\nHe grabs a coffee tin and shakes it. Rattle of screws.\n\nELI (CONT'D)\nKeep or toss?\n\nNORA\nHe sorted those by size, Eli.\n\nELI\nHe sorted everything by size.\n\nBeat. Eli tosses the tin into a trash bag. Nora flinches at the sound.\n\nINT. GARAGE – LATER\n\nThe shelves are half empty. Black bags line the wall. Eli is on a stepladder. Nora sits on an upturned bucket holding a cassette tape labeled in marker: \"NORA – AGE 6.\"\n\nNORA\nDid you know about this?\n\nELI\n(climbing down)\nWhat is it?\n\nShe turns it over. There's a tape player on the workbench, buried under newspapers. Eli digs it out. They look at each other.\n\nNORA\nWhat if it's nothing.\n\nELI\nThen it's nothing.\n\nHe presses PLAY. Hiss. Then, faintly, a CHILD'S VOICE singing off-key, and a MAN laughing close to the microphone.\n\nDAD (V.O.)\n(on tape)\nOkay, okay, one more time, from the top—\n\nNora covers her mouth. Eli sits down on the floor next to her bucket.\n\nThey listen. Neither of them moves to stop it.\n\nCUT TO BLACK." },
  { kind: "cause", name: "honors 11 essay on what caused the 1919 Boston Molasses Flood, opens with a narrative scene", text: "The Sweetest Disaster: What Caused the Great Molasses Flood\nHonors English 11 – Expository Essay\nGrace Hollister\n\nAt around 12:40 in the afternoon on January 15, 1919, people in Boston's North End heard a deep rumbling followed by a sound some witnesses compared to a machine gun. Seconds later, a wave of molasses, at some points estimated to be 25 feet high, rushed down Commercial Street at about 35 miles per hour. It crushed buildings, knocked a train off its tracks and trapped people and horses in a sticky mass that hardened as it cooled. Twenty-one people died and around 150 were injured. It sounds almost like a joke, but the Great Molasses Flood was a real tragedy, and it happened because of a series of decisions that put speed and profit ahead of safety.\n\nThe tank itself was the root of the problem. It was built in 1915 by the Purity Distilling Company, part of United States Industrial Alcohol, to store molasses that would be turned into industrial alcohol, which was in high demand for munitions during World War I. The company wanted it finished fast. The man in charge of the project, Arthur Jell, was a treasurer, not an engineer, and he never had the tank's plans checked by one. When the tank was finished, it was supposed to be tested by filling it with water. Instead, it was filled with only a few inches of water and then put into use.\n\nAlmost immediately the tank started to leak. Residents of the neighborhood collected the molasses dripping from the seams to take home. The company's response was not to fix the tank but to paint it brown so the leaks would be harder to see. Later investigations found that the steel walls were thinner than they should have been for a tank that size and that the steel itself was brittle.\n\nThe weather on the day of the disaster made things worse. A few days before, the temperature had been around 2 degrees Fahrenheit, but on January 15 it rose to about 40 degrees. A shipment of warmer molasses had recently been added to the tank, and fermentation inside may have increased the pressure. The weakened tank could not handle the strain and split apart.\n\nThe flood's effects reached beyond the North End. The victims' families and local businesses sued United States Industrial Alcohol, and after years of hearings, the court-appointed auditor ruled in 1925 that the company was responsible. The case helped lead to new rules in Massachusetts and elsewhere requiring that engineers and architects sign off on the plans for structures like this one. Today it seems obvious that a 50-foot tank holding more than two million gallons should be checked by an expert, but in 1919, it took twenty-one deaths for that to become the law." },
  { kind: "litreview", name: "Philippine senior high school thesis Chapter II, Review of Related Literature on social media and procrastination, ESL", text: "CHAPTER II\nREVIEW OF RELATED LITERATURE AND STUDIES\n\nThis chapter presents the related literature and studies that the researchers gathered from different sources which are relevant to the present study entitled \"Social Media Usage and Academic Procrastination Among Grade 12 Students of San Isidro National High School.\"\n\nForeign Literature\n\nAccording to Steel (2007), procrastination is when a person willingly delays a planned task even though they expect the delay will make things worse for them. Students who procrastinate usually know that delaying their tasks will give them negative results, yet they still choose to do so.\n\nBased on the study of Hernandez and Cole (2019), students who spend more than four hours a day on social networking sites reported higher levels of academic procrastination compared to those who spend less than two hours. The researchers explained that social media gives instant rewards like likes and comments, which makes it more attractive than doing schoolworks that has delayed rewards.\n\nMoreover, Akhtar et al. (2021) found that the habit of checking notifications during study time divides the attention of the students and makes them take longer to finish their tasks. However, the same study also stated that social media can be useful for academic purposes when it is used for group chats and sharing of learning materials.\n\nLocal Literature\n\nThe Philippines is known as one of the countries with the highest social media usage in the world. According to Dela Cruz (2020), Filipino students spend an average of three to four hours per day on Facebook, TikTok and YouTube.\n\nLocal Studies\n\nThe study of Ramos and Villanueva (2022) among senior high school students in Laguna revealed that there is a significant positive relationship between the time spent on TikTok and academic procrastination. Similarly, Bautista (2023) found that ABM and STEM students who use social media before sleeping tend to delay their performance tasks until the night before the deadline.\n\nOn the other hand, the study of Mendoza (2021) showed that not all social media use leads to procrastination. Students who use Facebook groups for class announcements were found to be more updated with their requirements.\n\nSynthesis\n\nThe gathered literature and studies show that social media usage has a relationship with academic procrastination of students, especially when the usage is for entertainment and for long hours. However, some studies also show that social media can help students when used for academic purposes. The present study is similar to the study of Ramos and Villanueva (2022) because both focus on senior high school students, but it differs because the present study will be conducted in San Isidro National High School and will include all social media platforms." },
  { kind: "presentation", name: "two-person startup pitch competition script for a campus food-waste app, with CLICK cues and timings", text: "Campus Venture Challenge – Final Round Pitch\n\"Leftover\" – speaker script (Theo + Amara)\n5 MIN HARD STOP. Practice with clicker.\n\n[SLIDE 1 – logo] (Theo, ~20 sec)\nEvery night at 8 p.m., our dining hall throws away food that was cooked for people who never showed up. We're Leftover, and we want to put that food in students' hands instead of the dumpster.\n\n[CLICK – SLIDE 2: the problem] (Theo, ~40 sec)\nWe spent two weeks counting. At Hartwell Dining alone, about 140 pounds of prepared food gets tossed on an average weeknight. Across all four dining halls that's roughly 25 tons a semester. Meanwhile, in our survey of 410 students, 1 in 3 said they'd skipped a meal in the past month because they couldn't afford it.\n\n[CLICK – SLIDE 3: solution / app screenshots] (Amara, ~60 sec)\nLeftover is a simple app. Thirty minutes before closing, dining staff log what's left: \"two trays of pasta, one pan of roasted veggies.\" Students get a push notification, reserve a portion, and pick it up in a reusable container for two dollars or one meal swipe. (point to screen) This is the staff view, three taps. This is the student view.\n\n[CLICK – SLIDE 4: pilot results] (Amara, ~45 sec)\nWe ran a three-week pilot with Hartwell this fall. 212 students signed up, we redistributed about 900 portions, and food waste on pilot nights dropped 38 percent. Dining Services has already asked us to expand.\n\n[CLICK – SLIDE 5: business model] (Theo, ~40 sec)\nThe two dollars covers the containers and a small margin. Our real revenue model is licensing to other universities, at around $4,000 a year per campus. There are thousands of colleges in the US and most of them have this exact problem.\n\n[CLICK – SLIDE 6: the ask] (Amara, ~30 sec)\nWe're asking for the $10,000 prize to pay for app development and to expand to all four dining halls next spring.\n\n[CLICK – SLIDE 7: team photo] (both)\nThank you. We'd love your questions.\n\n(if time – Q&A prep in other doc: food safety / liability, why not just donate to a food bank)" },
  { kind: "dbq", name: "middle school Mini-Q essay on how the Nile shaped Ancient Egypt, citing Document A-E", text: "Mini-Q Essay: How Did the Nile River Shape Ancient Egypt?\nName: Brayden K.   Period 3\n\nThe Nile River is the longest river in the world and it runs through the desert in Egypt. Without the Nile, there would be no Ancient Egypt. The Nile shaped Ancient Egypt in three ways: it gave them farming, it helped with travel and trade, and it affected their religion.\n\nThe first way the Nile shaped Egypt was farming. In Document A, the map shows that almost everyone in Egypt lived right next to the river because the rest is desert. Document B says that every year the Nile flooded and left behind black soil called silt. This made the land really good for growing wheat and barley. The Egyptians even called their land Kemet which means \"black land\" because of the soil.\n\nThe second way was travel and trade. Document C shows a boat on the Nile. The Nile flows north, but the wind blows south, so boats could float down the river and then use sails to go back up. This made it easy to move things like stone for the pyramids and to trade with other places.\n\nThe third way was religion. In Document D, there is a hymn to Hapi, who was the god of the Nile flood. The Egyptians thanked him for bringing food. Document E says that the Egyptians made their calendar based on the flood, with three seasons: flood, growing, and harvest. This shows the river was part of everything in their lives.\n\nIn conclusion the Nile shaped Ancient Egypt by giving them farming, travel, and religion. Herodotus called Egypt the \"gift of the Nile,\" and I agree because without it Egypt would just be desert." },
  { kind: "social", name: "neighborhood Facebook group post about a lost orange tabby cat", place: "www.facebook.com/groups/maplewoodneighbors", text: "LOST CAT – Maplewood / near Elm & 4th\n\nOur cat Biscuit got out last night (Tuesday) around 10pm. He is an orange tabby, neutered male, kind of chunky, with a white chin and one white back foot. He's microchipped but not wearing a collar. Very friendly but scared of loud noises so he might be hiding under a porch or in a garage.\n\nIf you live around Elm, 4th, or Cedar could you please check your garages and sheds? He usually comes if you shake a treat bag. Please message me or call/text 555-0193.\n\nWe miss him so much, our kids are heartbroken. Thank you neighbors!!" },
  { kind: "instructions", name: "coffee shop front-of-house closing checklist for baristas", text: "CLOSING CHECKLIST – Front of House\n(Grindstone Coffee – Main St)\nDo these IN ORDER. Initial at the bottom and leave on the clipboard for opener.\n\nAt 7:30 (30 min before close)\n- Turn off the 2nd espresso grinder, only keep one running\n- Start breaking down the pastry case, wrap leftovers and put on the donate shelf in the walk-in (label with date!)\n- Brew last batch of drip only if there are customers, otherwise dump and rinse\n\nAt 8:00 – lock the front door, flip sign\n- Count drawer down to $150, put the rest in the deposit bag, drop in safe. Two people present for this.\n- Backflush espresso machine with Cafiza – 5 cycles of 10 sec on / 10 sec off, then 5 with plain water\n- Soak portafilters and baskets in the Cafiza bucket for 20 min\n- Purge and wipe steam wands, soak tips if milk is crusted on\n- Empty and wash milk pitchers, put upside down on the mat\n- Wipe all counters + the pickup bar with sanitizer\n\nFloor\n- Chairs up on tables\n- Sweep, then mop starting from the back corner toward the door\n- Empty all trash + recycling, new bags in every bin\n- Restock cups, lids, sleeves for the morning\n\nBefore you leave\n- Turn off music, and all lights except the one over the register\n- Check back door is locked AND the bar is down\n- Set alarm (code is on the card in the office, do not write it here)\n\nCloser initials: ______   Date: ______\nAnything the opener should know: ____________________" },
  { kind: "script", name: "Spanish 2 class skit at the doctor's office, mostly in Spanish with English stage notes", text: "Spanish 2 – Skit: En el consultorio\nGroup 4: Carlos (doctor), Hannah (paciente), Noah (mamá + narrador)\n(we have to use at least 10 vocab words from Ch. 5 + the verb doler!!)\n\nNARRADOR: Hannah no se siente bien. Va al consultorio con su mamá.\n\nDOCTOR: Buenos días. ¿Qué te pasa?\nPACIENTE: Ay, doctor, me duele mucho la cabeza. Y también me duele la garganta.\nDOCTOR: ¿Desde cuándo te sientes así?\nPACIENTE: Desde el lunes. Y estornudo todo el tiempo. ¡Achú!\nMAMÁ: Y no quiere comer nada, doctor. Solo quiere ver TikTok.\nPACIENTE: ¡Mamá!\n(pause for audience to laugh)\nDOCTOR: Abre la boca y di \"ahh.\"\nPACIENTE: Ahhh.\nDOCTOR: Mmm. Tienes la garganta muy roja. ¿Tienes fiebre?\nMAMÁ: Sí, anoche tenía treinta y ocho grados.\nDOCTOR: Creo que tienes gripe. Necesitas descansar y beber mucha agua. Aquí tienes una receta para unas pastillas.\nPACIENTE: ¿Puedo ir a la escuela mañana?\nDOCTOR: No, debes quedarte en casa dos días.\nPACIENTE: (happy dance) ¡Qué lástima!\nMAMÁ: Hannah...\nDOCTOR: Y nada de TikTok. Necesitas dormir.\nPACIENTE: (fake faints)\nNARRADOR: ¡Fin!\n\nvocab check: doler, garganta, cabeza, estornudar, fiebre, receta, pastillas, gripe, consultorio, descansar = 10" },
  { kind: "dbq", name: "AP World DBQ on the Columbian Exchange citing lettered documents (Document A-G); could be mistaken for synthesis", text: "AP World History – DBQ\nPrompt: Evaluate the extent to which the Columbian Exchange affected societies in the Americas and Afro-Eurasia between 1492 and 1750.\nSam Whitacre\n\nWhen Columbus landed in the Caribbean in 1492, he connected two halves of the world that had developed apart for thousands of years. The exchange of plants, animals, people, and diseases that followed did not affect both sides equally. While Afro-Eurasian societies gained new crops that fueled population growth, the Columbian Exchange was far more destructive for the Americas, where disease and forced labor devastated Indigenous societies and led to the enslavement of millions of Africans.\n\nThe most immediate effect in the Americas was demographic collapse. Bernardino de Sahagún's account of smallpox in Tenochtitlan describes sores covering people's bodies so completely that the sick could not move or turn over (Document A). Because Sahagún gathered this account from Nahua informants decades later, it reflects Indigenous memory of the epidemic rather than a Spanish justification of conquest, which makes its description of suffering more credible. A population chart for central Mexico shows a drop from an estimated 25 million in 1519 to around 1 million by 1605 (Document B).\n\nThis collapse in turn reshaped labor. With Indigenous workers dying, Spanish and Portuguese colonists turned to enslaved Africans. Document D, written by a Jesuit observer of Brazilian sugar mills, calls enslaved people the \"hands and feet\" of the mill owner, showing that the colonial economy depended entirely on forced African labor. Outside the documents, more than 12 million Africans were forcibly transported across the Atlantic between the sixteenth and nineteenth centuries, which transformed West African societies by draining them of young people and increasing warfare tied to the trade.\n\nFor Afro-Eurasia, the effects were more positive in the short term. Document E, a table of crop yields, shows that maize and potatoes produced more calories per acre than wheat or rice. A Chinese local gazetteer notes that sweet potatoes could grow on dry hillsides where rice could not (Document F), which helped China's population grow rapidly during the Qing dynasty. Similarly, the potato became a staple in Ireland and Prussia.\n\nHowever, Afro-Eurasia did not escape harm entirely. Document G, a Spanish official's complaint about rising prices in Seville, shows that the flood of American silver drove up prices across Europe, hurting ordinary people whose wages did not keep up. Still, this harm is minor compared to the destruction of entire civilizations in the Americas.\n\nOverall, the Columbian Exchange connected the world, but it did so on deeply unequal terms. Afro-Eurasia gained food and wealth, while the Americas and Africa paid in lives." },
  { kind: "presentation", name: "solo health science presentation on the discovery of insulin with only sparse (slide) cues; could be mistaken for speech", text: "The Discovery of Insulin – Health Science presentation (solo, ~6 min)\n\nImagine being told in 1920 that your child has diabetes. There was no treatment. Doctors put patients on starvation diets, sometimes only a few hundred calories a day, just to keep them alive a little longer. Most children with type 1 diabetes died within a year or two of diagnosis.\n\nThat's the world Frederick Banting walked into. He was a young surgeon in Ontario, not a researcher, with a failing medical practice. But he had an idea he'd scribbled down late one night after reading an article about the pancreas, and he convinced Professor John Macleod at the University of Toronto to give him a lab for the summer of 1921.\n\n(slide 2)\n\nMacleod gave him a lab, ten dogs, and a student assistant named Charles Best. Supposedly Best and another student flipped a coin to see who would get to work with Banting. That summer was hot, the lab was cramped, and a lot of the experiments failed. But by the end of it, they had extracted something from the pancreas that lowered blood sugar in diabetic dogs.\n\n(slide 3 – Leonard Thompson photo)\n\nThe extract still wasn't pure enough for humans. That's where a biochemist named James Collip came in. He figured out how to purify it. In January 1922, a fourteen-year-old boy named Leonard Thompson, who was dying at Toronto General Hospital, got the first injections. The first batch caused a reaction, but after Collip's improved version, his blood sugar dropped and he started to recover.\n\n(slide 4)\n\nHere's the part I think is the most amazing. Banting, Best and Collip sold the patent to the University of Toronto for one dollar each. Banting believed insulin should belong to the world, not to him.\n\n(slide 5)\n\nIn 1923 Banting and Macleod won the Nobel Prize. Banting was angry that Best wasn't included, so he split his prize money with him, and Macleod split his with Collip.\n\n(slide 6)\n\nSo today, over a hundred years later, millions of people with type 1 diabetes are alive because of a summer of experiments in a hot lab in Toronto. And I think it's worth asking why something that was sold for one dollar costs some Americans hundreds of dollars a month today. Thank you." },
  { kind: "script", name: "local podcast episode script and interview plan with a beekeeper, HOST/GUEST lines and planned questions; could be mistaken for a feature interview", text: "Small Town Big Hearts – Ep. 14: \"The Bee Lady of Route 9\"\nRecording script / interview plan\nHost: Daniel  |  Guest: Margaret \"Peg\" Sorensen, Sorensen Apiary\n\n[COLD OPEN – play 10 sec of hive buzzing recorded on site]\n\nDANIEL: That sound you're hearing is about sixty thousand honeybees, and they are, I'm told, in a pretty good mood today. Welcome to Small Town Big Hearts, the show about the people who make this county what it is. I'm Daniel Ferraro. My guest today has been keeping bees on the same hillside off Route 9 for thirty-two years. Peg Sorensen, welcome.\n\nPEG: (let her introduce herself)\n\nDANIEL: So I have to start with the obvious. How does somebody become \"the bee lady\"?\n\nPEG: [from pre-interview: her late husband bought one hive in 1993 as a joke birthday present, she took over when he got bored after one summer]\n\nDANIEL: What does a normal day look like for you in, say, July?\n\nDANIEL: You told me you lost almost half your hives one winter. What happened?\n(follow up if she mentions mites – ask her to explain varroa simply, listeners won't know)\n\nDANIEL: People always ask about getting stung. How many times would you say?\n(she'll say she stopped counting – laugh, move on)\n\n[MIDROLL – sponsor read: Hilltop Hardware, 30 sec]\n\nDANIEL: You started teaching a beekeeping class at the library a few years back. Who shows up to that?\n\nDANIEL: If someone listening wants to help bees but isn't going to get a hive, what should they actually do?\n\nDANIEL: Last one, and I ask everyone this. What do you hope people in this town know about you?\n\n[OUTRO]\nDANIEL: Peg Sorensen, thank you. You can find Sorensen Apiary honey at the Saturday farmers market and at Hilltop Hardware. Next week, we're talking to the guy who has repaired every church organ in the county. Until then, be good to your neighbors.\n\n[OUTRO MUSIC]" },
  { kind: "cause", name: "9th grade world history essay on why the Late Bronze Age collapsed, neutral report-like tone; could be mistaken for informative", text: "The Late Bronze Age Collapse\nWorld History – Ancient Civilizations Unit\nPriyanka Shah\n\nAround 1200 BCE, the eastern Mediterranean was home to some of the most connected societies of the ancient world. The Hittite Empire ruled much of Anatolia, Egypt was at the height of the New Kingdom, Mycenaean palaces controlled mainland Greece, and the trading city of Ugarit on the Syrian coast sent ships loaded with copper, tin, grain and luxury goods across the sea. Within about fifty years, almost all of this was gone. The Hittite capital of Hattusa was abandoned, Ugarit was burned and never rebuilt, and the Mycenaean palaces were destroyed. Historians call this the Late Bronze Age Collapse.\n\nThere is no single explanation for the collapse, and historians still debate how much weight to give each factor. One of the most famous is the arrival of the \"Sea Peoples,\" groups of raiders and migrants mentioned in Egyptian records. Inscriptions at Medinet Habu describe Ramesses III fighting them off around 1177 BCE. Letters found in Ugarit also mention enemy ships attacking the coast. However, many historians now think the Sea Peoples were as much a symptom of the crisis as its cause, since people often take to the sea when their homelands are failing.\n\nClimate is another factor. Studies of pollen in sediment from the Sea of Galilee and other lakes show a long dry period beginning around this time. Drought would have caused crop failures, and Hittite letters ask Egypt for shipments of grain, which suggests famine. Some scientists have also found evidence of a series of earthquakes in the region, sometimes called an \"earthquake storm,\" which could have damaged cities already under stress.\n\nFinally, the trading system itself may have been part of the problem. Bronze requires both copper and tin, and tin had to be imported from far away. When trade routes were disrupted, kingdoms that depended on each other could not get what they needed. In his book 1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed, Eric Cline argues that this interdependence meant that problems in one place spread to others, creating a \"systems collapse.\"\n\nThe results lasted for centuries. In Greece, the Linear B writing system disappeared, and the region entered what is called the Greek Dark Age, with smaller populations and little long-distance trade. On the other hand, the shortage of tin may have encouraged the wider use of iron, which was more plentiful, and new groups such as the Phoenicians and the Israelites rose in the centuries that followed." },
  { kind: "problem", name: "10th grader's MLA-headed essay on cafeteria food waste, average quality", text: "Jaylen Brooks\nMs. Okafor\nEnglish 10, Period 4\n12 March 2026\n\nWasted Lunch\n\nEvery day at Hillcrest High, the trash cans by the cafeteria doors fill up with food that nobody touched. Unopened milk cartons, whole apples, trays of rice that got two bites taken out of them. I started paying attention to this after our environmental club did a waste audit in February and found that students threw away about 140 pounds of food in a single lunch period. That is a lot of food for one school, and if you multiply it by every school day it gets kind of ridiculous.\n\nPart of the problem is the rules. Federal lunch rules say students have to take a fruit or vegetable even if they don't want it, so a lot of people grab an apple and toss it on the way out. Another part of the problem is time. We get 25 minutes for lunch but by the time you get through the line it's more like 15, and people would rather talk to their friends than finish their food. Also some of the food just isn't good, honestly. The broccoli is gray.\n\nThere are a few things the school could do. The first and easiest is a share table. Students could put unopened items like milk, fruit, and packaged snacks on a table and anyone who is still hungry can take them. Other schools in our district already do this and the health department allows it as long as the items are sealed.\n\nA second solution is to make lunch longer, or at least move club meetings so they don't eat into lunch. This would cost nothing except schedule changes. A third idea is to let students give feedback on the menu. If the cafeteria knew which foods were getting thrown away the most, they could stop making them or cook them differently.\n\nSome people might say that a share table is unsanitary or that kids will abuse it. But if there are clear rules and a cafeteria worker watching it, those problems are small compared to throwing away 140 pounds of food a day. Whatever happens, the school should do something, because right now we are basically paying to fill dumpsters." },
  { kind: "news", name: "high school newspaper article on the robotics team qualifying for state", text: "Robotics Team Clinches First State Berth in School History\nBy Kiara Washington, Staff Writer\nWestbrook High's robotics team, the Gearheads, will compete at the state championship for the first time in the program's nine-year history after finishing second out of 34 teams at the Central Valley Regional on Saturday.\nThe team's robot, nicknamed \"Biscuit,\" won 9 of its 11 qualifying matches before losing a tight final to Lincoln Prep, 112–104. Under competition rules, both finalists advance to state.\n\"We were down by thirty in the semifinal and Biscuit's arm jammed,\" said junior driver Owen Tran. \"Marisol basically rebuilt the gripper in the pit with zip ties in eight minutes. That's the whole season right there.\"\nSenior captain Marisol Vega said the team changed its strategy this year, focusing on a simpler robot that could score reliably rather than one that tried to do everything. \"Last year we built something really ambitious and it broke every other match,\" she said. \"This year we asked, what's the one thing we can do better than anyone?\"\nFaculty adviser Greg Pallas, who started the club in 2017 with five students, said he was \"proud but not surprised.\" The team now has 22 members.\nThe state championship will be held April 18–19 in Sacramento. The team estimates it needs about $2,400 for travel and registration and is holding a fundraiser at Pizza Garage on April 4, where 20 percent of sales will go to the team." },
  { kind: "synthesis", name: "polished AP Lang synthesis essay arguing libraries should drop overdue fines", text: "Fines and the Open Door\n\nLibraries were founded on a simple promise: knowledge should be available to everyone, regardless of what they can pay. Overdue fines, which most American libraries have charged for over a century, quietly break that promise. While fines may seem like a reasonable way to encourage responsibility, the evidence suggests that they do little to get books back on time and a great deal to keep the poorest patrons away. Public libraries should eliminate overdue fines.\n\nThe strongest case against fines is who they actually hurt. As Source C reports, in one large urban system nearly 40 percent of blocked cards belonged to patrons in the city's lowest-income neighborhoods, many of them children whose accounts were frozen over a few dollars. A child who owes $10 cannot check out another book, and a parent juggling rent may decide the library is not worth the risk. Source A, a statement from the American Library Association, calls fines \"a form of social inequity\" for this reason. The fine is the same for everyone, but its weight is not.\n\nSupporters of fines argue that without a penalty patrons will simply keep books forever. Source E, an op-ed by a library board member, warns that eliminating fines \"rewards carelessness\" and will leave shelves empty. Yet the libraries that have actually tried it tell a different story. According to the data in Source B, after one Midwestern library system went fine-free, return rates stayed essentially the same, and returns in the first month actually rose as patrons with long-overdue books felt safe coming back. What brings books back, it turns out, is not the threat of a quarter a day but the habit of using the library.\n\nFines also cost libraries more than they bring in. Source D notes that fine revenue makes up less than one percent of a typical library's budget, while staff spend significant time collecting payments, handling disputes and mailing notices. That is time not spent helping a patron fill out a job application or find a picture book. Source F, an interview with a branch librarian, describes the change after her library went fine-free as \"the end of being a bill collector.\"\n\nNone of this means libraries should give up on accountability. Lost items can still be billed at replacement cost, and borrowing can still be paused until a long-overdue book comes back. But the daily fine, which punishes poverty more than lateness, has outlived its purpose. A library that wants everyone to walk through its doors should stop charging them for the privilege of coming back." },
  { kind: "letter", name: "5th grader's thank-you letter to a retiring school bus driver", text: "Dear Mr. Fontaine,\nThank you for driving bus 14 for all these years. My mom says you drove her to school too when she was little which is crazy. I have been on your bus since kindergarten and you always say \"good morning sunshine\" even when it is raining.\nThank you for waiting for me that time in 2nd grade when I forgot my backpack and ran back in the house. And thank you for letting us play the license plate game. I am sorry about the time Jacob spilled chocolate milk on the seat.\nI hope you have a great time being retired. My mom said you like fishing so I hope you catch a really big fish. We will miss you a lot. The new driver is nice but she doesn't say good morning sunshine.\nLove,\nHarper Quinn\nRoom 5B" },
  { kind: "lessonplan", name: "detailed 11th grade American Lit lesson plan on Harlem Renaissance poetry, standards and timed procedure", text: "Lesson Plan: Voices of the Harlem Renaissance\nTeacher: Ms. Renee Adeyemi\nCourse: American Literature (Grade 11)\nDate: Tuesday, Feb. 10\nLength: 55 minutes\n\nStandards\nCCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RL.11-12.4 — Determine the meaning of words and phrases as they are used in the text, including figurative language; analyze the impact of word choices on meaning and tone.\nCCSS.ELA-LITERACY.SL.11-12.1 — Initiate and participate effectively in collaborative discussions.\n\nLearning objectives\nStudents will be able to:\n1. Describe at least two social or historical conditions that shaped the Harlem Renaissance.\n2. Analyze how Langston Hughes uses blues rhythm and repetition in \"The Weary Blues.\"\n3. Compare the tone of two poems from the period in a short written response.\n\nMaterials\n- Slides with images of 1920s Harlem (Aaron Douglas murals, Lenox Avenue photographs)\n- Audio: Langston Hughes reading \"The Weary Blues\" with jazz accompaniment (1958 recording)\n- Printed copies of \"The Weary Blues\" (Hughes) and \"If We Must Die\" (Claude McKay)\n- Highlighters, exit ticket slips\n\nProcedure\nDo Now (5 min): On the board: \"What does it mean for art to 'speak for' a group of people? Can it?\" Students write 3–4 sentences.\nMini-lecture (10 min): The Great Migration, the growth of Harlem as a cultural center, key figures (Hughes, Hurston, McKay, Cullen, Douglas). Keep it brief, the focus is the poems.\nListening and annotation (15 min): Play the Hughes recording twice. First listen: just listen. Second listen: students highlight repeated lines and any words that \"sound like music.\" Quick pair-share: what does the repetition do?\nSmall group comparison (15 min): Groups of 3–4 read \"If We Must Die.\" Guiding questions on the slide:\na. What is the speaker's tone? Point to specific words.\nb. How is it different from \"The Weary Blues\"?\nc. Both poems respond to the experience of Black Americans in the 1920s. Why might they sound so different?\nGroups report out one answer each.\nExit ticket (10 min): In one paragraph, compare the tone of the two poems using at least one quotation from each.\n\nDifferentiation\n- ELL students receive a glossary of period terms (e.g., \"Lenox Avenue,\" \"syncopated\") and may complete the exit ticket with a sentence frame.\n- Students who finish early can begin reading Hurston's \"How It Feels to Be Colored Me\" (next class).\n\nAssessment\nFormative: annotations, group report-out, exit ticket (checked for use of textual evidence, not graded).\n\nHomework\nRead \"How It Feels to Be Colored Me\" and annotate three passages where Hurston's tone shifts." },
  { kind: "code", name: "intro programming Python script that averages grades from a CSV, with TODOs", text: "# grades.py\n# Reads grades.csv and prints each student's average and letter grade\n# Intro to Programming - Unit 5 project\n# by Tomas Herrera\n\nimport csv\n\ndef letter_grade(avg):\n    if avg >= 90:\n        return \"A\"\n    elif avg >= 80:\n        return \"B\"\n    elif avg >= 70:\n        return \"C\"\n    elif avg >= 60:\n        return \"D\"\n    else:\n        return \"F\"\n\ndef main():\n    students = {}\n    with open(\"grades.csv\", newline=\"\") as f:\n        reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n        for row in reader:\n            name = row[\"name\"]\n            score = float(row[\"score\"])\n            if name not in students:\n                students[name] = []\n            students[name].append(score)\n\n    # print results sorted by name\n    for name in sorted(students):\n        scores = students[name]\n        avg = sum(scores) / len(scores)\n        print(f\"{name:<15} {avg:6.1f}  {letter_grade(avg)}\")\n\n    # TODO: class average + highest/lowest\n    # TODO: crashes if a score is blank, fix this\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()" },
  { kind: "proposal", name: "7th grade science fair proposal form on light color and radish sprouting", text: "Science Fair Project Proposal\nName: Ava Lindqvist\nGrade 7, Mr. Haddad's class\nDue: Oct 3\nTitle: Does the Color of Light Change How Fast Radish Seeds Sprout?\nQuestion: Will radish seeds sprout faster under red, blue, green or white light?\nWhy I chose this: My grandpa grows vegetables in his basement with purple grow lights and I wanted to know if the color actually matters or if it's just for looks.\nHypothesis: I think the seeds under blue light will sprout fastest because I read that blue light helps plants grow leaves.\nVariables\n- Independent variable: color of light\n- Dependent variable: number of days until the seeds sprout, and how many sprout\n- Controlled variables: type of seed, amount of water, soil, temperature, distance from the light, hours of light per day\nMaterials\n- 4 LED bulbs (red, blue, green, white) - borrowing from my grandpa\n- 4 desk lamps\n- 40 radish seeds\n- 4 plastic trays with potting soil\n- a cardboard box divided into 4 sections so the light doesn't mix\n- ruler, spray bottle, notebook\nProcedure\n1. Plant 10 seeds in each tray at the same depth (1 cm).\n2. Put each tray in its own section of the box under a different color light, 20 cm away.\n3. Turn the lights on for 12 hours a day with a timer.\n4. Spray each tray with the same amount of water every morning.\n5. Check every day and write down how many seeds have sprouted.\n6. Do this for 10 days.\nSafety: The lamps could get hot so I will use LED bulbs and have an adult check the setup.\nTimeline: Set up Oct 10, collect data Oct 10-20, make graphs and board Oct 21-30.\nTeacher approval: ________" },
  { kind: "problem", name: "polished college composition essay on rural pharmacy closures weighing three solutions", text: "Pharmacy Deserts and the Limits of the Quick Fix\n\nWhen the only pharmacy in Harlan Springs closed in the spring of 2024, the town's 3,100 residents did not lose a store so much as a piece of infrastructure. The nearest replacement was thirty-eight miles away, across a mountain road that floods twice a year. Within months, the county health department reported that refills of blood pressure and diabetes medication had fallen by nearly a fifth. The pattern is not unique to one town. Independent and chain pharmacies alike have been closing in rural and low-income urban areas for more than a decade, squeezed by falling reimbursement rates set by pharmacy benefit managers and by the simple arithmetic of low prescription volume. The result is what researchers call a pharmacy desert: a community where residents cannot reasonably reach a pharmacist.\n\nThe consequences go beyond inconvenience. A pharmacist is often the most accessible health professional in a small town, the person who catches a dangerous drug interaction or notices that a patient has stopped picking up insulin. Without that contact, missed doses become emergency room visits, and emergency room visits become costs borne by everyone.\n\nThree remedies are most often proposed. The first is mail-order delivery, which many insurers already encourage. It is cheap and scalable, but it assumes a stable address, reliable postal service and a patient who can plan a month ahead; it also removes the pharmacist's face-to-face role entirely. The second is telepharmacy, in which a pharmacy technician staffs a small site while a licensed pharmacist supervises remotely by video. Several states, North Dakota first among them, have used this model for years with good safety records, though it requires changes to licensing law that many state pharmacy boards have been slow to make. The third is direct subsidy: state or county money to keep a struggling pharmacy open, usually tied to a minimum number of hours.\n\nOf these, telepharmacy offers the best balance. Mail order is a supplement, not a replacement, and subsidies tend to prop up a business model that has already failed without addressing why it failed. Telepharmacy, by contrast, lowers the cost of operating a site enough that a town of three thousand can support one, while keeping a human being at the counter. The obstacle is not technology but regulation. States that have not yet authorized remote supervision should do so, with clear rules on technician training and on how quickly a pharmacist must be reachable. It is a modest legislative change, and for towns like Harlan Springs it would mean the difference between a thirty-eight-mile drive and a five-minute one." },
  { kind: "news", name: "local newspaper report on a downtown water main break, AP style", text: "Water main break floods Front Street, closes businesses\nBy Thomas Gerhardt\nThe Millbrook Courier\n\nMILLBROOK — A 16-inch water main burst beneath Front Street early Tuesday, flooding two blocks of downtown, cutting water service to about 400 customers and forcing at least a dozen businesses to close for the day.\n\nCity Public Works Director Angela Moss said crews were called to the intersection of Front and Third streets at about 4:45 a.m. after a passing police officer reported water \"coming up through the asphalt like a fountain.\" Crews shut off the main by 6:30 a.m., but not before water had pooled up to a foot deep in places and seeped into the basements of several buildings.\n\n\"The pipe in that section dates to 1931,\" Moss said. \"It's on our replacement list. We were planning to get to it in 2028.\"\n\nFront Street will remain closed between Second and Fourth streets at least through Thursday while crews repair the pipe and the damaged road surface. Detours are posted along Elm Street.\n\nCustomers on Front, Second and Third streets were without water for most of the day. Service was restored to most by 5 p.m., though the city issued a precautionary boil-water notice for the affected area that will remain in effect until test results come back, likely Thursday.\n\nLena Castillo, owner of Castillo's Bakery at 214 Front St., said she lost a full morning's baking. \"We had the ovens going at four, and by five-thirty there was water under the door,\" she said. \"We'll be fine, but it's a hit.\"\n\nThe City Council is scheduled to discuss speeding up its water infrastructure plan at its Oct. 27 meeting. Residents with property damage can file a claim with the city clerk's office at City Hall." },
  { kind: "synthesis", name: "average timed AP Lang synthesis on the four-day school week, some usage errors", text: "Brianna Torres\nAP Lang - Timed Write #3\nSynthesis: four day school week\n\nMany school districts in the US have switched to a four day school week, mostly in rural areas. Supporters say it saves money and helps teachers but others say it hurts students learning. In my opinion school districts should be very careful before switching to a four day week because the downsides for students and families are bigger then the benefits.\nThe main reason districts do this is to save money. Source B says districts save on things like transportation, heating and cooling, and staff. But Source D shows the savings are actually pretty small, usually only around 1-2% of the budget. If a district is going to change the whole schedule for every family it should save more then 2 percent.\nAnother argument is that it helps teachers. According to Source A, some districts use the four day week to attract teachers because they can't pay as much as bigger districts. This makes sense, an extra day off is a big benefit. However, Source E says studies found students in four day weeks scored a little lower in math and reading over time. If the point of school is learning then this is a problem.\nAlso you have to think about families. Source C describes a single mom who has to pay for childcare on Fridays now, which costs her more money. Not every family can have a parent at home on the extra day, and younger kids can't stay home alone. Some districts offer programs on the fifth day but not all of them.\nOn the other hand Source F says students in one district liked the schedule and attendance went up because kids could schedule doctor appointments on Fridays instead of missing school. So it is not all bad.\nIn conclusion a four day school week might work for some districts but most should keep five days. The money saved is small and students and families are the ones who pay the price." },
  { kind: "letter", name: "formal complaint letter to a property manager about broken heat, adult ESL writer", text: "Elena Voronova\n1180 Birchwood Avenue, Apt. 3C\nColumbus, OH 43215\n\nNovember 18, 2026\n\nGreenfield Property Management\nAttn: Mr. Robert Haines, Property Manager\n400 Commerce Street, Suite 210\nColumbus, OH 43215\n\nRe: Heating not working in Apartment 3C – third request\n\nDear Mr. Haines,\n\nI am writing to you about the heating in my apartment, which is not working since October 29. This is the third time I am reporting this problem. I called the office on October 30 and on November 6, and both times I was told that a technician will come \"in a few days.\" Until today, nobody came.\n\nThe temperature in my apartment in the night is now 54 degrees. I live with my son, who is seven years old and has asthma, and his doctor said that cold air is making his breathing worse. We are using a small electric heater, but it is not enough for two rooms and I worry that it is not safe to use all night. Also my electricity bill is much higher because of it.\n\nAccording to my lease (section 9), the landlord is responsible for keeping the heating in working condition. I also understand that Ohio law requires landlords to make the repairs which are necessary to keep the apartment in fit and habitable condition.\n\nI am asking you to please send a technician to repair the heating no later than November 22. I also ask that you reduce my December rent by the amount of the extra electricity cost, which I can show you with my bills.\n\nI have always paid my rent on time for four years and I would like to solve this problem in a good way. If the heating is not repaired by November 22, I will contact the city code enforcement office.\n\nYou can reach me at 614-555-0187 or by email.\n\nSincerely,\n\nElena Voronova" },
  { kind: "lessonplan", name: "2nd grade math lesson plan on three-digit place value with base-ten blocks", text: "Grade 2 Math – Place Value to 1,000\nMrs. Kowalski\nWeek of 10/20, Day 2\nObjective: Students will represent three-digit numbers using hundreds, tens and ones with base-ten blocks and write them in expanded form.\nStandard: 2.NBT.A.1\nVocabulary: hundreds, tens, ones, digit, value, expanded form\nMaterials:\n* base-ten blocks (one bag per pair)\n* place value mats\n* whiteboards + markers\n* \"Build It\" task cards (in green folder)\nWarm-up (5 min)\nCount by 10s from 40 to 200 as a class. Then count by 100s to 1,000. Clap on every hundred.\nHook (5 min)\nShow the giant jar of cubes. \"How could we count these faster than one at a time?\" Take ideas. Lead to grouping in tens and hundreds.\nI do (10 min)\nModel 243 on the document camera: 2 flats, 4 rods, 3 units. Think aloud: \"The 2 is in the hundreds place so it means 200, not 2.\" Write 200 + 40 + 3. Do one more: 306 (point out the zero tens!).\nWe do (10 min)\nCall out numbers. Partners build on their mats, hold up. Check, then write expanded form on whiteboards.\nNumbers: 152, 418, 290, 507\nYou do (15 min)\nPartners pick task cards and build/record. Early finishers: \"Mystery number\" cards (I have 3 hundreds, no tens, and 8 ones. What number am I?)\nPull small group at back table: Ethan, Sofia R., Malik, June — still mixing up tens and ones. Start with 2-digit numbers only, then add hundreds.\nClosing (5 min)\nShow 470 and 407 side by side. \"Are these the same? Why not?\" Turn and talk.\nAssessment: exit slip — build and write 365 in expanded form. Collect and sort for tomorrow's groups." },
  { kind: "code", name: "JavaScript to-do list with DOM handling and localStorage", text: "// app.js — simple to-do list\n// adds tasks, marks done, deletes, saves to localStorage\n\nconst form = document.getElementById(\"todo-form\");\nconst input = document.getElementById(\"todo-input\");\nconst list = document.getElementById(\"todo-list\");\n\nlet todos = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"todos\")) || [];\n\nfunction save() {\n  localStorage.setItem(\"todos\", JSON.stringify(todos));\n}\n\nfunction render() {\n  list.innerHTML = \"\";\n  todos.forEach((todo, i) => {\n    const li = document.createElement(\"li\");\n    li.textContent = todo.text;\n    if (todo.done) li.classList.add(\"done\");\n\n    li.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n      todos[i].done = !todos[i].done;\n      save();\n      render();\n    });\n\n    const del = document.createElement(\"button\");\n    del.textContent = \"x\";\n    del.addEventListener(\"click\", (e) => {\n      e.stopPropagation(); // don't toggle done when deleting\n      todos.splice(i, 1);\n      save();\n      render();\n    });\n\n    li.appendChild(del);\n    list.appendChild(li);\n  });\n}\n\nform.addEventListener(\"submit\", (e) => {\n  e.preventDefault();\n  const text = input.value.trim();\n  if (!text) return;\n  todos.push({ text, done: false });\n  input.value = \"\";\n  save();\n  render();\n});\n\nrender();" },
  { kind: "proposal", name: "undergraduate sociology senior thesis proposal on delivery workers' sleep, formal sections and budget table", text: "Senior Thesis Proposal\nDepartment of Sociology\nPriya Natarajan\nAdvisor: Dr. Elena Ruiz\nSubmitted: September 22, 2026\n\nWorking title: \"Always On\": Algorithmic Incentives and Sleep Among App-Based Delivery Workers\n\n1. Background and significance\nApp-based delivery work is often marketed as flexible: couriers log in when they want and log off when they want. Yet a growing body of qualitative research suggests that this flexibility is shaped by the platforms themselves, through surge pay at late hours, incentive \"quests\" that reward long consecutive shifts, and acceptance-rate thresholds that penalize workers who decline orders. Little work has examined what these incentives mean for workers' sleep, despite strong evidence linking short and irregular sleep to injury risk, especially for people who spend their shifts on bicycles and scooters in traffic. This project asks whether, and how, platform incentive structures shape when and how much delivery workers sleep.\n\n2. Research questions\nRQ1. How do app-based delivery workers in a mid-sized city describe the relationship between platform incentives and their sleep schedules?\nRQ2. Do workers who rely on delivery work as their primary income report shorter or more irregular sleep than those who do it as a side job?\nRQ3. What strategies do workers use to protect their sleep, and how effective do they find them?\n\n3. Methods\nI will use a sequential mixed-methods design. In phase one, I will recruit 60 to 80 delivery workers through flyers at restaurant pickup areas and two worker-run online forums, and ask them to complete a short survey including the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index and questions about hours worked, platforms used and share of income from delivery. In phase two, I will conduct semi-structured interviews of 45 to 60 minutes with 12 to 15 survey participants, selected to vary by platform, income dependence and vehicle type. Interviews will be recorded with consent, transcribed and coded thematically in NVivo.\n\n4. Ethical considerations\nBecause some participants may be working on shared or borrowed accounts, I will not collect names, account information or immigration status. The protocol will be submitted to the IRB in October; no recruitment will begin before approval.\n\n5. Timeline\nOctober: IRB submission, finalize survey instrument\nNovember–December: survey recruitment and data collection\nJanuary–February: interviews and transcription\nMarch: coding and analysis\nApril: write-up; full draft to advisor April 10; defense late April\n\n6. Budget\nItem\tCost\nInterview incentives (15 x $25 gift cards)\t$375\nTranscription software (3 months)\t$45\nFlyer printing\t$20\nTotal\t$440\n\nFunding will be requested from the department's Undergraduate Research Fund." },
  { kind: "problem", name: "6th grader's short essay on litter at the skate park", text: "The Skate Park Trash Problem\nby Mateo R.\nThe skate park on Pine Street has a big trash problem. There are chip bags and soda cans everywhere and sometimes broken glass which is dangerous if you fall. My friend Eli cut his hand on a bottle last month and had to get stitches.\nThe reason there is so much trash is because there is only one trash can and it is all the way by the parking lot. Nobody wants to walk over there. Also the trash can is always full because nobody empties it enough.\nMy solution is to put two more trash cans right next to the bowls and the rails. Another solution is to have a clean up day once a month where skaters come and pick up trash and maybe get free pizza. I think if people helped clean it they would care more about keeping it clean. The city should do both of these things so the park is safe for everyone." },
  { kind: "news", name: "middle school newspaper article on a split lunch schedule, weak writer with typos", text: "NEW LUNCH SCHEDULE STARTS MONDAY\n\nby Brayden Cole, 7th grade\n\nStarting next Monday (Nov. 3) lunch at Oak Ridge Middle School is going to be different. Instead of every grade eating at the same time, there will be three lunches. 6th grade eats first at 10:50, 7th grade at 11:30 and 8th grade at 12:10.\n\nPrincipal Dr. Simmons said the reason is that the cafeteria was too crowded. \"We had almost 600 students in a room that is made for about 350,\" she said in the announcments on Friday. \"It wasn't safe and students weren't having enough time to eat.\"\n\nSome students are not happy about it. 8th grader Zoe Patel said \"12:10 is way to late, I'm going to be starving by then.\" Other students like it. \"Now I can actually find a seat,\" said 6th grader Liam Ng.\n\nTeachers will also have to change there schedules because classes will be split up by lunch. Mrs. Graham said \"it will be confusing for the first week but we'll figure it out.\"\n\nThe office said if you have questions you can ask your homeroom teacher." },
  { kind: "synthesis", name: "weak 11th grade synthesis essay on zoos with thin use of the sources", text: "Zoos\nZoos are a place where people can go to see animals from around the world. But some people think zoos are bad and should be closed. I think zoos are good if they treat the animals right.\nFirst zoos help endangered animals. Source A says that zoos have saved animals like the California condor from going extinct. If there were no zoos those animals would be gone forever. This shows that zoos are important.\nSecond, zoos teach people about animals. Kids can go to the zoo and see a real elephant instead of just on tv (Source D). This makes them care about animals more and maybe want to help them.\nBut some zoos are not good. Source B talks about how elephants in zoos get stressed and walk back and forth all day. That is sad and those zoos should be fixed or shut down.\nIn conclusion zoos are good because they save animals and teach people but they have to treat the animals right." },
  { kind: "letter", name: "8th grader's letter to her future self for an end-of-year assignment", text: "Dear Future Me (age 18),\n\nHi. It's me, you, in 8th grade. Mrs. Carver is making us write these and she says she'll mail them to us when we graduate high school, so if you are reading this then I guess she actually did it.\n\nRight now it's May 2026 and I'm 14. I'm 5'4 (hopefully you grew). My best friends are Tasha and Ren. If you're not friends with them anymore I hope it's for a good reason and not because of something dumb. I'm on the volleyball team and we lost to Jefferson in the semifinals, which still hurts. My favorite show is Avatar and I've watched it like four times.\n\nSome questions for you:\n1. Did you make varsity?\n2. Did you ever learn how to drive without freaking out?\n3. Do you still want to be a vet or did that change?\n4. Is Grandma Rose still doing okay?\n\nSomething I'm worried about right now is starting high school and not knowing anybody in my classes. You probably think that's funny now. Also I want you to remember that you were really nervous about the science fair this year and you ended up getting second place, so stop worrying so much about stuff before it happens.\n\nI hope you're happy and that you're nice to Mom even when she's annoying. Don't forget where you came from (that sounds so cheesy, sorry).\n\nLove,\nMe (Kayla, 14)\n\nP.S. If you have a dog by now, name it Pickles." },
  { kind: "lessonplan", name: "student teacher's rough osmosis lesson draft with the egg demo and a mentor comment, no timings", text: "Bio – osmosis lesson (draft for Mr. Huerta to look at)\nperiod 3 + 5, ~50 min\nStudent teacher: J. Albrecht\n\ngoal: students can explain osmosis as water moving across a semi-permeable membrane from high to low water concentration (and predict which way water moves in hypertonic / hypotonic / isotonic)\n\nmaterials\n- the 3 eggs I soaked in vinegar over the weekend (shells gone) → 1 in corn syrup, 1 in distilled water, 1 in saltwater?? (do I have time for 3? maybe just 2)\n- scale\n- worksheet (still need to make)\n- gummy bears for stations?? ask Mr H if we have budget\n\nplan\nstart - show the naked egg, pass it around (gloves!) - ask what happened to the shell. quick review of cell membrane from last week\nthen predict: what will happen if we put one in syrup and one in water. write predictions on board\nreveal the ones I set up yesterday - weigh them, compare to starting mass (wrote it down, it's in my notebook)\nnotes - hypertonic/hypotonic/isotonic. use the \"water goes where the salt is\" trick? or is that misleading. I don't want them to think the salt is moving\npractice - worksheet w/ diagrams of cells in different solutions, they draw arrows\nexit q: why does a slug shrivel up when you put salt on it?\n\n[MR. HUERTA COMMENT: Good start. Your timing is missing — that notes section will take longer than you think. Also make the egg reveal the hook, not the middle. I'd cut the saltwater egg and show it as a video for homework.]" },
  { kind: "code", name: "AP CSA Java BankAccount class with a heading block and a short explanation above the code", text: "Rohan Mehta\nAP Computer Science A – Period 6\nLab 4.2: BankAccount\nOct 14\n\nFor this lab I wrote a BankAccount class with a constructor, deposit and withdraw methods, and a method that applies monthly interest. The withdraw method returns false instead of letting the balance go negative. I tested it in BankTester.java (not pasted here).\n\npublic class BankAccount\n{\n    private String owner;\n    private double balance;\n    private double interestRate; // annual, ex. 0.03 for 3%\n\n    public BankAccount(String owner, double startingBalance, double interestRate)\n    {\n        this.owner = owner;\n        this.balance = startingBalance;\n        this.interestRate = interestRate;\n    }\n\n    public void deposit(double amount)\n    {\n        if (amount > 0)\n        {\n            balance += amount;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public boolean withdraw(double amount)\n    {\n        if (amount > 0 && amount <= balance)\n        {\n            balance -= amount;\n            return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    public void addMonthlyInterest()\n    {\n        balance += balance * (interestRate / 12);\n    }\n\n    public double getBalance()\n    {\n        return balance;\n    }\n\n    public String toString()\n    {\n        return owner + \": $\" + String.format(\"%.2f\", balance);\n    }\n}" },
  { kind: "proposal", name: "high school senior's informal capstone proposal for a seed library at the public library", text: "Capstone Proposal – Noor Haddad\n\nWhat I want to do:\nFor my senior capstone I want to start a seed library at the Eastfield branch public library. A seed library works like a regular library except instead of books you \"check out\" seeds. People take seed packets for free, grow the plants, and then at the end of the season they save some seeds from their plants and bring them back so other people can use them next year.\n\nWhy:\nEastfield doesn't have a grocery store with good produce (the closest one is the Kroger on Route 9) and a lot of families in my neighborhood have yards or balconies but don't garden because seeds and starter plants are expensive. I've been volunteering at the community garden on Mill St since 9th grade and the garden coordinator, Mrs. Abebe, said people ask her for seeds all the time. I also want to learn how to actually run something from start to finish, which is the point of capstone.\n\nHow:\nI already talked to the branch manager, Mr. Ortiz, and he said I can use an old card catalog cabinet they have in storage (!!). My plan is:\n1. Get seed donations from local nurseries and a couple of seed companies that donate to seed libraries\n2. Sort and label them into easy/medium/hard with growing instructions in English and Spanish\n3. Hold a launch event in March with a planting workshop\n4. Hold a seed saving workshop in September\n5. Track how many packets get checked out and how many come back\n\nWhat I need:\n- Envelopes, labels, printing (around $60, the library might cover it)\n- Seed donations\n- Someone to teach seed saving - Mrs. Abebe said she would do it\n\nMentors: Mrs. Abebe (community garden) and Mr. Ortiz (library)\n\nHow I'll know it worked: If at least 200 packets get checked out by June and some come back in the fall. Even if not many come back the first year I think getting people to grow food is a win." },
  { kind: "problem", name: "ESL college student's essay on motorbike traffic in Hanoi", text: "Traffic Jam Problem in Hanoi and How to Solve It\nHanoi is the capital of Vietnam and it is my hometown. It is a beautiful city with many lakes and old streets, but every person who live in Hanoi will tell you the same thing: the traffic is terrible. In the rush hour, from 7 to 9 in the morning and from 5 to 7 in the evening, the main roads become a sea of motorbikes. It can take one hour to go only five kilometers. My mother is a teacher and she must leave home at 6 o'clock to arrive school on time, even the school is not far.\nThere are many reasons for this problem. First, the city has more than six million motorbikes and the number is increase every year. Second, the roads in the old quarter are very narrow because they were built hundred years ago, so they cannot be wider. Third, the public bus is not convenient. The buses are often late and they also stuck in the same traffic, so people prefer to use their own motorbike.\nThe government has some solutions. One is the metro. The first metro line opened in 2021 and another one in 2024, and many people use it now, but it is still only few lines and does not go everywhere. I think the city should build more lines faster, because the metro does not use the road so it does not get traffic jam.\nAnother solution is to limit motorbikes in the city center. The city has plan to ban motorbikes in some districts in the future. Some people angry about this because motorbike is their job, for example delivery drivers and xe om drivers. So if the city want to ban, they must make the public transport good first, otherwise people cannot go to work.\nIn my opinion the best way is to combine. Build more metro, make more lanes only for bus, and after that slowly limit the motorbike. It will take a long time but I hope when I come back home after my study, the traffic in Hanoi will be better than now." },
  { kind: "news", name: "college newspaper report on the student senate approving an activity fee increase", text: "Student Senate approves $18 activity fee increase in 14-9 vote\nFee would rise to $96 per semester next fall if regents sign off\nBy Rafael Ocampo | News Editor\n\nAfter nearly three hours of debate Wednesday night, the Associated Students Senate voted 14-9 to raise the student activity fee from $78 to $96 per semester, the first increase since 2019.\n\nThe proposal, introduced by Finance Committee Chair Imani Brooks, would generate an estimated $410,000 a year. According to the committee's budget summary, about half would go to expanding late-night shuttle service to off-campus housing, with the rest split among registered student organizations, the campus food pantry, and Homecoming and spring concert programming.\n\n\"Clubs have been running on the same budget for seven years while costs have gone up across the board,\" Brooks said during the meeting. \"We're asking students to invest in things they've told us, in survey after survey, that they want.\"\n\nOpponents argued the increase would land hardest on students already struggling with tuition and housing costs. Sen. Marcus Yee, who represents the College of Engineering, said he had heard from \"dozens\" of constituents who opposed it. \"Eighteen dollars doesn't sound like much until you're choosing between that and groceries,\" Yee said. His amendment capping the increase at $10 failed 8-15.\n\nAbout 40 students attended, several of whom spoke during public comment. Most who spoke supported the shuttle expansion; two members of the Commuter Student Association said commuters would pay the fee without benefiting from the shuttle.\n\nThe increase must still be approved by the university's Board of Regents, which meets Nov. 14. If approved, it would take effect in fall 2027.\n\nAssociated Students President Dana Kowalczyk said she would sign the measure. \"This was a hard vote and people disagreed in good faith,\" she said. \"That's how it should work.\"" },
  { kind: "synthesis", name: "AP Lang synthesis essay on space exploration funding by an English-language learner", text: "Is Space Exploration Worth the Money?\n\nEvery year, United States spend billions of dollars for NASA and space exploration. Many people ask if this money should be use for problems on Earth like poverty and climate change instead. In my opinion, government should continue to fund space exploration, but it must also make sure the benefit come back to people on Earth.\n\nFirst, space exploration create many technology that we use every day. Source B lists some examples, like water filter, memory foam, and the camera in our phone, which came from NASA research. Without space program, maybe these inventions come much later. Also Source E explain that satellites help farmers to know the weather and help scientists to watch the climate change. So space money is not only for space, it also help Earth.\n\nSecond, the cost is not so big as people think. According to Source A, NASA budget is less than half of one percent of the federal budget. People think it is very big because the news always talk about rockets, but actually the government spend much more money for other things.\n\nHowever, some sources show the other side. Source C, a poem by a writer from a poor neighborhood, say it is not fair to send people to the moon when some children do not have enough food. I understand this feeling. Also Source F says private company like SpaceX now do many things that NASA did before, so maybe government do not need to spend so much.\n\nBut I think private companies only care about the profit, so government still need to do the science that do not make money, like study other planets. Source D says the James Webb telescope teach us about the beginning of universe, which no company will pay for.\n\nIn conclusion, space exploration is worth the money because it give us technology, knowledge, and it is not too expensive. But the government should explain better to the people how it help their life, so they will support it." },
  { kind: "letter", name: "casual letter to an older brother deployed with the Navy, no formal greeting or structure", text: "Marcus —\nok so mom said the mail gets to the ship faster if we write actual letters instead of the email thing because the email only works sometimes, so here's an actual letter. you're welcome.\nNothing huge here. Dad finally fixed the garage door after like three weekends of swearing at it. Tank ate one of your old socks out of the box in your room and we had to take him to the vet but he's fine, he threw it up in the car on the way there which was disgusting. I got my license!!! First try. Mom made me drive her to Costco as a \"test\" and gripped the door handle the whole time.\nSchool is fine. I'm taking chem and it's kicking my butt. Mr. Varga says hi, he asked about you when he found out I was your sister. I told him you're on a destroyer and he said \"of course he is\" which I think was a compliment??\nMom is doing better than when you first left. She still checks the news every morning though. Grandpa keeps asking if you've \"seen any action\" and we keep telling him you mostly fix radar stuff.\nI don't know what else. We all miss you. Your room smells weird now, Dad says it's because nobody opens the window. Write back when you can, even if it's short. And send a picture where you're actually smiling this time.\nlove you, don't do anything stupid\n— Nia" },
  { kind: "lessonplan", name: "Spanish I lesson plan on ordering at a restaurant, headings in Spanish", text: "Plan de lección – Español I\nUnidad 4: ¡A comer!\nLección: En el restaurante\nProfe: Sr. Diego Bautista\nDuración: 50 minutos\n\nObjetivos (students will be able to):\n1. Order food and drinks using \"Quisiera…\" and \"Me gustaría…\"\n2. Ask for the check and ask about prices (¿Cuánto cuesta…?)\n3. Use at least 8 new food vocabulary words in a role-play\n\nMateriales\n● Menú del \"Café Sabroso\" (printed, one per pair)\n● Vocab flashcards (la cuenta, el mesero / la mesera, el plato principal, el postre, la bebida, etc.)\n● Video clip: ordering at a café in Madrid (2:10)\n● Play money (euros)\n\nCalentamiento (5 min)\n¿Qué comiste ayer? — students answer with one food word they already know. Write on board.\n\nPresentación (10 min)\nIntroduce new vocab with flashcards and gestures. Choral repetition. Point out \"Quisiera\" as the polite form, compare to \"Quiero\" (which can sound demanding to a waiter).\n\nVideo (8 min)\nWatch the café clip once with no subtitles, once with Spanish subtitles. Students check off phrases they hear on the handout.\n\nPráctica guiada (10 min)\nModel a dialogue with a student volunteer, I am el mesero. Then pairs practice the scripted dialogue on page 2 of the handout, switching roles.\n\nRole-play (12 min)\nPairs get a menu and play money. Mesero/a takes the order, cliente orders a full meal (bebida, plato principal, postre) and asks for la cuenta. Clientes have a budget of 20 euros, so they have to ask prices to stay under. I circulate and listen with the speaking checklist.\n\nCierre (5 min)\n2 volunteer pairs perform. Exit ticket: write how you would ask for the check and how you would order a drink.\n\nModificaciones\nHeritage speakers: play the mesero and add a \"problem\" (no hay paella hoy, la sopa está fría) to push their partner.\nStudents with IEPs: dialogue frame with word bank.\n\nTarea: Cuaderno p. 88, ejercicios A y B." },
  { kind: "code", name: "college databases homework, five commented SQL queries on an animal shelter schema", text: "-- CIS 310 Homework 3: Shelter database queries\n-- Ines Carvalho\n\n-- 1. All dogs currently available for adoption, newest intake first\nSELECT animal_id, name, breed, intake_date\nFROM animals\nWHERE species = 'Dog'\n  AND status = 'Available'\nORDER BY intake_date DESC;\n\n-- 2. Number of adoptions per month in 2025\nSELECT EXTRACT(MONTH FROM adoption_date) AS month,\n       COUNT(*) AS adoptions\nFROM adoptions\nWHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM adoption_date) = 2025\nGROUP BY EXTRACT(MONTH FROM adoption_date)\nORDER BY month;\n\n-- 3. Animals that have been in the shelter more than 90 days\nSELECT a.name, a.species, CURRENT_DATE - a.intake_date AS days_in_shelter\nFROM animals a\nWHERE a.status = 'Available'\n  AND CURRENT_DATE - a.intake_date > 90\nORDER BY days_in_shelter DESC;\n\n-- 4. Adopters who adopted more than one animal\nSELECT p.first_name, p.last_name, COUNT(ad.animal_id) AS total\nFROM adopters p\nJOIN adoptions ad ON p.adopter_id = ad.adopter_id\nGROUP BY p.adopter_id, p.first_name, p.last_name\nHAVING COUNT(ad.animal_id) > 1;\n\n-- 5. Average length of stay by species (adopted animals only)\n-- not sure this is right, the numbers look high\nSELECT a.species, AVG(ad.adoption_date - a.intake_date) AS avg_days\nFROM animals a\nJOIN adoptions ad ON a.animal_id = ad.animal_id\nGROUP BY a.species;" },
  { kind: "proposal", name: "middle school teacher's mini-grant application for a classroom podcast studio, with budget table", text: "Riverside Education Foundation — Classroom Innovation Mini-Grant\nApplicant: Dana Whitcomb, 8th Grade ELA, Lakeview Middle School\nAmount requested: $1,110\n\nProject title: Lakeview Listens: A Student Podcast Studio\n\nProject summary\nI am requesting funds to set up a small podcast recording station in my classroom, so that my 8th graders can research, script, record and publish short audio pieces about people and places in our community. Over the spring semester, each of my four sections will produce a six-episode series, and selected episodes will be shared on the school website and played during morning announcements.\n\nNeed\nMany of my students who struggle to put their ideas on paper are articulate and funny out loud. Podcasting gives them a reason to write (every episode starts as a script), to revise (they hear exactly where a sentence falls flat), and to interview adults outside school. Right now we have no microphones, and students recording on phones in a noisy hallway get unusable audio.\n\nActivities and timeline\nJanuary: set up the station; mini-lessons on interviewing and scripting\nFebruary: students choose topics and arrange interviews\nMarch: recording and editing in small groups\nApril: publish episodes; listening party for families\n\nBudget\n2 USB microphones with stands\t$260\n2 pop filters\t$30\n2 pairs closed-back headphones\t$140\nAcoustic foam panels\t$90\nPortable recorder for field interviews\t$240\nRolling cart\t$150\nRelease forms, printing\t$40\nShipping and tax (est.)\t$160\nEditing software (Audacity)\t$0\nTotal\t$1,110\n\nEvaluation\nStudents will be assessed with a rubric covering research, script quality, interviewing and audio production. I will also survey students at the start and end of the project about their confidence in speaking and writing. Success means every student contributes to at least one published episode.\n\nSustainability\nAll equipment stays in Room 214 and will be used by future classes and by the school's new media club." },
  { kind: "problem", name: "unfinished problem-solution draft on light pollution with headings, bullet solutions and bracketed notes to self", text: "Light Pollution: Losing the Night Sky (DRAFT 2)\n[intro needs a hook - maybe the thing about the LA blackout in 1994 when people called 911 about the \"weird cloud\" which was the milky way?? check if this is real]\nMost people living in cities today have never seen the Milky Way. [stat: % of Americans who can't see it - I think its like 80%] This isn't because the stars went anywhere. It's because of light pollution, the excess artificial light from streetlights, parking lots, billboards and buildings that brightens the night sky and washes out everything but the moon and a few planets.\nThe problem\nLight pollution is more than just an issue for astronomers. Migrating birds use the stars to navigate and get drawn toward lit up buildings, where millions of them die from collisions every year. Sea turtle hatchlings crawl toward streetlights instead of the ocean. For humans, too much light at night has been linked to bad sleep because it suppresses melatonin [find source - Ms. D said the AMA put out a statement?]. And it's a waste of money, since a lot of outdoor lighting points up or sideways where nobody needs it.\nSolutions\n* Shielded fixtures: lights with a cover on top so they only point down. Cheap and works right away.\n* Warmer bulbs. Blue-white LEDs scatter more in the atmosphere than amber ones [explain this better]\n* Motion sensors and timers for parking lots and businesses so they aren't lit all night when nobody's there\n* Dark sky ordinances like Flagstaff AZ has had since 1958 (first in the world?)\n[which solution is best?? I think ordinances bc they make everyone do the other ones. need a paragraph on cost / pushback - businesses say they need light for security, is that true?]\nConclusion\nTBD" },
  { kind: "news", name: "short campus news brief on a black bear sighting with an update line and a correction (atypical)", text: "Bear spotted near North Campus dorms; no injuries reported\nA black bear was seen near Halvorsen Hall early Monday morning, according to an alert sent by University Police at 6:12 a.m.\nPolice said the bear, estimated at about 150 pounds, was seen going through a dumpster behind the residence hall before heading into the woods along Cedar Creek. No one was hurt.\nState wildlife officials say bear sightings are common this time of year as the animals look for food before winter. Police asked students not to approach bears, to keep food out of cars and to report sightings to 555-0142.\nUPDATE, 2:40 p.m.: Police said a bear, likely the same one, was seen again near the Cedar Creek trailhead around noon. Facilities will begin locking dumpster lids on North Campus Tuesday.\nCorrection: An earlier version of this story said the bear was seen on Sunday night. The first sighting was Monday morning." },
  { kind: "synthesis", name: "unfinished timed synthesis essay on tipping culture that stops mid-sentence (atypical)", text: "AP Lang practice synthesis — tipping\n(ran out of time, 40 min)\n\nWhen you buy a coffee now, the screen spins around and asks for 18, 20 or 25 percent before you've even gotten your drink. \"Tipflation,\" as Source D calls it, has turned a custom meant to reward good service into an automatic surcharge that customers feel pressured to pay. The United States should move away from tipping and toward paying service workers a full wage, with prices that reflect the real cost of the meal.\n\nThe current system is unfair to workers first. As Source A explains, federal law still allows restaurants to pay tipped employees as little as $2.13 an hour, as long as tips make up the difference to minimum wage. This means a server's rent depends on whether customers are in a good mood, whether it rains, whether the restaurant is slow on a Tuesday. Source F's data shows that tipped workers are more than twice as likely to live in poverty as other workers. A wage that swings this much isn't really a wage.\n\nTipping also lets bias into pay. Source C, a summary of consumer research, found that customers tip differently based on a server's race, age and appearance, so two people doing the same job equally well can take home very different amounts. Opponents argue, like the restaurant owner in Source B, that tipping motivates servers to work harder and that getting rid of it would raise menu prices by 20 percent and drive customers away. But customers are already paying that 20 percent; they're just paying it" },
  { kind: "letter", name: "6th grader's fan letter to author Kwame Alexander about The Crossover", text: "Dear Mr. Alexander,\nMy name is Isaiah Ward and I am in 6th grade at Carver Middle School in Durham, North Carolina. We read The Crossover in Ms. Patterson's class and I wanted to write to you because it is the first book I ever read all the way through without anybody making me.\nI play basketball too, and I have a twin sister (she doesn't play basketball, she swims). The part where Josh gets mad at JB for spending all his time with Miss Sweet Tea is exactly how I felt when my best friend got a girlfriend. I didn't know a book could be about that.\nI also liked that it was written in poems. I thought I hated poetry but your poems have rhythm like music and some of them bounce across the page like somebody dribbling. Me and my friend tried to write one like that about our sneakers.\nThe ending made me cry and I don't cry at books. I'm not going to say what happens in case my sister reads this first.\nMy questions are did you play basketball when you were a kid? And are you going to write more books like this? (I know about Booked, I'm reading it now.)\nThank you for writing it.\nSincerely,\nIsaiah Ward" },
  { kind: "lessonplan", name: "substitute teacher plans for 7th grade world history on the Silk Road, written as notes to the sub (atypical)", text: "SUB PLANS — Thursday 10/23\nMr. Pruitt, Room 118, 7th Grade World History\n\nThank you for covering! Everything you need is in the red folder on my desk. Kids know the routine.\n\nSchedule\nPeriod 1 – 8:05-8:52 – World History\nPeriod 2 – 8:56-9:43 – World History\nPeriod 3 – PLANNING (you're free)\nPeriod 4 – 10:40-11:27 – World History\nLunch\nPeriods 5 & 6 – same lesson as 1/2/4\n\nLesson: The Silk Road — trade and the spread of ideas\n1. Attendance on the seating chart (in folder). Please don't let them move seats, they will try.\n2. Warm-up (on the board already): \"Name something in your house that came from another country. How did it get here?\" 5 minutes, then ask 2-3 kids to share.\n3. Pass out the Silk Road map handout. Students read pages 212-215 in the textbook (Ch. 9, Sec. 2) and fill in the map: label Chang'an, Kashgar, Samarkand, Baghdad, Constantinople, and draw the route. They can work with the person next to them.\n4. Then the \"What traveled?\" chart on the back — goods, ideas, religions, diseases. At least 3 in each column.\n5. Last 10 min: exit question on the board — \"Which was more important to travel along the Silk Road, goods or ideas? Why?\" Collect on the way out.\n\nWhat they turn in: map handout + exit slip. Put them in the tray by the door, separated by period please.\n\nHeads up\n- Period 4 is chatty. Ava M. and Jordan T. are great helpers if you need something.\n- Daniel R. has a pass to go to the nurse whenever he needs, no questions.\n- If anyone finishes early they can read silently or start the Ch. 9 vocab.\n\nEmergency procedures and the evacuation map are in the blue folder by the door. Mrs. Lindgren next door (Rm 120) can help with anything.\n\nThanks again!\n— T. Pruitt" },
  { kind: "code", name: "Arduino sketch for an ultrasonic garage parking helper with LEDs, a one-line note on top (atypical)", text: "garage parking thing for dad's bday — green/yellow/red. works on the breadboard, still need to mount it\nint trigPin = 9;\nint echoPin = 10;\nint greenLed = 3;\nint yellowLed = 4;\nint redLed = 5;\n\nlong duration;\nint distance;\n\nvoid setup() {\n  pinMode(trigPin, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(echoPin, INPUT);\n  pinMode(greenLed, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(yellowLed, OUTPUT);\n  pinMode(redLed, OUTPUT);\n  Serial.begin(9600);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);\n  delayMicroseconds(2);\n  digitalWrite(trigPin, HIGH);\n  delayMicroseconds(10);\n  digitalWrite(trigPin, LOW);\n\n  duration = pulseIn(echoPin, HIGH);\n  distance = duration * 0.034 / 2;   // cm\n\n  Serial.println(distance);\n\n  // all off first\n  digitalWrite(greenLed, LOW);\n  digitalWrite(yellowLed, LOW);\n  digitalWrite(redLed, LOW);\n\n  if (distance > 100) {\n    digitalWrite(greenLed, HIGH);\n  } else if (distance > 40) {\n    digitalWrite(yellowLed, HIGH);\n  } else {\n    digitalWrite(redLed, HIGH);   // STOP\n  }\n\n  delay(100);\n}" },
  { kind: "proposal", name: "CS team's short project pitch for a campus lost-and-found app, bullets and a plan table (atypical)", text: "CS 290 – Project Pitch\nTeam: Lucas Ferreira, Hana Kim, Dev Patel\nLostly — a lost & found for campus\nProblem: right now if you lose something on campus you have to check 6 different places (library desk, student union, each dorm front desk, campus police). Most stuff never gets claimed. The union alone donates or throws out ~300 items a semester.\nIdea: one web app where front desks log found items with a photo + location, and students search or get notified when something matching their description shows up.\nMVP features\n● desk staff login + \"log found item\" form (photo, category, where found)\n● student search by category / date / building\n● \"I lost something\" alert → email when a match is logged\n● items auto-archive after 30 days\nStretch\n● image matching\n● map view\nStack: React + Firebase (auth, Firestore, storage)\nPlan\nWeek\tGoal\n3–4\twireframes, data model, talk to union desk staff\n5–6\tauth + log item form\n7–8\tsearch + alerts\n9\ttest with 2 real desks\n10\tbug fixes, demo\nRisks: desk staff might not use it if it's slower than the paper logbook → we'll make logging take under 30 sec and test with them in week 9." },
  { kind: "letter", name: "resident's letter to a city council member urging a no vote on closing a dog park (could be mistaken for oped)", text: "The Honorable Teresa Huang\nCouncilmember, District 4\nAshford City Hall\n100 Main Street\nAshford, CO 80419\n\nOctober 9, 2026\n\nDear Councilmember Huang,\n\nI am writing as a resident of your district and one of the roughly two hundred people who use the off-leash area at Bramble Park every week, to urge you to vote against Item 14 on the October 21 agenda, which would close the dog park and convert the space into overflow parking for the new aquatic center.\n\nI understand the pressure the city is under. The aquatic center has been a success, and its lot fills on weekends. But closing the only off-leash area on the east side of Ashford to solve a weekend parking problem trades a permanent loss for a temporary convenience. The parking study the city commissioned in June found that the lot is over capacity on fewer than 30 days a year, mostly summer Saturdays. A dog park is used every single day, in every season, by retirees, families and people who live in apartments with no yard.\n\nThere are better options. The Lutheran church across Rowan Street has offered its lot for weekend overflow, as it already does for the farmers market. A shuttle from the high school lot on peak days would cost a fraction of repaving a park. And if the city is serious about long-term parking needs, it can include a structure in the 2028 capital plan rather than paving over green space now.\n\nI would also point out that the dog park was built in 2015 largely with money raised by residents, including a $12,000 campaign by the Friends of Bramble Park. Closing it with three weeks' notice and no public hearing tells every volunteer in this city that their work can be erased by a line on a consent agenda.\n\nI ask that you vote no on Item 14, or at minimum move to table it until a public hearing can be held. I would be glad to meet with you or your staff.\n\nRespectfully,\n\nGregory Ashby\n2217 Rowan Street\n(970) 555-0133" },
  { kind: "proposal", name: "AP Chem independent investigation proposal on vitamin C loss in orange juice, with purpose, materials, procedure and a blank data table (could be mistaken for lab)", text: "Independent Investigation Proposal\nSofia Marchetti — AP Chemistry, Period 2\nSubmitted to Dr. Kwan for approval, 11/4\n\nTitle: Effect of Storage Temperature on Vitamin C Content of Orange Juice\n\nPurpose\nThe purpose of this investigation will be to determine how storage temperature affects the rate at which vitamin C (ascorbic acid) breaks down in orange juice after the carton is opened.\n\nBackground\nAscorbic acid is easily oxidized, and the rate of oxidation should increase with temperature. Juice cartons say \"refrigerate after opening\" and \"use within 7 days,\" but I want to measure how much vitamin C is actually lost. I will measure ascorbic acid by redox titration with iodine, using starch as the indicator: iodine reacts with ascorbic acid until all of it is used up, and then the excess iodine turns the starch blue-black.\n\nHypothesis\nIf juice is stored at higher temperatures, then its vitamin C concentration will decrease faster, because the oxidation reaction rate increases with temperature.\n\nMaterials (requested from stockroom)\n- 0.005 M iodine solution, approx. 500 mL\n- 1% starch indicator solution\n- 50 mL burette and stand\n- 10 mL volumetric pipette\n- 125 mL Erlenmeyer flasks (6)\n- 1 carton (1.5 L) store-brand orange juice, pulp-free\n- 3 sealed containers\n- access to the lab refrigerator (4 °C), a room temperature shelf (~22 °C), and the incubator set to 35 °C\n\nPlanned procedure\n1. Standardize the iodine solution against a known vitamin C standard (one 500 mg tablet dissolved in 200 mL distilled water).\n2. On day 0, titrate three 10 mL samples of fresh juice and record the average volume of iodine needed.\n3. Divide the remaining juice into the three containers and store one at each temperature.\n4. On days 2, 4, 6 and 8, titrate three 10 mL samples from each container.\n5. Calculate mg of ascorbic acid per 100 mL for each trial.\n\nData table (to be filled in)\nDay\t4 °C (mg/100 mL)\t22 °C (mg/100 mL)\t35 °C (mg/100 mL)\n0\n2\n4\n6\n8\n\nSafety\nIodine stains skin and is an irritant; goggles and gloves will be worn. No juice from the lab will be consumed.\n\nTimeline\nNov 10: standardization. Nov 12–20: data collection. Nov 24: analysis. Final report due Dec 5." },
  { kind: "synthesis", name: "AP Lang synthesis essay on renaming buildings that honor controversial historical figures, history-heavy (could be mistaken for dbq)", text: "Sam Okonkwo\nMr. Delgado\nAP English Language and Composition\n3 March 2026\n\nWhat a Name Is For\n\nSince 2020, dozens of American schools, streets and military bases that honored Confederate generals have been renamed, and arguments over many more are still going on. The question of whether a community should rename a building is really a question about what a name is for. A name on a school is not a history lesson; it is an honor, and communities are entitled to decide whom they honor. Cities and school boards should be willing to rename public buildings, provided they do it through an open process that teaches the history instead of erasing it.\n\nThe strongest argument for renaming is that a name is an endorsement. Source B points out that most Confederate-named schools in the South were not named in the 1860s but in the 1950s and 1960s, at the height of resistance to desegregation. Those names were chosen as a statement, and Source B's timeline makes clear what the statement was. Keeping them is not neutral. When a Black student walks into a school named for a man who fought to keep her ancestors enslaved, the building is still saying what it was meant to say in 1958.\n\nCritics argue that renaming erases history. Source D, an essay by a historian, warns that \"a society that sands the rough edges off its past loses the ability to learn from it.\" This concern deserves a serious answer, but it confuses honoring with remembering. Source E describes a city that moved its Confederate statues into a museum, where they are displayed with full historical context. The history was not erased; if anything, more people learned it, because the museum explained what the plaque on the pedestal never had.\n\nOthers worry about where renaming stops. Source F, a political cartoon, shows workers chiseling names off a long row of buildings until they reach one labeled \"Washington,\" suggesting that every founder had flaws and the logic of renaming has no limit. But Source C, a school board's renaming policy, draws a reasonable line: the board asks whether the person is honored mainly for the very actions that are objectionable. Washington is honored for founding the country, not for owning slaves; a general known only for leading a war to preserve slavery is a different case.\n\nFinally, the process matters as much as the result. Source A, a survey of residents in a town that renamed its high school, found that support for the new name rose sharply when students led the research and the public could weigh in. A rename imposed overnight breeds resentment; one decided in public becomes the history lesson its critics say they want.\n\nA name is a choice a community makes about itself, and communities change. Revisiting those choices openly and carefully is not erasing the past. It is taking it seriously." },
  { kind: "lessonplan", name: "9th grade FCS kitchen lab lesson plan built around a pancake recipe, mostly recipe steps (could be mistaken for instructions)", text: "FCS 1 — Kitchen Lab: Measuring & Mixing (Pancakes)\nGrade 9, 85-min block\n\nObjective: Students will measure dry and liquid ingredients accurately, explain the muffin method of mixing, and follow kitchen safety and sanitation procedures.\n\nBefore class: one recipe card per kitchen unit. Check every unit has a griddle, dry measuring cups, liquid measuring cup, whisk, spatula, 2 bowls.\n\nRecipe (one batch per unit, makes about 8)\nDry\n- 1 1/2 cups all-purpose flour\n- 3 1/2 tsp baking powder\n- 1 tbsp sugar\n- 1/4 tsp salt\nWet\n- 1 1/4 cups milk\n- 1 egg\n- 3 tbsp melted butter\n\nSteps\n1. Wash hands, tie back hair, aprons on.\n2. Spoon flour into the dry measuring cup and level it with the back of a knife. Do not scoop or pack.\n3. Whisk all dry ingredients together in the large bowl.\n4. Measure milk at eye level in the liquid cup. Whisk with the egg and melted butter in the small bowl.\n5. Make a well in the dry ingredients, pour in the wet, and stir with a spatula just until combined. Lumps are fine.\n6. Heat griddle to 375°F. Pour 1/4 cup batter per pancake.\n7. Flip when bubbles form on top and the edges look dry, about 2 minutes. Cook 1–2 minutes more.\n8. Clean up: wash, dry, put away. Wipe counters with sanitizer.\n\nTeaching points\n- Demo first (15 min): scoop one cup of flour and spoon another, weigh both on the scale so they see the difference (scooped can be 20–30% heavier).\n- Ask during mixing: why do we stop while it's still lumpy? (overmixing develops gluten → tough, flat pancakes)\n- Check each unit's griddle temperature before they pour.\n\nWrap-up (10 min)\nTaste and evaluate with the sensory chart (appearance, texture, flavor). Unit discussion: what would you change next time?\n\nAssessment: kitchen lab rubric (measuring, teamwork, safety, cleanup) + lab sheet question: \"Explain in your own words why the muffin method keeps pancakes tender.\"" },
  { kind: "rhetorical", name: "AP Lang rhetorical analysis of Reagan's Challenger address, MLA heading", text: "Jordan Whitaker\nMs. Okafor\nAP English Language and Composition\n14 November 2025\n\nComforting a Nation: Reagan's Challenger Address\n\nOn January 28, 1986, the space shuttle Challenger broke apart just 73 seconds after it launched, and all seven astronauts on board died. One of them, Christa McAuliffe, was a social studies teacher from New Hampshire, so millions of kids were watching it live in their classrooms. That evening President Ronald Reagan was scheduled to give the State of the Union, but instead he postponed it and gave a short speech from the Oval Office. In the address, Reagan uses pathos, a direct appeal to children, and an allusion to a famous poem in order to comfort a grieving nation and to convince Americans that the space program should keep going.\n\nReagan starts by putting the speech's normal purpose aside. He says he had planned to talk about the state of the union but that \"today is a day for mourning and remembering.\" By doing this he shows the audience that he is not acting like a politician right now, he is acting like someone who is mourning with them. This builds his ethos because people trust a leader who seems to feel what they feel. He also names all seven crew members one by one, which makes them seem like real people and not just \"the astronauts.\"\n\nThe most interesting choice in the speech is when Reagan speaks directly to \"the schoolchildren of America who were watching the live coverage.\" He knows a lot of kids saw the explosion and were probably scared or confused. He tells them that painful things like this are \"all part of the process of exploration and discovery.\" His tone here is gentle and simple, almost like a parent. This is important because if children are afraid of space, the future of the program is in danger, so Reagan is comforting them but he is also persuading them.\n\nReagan also addresses the people at NASA and tells them the country still believes in them. He says \"the future doesn't belong to the fainthearted,\" which turns the disaster into a reason to be brave instead of a reason to quit. This is a kind of logos, because he is reasoning that giving up would dishonor what the crew died for.\n\nFinally, Reagan ends with lines from the poem \"High Flight\" by John Gillespie Magee, saying the crew \"slipped the surly bonds of earth\" to \"touch the face of God.\" The religious language gives the audience hope that the astronauts are at peace. Ending on poetry instead of politics makes the speech feel more like a eulogy than a presidential address.\n\nOverall Reagan's speech is short, but every part of it has a purpose. He comforts the country and he also makes sure the tragedy does not end America's exploration of space." },
  { kind: "rhetorical", name: "college comp rhetorical analysis of Stanton's Declaration of Sentiments", text: "Borrowed Authority: Imitation as Argument in the Declaration of Sentiments\n\nWhen Elizabeth Cady Stanton read the Declaration of Sentiments aloud at the Seneca Falls Convention in July 1848, her audience would have recognized its opening within a single sentence. \"When, in the course of human events\" was not a phrase anyone in the Wesleyan Chapel needed explained. Stanton's decision to model her document almost line for line on the Declaration of Independence is the central rhetorical move of the text, and it does more than borrow a famous rhythm. By wrapping a radical demand inside the most respected political document in American life, Stanton makes it difficult for her readers to reject women's rights without also seeming to reject the founding principles of the republic.\n\nThe most obvious example is the revised self-evident truth: \"that all men and women are created equal.\" The insertion of two words is quiet, almost polite, but its effect is aggressive. Stanton does not argue that the founders were wrong; she argues that they were incomplete, and she invites the reader to finish their sentence for them. For an audience that included sympathetic men, Quakers, and abolitionists who already spoke the language of natural rights, this framing lowers the cost of agreement. Accepting Stanton's premise requires no new philosophy, only consistency.\n\nThe list of grievances works the same way. Jefferson catalogued the \"repeated injuries and usurpations\" of King George; Stanton catalogues those of \"man\" toward \"woman,\" and the anaphora of \"He has\" builds the same accusatory momentum. \"He has never permitted her to exercise her inalienable right to the elective franchise.\" \"He has compelled her to submit to laws, in the formation of which she had no voice.\" The repetition turns a broad social condition into a series of specific, nameable acts, each with an implied perpetrator. Readers in 1848 would have heard in this rhythm the case for revolution against a tyrant, and the parallel quietly assigns that role to the husband and the legislator.\n\nStanton's ethos is also built through the document's restraint. The grievances move methodically from the vote to property, marriage, divorce, education, the professions, and the church, which gives the impression of a careful legal brief rather than an outburst. This matters because Stanton anticipated exactly the charge that women were too emotional for public life. Near the end, she acknowledges that the convention expects \"no small amount of misconception, misrepresentation, and ridicule,\" and resolves to use \"every instrumentality within our power\" anyway. Naming the ridicule before it arrives disarms it; any newspaper that mocked the convention (and several did) was only fulfilling her prediction.\n\nThe imitation strategy has limits. Some readers at the time found the parallel presumptuous, and the demand for the vote was controversial even among the signers; the resolution passed only after Frederick Douglass spoke in its favor. A modern reader may also notice that Stanton's \"woman\" is implicitly white and middle class. But as a piece of persuasion aimed at its immediate audience, the Declaration of Sentiments is remarkably shrewd. Stanton understood that Americans would resist a new argument but might accept an old one applied honestly, and she built her case so that rejecting it meant arguing with Jefferson." },
  { kind: "rhetorical", name: "ESL student rhetorical analysis of the ASPCA shelter-animal TV commercial", text: "Rhetorical Analysis of ASPCA Commercial\n\nThe ASPCA commercial with Sarah McLachlan is maybe one of most famous commercial in America. Many people say they change the channel when it come on because it make them too sad. In this commercial the ASPCA want people to donate money every month to help the animals who are abused. The commercial use pathos very strong, and also some ethos, to make audience feel guilty and then give money.\n\nFirst, the commercial begin with slow sad song by Sarah McLachlan and show pictures of dogs and cats in the cages. The animals look very skinny and some have injury on their eyes or legs. The camera move slowly to the face of the animal so it look like the animal is looking to you. This is pathos because the viewer feel sorry and imagine maybe this is their own pet. The music is soft piano and her voice is sad, so even if you are not watching the TV, you hear it and feel emotional.\n\nSecond, Sarah McLachlan herself is speaking in the commercial and she say she is asking you to help. She is famous singer so people know her and trust her, this is ethos. Also she is holding a dog in some part, so it show she really care about animals, not only for money.\n\nThird, the commercial tell the price is only small, like sixty cents a day. This make the donation seem very easy, like it is nothing for the viewer. If they say eighteen dollar a month maybe people think it is a lot, but sixty cents is less than a coffee.\n\nIn my opinion the commercial is effective because it make people feel they must do something. But some people think it is too much and they just change the channel, so maybe it is too sad for some audience. Still, I think it work because everybody still remember it after many years." },
  { kind: "rhetorical", name: "unfinished AP Lang timed write on Queen Elizabeth II's 2020 coronavirus broadcast", text: "Prompt: On April 5, 2020, Queen Elizabeth II delivered a televised address to the United Kingdom and the Commonwealth during the coronavirus lockdown. Read the speech carefully. Write an essay that analyzes the rhetorical choices the Queen makes to convey her message of resilience.\n\nIn April of 2020 when the entire country was stuck at home and people were scared, Queen Elizabeth gave a rare televised speech, one of the only special ones of her whole reign besides the christmas messages. She was 93 and had lived through WW2 which she brings up on purpose. Her purpose is to make people feel calm and united and to keep following the lockdown rules, and she does this through references to shared history, inclusive language, and a hopeful ending that echoes a famous song from the war.\n\nFirst the Queen relies on her own ethos as someone who has been there before. She mentions that her first broadcast was in 1940 with her sister, speaking to children who had been evacuated. This is effective because most of her audience wasnt alive in 1940 but they know the stories, so she is basically saying I have seen the country get through worse. She doesnt have to say it directly, just by remembering it she reminds people she is the link to that generation. It also connects the sacrifices of then with now, staying home is like the \"evacuation\" of 2020 in a way.\n\nSecond, her language is very inclusive. She uses \"we\" and \"us\" constantly instead of \"you\". She thanks NHS workers and care workers and also \"those of you who are staying at home,\" which makes ordinary people feel like they are also doing something heroic just by not going out. This matters bc the audience is tired and maybe feeling useless, and she gives them a role.\n\nFinally the ending. She says \"we will meet again,\" which is a direct echo of the Vera Lynn song from WW2 that everyone in Britain would know. The repetition of \"we will be with\" builds up to it like" },
  { kind: "rhetorical", name: "9th grade short analysis of Lou Gehrig's farewell speech", text: "Lou Gehrig Speech Analysis\nPeriod 3\n\nIn 1939 Lou Gehrig gave a speech at Yankee Stadium after he found out he had ALS, a disease that would kill him. His purpose was to thank the fans and his team and to show that he was not feeling sorry for himself. His audience was the crowd at the stadium and also people listening on the radio.\n\nThe most famous part is when he says he considers himself \"the luckiest man on the face of the earth.\" This is surprising because he is actually very sick so you would expect him to be sad. This uses pathos because it makes the audience emotional, they feel sad for him but also proud of him.\n\nGehrig also uses ethos. He talks about the people he played with and his managers like Miller Huggins and Joe McCarthy, which reminds the audience he was part of a great team for 17 years. He also mentions his wife and his parents.\n\nAnother strategy is repetition. He keeps starting sentences with \"when\" and lists all the good things in his life. This makes his argument that he is lucky stronger because the list is so long.\n\nI think his speech was effective because people still remember it today." },
  { kind: "rhetorical", name: "rhetorical analysis of Swift's A Modest Proposal (could be mistaken for literary)", text: "Priya Raman\nENGL 210: Writing and Rhetoric\nProfessor Delgado\nMarch 3\n\nThe Calculating Narrator: How Swift Persuades by Pretending Not To\n\nJonathan Swift's \"A Modest Proposal\" (1729) is often taught alongside fiction because its narrator is so clearly a character, but the pamphlet was a piece of political persuasion aimed at real readers with real power. Swift's target audience was the Anglo-Irish landowning class and the English policymakers whose absentee rents and trade restrictions kept Ireland's poor starving. His purpose was to shame them into reform. What makes the essay work is that Swift never argues his actual position directly. Instead he invents a speaker who argues the opposite position so reasonably that the reader is forced to supply the moral objection themselves.\n\nThe speaker's ethos is that of a sober economist. He opens with a sympathetic scene of beggar mothers followed by \"three, four, or six children, all in rags,\" and he presents himself as someone who has \"turned my thoughts for many years upon this important subject.\" Readers in 1729 were used to pamphlets by \"projectors\" who offered tidy schemes to fix the national economy, and Swift copies their tone exactly. The calm, credentialed voice is what makes the turn so shocking: the man who seems most reasonable is the one proposing that the children of the poor be sold as food.\n\nSwift then parodies logos. The proposal is supported with arithmetic about how many of the kingdom's infants could be \"reserved for breed,\" the cost of nursing a child, and the price a landlord might pay for the meat. The numbers are internally consistent, and that is the point. By imitating the cold language of political economy, Swift shows his readers what their own policies look like when the human beings are removed from the calculation. The lack of any emotional appeal from the speaker becomes, ironically, the strongest emotional appeal in the text.\n\nThe most revealing passage comes near the end, when the speaker lists the \"other expedients\" he insists he will not consider: taxing absentees, buying Irish goods, \"teaching landlords to have at least one degree of mercy towards their tenants.\" These are Swift's real proposals, the ones he had already argued for plainly in earlier pamphlets without result. Placing them in a list of rejected ideas lets Swift slip his actual argument into the essay while keeping the satire intact. Readers who reach this passage realize that every sensible solution has been available all along.\n\nSome critics argue the satire was too subtle for its audience. Yet the essay's endurance suggests the strategy succeeded in a different way than Swift intended: it made apathy itself the thing being mocked, and that target has never gone out of date." },
  { kind: "lab", name: "high school chemistry titration lab report on acetic acid in vinegar, full sections", text: "Titration of Vinegar Lab\nMarisol Ortega, Ben Kowalczyk\nChemistry H – Period 6\nMr. Freidman\nDate performed: 2/11\n\nPurpose\nTo determine the molarity and percent by mass of acetic acid in store-bought white vinegar by titrating it with a sodium hydroxide solution of known concentration.\n\nHypothesis\nIf the vinegar label is accurate (5% acidity), then the titration should give an acetic acid concentration of about 0.83 M, because 5 g of acetic acid per 100 mL is 50 g/L and 50 g ÷ 60.05 g/mol = 0.83 mol.\n\nMaterials\n* 50 mL burette and burette clamp\n* ring stand\n* 0.250 M NaOH (standardized by teacher)\n* white vinegar (Great Value brand)\n* 5.00 mL volumetric pipette and bulb\n* 125 mL Erlenmeyer flasks (3)\n* phenolphthalein indicator\n* distilled water, wash bottle\n* white paper\n\nProcedure\n1. Rinse the burette with a small amount of NaOH and drain, then fill it with NaOH and record the initial volume.\n2. Pipette 5.00 mL of vinegar into an Erlenmeyer flask and add about 20 mL of distilled water and 3 drops of phenolphthalein.\n3. Place the flask on white paper under the burette.\n4. Add NaOH while swirling until a faint pink color lasts for 30 seconds.\n5. Record the final burette volume.\n6. Repeat for a total of 3 good trials.\n\nData\nTrial\tInitial volume (mL)\tFinal volume (mL)\tVolume NaOH used (mL)\n1\t0.40\t17.80\t17.40*\n2\t17.80\t34.30\t16.50\n3\t1.10\t17.80\t16.70\n4\t17.80\t34.70\t16.90\n*trial 1 overshot (solution turned dark magenta) so it was not used in the average\n\nObservations: The solution turned pink where the NaOH drops hit and then cleared when swirled. Near the endpoint the pink took longer to disappear.\n\nCalculations\nAverage volume NaOH (trials 2-4) = (16.50 + 16.70 + 16.90) / 3 = 16.70 mL\nmol NaOH = 0.01670 L × 0.250 mol/L = 0.004175 mol\nmol CH3COOH = mol NaOH = 0.004175 mol (1:1 ratio)\nMolarity of acetic acid = 0.004175 mol ÷ 0.00500 L = 0.835 M\ng/L = 0.835 × 60.05 = 50.1 g/L, so about 5.01 g per 100 mL\n% error compared to label = |5.01 − 5.00| / 5.00 × 100 = 0.2%\n\nAnalysis\nOur results were very close to the 5% printed on the label, which supports our hypothesis. The three trials we used were within 0.4 mL of each other, so the precision was pretty good. Trial 1 was way over because Ben opened the stopcock too far at the beginning and we didn't slow down near the endpoint. We assumed the density of vinegar is about 1 g/mL so that grams per 100 mL equals percent by mass, which is not exactly true (it's closer to 1.01), so our real percent by mass would be very slightly lower.\n\nSources of error include reading the burette meniscus from slightly above, which would make the volume look smaller, and judging the \"faint pink\" color differently on different trials.\n\nConclusion\nThe vinegar was 0.835 M acetic acid, or about 5.0% acetic acid, which matches the label. Titration is an accurate way to find the concentration of an acid as long as the endpoint is approached slowly." },
  { kind: "lab", name: "7th grade paper towel absorbency lab write-up", text: "Paper Towel Lab\nby Kaylee D.\n\nQuestion: Which brand of paper towel absorbs the most water?\n\nHypothesis: I think Bounty will absorb the most water because it is the most expensive and it feels the thickest.\n\nMaterials: 4 brands of paper towels (Bounty, Viva, Great Value, Sparkle), graduated cylinder, a bowl, water, a timer\n\nProcedure:\n1. Use one full sheet of each paper towel.\n2. Pour 100 mL of water into the bowl.\n3. Put the paper towel in the bowl for 10 seconds.\n4. Take it out and let it drip over the bowl for 5 sec.\n5. Pour the water left in the bowl back into the graduated cylinder and see how much is left. 100 minus that = how much got absorbed.\n6. Do it 2 times for each brand.\n\nResults:\nBrand\tTrial 1\tTrial 2\tAverage\nBounty\t42 mL\t39 mL\t40.5 mL\nViva\t37 mL\t40 mL\t38.5 mL\nGreat Value\t26 mL\t24 mL\t25 mL\nSparkle\t31 mL\t30 mL\t30.5 mL\n\nConclusion: My hypothesis was correct, Bounty absorbed the most water (40.5 mL) but Viva was really close. Great Value absorbed the least. One problem was that some water spilled when we poured it back into the cylinder so the numbers might not be exact. If I did it again I would do more trials and use a bigger bowl." },
  { kind: "lab", name: "college biology lab report on temperature and catalase activity, formal sections", text: "The Effect of Temperature on Catalase Activity in Potato Extract\n\nBIOL 1107L, Section 14\nLab partners: A. Nguyen, R. Castillo, T. Brennan\n\nIntroduction\nEnzymes are proteins that lower the activation energy of biochemical reactions, and their activity depends on their three-dimensional shape (Urry et al. 2020). Catalase, found in nearly all aerobic organisms, breaks down hydrogen peroxide (H2O2), a toxic byproduct of metabolism, into water and oxygen gas. Because enzyme structure is held together largely by hydrogen bonds and other weak interactions, temperature is expected to affect catalase activity: moderate increases should speed the reaction by increasing molecular collisions, while high temperatures should denature the enzyme. The purpose of this experiment was to measure catalase activity in potato extract at four temperatures. We hypothesized that activity would increase from 4°C to 37°C and decrease sharply at 60°C.\n\nMaterials and Methods\nCatalase extract was prepared by blending 50 g of peeled russet potato with 100 mL of cold distilled water and filtering the mixture through cheesecloth. Aliquots of extract were held for 10 minutes in an ice bath (4°C), at room temperature (22°C), in a water bath at 37°C, or in a water bath at 60°C. Activity was measured using the filter paper disk flotation method. Disks (6 mm diameter, cut with a hole punch) were soaked in extract for 5 seconds, then dropped into a test tube containing 10 mL of 3% H2O2 held at the same temperature as the extract. The time from when the disk touched the bottom of the tube to when it reached the surface was recorded with a stopwatch. Oxygen produced by the reaction collects on the disk and causes it to rise, so a shorter time indicates greater enzyme activity. Five disks were tested at each temperature. Rate was calculated as 1/time (s⁻¹).\n\nResults\nMean flotation time was shortest at 37°C and longest at 60°C (Table 1). At 60°C, two of the five disks had not risen after 120 seconds; these trials were recorded as 120 s, so the mean for this group is an underestimate of the true time. Bubbling was visibly reduced in the 60°C tubes compared with all other groups.\n\nTable 1. Mean disk flotation time and reaction rate of catalase at four temperatures (n = 5 per group).\nTemperature (°C)\tMean time (s)\tSD (s)\tRate (1/s)\n4\t21.6\t3.1\t0.046\n22\t12.4\t1.8\t0.081\n37\t8.9\t1.2\t0.112\n60\t97.3\t20.8\t0.010\n\n[Figure 1: rate vs. temperature – insert graph from Excel]\n\nDiscussion\nThe results support our hypothesis. Catalase activity increased between 4°C and 37°C, consistent with greater kinetic energy producing more frequent enzyme–substrate collisions. The dramatic drop at 60°C suggests that much of the catalase was denatured during the 10-minute incubation, altering the active site so that H2O2 could no longer bind effectively. Because two disks never rose, the real decline at 60°C was likely even larger than our data show.\n\nWe did not test temperatures between 37°C and 60°C, so we cannot identify the optimum temperature precisely; it may lie above 37°C. Several sources of error are possible. Disks were cut by hand and may have absorbed different volumes of extract, and the 60°C bath fluctuated between 57°C and 62°C during the experiment. The tubes also began to cool or warm toward room temperature once removed from the baths, so the reaction may not have occurred at exactly the labeled temperature. A future experiment should use a temperature-controlled block and include intermediate temperatures such as 45°C and 50°C.\n\nLiterature Cited\nUrry LA, Cain ML, Wasserman SA, Minorsky PV, Orr RB. 2020. Campbell biology. 12th ed. New York (NY): Pearson." },
  { kind: "lab", name: "high school engineering design report for a cardboard solar oven", text: "Solar Oven Design Report\nTeam 4: Elijah Brooks, Sofia Mendez, Tran Nguyen\nIntro to Engineering Design — Unit 3\n\nDesign Problem\nOur challenge was to design and build a solar oven that could reach at least 65°C (149°F) in 45 minutes using only sunlight, so that it could melt a s'more. The oven had to be built from materials in the classroom bin and fit inside a 50 cm × 50 cm × 50 cm space.\n\nCriteria and Constraints\n- Reach 65°C or higher in 45 min\n- Budget: $10 of \"class bucks\" (cardboard free, foil $1/sheet, plastic wrap $2, black paper $1, tape $1/roll)\n- No electricity or batteries\n- Must have a door or lid to put food in\n\nBrainstorming\nWe sketched three ideas: (1) a pizza box oven with a foil flap reflector, (2) a box-in-a-box oven with newspaper insulation between the walls, and (3) a funnel-shaped cone oven. We used a decision matrix to rate them on cost, ease of building, and expected heat. The box-in-a-box scored highest (22/30) because the insulation should keep heat in, so that's what we built.\n\nPrototype 1\nWe put a shoebox inside a larger shipping box and stuffed crumpled newspaper between them. The inside was lined with black construction paper and the top was covered with two layers of plastic wrap. One foil-covered cardboard flap reflected sunlight into the window.\n\nTesting Prototype 1 (outside, sunny, air temp 24°C)\nTime (min)\tTemp (°C)\n0\t24\n15\t41\n30\t52\n45\t56\n\nPrototype 1 did not reach 65°C. We noticed that the reflector flap kept blowing down and the plastic wrap had a gap at one corner where hot air was escaping.\n\nRedesign\nFor Prototype 2 we added three more foil reflectors (one on each side, angled at about 45°), propped them with pencils and tape, and sealed the plastic wrap edges with tape all the way around. We also painted the bottom of the inner box black instead of using paper. Total cost was $9.\n\nTesting Prototype 2 (outside, partly cloudy at the start, air temp 23°C)\nTime (min)\tTemp (°C)\n0\t23\n15\t47\n30\t63\n45\t71\n\nResults and Conclusion\nPrototype 2 met the goal and reached 71°C, and the marshmallow and chocolate melted. The extra reflectors made the biggest difference because they concentrated more sunlight into the box. If we had more time, we would add a thermometer we could read without opening the lid, since opening it to check the temperature let heat out each time, and we would retest both prototypes on the same fully sunny day to compare them fairly." },
  { kind: "lab", name: "rough early lab write-up on ruler-drop reaction time, no headings", text: "reaction time lab - rough draft\n\nSo in this lab we tested whether people react faster with their dominant hand than their non dominant hand. My hypothesis was that the dominant hand would be faster because you use it more so the brain is more used to sending signals to it. We used the ruler drop test where one person holds a meter stick above the other persons open hand with the 0 cm mark lined up with their thumb and then drops it without warning and they catch it as fast as they can. You read the cm where they caught it.\n\nwe did 5 trials each hand on 4 people (me, Dev, Hannah, Marcus). Averages:\nme: dominant (right) 14.2 cm, non dom 17.8 cm\nDev: dom (right) 12.6 cm, non dom 13.1 cm\nHannah: dom (left) 16.0, non dom 19.4\nMarcus: dom (right) 18.8, non dom 18.2 ← only one where non dom was better??\n\nTo change cm into time you use t = √(2d/g), so for 14.2 cm that's √(2 × 0.142 / 9.8) = 0.17 s. [need to do the rest of these, put in a table]\n\n[add bar graph here]\n\n3 out of 4 people were faster with dominant hand so my hypothesis was mostly supported but Marcus messes it up. He said he was getting tired by the end and his dominant hand trials were done second so maybe thats why. also Dev's two hands were almost the same. Its hard to tell with only 4 people. Errors: the dropper might have given away when they were going to drop it by moving their fingers, and some people were catching it with their fingers lower on the stick than others.\n\nconclusion - finish after table" },
  { kind: "lab", name: "physics projectile lab that is mostly calculations (could be mistaken for math)", text: "Lab 7 – Horizontal Projectile Prediction\nAaron Feld, Physics 1\n\nPurpose: use the launch speed and table height to predict where a steel ball rolling off the table will land, then test the prediction with carbon paper.\n\nMeasured:\ntable height h = 0.912 m\nball diameter = 2.50 cm = 0.0250 m\nphotogate time (avg of 3 runs) = 0.0127 s\n\nLaunch speed:\nv = d / t\nv = 0.0250 / 0.0127\nv = 1.969 m/s\n\nTime to fall (vertical, starts at 0 m/s):\nh = ½gt²\nt = √(2h / g)\nt = √(2(0.912) / 9.8)\nt = √(0.1861)\nt = 0.431 s\n\nPredicted horizontal distance:\nx = v · t\nx = 1.969 × 0.431\nx = 0.849 m = 84.9 cm\n\nPlaced target paper with carbon paper at 84.9 cm from the edge of the table (measured from a plumb bob).\n\nActual landing distances (cm): 82.1, 83.4, 81.7, 84.0, 82.8\naverage = 414.0 / 5 = 82.8 cm\n\n% error = |82.8 − 84.9| / 84.9 × 100\n= 2.1 / 84.9 × 100\n= 2.5%\n\nConclusion: Our prediction was 2.5% off and all five landings were short of the predicted spot. This is probably because the ball loses a little speed between the photogate and the edge of the table (the gate was about 6 cm back from the edge) and because of air resistance. The results show that horizontal and vertical motion are independent, because we could find the fall time using only the height, without knowing the horizontal speed." },
  { kind: "oped", name: "local business owner op-ed opposing downtown parking meters", text: "Parking meters won't save Front Street. They'll finish it off.\n\nMy family has run a shoe repair shop on Front Street for 31 years. In that time I have watched downtown Harlow Falls survive a mall opening on Route 9, a recession, the bridge closure of 2014, and a pandemic. I am not sure it will survive the city's plan to put paid parking on every block between Mill and Third.\n\nThe proposal before the city council would replace the free two-hour spaces downtown with meters charging $2 an hour, enforced from 9 a.m. to 7 p.m. The city says the meters will raise about $400,000 a year and encourage \"turnover\" so that shoppers can find spaces. I understand the budget pressure. But anyone who actually works on Front Street can tell you the problem is not that people are parking too long. It's that not enough people are coming at all.\n\nAsk my customers why they come downtown instead of going to the strip on Route 9, and the answer is almost always the same: it's easy. You pull up, you drop off your boots, you grab a coffee at Ruthie's, you leave. The moment that trip costs money and requires an app, it stops being easy. The plaza by the highway has a thousand free spaces. We will lose that competition.\n\nOther towns our size have tried this. When Millbrook installed meters on its main street, several merchants told the Herald their sales fell in the first year, and the town ended up making Saturdays free to bring people back. I would rather we learn from their mistake than repeat it.\n\nThere are better ways to raise money and manage parking. Enforce the two-hour limit we already have, which almost never happens. Open the empty county lot on Third Street to the public on evenings and weekends. Put the meters, if we must have them, in the commuter lot near the train station, where people park all day and rarely shop.\n\nDowntown is not a parking problem to be solved. It is a few dozen small businesses, most of them owned by people who live here, hanging on. The council votes on April 22. I hope its members will walk Front Street before then and ask us what we think.\n\nTeresa Molina owns Molina Shoe & Leather Repair at 118 Front St." },
  { kind: "oped", name: "student newspaper staff editorial on a school phone pouch policy", text: "EDITORIAL: The pouch policy is a good idea that's being run badly\n\nWhen Westbrook High rolled out magnetic phone pouches in August, the staff of The Lancer Ledger was skeptical. Three months in, we have changed our minds about the idea, but not about the way the school is carrying it out.\n\nFirst, credit where it's due. Lunch is louder in a good way. People are actually talking at tables again, and several teachers told us that the first ten minutes of class are no longer a negotiation. A survey the Ledger ran in October (182 responses) found that 58% of students think they focus better in class since the pouches arrived, even though 71% said they don't like the policy. Both of those things can be true.\n\nBut the policy depends on everyone following the same rules, and right now they don't. Some teachers let students unlock pouches to \"look something up\" during class; others confiscate a phone for being seen in the hallway. Unlocking stations are supposed to be at every exit, but the one by the gym doors has been broken since September, which means students who leave from practice have to walk all the way back to the main entrance. Students with medical needs who rely on phone apps, like glucose monitors, told us they had to explain their situation to three different staff members before getting an exception.\n\nNone of these problems are hard to fix. We are asking the administration to do three things:\n\n1. Publish one clear set of rules for when pouches can be opened, and make sure every teacher enforces the same ones.\n2. Fix or replace the broken unlocking station by winter break.\n3. Create a simple, confidential process for medical exceptions run by the nurse's office, so students aren't forced to explain their health conditions in the hallway.\n\nThe pouches can work. But a policy this strict only feels fair if it is applied fairly, and if the school wants students to buy in, it has to hold up its end.\n\nThe Ledger editorial board voted 7–1 in favor of this editorial." },
  { kind: "oped", name: "polished op-ed by an emergency room nurse on hospital staffing ratios", text: "I'm an ER nurse. The hallway beds are not the worst part.\n\nLast Tuesday at about 2 a.m., I was responsible for six patients at once. One was having a heart attack. One was an 84-year-old woman who had fallen at home and had been waiting for a bed upstairs for 31 hours. One was a teenager who had swallowed a bottle of pills. The other three were sick enough to be in an emergency department and stable enough that, on that night, they got almost none of my time.\n\nNobody died. I want to be clear about that, because the stories people tell about understaffed hospitals usually end with a tragedy, and most nights don't. Most nights end the way that one did: with an elderly woman who wet herself because no one could walk her to the bathroom in time, a teenager whose parents I spoke to for four minutes instead of twenty, and a nurse who drove home at 8 a.m. making a list of the things she had missed.\n\nThat is the quiet cost of understaffing, and it is why I am asking our legislators to pass the safe staffing bill now sitting in committee in the state Senate. The bill would set a maximum number of patients per nurse in each hospital unit, as California has done since 2004. In the emergency department, the limit would generally be four patients per nurse, and lower for trauma and critically ill patients.\n\nHospital associations oppose the bill, and their arguments deserve a fair hearing. They say there are not enough nurses to meet the ratios, that rural hospitals in particular would have to close beds, and that a rigid formula can't capture the difference between a quiet Sunday and a mass-casualty event. These are real concerns. But the shortage they describe is not simply a shortage of people with nursing licenses. It is a shortage of nurses willing to keep working under these conditions. Every nurse I trained with who has left the bedside has told me the same thing: it wasn't the blood or the hours, it was the feeling of never being able to do the job right. Safe ratios are not only a response to the staffing crisis. They are part of how you end it.\n\nThe flexibility argument also cuts the other way. Today, the only limit on how many patients I can be assigned is what my charge nurse can find coverage for, which means the \"flexibility\" almost always runs in one direction. A ratio is a floor, not a ceiling; hospitals could still staff above it on busy nights. They would simply no longer be allowed to staff below it.\n\nThere are details the bill should get right. It should include funding to help small and rural hospitals hire, phase in the ratios over two or three years, and give hospitals clear rules for genuine emergencies, so a bus crash on the interstate doesn't trigger a fine.\n\nBut the core idea is simple. We already accept that a pilot shouldn't fly too many hours in a row and that a classroom has a maximum size. Patients in an emergency room deserve the same basic protection. So do the people caring for them.\n\nThe woman who waited 31 hours apologized to me twice for being a bother. She was not a bother. She was a patient, and she deserved a nurse who had time for her.\n\nDana Whitcomb has been a registered nurse for 14 years and works in a community hospital emergency department." },
  { kind: "oped", name: "college newspaper op-ed draft on automatic textbook fees, with TK placeholders", text: "HEADLINE OPTIONS:\n- \"Inclusive Access\" is a bill you never agreed to\n- Opt out of the textbook fee before Sept. 20 (or don't, I guess)\n\nby Nate Osei, staff columnist (DRAFT 2 - send to Kira by Thurs)\n\nIf you look closely at your fall tuition bill, you might notice a line called \"Course Materials – IA\" that you don't remember signing up for. At Carrow University that charge averages [TK - get avg from bursar, I think around $280?] a semester, and for some STEM majors it's well over $400. It's the cost of \"Inclusive Access,\" a program the bookstore runs with publishers that automatically bills students for digital textbooks on the first day of class.\n\nThe pitch sounds great: every student has the book on day one, at a discount compared to the printed list price. And for some students in some classes, it is a good deal. But \"automatically billed unless you opt out\" is not the same thing as a good deal, and right now most students don't know they can opt out at all. In an informal poll I ran in two intro classes (n = 64, not scientific, I know), only 11 people knew the opt-out existed. [TK - double check it was 11, notes are at home]\n\nThe bigger problem is that the access codes expire at the end of the semester. You can't resell them, can't keep them for your next course, and can't buy a used copy instead. Students who would have spent $40 on a used edition from a senior are paying [TK]% more for a license that disappears in December.\n\nI'm not saying the university should end the program. I'm saying it should be opt-in, or at the very least, that the opt-out should be one button in the student portal instead of a form you have to email to the bookstore within the first ten days. Professors should also say on the syllabus whether an older edition will work.\n\nStudents are already paying enough. The least the university can do is ask before it charges us.\n\n[ending feels weak - maybe end w/ quote from Dani who got charged twice?]" },
  { kind: "oped", name: "short letter to the editor responding to a column on e-bikes on a rail trail", place: "www.valleyherald.com/opinion/letters/submit", text: "Regarding Carl Bendix's column \"E-bikes don't belong on the Ashby Rail Trail\" (Oct. 9): I'm 71, I had a knee replaced two years ago, and an e-bike is the only reason I'm still riding that trail at all. The problem isn't the motor, it's the speed. A road cyclist doing 25 mph on a carbon bike is just as dangerous as a kid on a throttle e-bike. Post a 15 mph limit for everyone and enforce it. Don't ban the rest of us.\n\nGordon Pruett\nAshby" },
  { kind: "oped", name: "class-assigned op-ed on funding a skate park, with MLA heading (could be mistaken for argumentative)", text: "Leo Castellanos\nMr. Haddad\nEnglish 10\n9 May 2026\n\nCedar Grove Needs a Skate Park, Not More Signs\n\nIf you've walked past the library plaza after 4 p.m., you've seen us: a dozen kids with skateboards, a few scooters, maybe a BMX bike, and at least one \"NO SKATEBOARDING\" sign. Every few weeks a police car pulls up, someone gets a warning, and everyone scatters to the bank parking lot until it's safe to come back. This has been going on for as long as I've been skating, which is six years. On Tuesday the Cedar Grove Town Council will vote on whether to spend $280,000 from the parks budget on a concrete skate park at Riverside Field. I'm asking readers of the Gazette to tell them yes.\n\nThe usual objection is cost. $280,000 is a lot of money, and I understand why taxpayers want to know what they're getting. But the town already spends money on skating, just in the worst possible way. According to the town's own public works reports, it spent more than $11,000 last year repairing ledges and benches at the library plaza and installing \"skate stoppers,\" those metal knobs bolted onto every edge. That money bought nothing. Kids still skate there, because there is nowhere else to go.\n\nA skate park would fix this. Towns like Brookfield and Lyle Center built parks in the last ten years, and both say complaints about skating downtown dropped almost to zero. A park also gives teenagers a place that is actually meant for them. Cedar Grove has three baseball diamonds, two tennis courts, and a dog park. It does not have a single public space built for something a lot of kids here actually do.\n\nSome neighbors near Riverside worry about noise and litter. Those are fair concerns, and the plan answers them: the park would close at dusk, sit on the far end of the field away from houses, and include trash cans and lighting. The Skate Cedar Grove group has also promised to run monthly cleanups, and we've already done two at the library plaza to prove we mean it.\n\nSkaters aren't asking for a favor. We are asking to be treated like every other group in town that has a place to play. Come to the council meeting on Tuesday at 7 p.m. at Town Hall, or email your council member before then. Tell them Cedar Grove's kids deserve better than a \"NO SKATEBOARDING\" sign." },
  { kind: "personal", name: "college freshman personal narrative about learning to drive stick from a grandfather", text: "Third Gear\n\nMy grandfather's truck was a 1997 Ford Ranger the color of weak tea, and it had a manual transmission because, as he put it, \"automatics are for people who don't want to know what their car is doing.\" I was fifteen and three-quarters the summer he decided I should learn on it. My mom said absolutely not. He said she had learned on the truck before this one and she had turned out fine. She said that was debatable. I was in the truck by Saturday.\n\nWe started in the parking lot of the Lutheran church on County Road 12, which was empty except on Sundays and had a single light pole in the middle that I became very afraid of. Grandpa explained the clutch with his hands, one flat palm sliding against the other. \"You're introducing them,\" he said. \"Engine, wheels. Slowly. They don't know each other yet.\"\n\nI stalled eleven times the first morning. I know because he counted, out loud, in the same calm voice each time. \"That's four.\" \"That's seven.\" He never raised it. When the truck lurched and died with a shudder that rattled the coffee cups in the cupholders, he would just say the number, wait for me to breathe, and tell me to try again. By the ninth stall I was crying, mostly from embarrassment, and he handed me a napkin from the glove box and said, \"Nine. You're getting closer. I can hear it.\"\n\nI didn't believe him. But on the twelfth try I let the clutch out at the right speed and pressed the gas at the right moment and the truck rolled forward like it had wanted to all along. I drove a full circle around the light pole in first gear, shaking, while he clapped once, loudly, and said, \"There it is.\"\n\nWe practiced every Saturday that summer. First the church lot, then the gravel road past the Hendersons' farm, then, finally, Highway 30, where I got the truck into third gear for the first time and felt it settle, the engine dropping into a low, satisfied hum. He rolled down his window and put his arm out like he was the one who'd done something. Maybe he was.\n\nWhat I didn't understand then was how much of that summer was not really about the truck. My grandmother had died the winter before, and my grandfather, who had been married to her for forty-six years, was living alone for the first time in his adult life. My mom had been worried about him. I think now that the lessons were partly her idea, a reason to get him out of the house every week, and partly his, a reason to be needed. I was so focused on the clutch I never noticed.\n\nI got my license that November. I drive an automatic now, a used Corolla, and I almost never think about gears. But last spring, when Grandpa's eyesight got bad enough that he had to stop driving, I was the one who took him to his appointments. The first time, he got into the passenger seat of my car and looked down at the floor. \"Two pedals,\" he said, disappointed. \"You've gone soft.\"\n\n\"I learned from a patient teacher,\" I told him. \"I never had to get tough.\"\n\nHe laughed, and then he was quiet for a while, watching the road. When we merged onto the highway he said, \"Third gear,\" under his breath, out of habit, and I didn't correct him." },
  { kind: "personal", name: "middle school personal narrative about losing a spelling bee", text: "The Spelling Bee\n\nLast year in 6th grade I was in the school spelling bee for the first time. My teacher Mrs. Patel picked two people from each class and I was one of them because I got 100 on every spelling test. My mom bought me a new shirt for it and my grandma said she would come watch even though she had to take two buses.\n\nI practiced every night for two weeks with the list they gave us. My dad would read the words and I would spell them while I was brushing my teeth or eating dinner. I knew all of them. Words like \"bureau\" and \"camouflage\" and \"silhouette\". I was sure I was going to win.\n\nThe day of the bee there was like 300 people in the gym and the microphone made this really loud squeak every time someone stepped up to it. My hands were sweating so much. I got through the first four rounds and then it was only me and an 8th grader named Tyler left.\n\nThen they gave me \"rhythm.\" I KNEW that word. I spelled it in my head the right way a hundred times before. But when I opened my mouth I said R-H-Y-T-H-E-M. As soon as I said the E I knew it was wrong, and the judge rang the little bell. Everyone clapped but I felt like I was going to throw up. Tyler spelled it right and then spelled his next word and won.\n\nI cried in the bathroom after. My grandma came in and found me and she said she was proud of me anyway because I was the youngest one up there. I said it didnt matter because I lost on an easy word. She said \"easy words are only easy when nobody is watching.\"\n\nI didnt get it then but I think I do now. Being nervous can make you mess up things you already know. This year I am going to do the bee again and I am going to practice in front of people, not just my dad." },
  { kind: "personal", name: "adult memoir essay about a late mother's recipe cards", text: "The Card for Lemon Bars\n\nMy mother kept her recipes in a tin box that had once held Danish butter cookies, which I suppose was its own kind of joke. When we cleaned out her apartment in March, it was on the counter next to the toaster, where it had been for my entire life. My brother took her good knives and the Dutch oven. I took the tin.\n\nThere are one hundred and twelve cards inside. I know because I counted them on the floor of my kitchen that first night, still in my coat, the way you count things when you don't know what else to do. Some are in her handwriting, which slants hard to the right as if each word is leaning toward the next. Some are in her mother's handwriting, a careful, looping cursive I barely recognize. A few are clipped from the backs of soup cans and taped to index cards, and one, for a casserole involving canned salmon and crushed potato chips, appears to be in the handwriting of a stranger. Nobody alive knows who.\n\nWhat I didn't expect was the marginalia. My mother annotated her recipes the way some people annotate books. \"Too sweet - cut sugar by 1/4.\" \"Dad hated this.\" \"Made for Joanie's shower 1988, ran out.\" On the card for pot roast, beneath the instructions, she had written, in pencil, \"M. likes the carrots best.\" M. is me. I was perhaps seven when I liked the carrots best. I am fifty-three now, and I had no idea she had noticed.\n\nThere is a particular kind of grief that lives in handwriting. A photograph shows you what someone looked like; handwriting shows you what they were doing with their hands while they were thinking about you. Every one of those cards is a small record of my mother standing in a kitchen, deciding something on our behalf. More salt. Less time in the oven. Not this one again; the kids won't eat it.\n\nI have been cooking from the tin since spring. I am not a good cook, and some of her instructions assume a knowledge I never acquired. \"Bake until it looks right.\" \"Enough flour.\" The card for lemon bars says only \"the usual crust,\" as though the usual crust were a fact of nature like gravity. I have made those lemon bars four times. They are not right yet. My husband says they are delicious. He is being kind.\n\nBut I have started, without quite meaning to, to write on the cards myself. In blue ink, beneath her pencil, I wrote on the lemon bar card: \"Try more butter? Crust crumbled. Nov.\" It felt like trespassing at first, and then it felt like the opposite, like answering a letter.\n\nMy daughter is nineteen and does not cook. Someday, I suppose, the tin will be hers. I hope she counts the cards. I hope she finds her name." },
  { kind: "personal", name: "ESL college student personal essay about a first winter after moving from Brazil to Minnesota", text: "Ana Clara Ribeiro\nESL 202 – Composition\nProf. Lindqvist\nNarrative Essay – Final Draft\n\nMy First Snow\n\nBefore I moved to Minnesota I had never seen snow in my life. I grew up in Recife, in the northeast of Brazil, where the temperature is almost always 30 degrees Celsius and in \"winter\" it only rains more. When my father got a job in Minneapolis and we moved in August 2023, everybody in my family was joking that we will become ice. I was laughing too, because I did not understand yet what it really means.\n\nThe first snow came in November. I remember I was in my English class at the community college and a girl next to me said \"oh no, it's starting.\" I looked to the window and saw small white things falling very slow, like ash. I was so excited that I took a video, and the other students were looking at me like I was crazy. For them it was normal, for me it was like a movie.\n\nBut after the excitement, the winter became very hard for me. In January the temperature was minus 25 and the sun goes down at 4:30 in the afternoon. I did not have the right clothes, I was wearing my cousin's jacket that was too thin and sneakers. One morning I waited for the bus for twenty minutes and I could not feel my fingers. I was crying at the bus stop and the tears felt cold on my face. I thought, why did we come here? I missed the beach, my grandmother, the noise of my street. Here everything was quiet and white and I felt very alone.\n\nWhat changed was a small thing. An old woman who lived next to our apartment, Mrs. Halvorsen, saw me walking to the bus in my sneakers. The next day she knocked on our door with a bag. Inside was boots, wool socks, and a hat that she knitted. She told me \"In Minnesota we don't let anyone be cold alone.\" I did not understand everything she said because my English was not good, but I understood that.\n\nAfter that I started to see the winter different. People here are not cold like the weather, they just show it in a different way, with actions more than words. Now it is my second winter. I have good boots and I know how to walk on ice without falling (most of the time). I still miss Recife every day. But when it snows I don't cry anymore. I think about Mrs. Halvorsen and I feel that this place can be my home too." },
  { kind: "personal", name: "personal essay in numbered vignettes about a summer job at a car wash", text: "Things I Learned at Sudz Car Wash, Summer Before Senior Year\n\n1.\nThe first thing they teach you is the towel fold. Quarters, then quarters again, so you always have a clean face. Ray, who had worked there eleven years and was missing the tip of one finger, showed me on my first morning and then made me do it fifty times while he watched. I thought it was hazing. By August I was folding the towels in my room at night without noticing.\n\n2.\nPeople will tell you a lot about themselves through their cupholders. Pacifiers, gas station receipts, a lottery ticket scratched halfway and abandoned. Once, a single red rose wrapped in plastic, wilted, in a minivan with a car seat in the back. I vacuumed around it.\n\n3.\nNobody tips on Tuesdays. I don't know why. Ray didn't know either, and he'd been keeping track since 2013.\n\n4.\nIn July a man in a white BMW yelled at me for leaving a streak on his rear windshield. He called me stupid and lazy and some other things. I said \"I'm sorry, sir\" three times and fixed it, and he drove off without paying the extra five for the wax he'd asked for. I went behind the vacuum station and sat on an upside-down bucket and shook. Ray came over and didn't say anything for a while. Then he said, \"You did the window right. He just had a bad day and you were standing in front of it.\" I have thought about that sentence more than almost anything a teacher has ever said to me.\n\n5.\nYour hands never fully dry. They get soft and wrinkled and then, by the end of the summer, they get tough in a way you can feel when you shake someone's hand.\n\n6.\nI had always thought of jobs like this as something you do before your real life starts. Ray had a mortgage and two daughters in college and a bass boat he was very proud of. This was his real life. It was a good one. I was embarrassed it took me a whole summer to understand that.\n\n7.\nOn my last day he gave me a towel, folded in quarters, then quarters again. It's in my desk drawer. I'm going to take it with me to college, and I am going to keep a clean face." },
  { kind: "coverletter", name: "high school student cover letter for a YMCA summer day camp counselor job", text: "Dear Hiring Manager,\n\nMy name is Jayden Morales and I am a junior at Lincoln High School. I am writing to apply for the Summer Day Camp Counselor position at the Eastside YMCA that I saw on your website. I think I would be a good fit for this job because I love working with kids and I am very responsible.\n\nI have a lot of experience with kids because I have three younger cousins that I babysit almost every weekend. I help them with homework and I make up games for them to play outside so they are not on their tablets all day. I also volunteered at my church's Vacation Bible School last summer where I was a group leader for 8 first graders for a week. It was a lot of work but it was really fun and the parents said their kids liked me.\n\nAt school I am on the JV soccer team and I am in Key Club. Being on a team taught me how to work with other people and how to stay positive even when things are going bad. I am also CPR certified from my health class.\n\nI am available to work from June 16 to August 15 and I can work full time. I have my own transportation.\n\nThank you for considering me for this position. I hope I can have an interview. You can call me at (555) 231-0847 or email me at jaydenm.soccer2009@gmail.com.\n\nSincerely,\nJayden Morales" },
  { kind: "coverletter", name: "college junior cover letter for a summer environmental consulting internship", text: "Rachel Kim\nBurlington, VT | rkim27@uvm.edu | (802) 555-0193\n\nOctober 12, 2026\n\nHiring Committee\nBramwell Environmental Group\n220 Pine Street, Suite 400\nPortland, ME 04101\n\nDear Members of the Hiring Committee,\n\nI am writing to apply for the Summer 2027 Environmental Field Technician Internship at Bramwell Environmental Group. As a junior majoring in Environmental Science with a minor in Geospatial Technologies at the University of Vermont, I am eager to apply my field sampling and GIS skills to the stormwater and wetland assessment projects that Bramwell is known for across northern New England.\n\nThis past summer, I worked as a research assistant in Dr. Helen Ashworth's watershed lab, where I collected and processed more than 200 surface water samples from tributaries of Lake Champlain. I measured turbidity, dissolved oxygen, and phosphorus levels, maintained chain-of-custody records, and entered results into the lab's database. Because many of our sites were only reachable on foot, I also learned to plan efficient sampling routes and to work safely in variable weather, often with just one partner.\n\nIn my GIS coursework, I built a land-cover change model for the Winooski River basin using ArcGIS Pro and publicly available NLCD data, and I presented the results at UVM's Student Research Conference in April. I am comfortable with GPS data collection, making maps for non-technical audiences, and cleaning messy field data in Excel and R.\n\nI am especially interested in Bramwell because of your work helping small municipalities meet their MS4 stormwater permit requirements. I grew up in a town of 3,000 people where the public works department was three employees, and I know how much those communities depend on outside expertise. I would value the chance to learn how consultants turn field data into practical recommendations for clients like that.\n\nI have attached my resume and would welcome the opportunity to discuss how I could contribute to your team this summer. Thank you for your time and consideration.\n\nSincerely,\nRachel Kim" },
  { kind: "coverletter", name: "polished career-changer cover letter, restaurant manager applying for a junior QA analyst role", text: "Dear Ms. Okonkwo,\n\nFor nine years I have run restaurants, and for most of those years my favorite part of the job was the part nobody else wanted: figuring out why things broke. Why the online ordering system double-charged customers on Friday nights. Why a new POS update made the kitchen printers skip every fourth ticket. Why our inventory counts never matched the software, and which of the three possible reasons was the real one. When I saw Ledgerline's posting for a Junior QA Analyst, I recognized the job I have been doing informally for years, and I am excited to apply for it.\n\nAs general manager of Harvest Table, a 140-seat restaurant with a catering operation and two delivery partners, I became the unofficial liaison between our staff and our software vendors. I documented bugs with screenshots, timestamps, and step-by-step reproduction instructions, and I learned quickly that a vague report (\"the printer is broken\") gets ignored while a precise one gets fixed. Over two years I filed more than sixty tickets with our POS provider; their support lead eventually asked whether I would join the beta testing group for new releases, which I did.\n\nTo build on that experience, I completed the Google IT Support certificate last year and am currently finishing a software testing course that covers test case design, regression testing, and the basics of Selenium and SQL. As part of that course I wrote a full test plan for a mock e-commerce site, and I would be glad to share it.\n\nWhat I bring that a new graduate may not is nine years of practice staying calm and methodical when something fails at the worst possible moment, in front of customers. I am used to prioritizing under pressure, explaining technical problems to non-technical people, and following up until an issue is actually resolved rather than temporarily patched. I also know what it is like to be the end user of software that wasn't tested well, and I would like to help make sure Ledgerline's restaurant clients never feel that way.\n\nThank you for considering my application. I would welcome the chance to talk about how my background could support your QA team.\n\nSincerely,\nMichael Torrance" },
  { kind: "coverletter", name: "short cover note typed into a job application portal for a science center front-desk role", place: "boards.greenhouse.io/harborsciencecenter/jobs/4471203", text: "Hi Harbor Science Center team,\n\nI'd love to be considered for the part-time Visitor Services Associate role. I'm a second-year education major at Bayview College and I spent last summer working the front desk at Lakeside Aquarium, so I'm comfortable selling tickets and memberships, answering the same question forty times with a smile, and handling a school group of 60 fourth graders arriving all at once. I'm available weekends and Tuesday/Thursday afternoons. Thanks for reading!\n\nPriya Nair" },
  { kind: "coverletter", name: "ESL applicant cover letter for a hotel front desk agent position", text: "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to apply for the position of Front Desk Agent at Riverside Grand Hotel which I find on Indeed website. I believe my experience in customer service and my language skills make me a good candidate for this position.\n\nI am from Ukraine and I moved to the United States in 2023. In my country I worked for four years as receptionist in a hotel in Lviv with 80 rooms. My responsibilities was check-in and check-out of guests, answering phone calls and emails, making reservations in the Opera system, and solving the problems of guests, for example when the room is not ready or they need a taxi to the airport. Many of our guests was from other countries so I speak Ukrainian, Russian, Polish and English.\n\nSince I came to America I work at a grocery store as cashier, and I also take English classes at the community college two evenings per week. I want to come back to work in the hotel industry because this is my profession and what I really enjoy. I like to meet people from different places and help them to have a good experience.\n\nI am hard working, punctual and I can stay calm when the guest is angry. I am available for morning, evening and weekend shifts.\n\nThank you for your time. I hope to have the opportunity to meet with you for an interview.\n\nBest regards,\nOlena Kravchenko\n(612) 555-0148" },
  { kind: "notes", name: "high school biology class notes on cell membrane transport", text: "Bio notes - Cell Transport 10/7\n\nCell membrane = phospholipid bilayer\n- heads = hydrophilic (love water), tails = hydrophobic (hate water)\n- \"selectively permeable\" -> lets some stuff in/out but not everything\n- proteins stuck in it (channel proteins, carrier proteins), cholesterol keeps it from getting too fluid\n- fluid mosaic model!! (Singer & Nicolson 1972)\n\nPASSIVE TRANSPORT (no energy/ATP)\n- moves high conc -> low conc (down the gradient)\n- diffusion: small nonpolar molecules (O2, CO2) slip right thru\n- facilitated diffusion: uses channel/carrier proteins, for big or charged stuff like glucose, ions\n- osmosis = diffusion of WATER\n* hypotonic solution -> water goes IN, cell swells (animal cell can burst = lysis)\n* hypertonic -> water goes OUT, cell shrinks (plant = plasmolysis)\n* isotonic -> no net movement\n* trick: water follows salt\n\nACTIVE TRANSPORT (needs ATP)\n- low -> high (against gradient)\n- sodium potassium pump: 3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in\n- endocytosis = cell takes stuff in by wrapping membrane around it\n- phagocytosis \"cell eating\" (big particles)\n- pinocytosis \"cell drinking\" (liquids)\n- exocytosis = vesicle fuses w/ membrane, dumps stuff out (ex. hormones, neurotransmitters)\n\nMs. R said know the egg lab for the test (egg in vinegar then corn syrup -> shrinks bc corn syrup is hypertonic)\n\nQ: does facilitated diffusion ever need ATP?? ask tmrw" },
  { kind: "notes", name: "college intro macroeconomics lecture notes on price elasticity of demand", text: "ECON 101 — Lec 9 — Elasticity (Tues 10/1)\n\nPrice elasticity of demand (PED) = how much Qd responds to a change in P\nPED = %ΔQd / %ΔP\n→ always negative bc law of demand, but we usually drop the sign (absolute value)\n\nMidpoint method (USE THIS ON EXAM, prof said twice)\n%ΔQ = (Q2 − Q1) / [(Q2 + Q1)/2]\n%ΔP = (P2 − P1) / [(P2 + P1)/2]\nwhy: gives same answer whether price goes up or down\n\nex from slides: coffee P $2 → $3, Qd 100 → 80\n%ΔQ = −20 / 90 = −22.2%\n%ΔP = 1 / 2.5 = 40%\nPED = 22.2/40 = 0.56 → inelastic\n\nCategories\n|PED| > 1 elastic (Qd changes more than P)\n|PED| < 1 inelastic\n= 1 unit elastic\nperfectly inelastic = vertical demand curve (insulin is his example)\nperfectly elastic = horizontal\n\nWhat makes demand more elastic?\n1. lots of close substitutes (Coke vs Pepsi)\n2. luxury vs necessity\n3. how broadly the market is defined (food = inelastic, \"Doritos\" = elastic)\n4. time horizon - more elastic in long run (gas example, ppl eventually buy a hybrid/move closer)\n5. share of budget\n\nTotal revenue test ← THIS will be on midterm\nTR = P × Q\n- if demand elastic: P↑ → TR↓\n- if inelastic: P↑ → TR↑\n- unit elastic: TR doesn't change\n→ why farmers can be worse off w/ a good harvest (inelastic demand for wheat, P falls a lot, TR falls)\n\ndidn't get the last slide (cross-price elasticity?) — check Canvas, he said posting by Thurs\n\nPS 4 due Fri 11:59, ch 5 #2, 4, 7, 11" },
  { kind: "notes", name: "short textbook reading notes on the Gilded Age with page numbers", text: "APUSH Ch. 16 reading notes — \"The Gilded Age\" pp. 512–531\n\n- name comes from Mark Twain + Charles Dudley Warner novel (1873), gold on the outside, junk underneath (p. 512)\n- politics: elections super close, high turnout (~80%!), parties = mostly patronage machines\n- Garfield shot 1881 by disappointed office seeker → Pendleton Act 1883 (civil service exams) p. 516\n- Rockefeller / Standard Oil = horizontal integration. Carnegie = vertical (owned mines, railroads, mills) p. 519–21\n- \"Social Darwinism\" — Herbert Spencer, William Graham Sumner, used to justify inequality\n- Sherman Antitrust Act 1890 — weakly enforced, ironically used vs unions (p. 524)\n- farmers: falling crop prices + debt → Grange, Farmers' Alliances → Populists (Omaha Platform 1892)\n\n?? still not sure what \"free silver\" actually means, look up / ask Mr. D" },
  { kind: "notes", name: "research notes on microplastics in drinking water, organized by source", text: "RESEARCH NOTES — microplastics in drinking water (for ENVS paper)\n\nThesis idea (rough): microplastics in tap + bottled water are basically everywhere, but health effects are still mostly unknown → need more research + filtration standards?\n\nSOURCE 1: Orb Media tap water study (2017) — Tyree & Morrison, \"Invisibles\"\n- tested ~159 tap water samples from different countries\n- 83% had plastic fibers overall, US highest (~94%)\n- mostly fibers, prob from clothes / laundry + air\n- NOT peer reviewed?? (journalism project w/ U of Minnesota researchers) — be careful how I cite this\n→ use for intro hook (the 94% stat)\n\nSOURCE 2: Mason, Welch & Neratko 2018 — bottled water (Frontiers in Chemistry)\n- 259 bottles, 11 brands, 9 countries\n- 93% had some microplastic\n- avg ~10.4 particles/L bigger than 100 µm, way more smaller ones\n- polypropylene most common → probably from the cap / bottling process\n→ body para 1: bottled ≠ safer\n\nSOURCE 3: WHO report \"Microplastics in drinking-water\" (2019)\n- says based on limited evidence, microplastics in drinking water don't seem to be a health risk at current levels\n- BUT says the data is weak, need more studies, esp on nanoplastics\n- normal water treatment removes >90% of particles\n→ counterargument para!! good bc it's the WHO\n\nSOURCE 4: need something on health effects in humans — try Google Scholar \"microplastics human placenta\" (heard about an Italian study ~2021?)\n\nmisc:\n- \"microplastic\" = < 5 mm. nanoplastic = < 1 µm (some say 100 nm, check)\n- Prof W said min 6 scholarly sources, only 2 so far really count" },
  { kind: "notes", name: "Cornell-style intro psychology notes on memory laid out as a two-column table", text: "Cornell Notes\nTopic: Memory (Ch. 7)\tName: Destiny Harris\nClass: AP Psych\tDate: 11/18\n\nQuestions / Cues\tNotes\nWhat are the 3 stages of memory?\tAtkinson-Shiffrin model (1968): sensory memory → short-term memory → long-term memory\nHow long does sensory memory last?\tIconic (visual) = less than 1 sec. Echoic (sound) = 3-4 sec. Sperling's experiment w/ letter grid showed we see more than we can report\nSTM capacity?\t7 ± 2 items (George Miller). Lasts ~20 sec without rehearsal. Chunking helps (phone #s, FBI CIA)\nDifference b/w STM and working memory?\tBaddeley: working memory is active, has parts: phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, central executive (+ episodic buffer added later)\nTypes of LTM\tExplicit (declarative): episodic (events, your 8th bday) + semantic (facts). Implicit (nondeclarative): procedural (riding a bike), priming, classical conditioning\nEncoding tips\tShallow → deep processing (Craik & Lockhart). Self-reference effect. Spacing effect > cramming. Testing effect!!\nWhy do we forget?\tEbbinghaus forgetting curve: drops fast then levels off. Interference: proactive (old blocks new) vs retroactive (new blocks old)\n\nSummary: Memory moves from sensory to short term to long term, and things only get into long term if we pay attention and encode them deeply. To remember better for the test I should space out studying and quiz myself instead of rereading." },
  { kind: "survey", name: "student council survey about library hours and study spaces", text: "Library & Study Space Survey\nBrought to you by Roosevelt High Student Council\n\nWe want to know how you use the library and what would make it better. This survey is anonymous and takes about 3 minutes. Results will be shared with Ms. Gallagher and the admin team at the December meeting.\n\n1. What grade are you in?\n○ 9th  ○ 10th  ○ 11th  ○ 12th\n\n2. How often do you use the school library?\n○ Every day\n○ A few times a week\n○ A few times a month\n○ Almost never\n○ Never\n\n3. When do you usually go to the library? (check all that apply)\n☐ Before school\n☐ During lunch\n☐ During my free period / study hall\n☐ After school\n☐ With my class\n\n4. What do you mostly use the library for? (check all that apply)\n☐ Studying alone\n☐ Group projects\n☐ Printing\n☐ Checking out books\n☐ Using the computers\n☐ Somewhere quiet to hang out\n☐ Other: ____________\n\n5. The library is currently open 7:15 a.m. – 3:30 p.m. Would you use it if it stayed open until 5:00 p.m.?\n○ Yes, at least once a week\n○ Maybe sometimes\n○ No\n\n6. How much do you agree with each statement? (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree)\na. It's easy to find a seat in the library during lunch.   1   2   3   4   5\nb. There is enough space for group work.   1   2   3   4   5\nc. The library is quiet enough to study.   1   2   3   4   5\nd. I feel welcome in the library.   1   2   3   4   5\n\n7. If we could add ONE thing, what should it be?\n○ More group study rooms\n○ A silent study zone\n○ More outlets / charging stations\n○ Longer hours\n○ Comfier seating\n○ Something else: ____________\n\n8. Anything else you want us to know?\n______________________________________\n\nThanks!!" },
  { kind: "survey", name: "college research methods questionnaire on student food insecurity, with consent statement", text: "Student Food Access Survey\n\nConsent: You are invited to take part in a research study conducted by students in SOC 340 (Research Methods) at Whitfield State University under the supervision of Dr. Lorraine Becker. The purpose of the study is to understand how often Whitfield students have trouble affording enough food. Participation is voluntary, you may skip any question, and you may stop at any time. The survey takes about 10 minutes. Your answers are anonymous; we will not collect your name or student ID. If you have questions, contact the research team at soc340foodstudy@whitfield.edu. By continuing, you confirm that you are 18 or older and agree to participate.\n\nSection A: About You\nA1. What is your current class standing?\nFreshman / Sophomore / Junior / Senior / Graduate student\n\nA2. Where do you currently live?\nOn campus, with a meal plan\nOn campus, without a meal plan\nOff campus, alone or with roommates\nOff campus, with parents or family\nOther (please specify): ________\n\nA3. Do you currently have a job? If yes, about how many hours a week do you work?\nNo / Yes, under 10 hours / Yes, 10–20 hours / Yes, more than 20 hours\n\nA4. Do you receive a Pell Grant?\nYes / No / Not sure\n\nSection B: Food in the Past 30 Days\nFor each statement, please tell us whether it was often true, sometimes true, or never true for you in the last 30 days.\n\nB1. \"I worried whether my food would run out before I got money to buy more.\"\nOften true / Sometimes true / Never true\n\nB2. \"The food that I bought just didn't last, and I didn't have money to get more.\"\nOften true / Sometimes true / Never true\n\nB3. \"I couldn't afford to eat balanced meals.\"\nOften true / Sometimes true / Never true\n\nB4. In the last 30 days, did you ever cut the size of your meals or skip meals because there wasn't enough money for food?\nYes / No\n\nB5. If yes, on how many days did this happen?\n1–2 days / 3–5 days / 6–10 days / More than 10 days\n\nB6. In the last 30 days, were you ever hungry but didn't eat because there wasn't enough money for food?\nYes / No\n\nSection C: Campus Resources\nC1. Before today, had you heard of the Hawk Pantry in the Student Union?\nYes / No\n\nC2. Have you used the Hawk Pantry this semester?\nNever / Once / 2–4 times / 5 or more times\n\nC3. If you have NOT used the Hawk Pantry, what are the main reasons? (Select all that apply)\nI don't need it\nI didn't know where it was\nThe hours don't work for me\nI feel embarrassed\nI think other students need it more\nOther: ________\n\nC4. On a scale of 1 to 5, how comfortable would you feel using a campus food pantry? (1 = very uncomfortable, 5 = very comfortable)\n1   2   3   4   5\n\nC5. Is there anything else you would like to share about food access at Whitfield?\n________________________________________\n\nThank you for your time. If you are experiencing food insecurity, the Hawk Pantry (Student Union, Room 114) is open Mon–Thu, 11 a.m.–6 p.m., no questions asked." },
  { kind: "survey", name: "auto repair shop customer feedback card", text: "DeLuca's Auto Service – How Did We Do?\n\nThank you for trusting us with your vehicle. Please take a minute to fill this out and drop it in the box at the front counter. Every card gets read by Sal.\n\nDate of service: ________   Vehicle: ________\n\nPlease circle a number: 1 = poor, 5 = excellent\nEase of scheduling your appointment\t1  2  3  4  5\nFriendliness of our staff\t1  2  3  4  5\nExplanation of the work needed\t1  2  3  4  5\nWas your car ready when promised?\t1  2  3  4  5\nFairness of price\t1  2  3  4  5\nCleanliness of your vehicle when returned\t1  2  3  4  5\n\nWere you contacted before any additional work was done?   YES   NO\n\nHow did you hear about us?\n☐ Friend/family  ☐ Google  ☐ Drove by  ☐ Repeat customer  ☐ Other ______\n\nWould you recommend DeLuca's to a friend?   Definitely   Probably   Not sure   No\n\nComments: ______________________________________________\n\nName/phone (optional, if you'd like us to follow up): ______________" },
  { kind: "survey", name: "company employee pulse survey on the hybrid work schedule, with a Likert grid", text: "Hybrid Work Pulse Survey – Q3\nPeople & Culture Team | Northgate Logistics\n\nHi all — it's been six months since we moved to the Tuesday–Thursday in-office schedule. Before leadership revisits the policy in October, we want to hear from you. This survey is confidential: responses are collected by our survey vendor and reported to us only in groups of 5 or more. It should take 5–7 minutes. Please complete it by Friday, Sept. 26.\n\nPart 1. Rate how much you agree with each statement.\nStatement\tStrongly disagree\tDisagree\tNeutral\tAgree\tStrongly agree\nI am as productive on in-office days as on remote days.\t○\t○\t○\t○\t○\nMy team uses in-office days for work that benefits from being together.\t○\t○\t○\t○\t○\nI have the equipment I need to work well from home.\t○\t○\t○\t○\t○\nI can usually find a desk or meeting room when I need one in the office.\t○\t○\t○\t○\t○\nMy commute on in-office days is manageable.\t○\t○\t○\t○\t○\nI feel connected to people outside my immediate team.\t○\t○\t○\t○\t○\nMy manager is flexible when I need to swap my in-office days.\t○\t○\t○\t○\t○\n\nPart 2.\n1. On a typical in-office day, how many hours do you spend on video calls anyway?\na. 0–1\nb. 1–3\nc. 3–5\nd. More than 5\n\n2. If you could choose, how many days per week would you work in the office?\na. 0\nb. 1\nc. 2\nd. 3\ne. 4 or 5\n\n3. Which department are you in?\na. Operations\nb. Sales\nc. Finance\nd. IT\ne. People & Culture\nf. Other / prefer not to say\n\nPart 3. Open comments (optional)\n4. What is one thing that would make in-office days more worthwhile?\n\n5. Anything else you'd like leadership to know?\n\nThank you for your honesty. We'll share a summary of the results at the October all-hands." },
  { kind: "survey", name: "middle school statistics project survey written by two students", text: "Math Project Survey - by Owen and Lucas (Mr. Park's 3rd period)\n\nPlease answer these for our statistics project!! Don't put your name.\n\n1. How do you usually get to school?\na. bus\nb. car (parent drives)\nc. walk\nd. bike/scooter\ne. other\n\n2. About how long does it take you to get to school?\na. less than 10 min\nb. 10-20 min\nc. 20-30 min\nd. more than 30 min\n\n3. Did you eat breakfast today? yes / no\n\n4. What time did you wake up today? ______\n\n5. On a scale of 1-10 how awake do you feel right now? (1 = basically asleep, 10 = super awake)\n\n6. Do you think school should start later? yes / no / dont care\n\nThank you!!!" },
  { kind: "survey", name: "teacher's end-of-unit feedback survey for an 8th grade ecosystems unit (could be mistaken for homework)", text: "Ecosystems Unit – Student Feedback\nMrs. Abernathy, 8th Grade Science\n\nI'm using your answers to decide what to keep and what to change when I teach this unit next year, so please be honest!\n\n1. How confident do you feel about each of these topics now? (1 = I'm lost, 4 = I could teach it)\nFood chains and food webs\t1\t2\t3\t4\nProducers, consumers, decomposers\t1\t2\t3\t4\nEnergy pyramids / the 10% rule\t1\t2\t3\t4\nBiotic vs. abiotic factors\t1\t2\t3\t4\nInvasive species\t1\t2\t3\t4\nCarrying capacity and population graphs\t1\t2\t3\t4\n\n2. Which activity helped you understand food webs the MOST?\na. The yarn web game (everyone was an organism)\nb. Owl pellet dissection\nc. The Yellowstone wolves video\nd. Building our own food web posters\ne. The online simulation (Ecosystem Builder)\n\n3. Which activity helped you the LEAST?\na. The yarn web game\nb. Owl pellet dissection\nc. The Yellowstone wolves video\nd. Food web posters\ne. Online simulation\n\n4. The pond water lab took three class periods. Was that\n○ too long  ○ about right  ○ too short\n\n5. How did you feel about the amount of homework in this unit?\n○ too much  ○ about right  ○ too little\n\n6. Did you like working with your lab group?\n○ yes  ○ mostly  ○ not really\n\n7. If you could change one thing about this unit, what would it be?\n\n8. What is one thing about ecosystems you think you'll still remember in 5 years?" },
  { kind: "summary", name: "10th grade summary and response to a magazine article on the four-day school week, MLA heading", text: "Jaylen Brooks\nMs. Ortega\nEnglish 10, Period 4\n14 October 2026\nSummary and Response: \"Four Days a Week\"\n\nThe article \"Four Days a Week\" by Dana Whitfield, published in Education Weekly Digest, is about school districts in rural America that have switched to a four-day school week. Whitfield explains that more than 900 districts have made the switch, mostly in states like Missouri, Colorado and Oklahoma. The main reason districts give is money. They think they will save on things like buses, heating and substitute teachers. Another reason is hiring. Whitfield interviews a superintendent in Missouri who says the four-day week is \"the only perk we can offer that the city schools can't.\"\n\nThe article also talks about the downsides. Whitfield says that the savings are usually smaller than districts expect, about 1 to 2 percent of the budget. She also mentions a study that found students in four-day districts did a little worse on state tests after a few years. Parents who work full time have to find childcare on Fridays, which costs them the money the district saved. The article ends by saying that the four-day week is \"a trade, not a free lunch,\" and that every community has to decide what it cares about more.\n\nI think the article was fair because it showed both sides and let people from the districts speak for themselves. My cousin goes to a school in southern Missouri that has a four-day week and she loves it, but my aunt has to pay a babysitter for my little cousin every Friday, so I could see both sides in my own family. One thing I wish the article did was talk to more students. Almost all the quotes are from adults, superintendents, teachers and parents, but students are the ones actually sitting in the classroom for the longer days.\n\nOverall I agree with Whitfield that it depends on the community. If I had to vote for my school I would probably vote no, because I already have a hard time paying attention in seventh period and I can't imagine an even longer day." },
  { kind: "summary", name: "college education-course reaction paper on Ken Robinson's TED talk, polished", text: "Reaction Paper 2: Ken Robinson, \"Do Schools Kill Creativity?\"\nEDUC 201, Foundations of Education\nKen Robinson's 2006 TED talk, \"Do Schools Kill Creativity?\", remains one of the most watched talks on the platform, and after viewing it twice I understand why. Robinson's central claim is that modern school systems, designed during the industrial era to meet the needs of industrialism, rank subjects in a hierarchy, with mathematics and languages at the top and the arts at the bottom, and in doing so educate children out of their natural creative capacities. He argues that creativity should be treated with the same status as literacy, and that a child who is not prepared to be wrong will never come up with anything original.\nRobinson supports his argument mostly through stories. The best known is the story of Gillian Lynne, a girl whose school thought she had a learning disorder because she could not sit still; a doctor instead recognized that she was a dancer, and she went on to choreograph Cats and The Phantom of the Opera. He also describes a six-year-old drawing a picture of God who assures her teacher that people will know what God looks like in a minute, and he jokes that university professors, the supposed endpoint of the system, tend to live in their heads.\nMy reaction is mixed. As someone planning to teach elementary school, I found the talk energizing, and the point about fear of being wrong rang true. In my practicum classroom this fall, I watched a second grader erase the same correct answer three times because she was not sure it was what the teacher wanted. That is exactly the habit Robinson is describing, and it starts early.\nHowever, the talk is stronger as inspiration than as evidence. Nearly every claim rests on an anecdote, and the humor, while effective, sometimes covers for the lack of data. Robinson never defines creativity in a way that could be measured, so it is hard to know what killing it would look like, or how we would notice if schools improved. The Gillian Lynne story also depends on one perceptive adult rather than on any change to the system, which arguably undercuts his own point about structural reform.\nStill, I think the talk is worth assigning to future teachers precisely because it is a provocation. It does not tell me what to do on Monday morning, but it makes me want to look harder at which kinds of intelligence my lessons reward and which ones they quietly ignore." },
  { kind: "summary", name: "ESL college student's summary of an intro sociology textbook chapter on socialization", text: "Summary of Chapter 5 – Socialization\nSOC 101 / Yuki Tanabe\n\nThis chapter is about socialization, which mean the process how people learn the norms, values and behavior of their society. The author say that we are not born knowing how to act, we learn it from other people during whole life.\n\nFirst the chapter explain the nature vs nurture debate. Some scientist believe genes decide personality but the chapter show cases like \"feral children\" who grow up without human contact and they can not speak or act normal even after rescued. This show that social contact is very important for development.\nNext part is about theorists. George Herbert Mead said the self develop through three stages, preparatory, play and game stage. In game stage children understand rules and the \"generalized other\". Also Charles Cooley has idea of \"looking glass self\", we see ourself through how other people react to us.\nThen chapter talk about agents of socialization: family, peer group, school, workplace, religion and mass media. Family is the first and most important one. School teach not only subjects but also hidden curriculum like waiting in line, being on time, raising hand.\n\nLast section is about socialization across the life course and resocialization, for example people who join military or go to prison have to learn new rules in \"total institution\" (Goffman).\n\nI think this chapter is interesting for me because when I moved to US at 17 I had to learn many new norms, like small talk with cashier, which is not normal in Japan. So I experienced something like resocialization myself." },
  { kind: "summary", name: "very short middle school summary of a news-for-kids article about octopus intelligence", text: "Name: Ava R.   Period 2\nArticle: \"Octopuses Escape Tanks, Unscrew Jars and Remember Faces\"\nSummary:\nThis article is about how smart octopuses are. Scientists found out that octopuses can open jars to get food, escape from their tanks, and even tell different people apart. One aquarium octopus kept squirting water at one worker it did not like. The article says octopuses have most of their neurons in their arms. I think it is cool and kind of creepy that they can remember faces." },
  { kind: "summary", name: "undergraduate kinesiology article critique of a standing-desk study, with headings", text: "Article Critique #2\nKIN 340: Physical Activity and Health in Schools\nMarisol Ferreira-Diaz\n\nCitation\nKessler, T., Ramos, J., & Tull, A. (2021). Effects of standing desks on on-task behavior in fourth-grade classrooms. Journal of School Health Research, 14(3), 211–224.\n\nSummary\nKessler and colleagues investigated whether replacing traditional seated desks with sit-stand desks changes how much time fourth graders spend on task. The study took place in six classrooms at two elementary schools in the same district. Three classrooms received sit-stand desks at the start of the spring semester and three kept their usual furniture. Trained observers recorded on-task behavior using momentary time sampling for twenty minutes, twice a week, for twelve weeks. Students also wore accelerometers for one week at baseline and one week at the end.\nThe authors report that on-task behavior rose from 71% to 79% in the standing-desk classrooms, while the comparison classrooms stayed around 72%. Sedentary time during school hours dropped by an average of 34 minutes per day in the intervention group. They conclude that sit-stand desks are a low-cost way to increase light physical activity without harming, and possibly while improving, classroom engagement.\n\nStrengths\nThe study uses direct observation rather than teacher ratings, which reduces some bias, and the observers were reportedly blind to the hypothesis. Combining behavioral data with accelerometer data is also a strength, since it shows the desks actually changed how much students moved and did not simply change how the room looked.\n\nLimitations\nThe biggest problem is the sample. With only six classrooms, which is the unit that received the intervention, the study is very small, and the classrooms were not randomly assigned; the principal chose which teachers received the new desks. Teachers who volunteer for new furniture may already run more engaging classrooms. The observers could see the desks, so they could not truly be blind to condition. Twelve weeks is also short enough that a novelty effect could explain much of the change. Finally, the authors do not report effect sizes or account for students being nested within classrooms, which may make the results look more certain than they are.\n\nOverall Evaluation\nThis is a useful pilot study, and its conclusion is reasonable as long as it is read as preliminary. I would not recommend that a district buy standing desks based on this article alone, but it makes a good case for a larger, randomized trial across more schools and a full school year." },
  { kind: "casestudy", name: "business school group case study of a family bakery's expansion, SWOT and recommendations, with a table", text: "Case Study: Rosalind's Bakehouse\nMGMT 3150 – Small Business Strategy\nPrepared by Owen Pruitt and Tasha Greer\n\nBackground\nRosalind's Bakehouse was founded in 2011 by Rosalind Marchetti in a converted gas station in Fairhaven, Ohio. The business sells bread, pastries and custom cakes, and it built a loyal following through farmers markets and word of mouth. In 2020 Marchetti's son, Daniel, joined as operations manager, and between 2021 and 2024 the company opened two additional locations: a café in the downtown district of nearby Brennan and a kiosk inside the Lakeshore Commons mall.\nRevenue and operating margin by location, 2025:\nLocation\tRevenue\tOperating margin\nFairhaven (original)\t$1,140,000\t14%\nBrennan café\t$620,000\t3%\nLakeshore kiosk\t$210,000\t-9%\n\nThe Problem\nDespite total revenue growth of nearly 60% since 2020, overall profit has fallen. Daniel Marchetti attributes the decline to labor costs and to the mall kiosk, which has never broken even. At the same time, the family is considering a fourth location after a developer offered a reduced lease in a new mixed-use building. The central question for the owners is whether Rosalind's should keep expanding, consolidate, or change its model.\n\nAnalysis\nA SWOT analysis clarifies the situation. The company's main strength is its reputation for quality in Fairhaven, where repeat customers account for an estimated 70% of sales. Its key weakness is that all baking happens at the original site, so products for Brennan and Lakeshore are driven over each morning, adding delivery costs and limiting freshness at the satellite locations. Opportunities include wholesale accounts with local restaurants and grocery stores, which several have requested, and online preorders for custom cakes, which currently require a phone call. Threats include rising flour and butter prices and a national bakery-café chain that opened two miles from the Brennan café in 2025.\nThe numbers suggest the problem is not demand but structure. The kiosk sells mostly impulse items at low prices while carrying mall rent and two full-time staff. The Brennan café is closer to profitable, but it is competing on atmosphere against a chain with a far larger marketing budget, an area where Rosalind's has no advantage.\n\nRecommendations\n1. Close the Lakeshore kiosk when its lease ends in March 2027 and reassign its staff to Fairhaven and Brennan.\n2. Decline the fourth retail location for now.\n3. Use the savings to add a second oven shift at Fairhaven and pilot a wholesale program with three to five restaurant accounts, which uses the company's real strength, its product, without new rent.\n4. Add online ordering for custom cakes, since these orders carry the highest margin of any product line.\n5. Re-evaluate the Brennan café after twelve months against a clear target, such as a 7% operating margin.\nThese steps would let Rosalind's grow in a way that builds on what made the original bakery successful instead of spreading the brand thin." },
  { kind: "casestudy", name: "nursing student's clinical patient case study of a heart failure patient, clinical shorthand", text: "NURS 312 Medical-Surgical Nursing I\nClinical Case Study – Week 6\nStudent: Brianna Holt   Clinical Site: St. Agnes Medical Center, 4 West\nPatient Profile\nR.T. is a 68 y/o male admitted 9/29 with acute exacerbation of chronic heart failure (HFrEF, EF 30%). PMH: HTN, type 2 DM, MI in 2019 w/ stent x2, CKD stage 3a. Retired long-haul truck driver, lives with wife in a 2-story home, bedroom upstairs. Former smoker (40 pack-years, quit 2019).\nHistory of Present Illness\nPt reports 5 days of increasing SOB, needing 3 pillows to sleep, and swelling in both ankles. Wife states he \"ran out of his water pill\" about a week before admission and did not refill it because the pharmacy was closed over the weekend. He also admits to eating \"a lot of deli meat and canned soup\" at a family reunion.\nAssessment Data (day 2 of admission)\nVS: BP 148/92, HR 98, RR 24, SpO2 91% on 2L NC, T 98.4\nWeight 104.6 kg (admission 106.9 kg, dry weight per pt ~101 kg)\nLungs: crackles bilateral bases. Heart: S3 present\n2+ pitting edema bilateral lower extremities\nBNP 1,240 pg/mL, K+ 3.4, Cr 1.6\nMeds: furosemide 40 mg IV BID, metoprolol succinate, lisinopril (held d/t Cr), insulin sliding scale\nNursing Diagnoses\n1. Excess fluid volume r/t compromised regulatory mechanisms and excess sodium intake AEB edema, crackles, weight gain, and S3.\n2. Impaired gas exchange r/t alveolar-capillary membrane changes (pulmonary congestion) AEB SpO2 91% and dyspnea.\n3. Deficient knowledge r/t medication regimen and sodium restriction AEB running out of diuretic and high-sodium diet.\nInterventions and Rationale\n- Daily weights, same time, same scale, before breakfast; strict I&O (tracks fluid status)\n- HOB elevated 45 degrees, O2 titrated to keep SpO2 above 92%\n- Monitor K+ with IV furosemide; pt received 20 mEq KCl PO on day 2\n- Fluid restriction 1.5 L/day, 2 g sodium diet\n- Teach-back education with pt and wife: daily weights at home, call MD for gain of 2-3 lb in a day or 5 lb in a week, reading labels for sodium, setting up auto-refill with pharmacy\nEvaluation\nBy end of shift on day 2, pt was down 2.3 kg from admission and SpO2 was 93% on 2L. Crackles persisted at bases. Pt was able to state 3 high-sodium foods to avoid and his daily weight goal; wife plans to set up mail-order pharmacy. Diagnosis #3 partially met. Will continue to monitor and reinforce teaching before discharge. One thing I would do differently is involve the wife earlier, since she does most of the cooking." },
  { kind: "casestudy", name: "high school economics case study on Blockbuster's decline, average writer, no headings", text: "Case Study: What Happened to Blockbuster?\nby Ethan Moreau\n\nIn 2004 Blockbuster had around 9,000 stores and tens of thousands of employees, and it seemed like every town had one. By 2010 the company filed for bankruptcy and today there is only one Blockbuster left, in Bend, Oregon. This case study looks at what went wrong and what the company could have done differently.\n\nBlockbuster's business was renting movies and video games from physical stores. A big part of its money came from late fees, around $800 million in the year 2000. Customers hated late fees but the company depended on them.\n\nIn 2000 the founders of Netflix, Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph, met with Blockbuster CEO John Antioco and offered to sell Netflix for $50 million. Blockbuster turned them down. At the time Netflix was a small company that mailed DVDs and it was losing money, so the decision made sense to some people back then.\n\nThe main problem was that Blockbuster did not want to change a business model that was still making money. Netflix had no late fees, which customers loved. When Blockbuster finally started its own online service in 2004 and got rid of late fees, it lost a lot of money doing it and some investors were angry. Antioco left in 2007 and the new CEO, Jim Keyes, focused on the stores again. Meanwhile Netflix started streaming in 2007 and Redbox kiosks were renting DVDs for $1 a night.\n\nIn my opinion Blockbuster's biggest mistake wasn't saying no to buying Netflix, it was going back to the stores after 2007 when they were finally starting to compete online. If I was in charge I would of kept the online service and closed stores faster, because stores were expensive to run (rent, workers, inventory).\n\nThe lesson from this case is that a company can be number 1 and still fail if it does not see how technology is changing what customers want. Kodak is another example of this." },
  { kind: "casestudy", name: "teacher candidate's messy draft case study of a struggling third-grade reader, notes to self", text: "EDU 455 – Literacy Assessment & Intervention\nCase Study Draft (Student \"M\")\nLauren Pike — DRAFT, still need to add scores!!\n\nBackground\nM is an 8 year old third grader at the Title I elementary school where I am doing my practicum. She is the youngest of three and lives with her mom and grandmother. English is her first language. Her classroom teacher, Mrs. K, referred her for the case study because she reads \"word by word\" and avoids reading aloud. M's attendance in 2nd grade was low (missed 23 days) because of a family move mid-year. She has no IEP and passed her hearing and vision screening in Sept.\n\nAssessment\nI gave M three assessments over two weeks:\n* Oral reading fluency (DIBELS 8) — [add WCPM and accuracy here, I think it was 52 WCPM? check binder]\n* Phonics survey — strong on CVC words and digraphs, inconsistent with vowel teams (read \"boat\" as \"bot\", \"rain\" as \"ran\") and multisyllable words\n* Informal reading inventory — instructional level end of 1st / beginning of 2nd grade. Comprehension was actually ok when I read to her (listening comp at grade level!!)\n\nAnalysis\nThe pattern points to a decoding problem more than a comprehension problem. Because M is spending so much effort sounding out vowel teams, her reading is slow and choppy, which makes it hard for her to hold on to meaning. Her listening comprehension being at grade level matters because it shows she understands language fine. I think the missed days in 2nd grade lined up with when vowel teams are usually taught, which might explain the gap (need to ask Mrs. K about the 2nd grade scope and sequence). The avoidance seems to come from embarrassment, she told me \"the other kids read faster than me so I just wait.\"\n\nRecommendations\n1. Explicit, systematic small group instruction on vowel teams 4x/week, 15 min (maybe use the UFLI lessons the school already has)\n2. Repeated reading with decodable texts to build fluency, tracking WCPM weekly so M can see a graph of her own progress\n3. Partner reading instead of round robin so she is not reading aloud in front of the whole class\n4. Keep reading grade level books TO her for vocabulary and comprehension\n[need a 5th? — Dr. Alvarez said 4 is fine]\n\nProgress monitoring — TBD after week 3" },
  { kind: "casestudy", name: "engineering ethics group case study of the 1981 Hyatt Regency walkway collapse", text: "Case Study: The Hyatt Regency Walkway Collapse (1981)\nENGR 2010 Engineering Ethics\nGroup 4: K. Abernathy, L. Duong, S. Mensah\n\nBackground\nOn the evening of July 17, 1981, about 1,600 people were gathered in the atrium of the Hyatt Regency hotel in Kansas City, Missouri, for a tea dance. Spectators were standing on suspended walkways that crossed the atrium at the second, third and fourth floors. At 7:05 p.m., the fourth-floor walkway, which hung directly above the second-floor walkway, broke loose, and both fell onto the crowded lobby floor. One hundred fourteen people were killed and more than 200 were injured. It remained the deadliest structural collapse in U.S. history until the September 11 attacks.\n\nThe Technical Failure\nIn the original design, the second- and fourth-floor walkways hung from the same continuous steel rods, which ran from the ceiling through the fourth-floor box beams and down to the second-floor beams. During construction, the steel fabricator, Havens Steel, proposed replacing each long rod with two shorter rods: one from the ceiling to the fourth-floor beam, and a second from the fourth-floor beam down to the second floor. The change looked minor on paper and made assembly easier, but it meant the fourth-floor box beam connection now carried the load of both walkways instead of one. Investigators from the National Bureau of Standards found that even the original connection would not have met the Kansas City building code, and the modified version roughly doubled the load on it. The nut and washer pulled through the bottom of the box beam.\n\nEthical Analysis\nThe central ethical failure was in communication and responsibility for review. The fabricator said it called the engineering firm about the change; the engineers said they were never asked to review connection calculations, which they believed were the fabricator's job. Shop drawings showing the double-rod arrangement nonetheless went out with the engineer of record's seal. Under the NSPE Code of Ethics, engineers must hold paramount the safety, health and welfare of the public and may only approve documents that conform to applicable standards. Applying a seal to drawings that had not been checked violates both. There was also a warning sign: in 1979 part of the atrium roof collapsed during construction, and the owner asked for a review of the structure, but the walkway connections were not specifically rechecked.\nIn 1985 Missouri revoked the professional engineering licenses of the engineer of record and the project engineer, and their firm lost its authority to practice in the state.\n\nLessons and Recommendations\n1. A design change, however small, must go back to the engineer responsible for it, with a written record of who checked what.\n2. A professional seal means personal responsibility for the entire document, not only the parts one firm prepared.\n3. Firms should use independent checks on critical connections, especially where one element supports others.\n4. Warning events, like the 1979 roof failure, should trigger broad reviews, not narrow ones.\nFor us as future engineers, the case shows that most disasters do not come from one bad calculation but from the gaps between people, where everyone assumes someone else is checking." },
  { kind: "feature", name: "high school newspaper profile of a retiring head custodian", text: "After 31 Years, \"Mr. V\" Hangs Up His Keys\nBy Sofia Lindqvist, Staff Writer\nIf you have ever lost a retainer in the cafeteria trash, there is a decent chance Ernesto Villalobos found it.\n\"Eleven retainers,\" he said, counting on his fingers. \"Two class rings. One prom ticket, the day before prom. That kid hugged me.\"\nVillalobos, known to nearly everyone at Westbrook High as Mr. V, will retire on Nov. 21 after 31 years as a custodian, the last 14 as head custodian. He started in 1995, when the school still had chalkboards in every classroom and the main gym was what is now the auxiliary gym.\nHis day starts at 5:30 a.m., when he unlocks the building, checks the boilers and walks every hallway \"to see what the night left me.\" He says he knows the building the way some people know a car. \"This school talks. The radiators in the 200 hall bang when it's going to snow. I'm not kidding, ask anyone.\"\nTeachers say his job has always gone beyond cleaning. Spanish teacher Rosa Delgado, who has worked at Westbrook for 22 years, remembers a night in 2008 when the power went out during the winter concert. \"Mr. V had flashlights out to every row in about five minutes,\" she said. \"The choir finished by flashlight. People still talk about it.\"\nStudents know him mostly from lunch, where he has been a fixture by the trash cans, quietly asking people to sort their recycling. Senior Malik Owens said Villalobos was the first adult at Westbrook to learn his name. \"Freshman year I didn't really know anybody,\" Owens said. \"He'd just say 'Malik, how's it going' every day. It sounds small but it wasn't.\"\nVillalobos grew up in El Paso and moved here in 1991 with his wife, Carmen. They have three children, two of whom graduated from Westbrook. He plans to spend his retirement fishing, visiting his grandchildren in Arizona and, he admits, probably stopping by.\n\"They told me I can come to the basketball games for free now,\" he said. \"So they're going to see me.\"\nA send-off will be held in the main gym on Nov. 20 during seventh period. Students can sign a card in the front office." },
  { kind: "feature", name: "neighborhood newsletter Q&A interview with a backyard beekeeper", text: "Meet Your Neighbor: Harriet Bloom of Bloom Hill Apiary\nMaple Ridge Neighborhood Association Newsletter, Fall Issue\n\nHarriet Bloom has kept bees on her half-acre lot on Juniper Lane for 17 years, and if you've bought honey at the Saturday market, you've probably tasted her work. We sat down on her porch (at a safe distance from the hives) to talk about bees, neighbors, and the year she got stung 40 times.\n\nHow did you get started?\nMy late husband bought me a starter hive for my 50th birthday as a joke, because I'd been reading about colony collapse and wouldn't stop talking about it. He thought it would sit in the garage. I had bees in it within a month.\n\nHow many hives do you have now?\nNine, which is too many, according to my knees. In a good year that's around 400 pounds of honey.\n\nWhat do the neighbors think?\nMost people don't notice them. Bees don't want anything to do with you unless you're standing in front of the hive. I face the hives toward the back fence so their flight path goes up and over. And I give everyone on the block a jar at Christmas. That helps.\n\nAbout those 40 stings...\n(Laughs.) That was 2014. I opened a hive on a cloudy day, which you should never do, and I wasn't wearing gloves because I thought I was tough. I was not tough. I sat in a cold bath and ate ice cream.\n\nWhat's the hardest part?\nLosing colonies over winter. Some years I lose one, some years four. Varroa mites are the big enemy. You do everything right and sometimes they just don't make it.\n\nWhat can neighbors do to help pollinators?\nDon't spray the dandelions! Plant something that flowers late in the season, like asters or goldenrod. And leave a little mess in your yard. Bees like a messy yard.\n\nAny plans to slow down?\nMy grandson is 14 and he's been suiting up with me since he was nine. I'm hoping to hand the hives over to him someday. Not yet, though.\n\nBloom Hill honey is available at the Maple Ridge Saturday Market through October, or by knocking on the door with the bee mailbox." },
  { kind: "feature", name: "freelancer's unfinished magazine trend feature on retirees and pickleball, with TK placeholders", text: "DRAFT for Valley Living, Jan issue. ~1,200 words when done. Need photos from Gwen.\nThe Dink Generation\nIn Cedar Hollow, the hottest courts in town belong to people who remember the moon landing.\nAt 7:15 on a Tuesday morning in October, it is 38 degrees at Cedar Hollow Community Park, and every one of the eight pickleball courts is full. A man in a knit hat and a knee brace slaps a paddle against his thigh to stay warm while he waits. A woman with a white braid down her back calls the score, \"four, two, one,\" in a voice that carries across the parking lot.\nMost of the players here are over 60. Several are over 80. Nobody seems to think that's unusual.\n\"I hadn't played a sport since high school softball,\" said Lorraine Pettibone, 71, a retired dental office manager who started playing in 2022 after her doctor told her to find something that got her out of the house. She now plays five mornings a week. \"I've made more friends in four years on this court than I did in thirty years at church. Don't print that.\" (CHECK w/ Lorraine, she laughed but maybe she meant it)\nPickleball, a cross between tennis, badminton and ping-pong played with a perforated plastic ball, has been called the fastest-growing sport in America for several years running. Nationally, about TK million people played in 2025, according to TK (SFIA? get latest number). But in retirement-heavy towns like Cedar Hollow, where TK percent of residents are over 65, the sport has become something closer to infrastructure.\nThe city converted two of its tennis courts to pickleball in 2021, after a petition with 340 signatures. It added four more last year. Parks director Dwayne Kesey said demand still outpaces space. \"We get more emails about pickleball than about potholes,\" he said. \"I'm not exaggerating. I could show you.\"\nThe appeal, players say, is partly physical (the court is smaller than a tennis court, and the underhand serve is easier on old shoulders) and partly social. Games are short, partners rotate, and the bench by the gate turns into a sort of coffee klatch. A group calling itself the Early Dinks has been meeting since 2022 and now has a group chat of TK members (ask Ray).\nNot everyone is thrilled. Residents of the townhomes along Birch Street have complained about the noise, a sharp pop that one neighbor compared to \"a nail gun that never stops.\" The city has installed sound-dampening fence wraps and limited play to 7 a.m. to 9 p.m., but TK (follow up w/ HOA president, call back Thurs).\n[section on injuries: Dr. Nair at Valley Ortho said ER visits are up, need exact quote]\n[ending: Lorraine on the court at sunset? or Ray's 90th birthday game?]" },
  { kind: "feature", name: "7th grade family history interview write-up with great-grandma, Q&A, young writer", text: "Family History Interview\nby Kayden Wu, 7th grade Social Studies, Mr. Halvorsen\n\nFor this project I interviewed my great-grandma, Dorothy \"Dot\" Kaminski. She is 86 and lives in Toledo, Ohio. I interviewed her on FaceTime on Sunday. My mom helped because Grandma Dot's hearing aid kept beeping.\n\nQ: What was your first job?\nA: I was a telephone operator for Ohio Bell when I was 18. It was 1958.\nQ: What did a telephone operator do?\nA: She said when people picked up the phone a lot of the time they got an operator and said who they wanted to call, and she plugged a cord into a big board with lots of holes to connect them. There were like 40 girls in one room all doing it at the same time. She said it was really loud and you had to wear a headset all day.\nQ: What was hard about the job?\nA: The supervisors were really strict. You had to sit up straight and you could not talk to the girl next to you. You had to ask permission to go to the bathroom! Also some callers were rude and you had to stay polite no matter what.\nQ: What was the best part?\nA: She liked her friends there, they went out for pie after work on Fridays. She met my great-grandpa because he kept calling the long distance line to talk to his brother in the army and she always answered. (I didnt know that!!)\nQ: Why did you stop?\nA: She stopped when she had my grandpa in 1963 because back then a lot of women had to quit when they had a baby.\n\nWhat I learned: I learned that jobs were really different back then and that women had a lot of rules that were unfair. I also learned that phones used to need real people to work. I think it is kind of sad that her job doesn't exist anymore but she said \"that's how it goes, honey.\"" },
  { kind: "feature", name: "college newspaper place feature on a 24-hour diner near campus, polished", text: "The Starlite Never Closes. Neither, It Seems, Does Anyone Inside It.\nBy Theo Marchand | Arts & Life\n\nAt 2:40 on a Thursday morning, the Starlite Diner on Route 9 holds three architecture students hunched over a foam-core model, a nurse in purple scrubs eating pancakes alone, two men in hunting camouflage, and a booth of sophomores loudly arguing about whether a hot dog is a sandwich. The jukebox, which has not worked since roughly 2016, glows anyway.\n\"You see everybody here,\" said Gloria Benítez, who has worked the overnight shift for nine years. \"Doctors, drunks, the football team, a bishop once. Everybody gets the same coffee.\"\nThe Starlite has been open 24 hours a day since 1974, closing only on Christmas and, according to a framed newspaper clipping by the register, for three days during the blizzard of 1993. It is a classic stainless-steel diner car, shipped in pieces from New Jersey, with eleven counter stools and a menu so long it has a table of contents.\nFor students, it fills a gap the university does not. Dining halls close at 9 p.m. on weeknights, and the library's 24-hour floor does not allow food. \"It's the only place within walking distance where you can sit for four hours and nobody kicks you out,\" said Priya Raman, a junior in architecture, who estimated she had spent \"more hours at the Starlite than in studio\" during her sophomore year. \"That's an exaggeration. Barely.\"\nOwner Nick Pappas, whose father bought the diner in 1981, says students make up about a third of overnight business during the semester. He has watched them change. \"Twenty years ago they came in after the bars. Now they come in with laptops,\" he said. \"Honestly, the laptop crowd tips better.\"\nKeeping a business open around the clock has not gotten easier. Pappas says he has struggled to hire overnight cooks since the pandemic, and food costs have pushed the price of the signature Moonlight Special (two eggs, hash browns, toast and bacon) from $5.99 to $9.49 in four years. He considered closing overnight in 2022. Regulars talked him out of it, he said, including one who offered to work the grill for free.\n\"I told him no, he can't cook,\" Pappas said. \"But it meant something.\"\nBack in the corner booth, the architecture students are trimming foam with box cutters. Their model is due at 9 a.m. Benítez refills their coffee without asking and sets down a plate of fries nobody ordered.\n\"On the house,\" she said. \"Midterms.\"" },
  { kind: "college", name: "polished Common App personal statement about working at the family laundromat and learning languages", text: "At Suds & Spin on Fulton Avenue, I learned to say \"your whites are done\" in six languages.\n\nMy mother bought the laundromat when I was ten, with savings from twelve years of cleaning other people's houses. The machines are loud enough that most people don't bother talking, so for the first year I mostly learned the language of the machines themselves: the hiccup of washer 14 before its drum stops, the smell of a dryer belt about to snap, the way quarters jam in the change machine when it is humid. My job was to wipe the folding tables and tell my mother when something sounded wrong.\n\nBy thirteen, my job was customers. On a Saturday morning, Mrs. Okonkwo comes in with her church linens, the Tran brothers bring work uniforms that smell like the fish market, and a rotating set of grad students arrive who never seem to own enough socks. My mother speaks Spanish and enough English to run the business. Everyone else spoke something else.\n\nSo I started learning. It began with survival phrases, \"the machine takes quarters only\" and \"the dryer is broken, use number 6,\" which I wrote phonetically on index cards and taped behind the counter. Mrs. Okonkwo taught me \"ẹ káàárọ̀,\" good morning in Yoruba, and corrected my tones every single week until I got them right. Mr. Tran taught me to count to ten in Vietnamese, then laughed when I counted his quarters with it. A Haitian grandmother who came in on Tuesdays taught me Kreyòl mostly so she could tease me about my accent.\n\nI want to be honest: I am not fluent in six languages. I am fluent in two, and in the other four I can say about thirty things, most of which involve laundry. But somewhere in those index cards I discovered that I love the moment when a language stops being noise and becomes meaning. The first time I understood a whole sentence of Vietnamese, Mr. Tran telling his brother that the girl at the counter was learning fast, I had to go into the back room because I was smiling so hard.\n\nThat curiosity followed me into school. I took Latin freshman year because it was the only language that fit my schedule, and I expected to hate it. Instead, declensions felt like the dryer belts: a system that makes sense once you hear the pattern underneath. Last summer I took a free online linguistics course and spent an embarrassing number of hours tracing how the word for \"soap\" travels across languages. (It is often borrowed, which makes sense. Soap gets traded.)\n\nWhat the laundromat really taught me, though, is that language is less about vocabulary than attention. The people who come into Suds & Spin are often tired, and often far from where they grew up. When I greet Mrs. Okonkwo in Yoruba, I am not showing off. I am telling her that I noticed her, that I listened long enough to learn something that matters to her. She tells me I still sound like a parrot with a cold, and she is right, but she smiles every time.\n\nThe index cards behind the counter have grown into a binder, organized by language, which new employees use and add to. Last month a customer from Ethiopia added a page in Amharic without my asking. I think it is my favorite thing I have ever made, and I barely made it.\n\nI want to study linguistics because I want to understand how people make meaning out of noise. But I already know the most important part: you have to keep showing up, and you have to listen." },
  { kind: "college", name: "short \"Why Macalester\" supplement typed into the application portal", place: "apply.commonapp.org/member/questions/college/7341", text: "At Macalester, I want to keep asking the question that started with a paper map of Saint Paul taped to my bedroom wall: why do neighborhoods end where they do? I first noticed it riding the bus over I-94 at Dale Street, and later learned how the highway cut through the Rondo neighborhood in the 1960s. Macalester's Geography department, with its focus on urban geography and GIS and its ties to Twin Cities organizations, would let me study that question in the city where it began for me. I am especially drawn to the community-based research projects in the urban courses and the chance to work in the GIS lab early on. Outside class, I would keep playing ultimate and find somewhere to volunteer with neighborhood groups. Mostly, I want to be at a school where the city is part of the classroom." },
  { kind: "college", name: "average-quality local Rotary scholarship essay on becoming a dental hygienist, prompt pasted at top", text: "Scholarship Essay – Brookfield Rotary Club Vocational Scholarship\nPrompt: Describe your career goals and explain how this scholarship will help you achieve them. (300-500 words)\n\nMy career goal is to become a registered dental hygienist. When I was in 8th grade I had a really bad toothache that turned out to be an abscess. My family did not have dental insurance at the time so we waited until it was really bad. When we finally went to a free clinic in Hartford, the hygienist, her name was Renee, explained everything to me and my mom in a way that made us not scared. She even showed me on a model what was happening. I remember thinking that I wanted to be able to do that for someone.\n\nSince then I have been working toward this goal. I took Anatomy and Physiology my junior year and got an A-, and this year I am taking Chemistry, which is harder for me but I am getting through it with tutoring. I also volunteer on Saturdays at a community dental clinic, where I help with check-in and translate for Spanish speaking patients. Seeing how many people come, some of them waiting in line since early morning, showed me how big the need is.\n\nI have been accepted to the Dental Hygiene program at Northgate Community College starting in the fall. The program is two years and includes clinical rotations. After I get my license I want to work in a community health center, because I want to help families like mine who don't have a lot of options. Later on I might go back for my bachelors so I can teach or work in public health.\n\nThis scholarship would help me a lot because I am paying for college myself with help from my mom. The program has extra costs that are not covered by my financial aid, like the instrument kit which is about $2,000, scrubs, and the licensing exam. Right now I work 20 hours a week at Stop & Shop, and if I don't have to work as many hours I can focus more on my classes, especially science.\n\nThank you for considering my application. I promise to make Brookfield proud and to give back to the community that helped me." },
  { kind: "college", name: "graduate school statement of purpose for an MPH from a 911 dispatcher changing careers", text: "Statement of Purpose\nMaster of Public Health, Epidemiology Concentration\nDenise Carrow-Lyle\nFor eleven years, my job was to keep strangers alive for the six to eight minutes it takes an ambulance to arrive. As a 911 dispatcher for the Marion County Emergency Communications Center, I have coached callers through CPR, talked a teenager through delivering her sister's baby, and stayed on the line through more overdose calls than I can count. That last category is why I am applying to your program.\nBetween 2018 and 2023, overdose calls in our county roughly tripled. I noticed it before the official reports did, because I could hear it: the same apartment complexes, the same stretch of highway motels, sometimes the same caller twice in one week. At first I kept a private tally on a legal pad. In 2021 I brought it to our medical director, and with her support I began working with the county health department to map overdose-related calls by address and time of day. That map helped the department decide where to place three naloxone vending machines and where to send its outreach van on Friday nights, when calls spiked. It was the most meaningful work of my career, and it was also the moment I realized how little I knew. I could see a pattern, but I could not tell whether it was real or what was driving it, and I could not have defended my methods to anyone with training.\nSince then I have prepared deliberately. I finished my bachelor's in health sciences online in 2023 while working nights, graduating with a 3.7. This past year I took Introduction to Biostatistics and Introduction to Epidemiology through a community college and earned A's in both. I have taught myself enough R to clean and map our dispatch data, though I now understand the difference between making a map and making an inference.\nI am drawn to your epidemiology concentration, and in particular to Dr. Helen Osei's work on syndromic surveillance using emergency medical services data, as well as to the practicum structure, which would let me keep working alongside a local health department. My long-term goal is to work in applied surveillance for a state or county health department, building systems that turn the data emergency services already collect into faster warnings for communities.\nI am not a typical applicant. I am 39, I have spent my career on a headset rather than in a lab, and I will be the first in my family to attend graduate school. But I have spent eleven years listening closely to what is happening in my community in real time, and I believe that experience will make me a better epidemiologist. I am ready to learn the methods that will let me prove what I can already hear." },
  { kind: "college", name: "messy unfinished Common App draft about stuttering, with notes to self and counselor feedback", text: "common app draft 2 (STUTTER ONE) — ~480 words after cutting, needs an ending\n**Ms. Feld said the opening is too slow, maybe start at the debate tournament instead?**\n\nMy name starts with a hard B, which is the worst letter for someone who stutters. Ben. One syllable. You would think it would be easy. For most of my life it has been the hardest word I say.\n\nI started stuttering when I was four. My parents tried speech therapy, a lot of it, and some things helped, like slowing down and \"easy onset,\" where you start a word gently, almost breathing into it. But blocks still happen. A block is when the word is right there and your throat just closes on it. It feels like pushing on a door that someone is holding shut from the other side.\n\n[cut this paragraph? too much explaining?] In middle school I got really good at hiding it. I would switch words at the last second, so \"bus\" became \"the ride\" and \"Tuesday\" became \"the day after Monday,\" which is not suspicious at all. I would pretend not to know answers in class rather than say them. Teachers wrote \"Ben should participate more\" on every report card. They didn't know I knew every answer.\n\nIn ninth grade my best friend Owen signed us both up for debate as a joke, or at least I thought it was a joke. Debate is basically the worst possible activity for someone who stutters. There is a timer. Every second you are blocked is a second you lose.\n\nMy first round, I blocked on the word \"because\" for what my partner later told me was six seconds. It felt like a year. The judge didn't look up. The other team waited. And then I said it, and kept going, and we lost, but not by much.\n\nWhat I learned in debate is something I could never learn in therapy: people mostly wait. Not everyone, one judge wrote \"speaker needs to work on fluency\" on my ballot, which, thanks. But most people will wait for you if you just keep going. [this is the main point — make it stronger??]\n\nBy junior year Owen and I were breaking to elimination rounds. I still stutter. I stuttered in the semifinal at the state tournament, on the word \"benefit,\" which has my letter in it twice. We won that round anyway.\n\nNow I don't switch words as much. I say \"bus.\" I raise my hand. I introduce myself first, before anyone can ask, and when I block on Ben I let it happen and then say it.\n\n[ENDING — something about college? speaking up in seminars? Ms. Feld said DON'T end with \"this made me who I am today\" lol]" },
  { kind: "recommendation", name: "AP Chemistry teacher's college recommendation letter for a senior, formal", text: "Roosevelt High School\nScience Department\nElmwood, Illinois\n\nOctober 9, 2026\n\nTo the Admissions Committee:\n\nIt is my pleasure to recommend Priya Nandakumar for admission to your university. I have taught Priya in two courses, Honors Chemistry as a sophomore and AP Chemistry last year, and I also coach the Science Olympiad team on which she has competed for three years. In nineteen years of teaching I have written many of these letters; few have been as easy to write.\n\nPriya is a gifted student. She earned the highest grade in both of my courses and a 5 on the AP exam, but what sets her apart is how she learns. Most strong students want the right answer. Priya wants to know why the wrong answers are wrong. During our unit on equilibrium, she stayed after class three days in a row because she was bothered that Le Chatelier's principle seemed to \"predict without explaining.\" She eventually wrote a two-page explanation for herself using reaction quotients and brought it to me to check. It was correct, and it was clearer than the one in our textbook. With her permission, I now use it with my current students.\n\nShe is also generous with what she knows. Priya organized a Thursday lunch study group that grew from four students to nearly twenty by spring. She did not simply hand out answers. I watched her ask a struggling classmate a series of small questions until he solved a titration problem himself, and then she had him explain it to the next person. Several students told me the group was the reason they passed the exam.\n\nOn the Science Olympiad team, Priya competes in Chemistry Lab and Forensics and was elected captain this year. When our lab event materials were left behind at a regional tournament, she calmly reorganized the schedule, borrowed equipment from another school, and still placed second in her event. Her teammates trust her because she stays steady and never makes anyone feel small.\n\nIf I have one criticism, it is that Priya holds herself to a standard that sometimes makes her reluctant to turn in work she considers unfinished. She has gotten much better at this, and I believe college will teach her the rest.\n\nPriya plans to study chemical engineering, and I have no doubt she will thrive in a demanding program. She is the kind of student who makes a classroom better for everyone in it. I recommend her with enthusiasm and without reservation. Please contact me if I can provide any further information.\n\nSincerely,\n\nMark Albrecht\nAP Chemistry and Honors Chemistry\nRoosevelt High School\nmalbrecht@rhs-elmwood.org" },
  { kind: "recommendation", name: "short plain employer reference from a restaurant owner for a former shift manager", text: "To Whom It May Concern,\nI am writing to recommend Marcus Bell, who worked for me at La Cocina de Tere from June 2022 until August 2026. He started as a line cook and after one year I promoted him to shift manager on weeknights.\nMarcus is very dependable. In four years he only called out sick two times and he never was late that I remember. As shift manager he was in charge of opening and closing, counting the drawer, and managing 4 to 6 employees. He was good with the new workers, patient with them, and the customers liked him. When we had a health inspection in 2024 he was the manager on duty and we got a 98.\nHe is leaving because he is moving to Sacramento with his family, not because of any problem. I would hire him again any day. If he applies with you, you are lucky.\nPlease call me if you have any questions.\nTeresa Fuentes\nOwner, La Cocina de Tere\n(555) 302-7781" },
  { kind: "recommendation", name: "biology professor's detailed letter for an undergraduate applying to an ecology PhD program", text: "Department of Biological Sciences\nNorthern Appalachian State University\nOctober 30, 2026\n\nDear Members of the Graduate Admissions Committee,\n\nI write in strong support of Jonah Whitcombe's application to your PhD program in Ecology and Evolutionary Biology. I have known Jonah for more than two years, first as a student in my upper-level Herpetology course in fall 2024, where he earned an A, and since then as an undergraduate researcher in my lab, where he is completing an independent honors thesis. In twenty-two years I have supervised more than forty undergraduate researchers; Jonah is among the top three.\n\nJonah's thesis asks whether road density affects the timing of spring breeding migrations in spotted salamanders (Ambystoma maculatum). The question was his, not mine. It grew out of a volunteer \"salamander crossing\" night he attended as a sophomore, where he noticed that animals near the busier road seemed to move later in the evening. He designed a two-season study at vernal pools across a road-density gradient, recruited and trained eleven volunteers, and built a drift fence and pitfall array that he has since improved twice. He has recorded more than 1,900 individual crossings.\n\nWhat impressed me most was how he handled a setback. In the first season, an unusually dry March meant three of his eight pools barely filled, and his sample sizes at the high-density sites were too small to analyze. Rather than force a result, he re-examined his design, added two sites, and incorporated temperature and precipitation data from the nearest weather station so that he could separate weather effects from road effects. His second-season results suggest a delay of roughly forty minutes in nightly migration onset at high road-density pools, a finding he presented as a poster at a regional amphibian and reptile conservation meeting this spring. He is now drafting a manuscript, which I expect we will submit this winter with Jonah as first author.\n\nJonah's analytical skills are well beyond those of most undergraduates. He taught himself generalized linear mixed models in R, and when he reached the limits of what I could help with, he sought out a statistician in our mathematics department on his own. His code is clean and well documented; I have begun using his scripts as templates for other students.\n\nHe is also an excellent colleague. Jonah mentors two first-year students in the lab, and he has a gift for explaining fieldwork in a way that makes it feel both rigorous and fun. He is honest about uncertainty, both in his data and in his own understanding, which I consider one of the most important qualities a scientist can have.\n\nIf there is an area for growth, it is scientific writing; his first drafts are thorough but long. He has worked hard on this over the past year, and his thesis introduction is now concise and well organized.\n\nI have no doubt that Jonah will thrive in a doctoral program and go on to an independent career in amphibian ecology or conservation. I recommend him with the highest enthusiasm. Please do not hesitate to contact me if I can be of further help.\n\nSincerely,\nLaura Brennan, PhD\nProfessor of Biology" },
  { kind: "recommendation", name: "neighbor's character reference letter to a judge before a young man's sentencing", text: "The Honorable Patricia Wexler\nDelmont County Court of Common Pleas\nRe: Character reference for Tyler Ashworth, Case No. CR-2026-0412\n\nDear Judge Wexler,\n\nMy name is Gail Rinaldi and I have lived next door to the Ashworth family for 16 years. I am a retired school secretary. I've known Tyler since he was about 6 years old riding his bike up and down our street. I am writing because I understand he will be sentenced for the accident in March, and I want you to know the young man I know.\n\nI am not writing to make excuses for what Tyler did. Driving too fast on Route 30 that night was wrong and he knows the Hendricks family is still dealing with injuries because of it. I have talked with him many times since then and I have never once heard him blame anyone else. He told me \"I think about it every single day Mrs. R.\" and I believe him.\n\nBefore this happened Tyler was the boy who shoveled my driveway every winter after my husband passed, without being asked, and wouldn't take money for it. When my basement flooded in 2022 he and his dad carried out boxes for two days. He has worked at Kessler's Hardware since he was 16 and the owner trusts him to close the store.\n\nSince the accident he has finished the driving course, he has done his community service at the food bank and I am told he kept going after his hours were done. He sold his car and takes the bus to work.\n\nI know you have to think about the family who was hurt, and I pray for them too. But I am asking you to consider that Tyler is 20 years old and that he is a good person who made a terrible decision. I believe he will spend the rest of his life making up for it if he is given the chance.\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this.\n\nRespectfully,\nGail Rinaldi\n418 Sycamore Lane\nDelmont, PA" },
  { kind: "recommendation", name: "casual high school peer recommendation written by a friend for a college application", text: "Dartmouth Peer Recommendation — for Isabel Santoro\nWritten by: Noor Haddad (friend, physics lab partner 11th and 12th grade)\nI have known Izzy since we were assigned as lab partners in Physics junior year, which I was not happy about at first because she corrected my significant figures on the first day. Now she is one of my best friends and I'm still kind of mad about the sig figs.\nThe thing you should know about Izzy is that she is the person everyone calls when something goes wrong. When our friend Dev's grandma got sick last spring and he was missing a lot of school, Izzy made a shared Google Doc with notes from every class he missed and got like six people to add to it. She never told anyone she was the one who started it. I only know because I looked at the edit history.\nShe is also really funny in a dry way that teachers don't always notice. She runs a \"bad science puns\" whiteboard in the physics room that Mr. Castillo pretends to hate but secretly adds to himself.\nIzzy is not perfect. She overcommits (last year she was in four clubs and the pit orchestra for the musical at the same time) and when she gets stressed she goes quiet. But she always follows through. I have never seen her drop something she promised someone.\nAt Dartmouth I think she would be the person on her floor who knows everyone's name the first week and remembers who is allergic to peanuts. She would also probably start a club. Possibly about puns.\nI would trust Izzy with anything. I really hope you take her." },
  { kind: "outline", name: "high school Roman numeral outline for an argumentative essay on lowering the voting age", text: "Tess Kowalczyk\nGov/Econ – Mr. Liang\nArgumentative Essay Outline\nTopic: Should the voting age be lowered to 16?\nThesis: The voting age in the United States should be lowered to 16 because 16-year-olds are affected by government decisions, are capable of making informed choices, and would be more likely to become lifelong voters.\nI. Introduction\nA. Hook: In Takoma Park, Maryland, 16-year-olds have been able to vote in city elections since 2013.\nB. Background: the 26th Amendment lowered the voting age from 21 to 18 in 1971, partly because of the Vietnam War\nC. Thesis\nII. Body Paragraph 1: 16-year-olds are affected by laws\nA. They can work and pay income taxes\nB. They can drive\nC. School funding, climate policy, gun laws all affect teens directly\nIII. Body Paragraph 2: they are capable\nA. Research on \"cold cognition\": 16-year-olds reason as well as adults when they have time to think (Steinberg)\nB. Most students take civics around 16-17, so the knowledge is fresh\nIV. Body Paragraph 3: it builds habits\nA. Voting is a habit; people who vote in their first eligible election are more likely to keep voting\nB. 18 is a bad time to start: moving out, college, new address\nC. In Takoma Park, turnout among 16-17 year olds was higher than among adults in 2013\nV. Counterargument and Rebuttal\nA. Counter: teens will just vote like their parents\nB. Rebuttal: many don't, and adults are influenced by their families too\nVI. Conclusion\nA. Restate thesis\nB. Call to action: support local efforts, write to state representatives" },
  { kind: "outline", name: "college public speaking informative speech outline on the history of purple dye, with transitions and references", text: "COMM 110 – Informative Speech Outline\nDanielle Ofori\nTopic: The history of the color purple\nGeneral Purpose: To inform\nSpecific Purpose: To inform my audience about how purple went from the most expensive color in the world to an everyday color.\nCentral Idea: Purple was a symbol of royalty for centuries because it was incredibly hard to make, until an accidental discovery in 1856 made it available to everyone.\n\nINTRODUCTION\nI. Attention getter: Raise your hand if you're wearing something purple. In ancient Rome, wearing the wrong shade of purple could get you into serious trouble.\nII. Relevance: We see purple everywhere, from candy wrappers to streaming logos.\nIII. Credibility: I'm an art history minor and I spent the last two weeks reading about the history of textile dyes.\nIV. Preview: Today I'll talk about how purple was made in the ancient world, how it became the color of power, and how an 18-year-old chemistry student changed everything.\n(Transition: Let's start with a snail.)\n\nBODY\nI. Tyrian purple was made from sea snails.\nA. The Phoenician city of Tyre produced it from murex snails.\nB. It took roughly 12,000 snails to make just 1.4 grams of dye (Garfield, 2001).\nC. The process smelled so bad that dye works were built outside the cities.\n(Transition: Because it was so hard to make, only the very rich could afford it.)\nII. Purple became the color of power.\nA. Roman senators wore a purple stripe; emperors wore all purple.\nB. Byzantine emperors' children were \"born in the purple.\"\nC. In Elizabethan England, sumptuary laws reserved purple silk for the royal family (St. Clair, 2016).\n(Transition: For almost two thousand years this didn't change, until 1856.)\nIII. William Henry Perkin accidentally invented mauve.\nA. He was 18 and trying to make quinine to treat malaria.\nB. Instead he got a dark sludge that dyed silk purple.\nC. Mauveine became a fashion craze; Queen Victoria wore it in 1862.\nD. It started the synthetic dye industry, which led to advances in chemistry and medicine.\n\nCONCLUSION\nI. Review: snails, emperors, and a teenage chemist.\nII. Restate central idea.\nIII. Closing: Next time you see purple, remember that you're looking at something that was once worth more than its weight in gold.\n\nReferences\nGarfield, S. (2001). Mauve: How one man invented a color that changed the world. W. W. Norton.\nSt. Clair, K. (2016). The secret lives of colour. John Murray." },
  { kind: "outline", name: "messy rough bullet outline for a sociology research paper on gentrification, notes to self", text: "research paper outline (soc 220) - gentrification in eastside\ndue fri, rough!!\n- intro\n  - start w/ the taqueria on 5th that closed? (ask prof if ok to use \"I\")\n  - define gentrification — Ruth Glass coined term 1964 (London)\n  - thesis: gentrification in Eastside Marlow 2010-2025 raised property values but pushed out longtime renters, mostly Latino families, and the city's affordable housing rules didn't stop it\n- background\n  - Eastside history - industrial, then working class, redlined in 1930s (HOLC map, find it on Mapping Inequality)\n  - 2012 light rail station opens → developers show up\n- section 1: what changed\n  - median rent — census ACS 2010 vs 2023 (need actual #s!!)\n  - new businesses: coffee shops, brewery, yoga studio lol\n  - demographic shift ??? find source\n- section 2: who got displaced\n  - interviews? no time. use Marlow Tenants Union report (2022)\n  - eviction data — Eviction Lab (Princeton)\n- section 3: policy\n  - 2016 inclusionary zoning law — 10% of units affordable, but developers could pay a fee instead and most did\n  - compare to Portland? or skip\n- counter: some longtime homeowners benefited (sold for a lot)\n  - BUT renters ≠ owners, most Eastside residents rented\n- conclusion\n  - what should city do — community land trust? rent stabilization?\n  - circle back to taqueria\nsources i have: Glass 1964, Freeman \"There Goes the 'Hood\" (2006), Tenants Union report, Eviction Lab\nneed 3 more peer reviewed!!!" },
  { kind: "outline", name: "6th grader's simple five-paragraph essay plan on why summer is the best season", text: "My 5 Paragraph Essay Plan\nName: Lily P.   Class: ELA 6\nTopic: Summer is the best season\n\nParagraph 1 (Intro)\nHook: Imagine waking up with no alarm clock.\nThesis: Summer is the best season because there is no school, you can go swimming, and there are fun holidays.\n\nParagraph 2: No school\n● sleep in\n● more time with friends\n● example: last summer i read 12 books just for fun\n\nParagraph 3: Swimming\n● pool at my grandma's apartment\n● lake trip with my cousins\n● it's too cold the rest of the year\n\nParagraph 4: Holidays\n● 4th of July fireworks\n● my birthday is in July!\n● family reunion\n\nParagraph 5 (Conclusion)\n● restate thesis\n● end with \"That is why summer is the best season of all.\"" },
  { kind: "outline", name: "senior capstone detailed decimal outline on wildfire smoke and school closures", text: "Senior Capstone — Detailed Outline (v2)\nEnvironmental Health Sciences\nWorking title: Clearing the Air, Closing the Doors: Wildfire Smoke and School Closure Decisions in the Pacific Northwest, 2017–2025\nAdvisor: Dr. M. Saldana\n\n1. Introduction\n1.1 Problem: wildfire smoke days are increasing, and districts have no consistent rule for when to close\n1.2 Research question: What AQI thresholds and other factors do districts use to decide closures, and are those decisions consistent with health guidance?\n1.3 Significance: lost instruction days vs. respiratory exposure, especially for students with asthma\n2. Background\n2.1 PM2.5 health effects in children (EPA Integrated Science Assessment 2019; Aguilera et al. 2021)\n2.2 The AQI and current guidance for schools (EPA guidance; Washington and Oregon state guidance)\n2.3 Indoor air: HVAC filtration, MERV-13, portable air cleaners\n3. Methods\n3.1 Sample: 48 school districts in WA and OR, selected by smoke exposure\n3.2 Data: district closure announcements (2017–2025), AirNow daily AQI, district HVAC info from facilities surveys\n3.3 Analysis: descriptive stats; logistic regression of closure (yes/no) on AQI, day of week, HVAC status, district size\n3.4 Interviews with 6 superintendents and facilities managers (IRB approved 2/14)\n4. Results (preliminary)\n4.1 AQI on closure days ranged from 151 to 412\n4.2 Districts with upgraded HVAC closed at higher AQI\n4.3 Rural districts more likely to stay open (transportation and school meal concerns)\n5. Discussion\n5.1 Inconsistency between districts is an equity issue\n5.2 Tradeoff: a filtered school can have cleaner air than a student's home\n5.3 Limitations: announcements incomplete; no direct measure of indoor air quality\n6. Recommendations\n6.1 State-level decision framework (outdoor AQI plus indoor monitoring)\n6.2 Funding for filtration upgrades in rural districts\n7. Conclusion" },
  { kind: "homework", name: "high school US history guided reading questions on the Progressive Era with student answers", text: "Name: Caleb Sutter   Date: 10/13   Per: 6\nChapter 17, Section 1 — The Origins of Progressivism\nGuided Reading\nDirections: Answer each question in complete sentences using your textbook (pp. 512–519).\n\n1. What were the four main goals of the progressive movement?\nProtecting social welfare, promoting moral improvement, creating economic reform, and fostering efficiency.\n\n2. Who were the muckrakers? Give one example and what they wrote about.\nMuckrakers were journalists who wrote about corruption in business and government. Ida Tarbell wrote about the Standard Oil Company and how John D. Rockefeller crushed his competition.\n\n3. What was the purpose of the YMCA and the Social Gospel movement?\nThey tried to help poor people in cities with things like libraries, classes and gyms, and they believed Christians should fix society's problems.\n\n4. How did Frederick Winslow Taylor try to make workplaces more efficient?\nHe studied how long each task took and broke jobs into small steps so workers could go faster. This was called scientific management. Workers didn't like it because it was boring and they got hurt more.\n\n5. Explain the difference between the initiative, the referendum, and the recall.\nInitiative - citizens can propose a law. Referendum - people vote on a law. Recall - voters can remove an elected official before their term is over.\n\n6. What did the 17th Amendment do?\nIt made it so senators are elected directly by the people instead of by state legislatures.\n\n7. Why did some reformers support prohibition? Who opposed it?\nI think because alcohol led to domestic violence and men spending their paychecks at saloons. Saloon owners and immigrants opposed it. (also brewers?)\n\n8. In your opinion, which progressive reform had the biggest impact? Explain.\nThe 17th Amendment because it gave people more power to choose who represents them, and before that big businesses could basically buy senators through the state legislature." },
  { kind: "homework", name: "teacher's biology homework handout on cell transport, questions only with no answers", text: "Biology — Unit 3 Homework #4\nCell Transport: Diffusion, Osmosis, and Active Transport\nDue Thursday. Use your notes from Tuesday's egg lab and Section 7.3.\nPart A — Vocabulary in context\n1. In your own words, explain the difference between passive and active transport. Which one requires energy, and where does that energy come from?\n2. What is a concentration gradient? Draw a quick sketch with arrows to show molecules moving \"down\" a gradient.\n3. Define selectively permeable. Why is this an important property of the cell membrane?\nPart B — Apply it\n4. A red blood cell is placed in a beaker of distilled water. Describe what will happen to the cell and explain why, using the term hypotonic.\n5. Why do grocery stores mist water on lettuce and other vegetables? Use the word osmosis in your answer.\n6. Our egg in corn syrup shrank overnight. Was the corn syrup hypertonic, hypotonic, or isotonic compared to the inside of the egg? How do you know?\n7. Glucose enters many cells through a carrier protein without using ATP. What type of transport is this?\n8. The sodium-potassium pump moves 3 Na+ out of the cell and 2 K+ in. Is this active or passive transport? Explain.\nPart C — Think about it\n9. Freshwater fish and saltwater fish face opposite problems with water balance. Pick one and explain what problem it has and how it might solve it.\n10. Explain why you can't water houseplants with salt water, using what you know about osmosis.\nReminder: Quiz on Friday covers 7.1–7.3!" },
  { kind: "homework", name: "middle schooler's sloppy answers to reading questions on a Langston Hughes short story", text: "\"Thank You, M'am\" by Langston Hughes — Reading Questions\nName: Jordyn   Period: 3\n1. Why does Roger try to steal Mrs. Jones's purse?\nhe wanted money to buy blue suede shoes\n2. What does Mrs. Jones do after she catches him?\nShe kicks him (lol) and then drags him to her house and makes him wash his face.\n3. Why doesn't Roger run away when he has the chance? Use evidence from the story.\nBecause he wanted her to trust him. The story says \"he did not want to be mistrusted now.\"\n4. How does Mrs. Jones relate to Roger? What does she tell him about her past?\nShe says she did things she is not proud of when she was young too. She doesn't say what. I think this is why she isnt mad at him.\n5. What is the theme of the story?\nkindness can change a person more than punishment\n6. Why do you think Roger can't say anything more than \"thank you, ma'am\" at the end?\nBecause he is shocked that she gave him ten dollars and was nice to him. He wanted to say more but he couldn't get it out and then she shut the door. Also maybe he is ashamed.\n7. If you were Mrs. Jones would you have called the police? why or why not\nNo probably not because he was just a kid and she could tell nobody was taking care of him at home. But i would be scared to bring him to my house lol" },
  { kind: "homework", name: "college economics problem set on externalities and public goods typed into a Canvas text box", place: "canvas.instructure.com/courses/77310/assignments/512044", text: "ECON 102 Problem Set 5 – Externalities and Public Goods\nHannah Liu\n\n1. Explain why a negative externality leads to overproduction in a competitive market.\nBecause the producer only pays the private cost, not the cost the pollution (or whatever it is) imposes on third parties. So the supply curve reflects private marginal cost, which is below social marginal cost, and the market ends up where demand = private MC, which is a bigger quantity than the social optimum where demand = social MC.\n\n2. A paper mill dumps waste into a river, harming a downstream fishery. Describe two ways the government could correct this, and one advantage of each.\n(a) A Pigouvian tax per ton of waste equal to the marginal external damage. Advantage: the mill decides how to cut pollution itself, so reductions happen where they're cheapest. (b) A regulation setting a maximum amount of waste. Advantage: more certainty about the actual pollution level, which matters if damage gets severe past a certain point.\n\n3. According to the Coase theorem, under what conditions could the mill and the fishery solve this without government? Why might those conditions not hold?\nIf property rights are clearly defined and transaction costs are low, they could bargain to the efficient outcome no matter who holds the right. With one mill and one fishery that might work, but if there are lots of affected people downstream, bargaining gets really costly and there's a free rider problem.\n\n4. Is a lighthouse a public good? Explain using the terms rival and excludable.\nTraditionally yes, because one ship seeing the light doesn't stop another ship from seeing it (non-rival) and you can't stop a ship from seeing it (non-excludable). But Coase wrote a paper arguing British lighthouses were actually funded by port fees, so you can get excludability by bundling it with something else.\n\n5. Give an example of a positive externality from your own life and say whether you think it is underprovided.\nMy neighbor's flower garden. I enjoy it walking by but I don't pay for it. Probably slightly underprovided in theory but not a big deal policy-wise. A better example is getting a flu shot, which also protects the people around me. That one is underprovided, which is why a lot of places give them out free." },
  { kind: "homework", name: "Spanish 2 preterite vs imperfect worksheet with a student's answers, mostly in Spanish", text: "Español 2 – Hoja de práctica: Pretérito vs. Imperfecto\nNombre: Grace Whitman   Fecha: 14/10\nA. Completa con la forma correcta del verbo en el pretérito o el imperfecto.\n1. Cuando yo ___ (ser) niña, ___ (vivir) en Ohio. — era, vivía\n2. Ayer mi hermano ___ (romper) la ventana. — rompió\n3. Todos los veranos nosotros ___ (ir) a la playa. — íbamos\n4. Mientras mi mamá ___ (cocinar), el teléfono ___ (sonar). — cocinaba, sonó\n5. ¿Qué hora ___ (ser) cuando tú ___ (llegar)? — era, llegaste\n6. Ellos ___ (estar) cansados porque ___ (correr) cinco millas. — estaban, corrieron\n7. El año pasado yo ___ (tener) un accidente. — tuve (I think??)\nB. Explain in English why you chose the imperfect in #3.\nBecause it happened over and over (habitual). \"Todos los veranos\" is a clue phrase for the imperfect.\nC. Escribe tres oraciones sobre tu niñez usando el imperfecto.\n1. Cuando era pequeña, me gustaba jugar con mi perro.\n2. Mi abuela siempre hacía galletas los domingos.\n3. Yo tenia miedo de la oscuridad." },
  { kind: "recommendation", name: "tricky: LinkedIn recommendation for a former UX designer colleague (could be mistaken for social)", place: "www.linkedin.com/in/marisa-quell/edit/forms/recommendation/write/", text: "I had the pleasure of working with Marisa for two years at Fernway Health, where she was lead UX designer on our patient scheduling app and I was the product manager. Marisa has a rare ability to turn messy research into decisions the whole team can get behind. When our no-show rate was stuck, she ran 20+ patient interviews in three weeks and redesigned the reminder flow, which cut no-shows by 18%. She's also the person who makes engineers feel heard in design reviews. Any team would be lucky to have her." },
  { kind: "college", name: "tricky: Common App personal statement about restoring watches with a grandfather, no prompt shown (could be mistaken for personal)", text: "Personal Statement — draft 4\nMy grandfather's workbench is three feet wide, and for most of my childhood that was the size of the world I wanted to live in.\nHe repaired watches in the back of a pharmacy in Scranton for forty-one years. When I was seven, he gave me a broken Timex and a jeweler's screwdriver and told me to take it apart and put it back together. It took me two weeks, and when I was done there was a spring left over. He held it up to the lamp and said, \"Now you know where it doesn't go.\"\nI spent most Saturdays after that on a stool next to him. He did not explain much. Instead, he narrated what his hands were doing: \"Lifting the balance. Careful. Careful. There.\" I learned the names of things before I knew what they did, pallet fork, escape wheel, hairspring, and I learned that the most important tool on the bench was not a screwdriver or tweezers but a folded piece of paper he used to cover the parts he wasn't working on, so they wouldn't get lost or dusty.\nWhen I was fourteen, his hands started to shake. The diagnosis was Parkinson's, and the shop closed within a year. For a while he stopped coming down to the basement where we had moved the bench. Then one Saturday he did, and he sat on my stool, and I sat in his chair, and he told me what to do.\nThat is how we worked for the next two years. His eyes and my hands. He was a much stricter teacher when he couldn't do it himself. He made me redo a crystal replacement four times because there was a fingerprint under the glass that I swore was invisible. It wasn't.\nSomewhere in those Saturdays I realized that what I loved about watches was not the watches. It was the moment of finding the one thing that was wrong in a system of a hundred things that were right. A watch that runs fast is almost never broken everywhere. Usually it is one hairspring touching itself, one speck of dust in the wrong place. You find it by being patient enough to look at everything.\nI started seeing that everywhere. When our robotics team couldn't figure out why our arm drifted left, I spent a lunch period watching it move instead of rewriting code, and found a set screw that had worked loose. When my little sister was failing fractions, I sat with her and watched her work until I saw that she was flipping the wrong number when she divided. One thing wrong. Everything else right.\nMy grandfather is eighty now. He still comes down to the basement most Saturdays, mostly to criticize. Last month I finished restoring his own watch, a 1962 Hamilton he wore on his wedding day, which hadn't run in twenty years. When I handed it to him, he held it up to the lamp the way he used to, listened, and said, \"Fast. Two seconds a day, maybe.\"\nThen he smiled, because two seconds is nothing, and because he knew I'd fix it anyway.\nNext fall the bench will stay in Scranton. But I've learned that the size of the world isn't the size of the bench. It's the size of whatever you're willing to look at closely, and I intend to keep looking." },
  { kind: "feature", name: "tricky: local newspaper feature on the county's last independent bookstore before it closes, opens with a hard-news lede (could be mistaken for news)", text: "Last Chapter: After 47 Years, Paper Moon Books Turns the Final Page\nBy Renata Okoye, Harbor Gazette\n\nPORT ELLIS — Paper Moon Books will close for good on Oct. 31, ending 47 years on Front Street and leaving Kittredge County without an independent bookstore.\n\nBut on a gray Wednesday afternoon two weeks before the end, the store does not feel like it is closing. The bell over the door rings every few minutes. A boy of about six is lying on his stomach in the children's section, reading a book about sharks out loud to nobody. The store cat, a heavy orange tabby named Mr. Darcy, has claimed the stack of half-price cookbooks by the register and will not be moved.\n\n\"People keep apologizing to me,\" said owner Frances Lindahl, 74. \"Like somebody died. Nobody died. I'm tired, and the rent went up, and my knees went down. That's all it is.\"\n\nLindahl started at Paper Moon as a part-time clerk in 1981, the year she moved to Port Ellis after a divorce with \"two suitcases and a box of paperbacks.\" She bought the store from its founders seven years later with a loan from her brother. She has survived a lot since then: the chain superstore that opened at the highway mall in 1996 (and closed in 2011), the rise of online retail, a 2009 flood that ruined most of the basement stock, and the pandemic, when she delivered orders herself in a 2004 Subaru.\n\nWhat she could not survive, she said, is a new lease. The building was sold last year, and the new owners are raising the rent from $2,100 to $3,800 a month. Lindahl said she thought about moving, but \"a bookstore isn't the books. It's the building, and the people who know where it is.\"\n\nCustomers have been coming in all month to say goodbye, and most bring a story. Dale Whitaker, a retired lobsterman, says he bought every book his daughter read in high school at Paper Moon \"because Fran wouldn't let her get away with the CliffsNotes.\" Elementary school librarian Carla Mensch says Lindahl has donated books to Port Ellis classrooms for as long as she can remember. \"She'd just show up with a box,\" Mensch said. \"No note, no receipt, nothing.\"\n\nA group of regulars has started talking about forming a cooperative to reopen the store in a smaller space. Lindahl wishes them well but says she won't be involved. \"I'll be a customer,\" she said. \"I've always wanted to be a customer here. I hear the owner's very opinionated.\"\n\nRemaining stock is 50 percent off through closing day. Mr. Darcy is not for sale." },
  { kind: "outline", name: "tricky: research paper outline on the Dust Bowl crammed with facts and source notes (could be mistaken for notes)", text: "US History Research Paper — Outline\nTopic: The Dust Bowl, natural disaster or man-made?\nWorking thesis: The Dust Bowl was caused less by drought than by the way farmers, pushed by government policy and high wheat prices, plowed up the southern Plains in the 1910s and 20s.\nI. Intro\nA. Hook: Black Sunday, April 14, 1935 — \"black blizzard,\" people thought the world was ending\nB. Background: southern Plains (OK + TX panhandles, SW Kansas, SE Colorado, NE New Mexico)\nC. Thesis (above)\nII. Before the dust: the \"Great Plow-Up\"\nA. Homestead Acts (1862; Enlarged Homestead Act 1909) brought settlers onto dry land\nB. WWI wheat prices high → gov't encouraged planting (\"wheat will win the war\")\nC. Tractors + disc plows → millions of acres of native grass plowed under (Egan p. 42?? check page)\n1. grass roots had held the soil in place for thousands of years\nD. 1920s: wheat prices drop, farmers plow MORE to make up the difference\nIII. The drought (1930s)\nA. Severe drought starting 1930-31\nB. Bare soil + wind = dust storms; 14 storms in 1932, 38 in 1933 (Ken Burns doc)\nC. Health: \"dust pneumonia,\" esp. children and the elderly\nIV. Effects\nA. Migration: ~2.5 million people left the Plains states by 1940 (not all went to California)\nB. But most people stayed (Egan's whole point)\nC. Economic: farm foreclosures, loss of topsoil\nV. Government response (counterargument?? or maybe not)\nA. Hugh Hammond Bennett testifies to Congress as a dust storm reaches DC, 1935 → Soil Conservation Service\nB. Shelterbelt project: 220 million trees\nC. Contour plowing, paying farmers to leave land fallow\nD. ** this shows people at the time knew it was man-made, so use it as EVIDENCE for thesis, not counter\nVI. Conclusion\nA. Restate thesis\nB. Connect to today? Ogallala aquifer, drought on the Plains now (maybe too much)\nSources so far:\n- Egan, Timothy. The Worst Hard Time (2005)\n- Burns, Ken. The Dust Bowl (PBS, 2012)\n- Library of Congress / NOAA page on Black Sunday\n- need 2 more scholarly, Worster \"Dust Bowl: The Southern Plains in the 1930s\"?" },
  { kind: "literary", name: "10th grade MLA essay on Candy's dog as foreshadowing in Of Mice and Men", text: "Jasmine Ortega\nMr. Kowalski\nEnglish 10, Period 3\n14 October 2026\nThe Dog That Told Us Everything\nIn John Steinbeck's novel Of Mice and Men, the shooting of Candy's old dog seems like a small moment in the bunkhouse, but it is actually one of the most important scenes in the book. Steinbeck uses the dog to show how the ranch treats anything that is no longer useful, and he uses it to prepare the reader for the ending, when George has to make the same choice about Lennie.\nWhen Carlson complains about the dog, he only talks about how bad it smells and how it is \"no good\" to anybody, including itself (44). Carlson does not care that the dog has been Candy's companion for years. To him the dog is a problem that needs to go away. Candy tries to argue that he has had the dog since it was a pup, but nobody really listens, and once Slim agrees with Carlson the conversation is over. Since Slim is the person everybody on the ranch respects, his word is final. This shows that on the ranch, being old or weak means you don't get a say.\nThe most important part is what Candy says to George afterward. He admits he \"ought to of shot that dog\" himself instead of letting a stranger do it (61). At first this line just seems like Candy feeling guilty. But by the end of the novel George acts on it, whether he means to or not. When Lennie kills Curley's wife and the men are coming with guns, George does not let Curley or Carlson get to Lennie first. He does it himself, and he uses Carlson's Luger, the same gun that killed the dog.\nSome people in our class thought George was being cruel at the end. I think Steinbeck wanted us to see it as the opposite, and the dog scene is why. Candy's regret teaches George that if something terrible has to happen to someone you love, it is better if it comes from you and if it is quick. The dog is old and Lennie is not, but they are both helpless, and both are treated as a problem by the men on the ranch.\nIn conclusion, Candy's dog is not just a side detail. It foreshadows Lennie's death and shows how harsh the ranch is, where loyalty does not protect you. Steinbeck makes us feel bad about the dog first so that we understand George's choice at the end." },
  { kind: "literary", name: "college close reading of form and restraint in Elizabeth Bishop's \"One Art\"", text: "The Parenthesis That Breaks: Control and Collapse in Bishop's \"One Art\"\n\nElizabeth Bishop's \"One Art\" performs mastery so convincingly that a first reading can take it at its word. The speaker insists from the opening line that \"the art of losing isn't hard to master,\" and the villanelle's form seems to agree: two refrains returning on schedule, a rhyme scheme built on only two sounds, nineteen lines that never once lose their footing. Yet the poem's real subject is the gap between that composure and what it is built to hold back. Bishop uses the strictest of fixed forms not to demonstrate control but to stage its failure, and the failure arrives, quite literally, in parentheses.\n\nThe losses escalate with almost comic precision. The speaker begins with door keys and \"the hour badly spent,\" moves to names and places, then to \"my mother's watch,\" then to houses, then to cities, rivers, a continent. Each stanza enlarges the scale while the refrain stays the same, which produces a kind of deadpan irony: the claim that none of this will bring disaster sounds less convincing each time it has to be repeated. A villanelle forces its refrains back whether or not the speaker still believes them, and Bishop exploits that compulsion. The form makes repetition look like denial.\n\nThe final stanza turns from things to a person, the \"you\" whose joking voice and gesture the speaker loves. Here the syntax begins to strain. The refrain is altered for the first time, hedged by \"it's evident,\" and then comes the interruption: \"(Write it!).\" The parenthetical command is the poem's most important moment because it is the only place where the speaker addresses herself. Everything before it has been instruction aimed outward, a lesson in losing delivered as if to a student. Now the speaker has to force her own hand to finish the sentence and admit that this loss \"may look like\" disaster.\n\nThat hedge is worth noticing. Even at the breaking point Bishop refuses a clean confession; the speaker concedes only an appearance. But the italics, the exclamation point and the parenthesis together register a pressure the form cannot absorb. Bishop's surviving drafts of the poem, which run to more than a dozen versions, show how long she worked to keep that final turn understated, and the result is a poem whose restraint is itself the evidence of grief.\n\n\"One Art\" is often read as a poem about acceptance. I would argue it is closer to a poem about the cost of pretending to accept. The villanelle hands the speaker a script for mastery, and the last line shows her reading it aloud at the very moment it stops being true." },
  { kind: "literary", name: "ESL student essay on tradition and the black box in \"The Lottery\"", text: "The Lottery Essay\nIn the story \"The Lottery\" by Shirley Jackson, the author show that people follow tradition even when the tradition is very cruel and nobody remember why they do it. The village have the lottery every year in June and at the end the person who win is stoned by every people, also by her own family. I think the main symbol of this is the black box.\nThe black box is very old and broken. Jackson write that it grew \"shabbier\" every year and the paint is falling off. Mr. Summers want to make a new box but nobody want to change it because it is tradition. This is show that the villagers care more about keep the old way than about if it make sense. Even they already change some part, like they use paper slips instead of wood chips, but they don't stop the lottery.\n\nAlso Old Man Warner is important character for this theme. He call the other towns that stop the lottery a \"pack of crazy fools\". He is the oldest man and he did the lottery many many times, so he think it is necessary. In my country also there is some old tradition that old people say we must keep, so I understand why the young people in the story don't say nothing.\nWhen Tessie Hutchinson is chosen she yell \"It isn't fair\". But before she was chosen she was laughing and talk with neighbors, so she didn't think it was unfair until it happen to her. I think this is the most scary part, because Jackson show that people only care about fairness when they are the victim.\n\nIn conclusion the black box and the characters show that tradition can be dangerous when people follow it without thinking. The story is short but it make me think about my own traditions." },
  { kind: "literary", name: "middle school theme paragraph on memory in The Giver", text: "The Giver Theme Paragraph\nby Owen M.\nIn The Giver by Lois Lowry, one theme is that memories are painful but you need them to have a real life. In Jonas's community nobody has memories except the Receiver. Because of this they don't have war or hunger but they also don't have love or colors. When Jonas gets the memory of the sled ride he feels happy, but later he gets the memory of the war and it hurts him so bad. The Giver tells Jonas that the memories are what give you wisdom. I think Lowry is saying that if you take away all the bad stuff you take away the good stuff too. This is shown when Jonas asks his parents if they love him and his mom tells him love is a word that is so meaningless it is almost obsolete. Jonas realizes his family can't feel things the way he can now. At the end he leaves so the memories will go back to everyone, even the painful ones, because he thinks a life with feelings is better than a safe life with none." },
  { kind: "literary", name: "unfinished draft with bracketed notes on Mama's plant in A Raisin in the Sun", text: "DRAFT 2 - don't submit yet!!\nWorking thesis: In A Raisin in the Sun, Lorraine Hansberry uses Mama's plant to stand for the family's dream, which survives even when it doesn't get enough light. [maybe say \"sun\" instead, more connected to the title??]\nIntro - [hook here, something about the Langston Hughes poem / where the title comes from]\nBody 1 - what the plant is\nThe first time we see the plant is in Act One, when Mama goes straight to the window to take care of it. She says it \"expresses ME\" [check exact wording + page #]. The plant is small and weak because the Younger apartment only has one window and barely any sunlight comes in. This is just like the family, who are crowded into a small apartment on Chicago's South Side and have not had much chance to grow. Mama keeps watering it anyway, which shows she won't give up on things even when the conditions are against them.\nBody 2 - plant + Mama's dream of a house with a garden\nMama tells Ruth that she and Big Walter always planned on a house with a little garden in back. [find quote - Act 1 scene 1 I think]. The plant is basically the garden she never got, shrunk down to fit on a windowsill. So when Mama buys the house in Clybourne Park she is finally giving the dream more room.\nBody 3 - the ending\nThe very last thing Mama does before leaving is come back in for the plant. [Ms. P said this is the strongest evidence, USE IT] Everyone else is already downstairs with the furniture, but she goes back. This shows the dream is still alive and it is going with them, even after Walter lost the money and even though the neighborhood doesn't want them there. Hansberry ends the play on the plant instead of on Walter, which [finish this - something about how the dream belongs to the whole family not just one person]\nConclusion - [tie back to Hughes - he asks if a dream deferred dries up \"like a raisin in the sun\" - the plant DIDN'T dry up]" },
  { kind: "literary", name: "8th grade analysis of the hatchet as a symbol in Hatchet (could be mistaken for bookreport)", text: "Hatchet Essay\nLena Watts\nLanguage Arts 8 - Mrs. Dunleavy\nHatchet by Gary Paulsen is about a thirteen-year-old boy named Brian Robeson who is flying in a small plane to visit his dad in the Canadian oil fields. The pilot has a heart attack and dies, and Brian has to crash land the plane in a lake. After that he is alone in the woods with nothing except his clothes, a windbreaker, and a hatchet his mom gave him right before he left. The book is mostly about how he survives for 54 days until he is rescued.\nEven though the hatchet seems like a small thing, I think it is the most important symbol in the book because it shows how Brian changes from a kid who depends on other people into someone who depends on himself.\nAt the beginning Brian almost doesn't want the hatchet. His mom gives it to him at the airport and he thinks it is kind of embarrassing and only puts it on his belt so she won't be upset. He is also angry at her because of \"the Secret,\" which is that he saw her with another man before the divorce. So the hatchet is connected to his mom and to the divorce, which he doesn't want to think about.\nThen in the woods the hatchet becomes everything. He uses it to make his shelter better and to make spears and a bow. The biggest moment is when he throws it at a porcupine in the dark and it hits the rock wall and makes sparks. That is how he figures out how to make fire. Paulsen makes it a big deal because fire changes everything for Brian. He can cook, keep warm, and keep animals away. After that the hatchet is not about his mom anymore, it is his tool that he earned.\nNear the end Brian gets the survival pack out of the plane and finds a rifle, and he feels like the rifle makes him not part of the woods anymore. I think this is the same idea as the hatchet. The hatchet was something he had to learn to use with his own hands and it made him smarter. The rifle just does the work for him.\nI really liked this book and I would recommend it to anyone who likes adventure. But the main thing I noticed is that the title is not random. The hatchet starts as a present Brian doesn't want and ends as the thing that taught him who he is." },
  { kind: "essay", name: "middle school descriptive essay about a neighborhood laundromat", text: "The Laundromat on Fifth Street\nThe laundromat on Fifth Street smells like warm cotton and that blue soap that comes in the giant jugs. When you walk in the door a little bell rings, but nobody looks up because it rings all the time. The floor is white tiles with black specks, and some of the tiles are cracked near the folding tables where people drop their baskets.\nOn Saturday mornings it is loud. The dryers on the back wall go thump, thump, thump because somebody always puts shoes in them even though the sign says not to. The washers make a swishing sound and then a long whine when they spin. There is a TV in the corner that is always on a cooking show with the sound turned all the way down, so you just watch a man chop onions without hearing anything.\nMy favorite spot is the orange plastic chair by the window. From there you can see the whole room. An old man reads the newspaper and licks his finger every time he turns the page. Two little kids race the laundry carts even though their mom keeps telling them to stop. A lady folds towels so perfectly they look like they came from a store.\nIn the winter the windows fog up from the heat of the dryers and you can draw on them with your finger. Outside it is gray and freezing, but inside it feels like the whole room is breathing warm air on you.\nSome people think a laundromat is boring. I think it is one of the only places where everybody in the neighborhood just sits together and waits, and nobody is in a hurry." },
  { kind: "essay", name: "college composition definition essay on the word \"cringe\"", text: "Ryan Delacroix\nENGL 1010: College Composition I\nProf. Haddad\nEssay 2: Definition\nWhat We Mean When We Say \"Cringe\"\n\nTen years ago, \"cringe\" was mostly a verb. You cringed at a bad joke or at the sound of a fork scraping a plate. Now it is just as often an adjective (\"that video is so cringe\") or even a noun (\"pure cringe\"), and it has become one of the most common judgments people my age make about each other. But when I asked five friends to define it, I got five different answers. That disagreement says something about what the word actually does.\n\nThe dictionary meaning is simple enough: to cringe is to recoil or flinch, usually out of embarrassment or disgust. The older sense was physical. Your body pulled back from something. What has changed is that the word now names the thing that causes the flinch, and the flinch is almost always secondhand. Something is cringe when someone else is embarrassing themselves and does not seem to know it.\n\nThat last part matters. Most of my friends agreed that a person who is self-aware cannot be cringe. A comedian who tells a deliberately terrible joke is not cringe, but an uncle who tells the same joke expecting a big laugh is. So cringe is not really about the quality of the joke. It is about the distance between how someone sees themselves and how others see them. The word measures sincerity that misses its target.\n\nThis is also why the word can be cruel. Calling something cringe often punishes people for caring openly about something, whether that is a hobby, a fandom, or a song they wrote and posted online. One friend pointed out that half the things she called cringe in middle school, like anime and musical theater, are things she loves now. She had not changed her taste so much as her fear of being seen enjoying it.\n\nThere is a newer, softer use too. People say \"cringe culture is dead,\" or call their own old posts cringe with a kind of affection. In that sense the word is turning back on itself and becoming a way to admit that everyone has been embarrassing at some point.\n\nSo my definition is this: \"cringe\" is the feeling of watching someone be more sincere than the moment allows, and the label we give that person so we do not have to feel it ourselves. Whether the word works as an insult or as affection depends on whether the speaker is willing to include themselves." },
  { kind: "essay", name: "exploratory essay on what we owe strangers, prompted by a moment on a train", text: "What Do We Owe a Stranger?\nLast month a man on my train dropped a full bag of oranges, and I watched about fifteen people, myself included, step around them. Two people stopped to help. I have been thinking about it ever since, not because it was dramatic but because it was so ordinary, and I am not sure what I think the right response was.\nOne way to look at it is through the idea of a duty of easy rescue. Philosophers like Peter Singer argue that if you can prevent something bad from happening at little cost to yourself, you should. Picking up oranges is about as low-cost as it gets. By that standard I failed, and so did most of the car. But the standard feels too demanding when I take it seriously. If I owe help whenever it is cheap, then I owe it constantly, to every person who needs directions, every lost wallet, every fundraiser on the sidewalk. Nobody actually lives that way, and I am not sure a life organized around it would be livable.\nAnother view is that obligations come from relationships. I owe my sister things I don't owe a stranger, and that seems right, not selfish. On this view the man with the oranges was owed politeness and nothing else. This is more comfortable, but it explains too little. It cannot say why the two people who helped seemed better than the rest of us, and I think they did.\nMaybe the answer is not about obligation at all. The people who stopped didn't look like they were calculating anything. One of them was laughing. Maybe what we owe strangers is less a duty than a kind of attention, a habit of noticing, and the failure on the train was not exactly moral but a blindness we practice every day to get through crowded places.\nI don't think that settles it. Attention can't be required the way a debt can, and if helping depends on noticing, then people who are tired or anxious or new to the city get let off the hook in a way that seems unfair to the man on his knees collecting oranges. I keep going back and forth. What I do know is that next time I'd like to be the one who stops, and I'm still working out whether that is because I should or just because I want to be that kind of person." },
  { kind: "essay", name: "10-minute in-class warm-up response on what makes a place feel like home", text: "Period 2 - Warm-up response (10 min)\nPrompt: What makes a place feel like home? Use at least one specific example.\nI think a place feels like home when you don't have to think about how to act there. At school I'm always kind of watching myself, like what I'm saying and who is around. But at my aunt's apartment I can walk in, take my shoes off, and go straight to the fridge without asking. Nobody even says anything. It's not the nicest apartment and it's kind of small, but it feels more like home than some places I've actually lived. So I don't think home is about the building or even how long you've been there. It's about being allowed to relax. A hotel can be really nice but it never feels like home because you're a guest. Home is the place where you stop being a guest." },
  { kind: "essay", name: "humorous classification essay sorting the people in a group chat", text: "The Five People in Every Group Chat\nEvery group chat, whether it's for a soccer team, a family, or a chemistry lab section, ends up with the same cast of characters. After being in more group chats than I can count (I have 31 right now, and I checked), I've noticed they sort pretty neatly into five types.\nThe first is the Organizer. This is the person who made the chat, named it something reasonable like \"Bio Lab Group 4,\" and actually uses it for its purpose. The Organizer sends messages with complete sentences and question marks. They ask things like \"Can everyone do Thursday at 6?\" and then wait, with growing despair, for answers that never come.\n\nThe second type is the Ghost. The Ghost was added to the chat and has never typed a word. You are not entirely sure the Ghost has notifications on, or a phone. But every so often a read receipt appears with the Ghost's name, proving they are out there, watching.\nThird is the Reactor. The Reactor does not send messages, only reactions. Every message gets a thumbs up, a laughing face or a heart, sometimes within seconds. The Reactor technically participates more than anyone, and yet has never once answered a question.\n\nThe fourth type is the Derailer. A chat created to plan a group project will, under the Derailer's influence, become a forty-message debate about whether a hot dog is a sandwich. The Derailer is usually the funniest person in the chat, which is exactly why nobody stops them.\nFinally, there is the Midnight Texter, who remembers at 1:14 a.m. that the project is due tomorrow and sends six messages in a row, each one shorter and more panicked than the last.\nMost people are not just one type. I am an Organizer in my lab chat and a Ghost in my extended family chat, which my grandmother has mentioned. But knowing the types helps. If you are trying to get something done, find the Organizer, ignore the Derailer, and accept that the Ghost was never coming." },
  { kind: "pressrelease", name: "startup announces a mobile invoicing app for tradespeople", text: "FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nLedgerly Launches Mobile Invoicing App Built for Plumbers, Electricians and Other Independent Trades\nNew app lets tradespeople send an invoice from the job site in under a minute and get paid by card or bank transfer\nCOLUMBUS, Ohio, Oct. 14, 2026 — Ledgerly, a Columbus-based software startup, today announced the public launch of Ledgerly Field, a mobile invoicing and payments app designed for independent tradespeople who spend their workdays on job sites rather than behind a desk.\nLedgerly Field allows users to build an invoice from a photo of a handwritten estimate, add parts and labor from a saved price list, and text the invoice to a customer before leaving the driveway. Customers can pay by card, Apple Pay or bank transfer, and funds are deposited as soon as the next business day.\n\"Most invoicing software assumes you're sitting at a computer at the end of the day,\" said Dana Whitfield, co-founder and CEO of Ledgerly. \"Our customers are in crawl spaces and on rooftops. We built Ledgerly Field so they can finish the paperwork in the truck and stop chasing payments on the weekend.\"\nDuring a six-month pilot with 400 tradespeople in Ohio and Indiana, users reported getting paid an average of 11 days faster than with paper invoices. Seventy percent of pilot users said they no longer did invoicing at home after work.\nKey features include:\n* Photo-to-invoice: turns a picture of a paper estimate into an editable invoice\n* Saved price lists for common parts and labor rates\n* Automatic payment reminders sent by text\n* Exports to QuickBooks and Xero\n* Works offline and syncs when a signal returns\nLedgerly Field is available today on iOS and Android. The app is free for up to five invoices a month; unlimited invoicing costs $19 per month, plus standard payment processing fees.\nAbout Ledgerly\nFounded in 2023, Ledgerly builds simple financial tools for small and independent businesses. The company is backed by Rust Belt Ventures and Lakeshore Capital and employs 34 people in Columbus. Learn more at ledgerly.io.\nMedia Contact\nPriya Raman\nCommunications Manager, Ledgerly\npress@ledgerly.io\n(614) 555-0182\n###" },
  { kind: "pressrelease", name: "school district news release on a new career and technical education center", text: "Maple Ridge Unified School District\nOffice of Communications\nNEWS RELEASE\nFor release: Monday, October 12, 2026\nContact: Tom Esquivel, Public Information Officer, 555-301-7720, tesquivel@mrusd.org\nMAPLE RIDGE UNIFIED TO OPEN NEW CAREER AND TECHNICAL EDUCATION CENTER IN JANUARY\nMAPLE RIDGE, Wash. — Maple Ridge Unified School District will open the Hollis Career and Technical Education Center to students at the start of the spring semester on January 6, 2027. The 42,000-square-foot facility, located next to Maple Ridge High School, will house programs in welding, automotive technology, health sciences, construction trades and computer networking.\nThe center was funded through the $68 million facilities bond approved by district voters in November 2024. Construction began in March 2025 and is on schedule and approximately $1.2 million under budget, according to district facilities staff.\n\"For years we've had more students who want to take these classes than we've had room for,\" said Superintendent Dr. Angela Morrow. \"This building means a student who wants to be a nurse or an electrician can start working toward that in ninth grade, with real equipment and real industry partners.\"\nThe center will serve students from all three district high schools, with shuttle service running between campuses. Approximately 650 students are expected to enroll in at least one CTE course in the first semester. Several programs will offer dual credit through Cascade Valley Community College and preparation for industry certifications.\nLocal employers including Northline Electric, Ridgeview Medical Center and Pacific Auto Group have partnered with the district to provide equipment, guest instructors and paid summer internships.\nThe community is invited to a ribbon-cutting ceremony and open house on Saturday, December 12, 2026, from 10 a.m. to 1 p.m. Tours of the facility will be offered, and students will demonstrate projects from current CTE classes.\nRegistration for spring CTE courses is open now through November 20. Families can find course descriptions and registration information at mrusd.org/cte.\n-END-" },
  { kind: "pressrelease", name: "volunteer-written release from an animal rescue about a grant for a cat room", text: "Press Release - for immediate release\nSecond Chance Paws Receives $50,000 Grant to Build New Cat Room\nSecond Chance Paws Animal Rescue in Dellwood is excited to announce that we have been awarded a $50,000 grant from the Harmon Family Foundation! The grant will pay for building a new free-roaming cat room at our shelter on Route 9, which will have space for up to 30 cats at a time.\nRight now our cats live in stacked kennels in a back room that used to be a storage area. Our shelter director Linda Pruett said \"Cats in kennels get stressed and stressed cats don't show their personalities, so people walk right past them. When they can move around and play, adopters can see who they really are.\" Last year Second Chance Paws took in 412 cats and adopted out 318.\nConstruction will start in November and the room should be finished by March. We will be having a \"Purr-ty for the Cat Room\" fundraiser on Nov. 7 at Dellwood Brewing Co. from 5-8pm to raise money for cat trees, toys and furniture that the grant doesn't cover.\nSecond Chance Paws is a 501(c)(3) nonprofit, no-kill rescue that has been serving Dellwood County since 2011. We are run almost entirely by volunteers.\nFor more information contact:\nMegan Lowry (volunteer coordinator)\nsecondchancepaws.dellwood@gmail.com\n555-848-2210" },
  { kind: "pressrelease", name: "indie band and label announce a debut album and winter tour", text: "FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nLOW TIDE LANTERN ANNOUNCE DEBUT LP \"SALT IN THE SPEAKERS,\" OUT NOV. 20\nPortland, ME — Indie four-piece Low Tide Lantern will release their first full-length album, Salt in the Speakers, on November 20 via Driftwood Records. The band recorded the 10-track LP over three winter weeks in a converted sardine cannery in Eastport, Maine, live to tape, with most songs finished in two takes or fewer.\nThe first single, \"Ferry Schedule,\" is out today on all streaming platforms, along with a video shot entirely on a 1990s camcorder that drummer Pete Albers found at a yard sale.\n\"We wanted it to sound like the room,\" says singer and guitarist Nora Kessling. \"You can hear the wind on a couple songs. We left it in.\"\nLow Tide Lantern formed in 2022 and have built a following around New England with their loose, harmony-heavy live shows. Their 2024 EP Fog Days was named one of the best local releases of the year by the Portland Weekly Ledger.\nThe band will tour the Northeast this winter:\nNov. 21 - Portland, ME - The Breakwater Room (album release show)\nNov. 22 - Portsmouth, NH - Ropewalk Hall\nNov. 28 - Boston, MA - The Long Wharf Social\nDec. 4 - Providence, RI - Gaslight Theatre\nDec. 5 - Brooklyn, NY - Cormorant\nPress photos, the album stream and interview requests: hello@driftwoodrecords.net\n###" },
  { kind: "pressrelease", name: "food company voluntary recall of trail mix for undeclared peanuts, with lot table", text: "Hollow Creek Foods Issues Voluntary Recall of Mountain Berry Trail Mix Due to Undeclared Peanuts\nFOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE — October 9, 2026\nCompany Contact: Consumer Affairs, 1-800-555-0147, Monday–Friday, 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. CT\nGREEN BAY, Wis. — Hollow Creek Foods of Green Bay, Wisconsin, is voluntarily recalling select lots of its 10-ounce Mountain Berry Trail Mix because the product may contain peanuts that are not declared on the label. People who have an allergy or severe sensitivity to peanuts run the risk of a serious or life-threatening allergic reaction if they consume this product.\nThe recalled trail mix was distributed to grocery stores in Wisconsin, Minnesota, Iowa and Illinois between August 28 and October 3, 2026. The product comes in a clear, resealable plastic bag with a purple label. The lot code and best-by date are printed on the back of the bag below the nutrition facts panel.\nProduct\tSize\tUPC\tLot Code\tBest By\nMountain Berry Trail Mix\t10 oz\t0 84512 33019 6\tHC26-0812\t02/12/2027\nMountain Berry Trail Mix\t10 oz\t0 84512 33019 6\tHC26-0819\t02/19/2027\nMountain Berry Trail Mix\t10 oz\t0 84512 33019 6\tHC26-0826\t02/26/2027\nNo other Hollow Creek Foods products are affected by this recall.\nTwo allergic reactions have been reported to date. Neither required hospitalization.\nThe recall was initiated after the company discovered that a shared packaging line was not fully cleaned between production runs of its Mountain Berry Trail Mix and its Classic Peanut Trail Mix. The company has since revised its cleaning and changeover procedures and retrained packaging staff.\nConsumers who have purchased the recalled product should not eat it. They may return it to the place of purchase for a full refund or discard it. Consumers with questions may contact Hollow Creek Foods Consumer Affairs at 1-800-555-0147.\nThis recall is being made with the knowledge of the U.S. Food and Drug Administration.\n###" },
  { kind: "pressrelease", name: "community college announces a free tuition program, written in news style (could be mistaken for news)", text: "Riverbend Community College to Cover Full Tuition for County High School Graduates Starting Fall 2027\nBy the Riverbend Community College Office of Marketing and Communications\nOctober 13, 2026\nLAKE ARBOR, Mich. — Graduates of any high school in Tamarack County will be able to attend Riverbend Community College tuition-free beginning in fall 2027, the college announced Tuesday.\nThe program, called Riverbend Promise, will cover any tuition and mandatory fees remaining after federal and state grants for students who enroll within 16 months of graduating high school and keep at least a 2.0 GPA. Students may use the program for up to three years or until they earn an associate degree or certificate.\nThe college estimates that around 900 students will use the program in its first year, roughly 300 more than currently enroll from county high schools. Funding comes from a $14 million, ten-year commitment from the Tamarack County Community Foundation, along with existing college funds.\n\"We kept hearing from families that cost was the reason their kids weren't even applying,\" Riverbend President Dr. Marcus Ellery said at an event at Lake Arbor High School attended by about 200 seniors. \"Now the answer to 'Can we afford college?' is yes.\"\nSeniors at the event said the announcement changed their plans. Lake Arbor senior Kayla Brandt, who works part-time at a hardware store and had planned to wait a year before applying anywhere, said she now intends to start Riverbend's nursing prerequisites next fall.\nRiverbend joins a growing number of community colleges in Michigan and around the country that offer tuition-free programs for local graduates.\nStudents must complete the FAFSA and apply to Riverbend by August 1 of their graduation year to qualify. Information sessions will be held at each county high school in November.\nAbout Riverbend Community College\nRiverbend Community College serves about 6,400 students each year at its main campus in Lake Arbor and two satellite centers. It offers more than 60 associate degree and certificate programs.\nMedia contact: Jenna Osei, Director of Communications, (269) 555-0164, josei@riverbendcc.edu" },
  { kind: "reflection", name: "11th grade reflection on a group documentary project about the Triangle Shirtwaist fire", text: "Mia Castellano\nAP U.S. History - Mr. Brandt\nUnit 6 Project Reflection\nFor our Unit 6 project my group (me, Devon, Hailey, and Marcus) made a 9 minute documentary about the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory fire and how it changed labor laws. Overall I'm proud of how it turned out but the process was definitely not smooth.\nWhat went well: I think our research was really strong. Hailey found transcripts from the trial and Devon found old photographs from the Library of Congress that made the video look a lot more professional. I was in charge of the script and narration and I think the second half, where we explained the Factory Investigating Commission, was the clearest part of the whole video. When we showed it in class a few people said they didn't know the fire led to actual laws, which was kind of the point.\nWhat didn't go well: we spent way too long on research and didn't start editing until four days before it was due. Marcus was supposed to edit but his laptop couldn't handle the software, so we had to switch to my laptop at the last minute and I ended up doing most of the editing at like midnight. The audio is uneven in a couple places because we recorded narration on different days in different rooms. Also our ending was rushed and kind of just stops.\nWhat I learned: I learned a lot about the Progressive Era, obviously, but I also learned that I take on too much when I'm worried something won't get done. Instead of telling Mr. Brandt or talking to the group early, I just did it myself and then was annoyed about it. That's not really fair to anyone.\nWhat I would do differently: Make a real schedule with deadlines for each part on the first day, and test the editing software before we need it. I would also record all the narration in one session. And I would say something sooner when the work isn't balanced instead of waiting until the night before." },
  { kind: "reflection", name: "civil engineering intern's weekly reflection on a traffic study", text: "Week 6 Internship Reflection\nCE 395 - Professional Practice Internship\nIntern: Daniel Kwon | Site: Harlan & Pierce Engineering, Transportation Group\n\nThis week I spent most of my time helping with the traffic study for the Elm Street and 4th Avenue intersection redesign. My supervisor, Rachel, had me go out with one of the technicians on Tuesday and Thursday to set up tube counters and record turning movements during the morning and evening peaks. On Friday I entered the counts into a spreadsheet and started building the existing-conditions model in Synchro.\n\nThe fieldwork was eye-opening in a way I didn't expect. In class, traffic volumes are just numbers in a problem statement. Standing at the corner at 7:30 in the morning, I noticed things the counts don't capture, like how many drivers cut through the gas station lot to avoid the left turn, and how school buses block the right lane for almost a full signal cycle. When I mentioned the gas station cut-through to Rachel, she said that was exactly the kind of thing that ends up mattering in the final recommendations, and she asked me to write it up as a field note.\n\nThe modeling part was harder. I thought I understood signal timing from CE 361, but I had trouble setting up the phasing correctly and spent most of Friday morning stuck. I was hesitant to ask for help because everyone seemed busy, and I lost a couple of hours that way. When I finally asked, one of the junior engineers walked me through it in about fifteen minutes. Lesson learned: in an office, asking a quick question is cheaper than staying stuck.\n\nSomething I want to work on next week is my field notes. Mine were pretty disorganized, and when I went to enter the data I had to guess at a few of my own abbreviations. I'm going to make a template before we go back out.\n\nOverall this week made me more interested in transportation as a specialty. I like that the work connects the calculations to what people actually experience when they drive or walk through a place." },
  { kind: "reflection", name: "nursing student clinical reflection structured by Gibbs' cycle", text: "NURS 312 Clinical Reflection #3 (Gibbs' Reflective Cycle)\nStudent: K. Abara\nClinical site: Med-Surg, 4 West\nDescription\nDuring my clinical shift on 10/8 I was assigned to an 82-year-old patient recovering from a hip replacement. While I was helping her get ready to ambulate for the first time post-op, she became tearful and told me she was afraid of falling again because that is how she broke her hip. My instructor was with another student and the patient's nurse was in a different room. I stopped, sat down with her, and asked what specifically worried her. After talking for a few minutes we agreed to just sit at the edge of the bed and dangle her legs, and then try standing with the walker with two staff present later in the shift.\nFeelings\nAt first I felt panicked because I had a plan for the morning and was worried about falling behind. I also felt unsure if I was allowed to change the plan without asking. Once we started talking I felt calmer and more confident, and afterwards I felt good that she trusted me.\nEvaluation\nWhat went well was that I didn't push her, and she ended up walking 20 feet with PT in the afternoon. What did not go well was that I didn't communicate the change right away. The nurse found out when she came in to give meds, and she was understanding but said she would have liked to know sooner because it affected the PT schedule.\nAnalysis\nFear of falling is very common in older adults after a fall-related injury and can actually increase fall risk by making them limit their activity. Taking time to address her fear was appropriate and patient-centered. However, nursing care is a team process, and any change to the plan needs to be communicated, for example using SBAR, so the whole team is on the same page. I focused on the patient but forgot the system around her.\nConclusion\nI learned that listening to a patient's fears is part of mobility care, not separate from it. I also learned that good care for one patient can cause problems if the rest of the team doesn't know about it.\nAction Plan\nNext clinical I will notify the primary nurse right away when a patient refuses or modifies an activity, using a quick SBAR. I also want to read more about fall-prevention interventions for post-op orthopedic patients, especially ways to assess fear of falling." },
  { kind: "reflection", name: "short 7th grade reflection on writing a persuasive essay", text: "my reflection on my persuasive essay\nI wrote my persuasive essay about why school should start later. I think I did good on my reasons, especially the one about teenagers needing more sleep because I found a fact about it. The hardest part was the counterargument because I didnt really know what the other side would say, I just said some parents work early. Next time I would start earlier because I wrote most of it the night before and I didnt have time to check my spelling. I also want to make my conclusion better because it was kind of short. I learned that a persuasive essay needs evidence not just your opinion." },
  { kind: "reflection", name: "first-year 5th grade teacher's post-observation self-reflection answering coach prompts", text: "Post-Observation Self-Reflection\nTeacher: Renata Flores, Grade 5\nObserver: Mr. Okafor (Instructional Coach)\nLesson observed: Adding fractions with unlike denominators, 10/6/2026\n1. What were your goals for this lesson, and to what extent were they met?\nMy goal was for students to use fraction strips to find a common denominator and then explain why they could not just add the denominators. Based on the exit tickets, about 18 of 26 students correctly added two fractions with unlike denominators, so the first part was mostly met. The explanation part was weaker. Only a handful of students could say in words why 1/2 + 1/3 is not 2/5.\n2. What evidence of student learning did you see during the lesson?\nThe partner work with the fraction strips was the strongest part. I heard a lot of good talk, for example Jayden telling his partner \"you can't add them yet, they're not the same size pieces.\" That was exactly the understanding I wanted.\n3. What would you change if you taught this lesson again?\nI spent too long on the warm-up (almost 12 minutes instead of 5), and that cut into our closing discussion, which is where I had planned to get students explaining their thinking. I also realize now that I modeled the procedure before students had a chance to explore, which may be why so many of them relied on the steps instead of the reasoning. Next time I would let them try with the strips first and then name the procedure together.\n4. How did you meet the needs of diverse learners?\nI had pre-cut strips for students with fine motor needs and a sentence frame on the board for my ELs (\"I can't add these yet because...\"). I don't think I checked in enough with the two students who were absent for the equivalent fractions lesson last week. They were lost for most of the partner work.\n5. What support would you like from your coach?\nHelp with pacing, maybe a timer routine, and ideas for closing discussions that work in under ten minutes." },
  { kind: "reflection", name: "NHS service-hours reflection on being a counselor at a diabetes camp (could be mistaken for personal)", text: "Service Learning Reflection - Camp Sugarpine\n(Required for National Honor Society service hours, minimum 300 words)\nThis summer I volunteered for two weeks as a junior counselor at Camp Sugarpine, a sleepaway camp in the mountains for kids ages 8 to 14 who have type 1 diabetes. I was assigned to a cabin of nine-year-old boys with a senior counselor named Eli.\nThe first night was rough. One of my campers, Theo, had never slept away from home before, and his blood sugar went low around 2 a.m. The nurse came and handled it, but Theo was scared and crying and kept asking for his mom. I didn't know what to do so I just sat on the floor next to his bunk and talked about Pokémon until he fell asleep. I remember thinking I was in way over my head.\nBy the end of the first week things were different. I learned how to read the kids' glucose monitors and recognize the signs of a low before they said anything, like when someone got quiet and pale during capture the flag. More than that, I saw how normal everything was for them there. At school, some of these kids are the only one who has to leave class to check their blood sugar. At camp, everyone did finger sticks before meals at the same table, joking around, and nobody thought twice. Theo told me on the last day that it was the first time he didn't feel \"like the weird one.\"\nGoing in, I thought my job was to help supervise activities. What I actually learned is that a big part of helping people is making a situation feel normal for them. I also learned I'm calmer in emergencies than I thought, as long as I know what the plan is.\nIf I do this again next summer, which I want to, I would ask for more training on the medical side before camp starts so I'm not learning everything in the first few nights. I would also spend more time getting to know the quieter campers early instead of mostly hanging out with the loud ones.\nThis experience made me seriously think about pediatric nursing as a career, which I had never considered before." },
  { kind: "memo", name: "HR memo announcing a new paid time off request process", text: "MEMORANDUM\nTO: All Staff\nFROM: Lorraine Patel, Director of Human Resources\nDATE: October 14, 2026\nRE: New Paid Time Off Request Process Beginning November 1\nEffective November 1, 2026, all paid time off (PTO) requests must be submitted through the Employee Self-Service portal in Workday. Paper request forms and email requests to managers will no longer be accepted after October 31.\nWhy we're making this change\nOver the past year, HR has received a number of questions about PTO balances that did not match employees' own records. Most of these discrepancies came from paper forms that were approved but never entered into payroll. Moving all requests into one system will keep balances accurate in real time and give managers a single place to review and approve requests.\nWhat you need to do\n1. Log in to Workday and select \"Time Off\" from the home screen.\n2. Choose the type of leave (vacation, sick, personal or bereavement) and the dates requested.\n3. Add a comment if needed and submit. Your manager will receive an automatic notification.\n4. You will receive an email when your request is approved or denied.\nRequests for vacation of three or more consecutive days should be submitted at least two weeks in advance, as outlined in the Employee Handbook (Section 6.2). Sick leave may be entered on the day of the absence or upon your return.\nPTO already approved on paper for dates after November 1 will be entered by HR. You do not need to resubmit it, but please check your Workday balance by November 7 and contact HR if anything looks incorrect.\nTraining\nHR will hold two short training sessions in Conference Room B on Wednesday, October 21 at 10:00 a.m. and Thursday, October 22 at 3:00 p.m. A step-by-step guide is also available on the intranet under HR > Forms & Guides.\nQuestions may be directed to hr@castellanlogistics.com or ext. 2240." },
  { kind: "memo", name: "middle school principal's memo to teachers on benchmark testing week", text: "Westbrook Middle School\nTo: Teachers and Instructional Staff\nFrom: Dr. Harold Simmons, Principal\nCC: Ms. Tran, Assistant Principal; Mr. Bell, Testing Coordinator\nDate: October 13, 2026\nSubject: Fall Benchmark Testing Week, Oct. 26–30\nFall benchmark testing in reading and math will take place the week of October 26. Please read the following carefully and send any questions to Mr. Bell by Friday, Oct. 23.\nSchedule\nMonday 10/26 — Grade 6 Reading (periods 1–3)\nTuesday 10/27 — Grade 7 Reading (periods 1–3)\nWednesday 10/28 — Grade 8 Reading (periods 1–3)\nThursday 10/29 — Grades 6–8 Math (periods 1–3, by grade level hallway)\nFriday 10/30 — Make-up sessions in the library\nPeriods 4–7 will follow the regular bell schedule each day. Lunch times do not change.\nProctor expectations\n- Pick up your testing folder and Chromebook cart from the library by 7:45 a.m.\n- Student phones must be collected and stored at the front of the room before testing begins. Staff phones should be put away as well, except for the emergency phone in your folder.\n- Do not read test questions aloud or define words for students unless the student has a read-aloud accommodation listed in your folder.\n- Post the \"Testing — Do Not Disturb\" sign on your door.\n- Students who finish early may read silently. No other devices or homework.\nAccommodations\nStudents with IEPs and 504 plans that include small group or extended time will be tested in rooms 112 and 114 with Ms. Garza and Mr. Pham. Please do not keep these students in your own room for testing. A list will be in your folder.\nAbsences\nIf a student is absent, mark them on the roster and return it with your folder. Make-ups will be scheduled by Mr. Bell.\nThank you for helping our students do their best. I know testing weeks disrupt instruction, and I appreciate your flexibility." },
  { kind: "memo", name: "short kitchen manager memo about the walk-in cooler before a health inspection", text: "MEMO\nTo: All Kitchen Staff (AM + PM)\nFrom: Carlos, Kitchen Manager\nDate: 10/12\nRe: Walk-in cooler\nHealth inspection is coming sometime in the next 2 weeks. Starting today:\n- Everything in the walk-in gets labeled and dated. No exceptions. Tape and markers are on the shelf by the door.\n- Raw meat on the BOTTOM shelf only.\n- Nothing on the floor. Not even \"for a second.\"\n- Last person out at close checks the temp log and signs it.\nI found three unlabeled containers this morning and had to toss them. That's money in the trash. If you're not sure where something goes, ask me or Jess.\nThanks.\n-C" },
  { kind: "memo", name: "student government finance memo to club leaders about budget request deadlines", text: "MEMORANDUM\nTO: Presidents and Treasurers of All Chartered Student Organizations\nFROM: Aaliyah Brooks, SGA Vice President of Finance\nDATE: September 29, 2026\nSUBJECT: Spring 2027 Budget Request Deadline and Changes\n\nThis memo is to remind all chartered student organizations that Spring 2027 budget requests are due by 11:59 p.m. on Friday, October 30. Late requests will not be considered for spring funding. There are no exceptions to this deadline, including for technical problems, so please don't wait until the last day.\n\nThere are two changes to the process this year:\na. All requests must now be submitted through the new CampusGroups finance form. The Google Form used last year has been closed.\nb. Organizations requesting more than $1,500 must attend a 10-minute budget hearing with the Finance Committee during the week of November 9. Sign-up links will be emailed after the deadline.\n\nPlease remember that the Finance Committee cannot fund food for general meetings, gifts or prizes over $25, or travel for fewer than three members. Itemized quotes are required for any purchase over $200.\n\nTreasurers who have not yet completed treasurer training must do so before submitting a request. The training takes about 30 minutes and is on the SGA Canvas page.\n\nIf you have questions, office hours are Tuesdays and Thursdays from 2 to 4 p.m. in Student Union Room 214, or email finance@mvcsga.org." },
  { kind: "memo", name: "public policy class briefing memo to a councilmember on regulating shared e-scooters", text: "PPOL 340: Policy Analysis and Writing\nPolicy Memo 2 — Final Draft\nTO: Councilmember Teresa Vance, District 3, Fairhaven City Council\nFROM: Jordan Ellis, Legislative Intern\nDATE: October 9, 2026\nRE: Options for Regulating Shared E-Scooters Ahead of the November 17 Vote\nSummary\nThe council will vote in November on whether to renew Fairhaven's pilot permit for shared electric scooters, which expires December 31. Complaints about sidewalk parking have tripled since the pilot began, but ridership data suggests scooters are replacing a meaningful number of short car trips downtown. This memo outlines three options and recommends renewing the program with designated parking corrals and a smaller fleet cap.\nBackground\nThe 18-month pilot allowed two operators to deploy up to 600 scooters each within the downtown and university districts. According to data the operators reported to the city, riders took about 410,000 trips during the pilot, with a median trip length of 1.1 miles. A rider survey by the city's transportation office found that 34 percent of respondents would otherwise have driven or used a ride-hailing service.\nAt the same time, 311 complaints related to scooters rose from 48 in the first quarter of the pilot to 151 in the most recent quarter. Most concerned scooters blocking sidewalks, curb ramps and bus stops. The Fairhaven Disability Advocacy Council has testified twice asking the council to end the program.\nOptions\nOption 1: End the program. This eliminates sidewalk complaints and enforcement costs but removes a transportation option used heavily by students and downtown workers. It may also push riders toward privately owned scooters, which the city cannot regulate in the same way.\nOption 2: Renew without changes. This preserves ridership but does not address the main complaint and is unlikely to win support from advocacy groups or District 3 residents.\nOption 3: Renew with conditions. Require scooters downtown to be parked in designated painted corrals (riders could not end a trip outside one), reduce the fleet cap from 1,200 to 900 total, and fine operators $50 per scooter not removed from an accessibility obstruction within two hours.\nRecommendation\nI recommend Option 3. Mandatory parking zones directly target the source of most complaints, and a smaller fleet would reduce the number of idle scooters on downtown sidewalks. The roughly $85,000 needed to paint 120 corrals could be covered by raising the per-scooter permit fee from $50 to $120, which both operators indicated they could absorb.\nNext Steps\nIf you support this approach, transportation staff could draft amendment language for the November 3 committee meeting. I would also suggest meeting with the Disability Advocacy Council before the vote so their concerns are reflected in the amendment." },
  { kind: "memo", name: "lab manager memo summarizing decisions from a safety meeting (could be mistaken for meeting)", text: "MEMO\nTo: All Graduate Students and Research Staff, Okonkwo Lab\nFrom: Dr. Sarah Lindqvist, Lab Manager\nDate: October 6, 2026\nRe: Decisions from the October 3 lab safety meeting\nThank you to everyone who came to Friday's safety meeting. For those who could not attend, here is a summary of what was decided. These changes take effect Monday, October 12, and apply to everyone working in Rooms 310–314.\n1. Fume hood sign-up. The shared hood in 312 will now be reserved through the lab calendar in 2-hour blocks. Walk-up use is fine if the hood is open, but reservations take priority.\n2. After-hours work. Anyone working after 7 p.m. or on weekends must text the after-hours group chat when they arrive and leave. No one may use the solvent cabinet or work with anything on the high-hazard list while alone in the lab.\n3. Waste labels. Every hazardous waste container must have a completed label as soon as the first drop goes in, not when the container is full. EHS cited us for this in September.\n4. Eyewash checks. Weekly eyewash flushing will rotate among graduate students. Marisol will post the schedule; Ben has the first week.\n5. Lab coats. The lab will order new flame-resistant coats for everyone working with pyrophorics. Please give your size to Ben by October 9.\nTwo items were raised but not decided: whether to move the -80 freezer out of 314, and whether undergraduates need a separate orientation. Dr. Okonkwo will follow up on both at the November group meeting.\nIf you have concerns about any of these changes, please come talk to me directly." },
  { kind: "studyguide", name: "high school biology vocabulary list for cellular respiration", text: "Unit 4 Vocab - Cellular Respiration\nBio H | Test Thurs 10/22\n1. Cellular respiration - process cells use to break down glucose and make ATP. Overall: C6H12O6 + 6O2 → 6CO2 + 6H2O + ATP\n2. ATP (adenosine triphosphate) - the main energy molecule of the cell; releases energy when the third phosphate is removed\n3. Aerobic - requires oxygen\n4. Anaerobic - does not require oxygen\n5. Glycolysis - first step; glucose is split into 2 pyruvate in the cytoplasm; makes 2 ATP (net) and 2 NADH; anaerobic\n6. Pyruvate - 3-carbon molecule made at the end of glycolysis\n7. Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle) - in the mitochondrial matrix; breaks down pyruvate, releases CO2, makes NADH, FADH2 and 2 ATP\n8. Electron transport chain (ETC) - in the inner mitochondrial membrane; uses electrons from NADH and FADH2 to pump H+ ions; makes most of the ATP (about 32-34)\n9. NADH / FADH2 - electron carriers, bring high-energy electrons to the ETC\n10. Mitochondria - organelle where Krebs and ETC happen (\"powerhouse\")\n11. Matrix - fluid inside the inner membrane of the mitochondria\n12. Cristae - folds of the inner membrane; more surface area = more ETC\n13. ATP synthase - enzyme that makes ATP as H+ flows back through it\n14. Fermentation - anaerobic process that lets glycolysis keep going by recycling NAD+\n15. Lactic acid fermentation - in muscle cells and some bacteria; pyruvate → lactic acid\n16. Alcoholic fermentation - in yeast; pyruvate → ethanol + CO2\n** Know where each stage happens and how much ATP each one makes!!" },
  { kind: "studyguide", name: "APUSH Period 3 front/back flashcards on the Revolution era", text: "APUSH Period 3 Flashcards (1754-1800)\nFront: Albany Plan of Union (1754)\nBack: Ben Franklin's plan to unite the colonies under one government for defense during the French & Indian War. Rejected by the colonies + Britain. \"Join or Die\" cartoon.\nFront: Proclamation of 1763\nBack: Britain bans colonists from settling west of the Appalachians to avoid conflict w/ Native Americans. Colonists angry - felt they'd earned the land.\nFront: Stamp Act (1765)\nBack: Direct tax on printed materials (newspapers, legal docs, playing cards). Led to Stamp Act Congress + boycotts. \"No taxation without representation.\" Repealed 1766.\nFront: Declaratory Act (1766)\nBack: Passed same day the Stamp Act was repealed - Parliament says it can make laws for the colonies \"in all cases whatsoever.\"\nFront: Townshend Acts (1767)\nBack: Taxes on imports (glass, paper, paint, lead, TEA). Colonial boycotts → most repealed in 1770 except the tea tax.\nFront: Common Sense (1776)\nBack: Thomas Paine pamphlet arguing for independence in plain language. Sold 100,000+ copies in a few months. Swung public opinion.\nFront: Battle of Saratoga (1777)\nBack: Turning point of the Revolution - American victory convinced France to ally with the US.\nFront: Articles of Confederation\nBack: First US constitution (ratified 1781). Weak central gov - no power to tax, no executive, needed 9/13 states to pass laws, 13/13 to amend.\nFront: Shays' Rebellion (1786-87)\nBack: Massachusetts farmers revolt over debt/taxes. Showed weakness of the Articles → Constitutional Convention.\nFront: Great Compromise\nBack: Bicameral Congress - House by population (VA Plan) + Senate equal (NJ Plan). Roger Sherman." },
  { kind: "studyguide", name: "6th grade teacher's plate tectonics test review sheet with a table", text: "Plate Tectonics Test Review\n6th Grade Science - Ms. Albright\nTest is Wednesday, October 21! Use this sheet AND your notebook to study. Answers are on the back (don't peek until you try!).\nPart 1: Know these words\n● crust - the thin, rocky outer layer of the Earth\n● mantle - the thick, hot layer under the crust; it moves very slowly\n● core - the center of the Earth (outer core is liquid, inner core is solid)\n● tectonic plates - giant pieces of the Earth's crust and upper mantle that move\n● convection currents - the slow movement of heated material in the mantle that pushes the plates\n● Pangaea - the supercontinent that existed about 250 million years ago\n● fault - a crack in the crust where rocks move\nPart 2: Three kinds of plate boundaries\nBoundary\tHow the plates move\tWhat it makes\tExample\nDivergent\tApart\tRift valleys, mid-ocean ridges\tMid-Atlantic Ridge\nConvergent\tTogether\tMountains, volcanoes, trenches\tHimalayas\nTransform\tSlide past each other\tEarthquakes\tSan Andreas Fault\nPart 3: Be ready to answer\n1. What evidence did Alfred Wegener use to support continental drift? (Hint: there are 3 kinds - think puzzle pieces, fossils, and rocks.)\n2. Why do most earthquakes and volcanoes happen near plate boundaries?\n3. Draw and label the layers of the Earth.\n4. Explain how convection currents in the mantle move the plates. Use the words heat, rise and sink.\n5. If two plates are moving apart under the ocean, what will form?\nStudy tip: Make flashcards for Part 1 and have someone at home quiz you!" },
  { kind: "studyguide", name: "messy college student study guide for an Intro Psych midterm", text: "PSYC 101 MIDTERM STUDY GUIDE (chs 1-6)\nexam is 10/20 in Lindell Hall NOT the regular room!!\n50 MC + 2 short answer. prof said short answer will be research methods + one of the memory models\nCH 1 - history / perspectives\n- Wundt = first psych lab 1879 (structuralism, introspection)\n- James = functionalism\n- Watson + Skinner = behaviorism (only observable behavior)\n- know the 7 perspectives and be able to match an example to each one (she LOVES these questions)\nCH 2 - research methods ***SHORT ANSWER\n- IV = what you change, DV = what you measure\n- correlation ≠ causation!!! (third variable problem)\n- random assignment vs random sampling - DIFFERENT. assignment = into groups, sampling = who's in the study\n- double blind = neither participants nor researchers know who is in which group\n- placebo effect\n- operational definition = exactly how you measure something\nCH 3 - bio\n- neuron: dendrites → cell body → axon → terminals\n- neurotransmitters: dopamine (reward, movement), serotonin (mood), acetylcholine (muscles, memory), GABA (inhibitory)\n- lobes: frontal (planning, personality), parietal (touch), temporal (hearing), occipital (vision)\n- Phineas Gage!\nCH 4 - sensation + perception\n- absolute threshold vs difference threshold (JND)\n- Weber's law - ??? ask in review session\nCH 5 - consciousness / sleep\n- sleep stages, REM = dreams\n- circadian rhythm\nCH 6 - learning\n- classical conditioning (Pavlov): UCS, UCR, CS, CR - PRACTICE IDENTIFYING THESE\n- operant (Skinner): pos/neg reinforcement vs punishment. negative reinforcement is NOT punishment\n- schedules of reinforcement: fixed/variable ratio/interval. slot machine = variable ratio\nmemory models were ch 7?? check if that's even on the exam" },
  { kind: "studyguide", name: "Spanish 3 review sheet on preterite vs imperfect with a conjugation table", text: "Repaso: el pretérito vs. el imperfecto\nSpanish 3 - Quiz Friday\nWhen do I use which??\nPRETERITE = completed actions, one time, specific start/end\n* Ayer comí pizza. (one time, done)\n* Viví en México por dos años. (specific length = preterite)\n* Trigger words: ayer, anoche, el año pasado, una vez, de repente\nIMPERFECT = habits, descriptions, background, \"used to / was ___ing\"\n* Cuando era niño, comía pizza todos los viernes. (habit)\n* Hacía sol y los pájaros cantaban. (description/background)\n* Time, age, feelings in the past: Eran las tres. Tenía diez años. Estaba cansada.\n* Trigger words: siempre, todos los días, a menudo, mientras, de niño\nBOTH in one sentence: imperfect = what was going on, preterite = what interrupted it\n* Yo leía cuando sonó el teléfono.\nRegular endings\n\thablar (pret.)\thablar (imp.)\tcomer (pret.)\tcomer (imp.)\nyo\thablé\thablaba\tcomí\tcomía\ntú\thablaste\thablabas\tcomiste\tcomías\nél/ella/Ud.\thabló\thablaba\tcomió\tcomía\nnosotros\thablamos\thablábamos\tcomimos\tcomíamos\nellos/Uds.\thablaron\thablaban\tcomieron\tcomían\nOnly 3 irregular imperfects!! ser (era), ir (iba), ver (veía)\nPractice - which one? (answers at bottom)\n1. Mi abuela siempre ____ (cocinar) los domingos.\n2. El tren ____ (llegar) a las 8:15.\n3. Nosotros ____ (dormir) cuando empezó la tormenta.\nAnswers: 1. cocinaba  2. llegó  3. dormíamos" },
  { kind: "math", name: "Algebra 1 homework solving systems by substitution and elimination", text: "Alg 1 - 5.3 HW Systems of Equations\n#4  Solve by substitution\ny = 2x - 3\n3x + y = 12\n3x + (2x - 3) = 12\n5x - 3 = 12\n5x = 15\nx = 3\ny = 2(3) - 3\ny = 3\n(3, 3)\ncheck: 3(3) + 3 = 12 ✓\n#8  Solve by elimination\n2x + 3y = 7\n4x - 3y = 5\nadd them:\n6x = 12\nx = 2\n2(2) + 3y = 7\n4 + 3y = 7\n3y = 3\ny = 1\n(2, 1)\n#11\nx + 4y = -6\n3x - 2y = 10\nmultiply first eq by -3\n-3x - 12y = 18\n3x - 2y = 10\n-14y = 28\ny = -2\nx + 4(-2) = -6\nx - 8 = -6\nx = 2\n(2, -2)\ncheck 3(2) - 2(-2) = 6 + 4 = 10 ✓" },
  { kind: "math", name: "calculus related rates problems, sliding ladder and draining cone", text: "Related Rates - Problem 3\nA ladder 13 ft long is leaning against a wall. The bottom slides away from the wall at 2 ft/s. How fast is the top of the ladder sliding down when the bottom is 5 ft from the wall?\nLet x = distance from wall to bottom, y = height of top\nx^2 + y^2 = 13^2\n2x(dx/dt) + 2y(dy/dt) = 0\nwhen x = 5: 25 + y^2 = 169\ny^2 = 144\ny = 12\ndx/dt = 2\n2(5)(2) + 2(12)(dy/dt) = 0\n20 + 24(dy/dt) = 0\ndy/dt = -20/24 = -5/6 ft/s\nThe top is sliding down at 5/6 ft/s (≈ 0.83 ft/s).\n\nProblem 4\nWater drains from a conical tank (vertex down) with radius 4 m and height 10 m at a rate of 3 m^3/min. How fast is the water level falling when the water is 6 m deep?\nV = (1/3)πr^2 h\nsimilar triangles: r/h = 4/10 → r = (2/5)h\nV = (1/3)π(2h/5)^2 h = (4π/75)h^3\ndV/dt = (4π/25)h^2 (dh/dt)\n-3 = (4π/25)(36)(dh/dt)\n-3 = (144π/25)(dh/dt)\ndh/dt = -75/(144π)\ndh/dt = -25/(48π) ≈ -0.166 m/min\nwater level dropping at about 0.17 m/min" },
  { kind: "math", name: "chemistry stoichiometry practice with limiting reactant and percent yield", text: "Chem - Stoichiometry Practice\nName: Tariq H.   Per. 5\n1) How many grams of water are produced when 8.0 g of H2 react with excess O2?\n2H2 + O2 → 2H2O\n8.0 g H2 × (1 mol H2 / 2.02 g H2) = 3.96 mol H2\n3.96 mol H2 × (2 mol H2O / 2 mol H2) = 3.96 mol H2O\n3.96 mol H2O × (18.02 g / 1 mol) = 71.4 g H2O\nanswer: 71 g H2O (2 sig figs)\n2) 25.0 g of Al reacts with 40.0 g of Cl2. Which is the limiting reactant and how much AlCl3 forms?\n2Al + 3Cl2 → 2AlCl3\nAl: 25.0 g / 26.98 g/mol = 0.927 mol Al\nCl2: 40.0 g / 70.90 g/mol = 0.564 mol Cl2\nfrom Al: 0.927 mol Al × (2 mol AlCl3 / 2 mol Al) = 0.927 mol AlCl3\nfrom Cl2: 0.564 mol Cl2 × (2 mol AlCl3 / 3 mol Cl2) = 0.376 mol AlCl3\nCl2 is limiting (makes less)\n0.376 mol × 133.33 g/mol = 50.1 g AlCl3\n3) percent yield if 42.6 g AlCl3 is actually collected\n% yield = actual / theoretical × 100\n= 42.6 / 50.1 × 100 = 85.0%" },
  { kind: "math", name: "physics projectile motion homework with given/find steps", text: "Physics HW 3.4 - Projectile Motion\n#2. A ball is kicked from the ground at 18 m/s at an angle of 35° above horizontal. Find (a) time in the air, (b) max height, (c) range.\nGiven: v0 = 18 m/s, θ = 35°, g = 9.8 m/s^2\nv0x = 18cos35 = 18(0.819) = 14.7 m/s\nv0y = 18sin35 = 18(0.574) = 10.3 m/s\n(a) time to top: vy = v0y - gt\n0 = 10.3 - 9.8t\nt = 1.05 s\ntotal time = 2(1.05) = 2.10 s\n(b) max height: vy^2 = v0y^2 - 2gΔy\n0 = (10.3)^2 - 2(9.8)Δy\nΔy = 106.1 / 19.6\nΔy = 5.41 m\n(c) range: x = v0x · t\nx = 14.7 × 2.10\nx = 30.9 m\n#3. A rock is thrown horizontally off a 20 m cliff at 6 m/s. How far from the base does it land?\nvertical: Δy = ½gt^2\n20 = 4.9t^2\nt^2 = 4.08\nt = 2.02 s\nhorizontal: x = 6(2.02) = 12.1 m" },
  { kind: "math", name: "geometry two-column congruence proofs with a teacher note and paragraph version", text: "Geometry - Ch. 4 Proofs\nProof #6\nGiven: AB ≅ CB, BD bisects ∠ABC\nProve: △ABD ≅ △CBD\nStatements\tReasons\n1. AB ≅ CB\t1. Given\n2. BD bisects ∠ABC\t2. Given\n3. ∠ABD ≅ ∠CBD\t3. Definition of angle bisector\n4. BD ≅ BD\t4. Reflexive Property of Congruence\n5. △ABD ≅ △CBD\t5. SAS\nProof #7\nGiven: △ABD ≅ △CBD (from #6)\nProve: D is the midpoint of AC\nStatements\tReasons\n1. △ABD ≅ △CBD\t1. Given\n2. AD ≅ CD\t2. CPCTC\n3. D is the midpoint of AC\t3. Definition of midpoint\n** Mr. Lee said #7 is missing a step - need to say D is on AC?? ask tomorrow\nParagraph version of #6 (extra credit):\nSince AB ≅ CB is given and BD bisects ∠ABC, the angles ∠ABD and ∠CBD are congruent by the definition of an angle bisector. BD is congruent to itself by the reflexive property. So the two triangles have two pairs of congruent sides and the included angles are congruent, which means △ABD ≅ △CBD by SAS." },
];
